const MC_QUESTIONS = [{"id": 1, "chapter": "第一章 探索性分析", "topic": "单变量数据分析", "stem": "One element in the dotplot above is labeled X. Which of the\nstatements below is true?", "options": {"A": "X has the smallest z-score, in absolute value, of any element in\nthe set.", "B": "X has the largest z-score, in absolute value, of any element in the\nset.", "C": "X has the smallest value of any element in the set.", "D": "A boxplot will plot X as an isolated point.", "E": "A back-to-back stemplot will show two symmetric clusters.\nCHAPTER\n1"}, "image": "img/q1.png", "answer": "A", "explanation": "点图左右两簇对称，X 位于中心（即均值附近），其 z 分数约为 0，是全组绝对值最小的。"}, {"id": 2, "chapter": "第一章 探索性分析", "topic": "单变量数据分析", "stem": "The histogram shown above might correspond to which of the\nboxplots below?", "options": {"A": "", "B": "", "C": "", "D": "", "E": ""}, "image": "img/q2.png", "answer": "B", "explanation": "直方图左偏（50–60 最高、向左拖尾）：最小值 0、Q1≈26、中位数≈45、Q3≈54。选项 B 的箱线图须自 0、箱体 20–50、中位数位于箱体右侧 2/3 处，最吻合。"}, {"id": 3, "chapter": "第一章 探索性分析", "topic": "单变量数据分析", "stem": "All students at a private middle school are required to sign up for\none of the three clubs: chess, drama, or math. The graph below\nshows the distribution of participation by grade level.\nWhich of the following statements is incorrect?", "options": {"A": "30 percent of the 6th graders joined the math club.", "B": "The number of 8th graders who joined the chess club is the same\nas the number of 8th graders who joined the math club.", "C": "The number of 6th graders who joined the drama club is the\nsame as the number of 7th graders who joined the drama club.", "D": "The percent of 8th graders who joined the math club is twice the\npercent of 7th graders who joined the math club.", "E": "The percent of 6th graders who joined the chess club is the same\nas the percent of 8th graders who joined the drama club."}, "image": "img/q3.png", "answer": "C", "explanation": "分段条形图中六年级与七年级戏剧俱乐部都占 50%，但两年级总人数未必相同，人数相等的说法不成立；其余选项均可由图直接验证为真。"}, {"id": 4, "chapter": "第一章 探索性分析", "topic": "单变量数据分析", "stem": "Which of the following is a true statement about stemplots?", "options": {"A": "Stemplots are equally useful for small and for very large data\nsets.", "B": "Stemplots are useful both for quantitative and for categorical\ndata sets.", "C": "Stemplots can show symmetry, gaps, clusters, and outliers.", "D": "Stems should be skipped only if there is no data value for a\nparticular stem.", "E": "Whether or not to provide a key depends on the relative\nimportance of the data."}, "image": null, "answer": "C", "explanation": "茎叶图能显示对称、缺口、簇和离群值；A（大样本不适用）、B（仅适用定量数据）、D（空茎也须保留）、E（必须提供 key）均错误。"}, {"id": 5, "chapter": "第一章 探索性分析", "topic": "单变量数据分析", "stem": "Which of the following is incorrect?", "options": {"A": "In histograms, frequencies can be determined from relative\nheights.", "B": "In histograms, relative areas correspond to relative frequencies.", "C": "Boxplots, dotplots, stemplots, and histograms can all show\nskewness.", "D": "Sets with different distribution shapes can have identical\nboxplots.", "E": "Both dotplots and stemplots can show symmetry, gaps, clusters,\nand outliers."}, "image": null, "answer": "A", "explanation": "直方图中频率由面积而非单纯高度决定（组距不等时高度失效），A 错误；B、C、D、E 都是正确说法。"}, {"id": 6, "chapter": "第一章 探索性分析", "topic": "单变量数据分析", "stem": "If three stations are selling gas for $2.10 per gallon, six are\nselling gas for $2.05 per gallon, and ten are selling gas for $2.00\nper gallon, what is the median price per gallon among these\nstations?", "options": {"A": "$2.00", "B": "$2.025", "C": "$2.0316", "D": "$2.05", "E": "$2.075"}, "image": null, "answer": "A", "explanation": "19 个站点排序：10 个 2.00、6 个 2.05、3 个 2.10，中位数为第 10 个值，即 2.00 美元。"}, {"id": 7, "chapter": "第一章 探索性分析", "topic": "单变量数据分析", "stem": "Suppose the average score of a national exam is 1,000 with a\nstandard deviation of 200. If each score is increased by 50 and\nthen each result is increased by 5 percent, what are the new mean\nand standard deviation?", "options": {"A": "μ = 1,100 and σ = 200", "B": "μ = 1,100 and σ = 210", "C": "μ = 1,102.5 and σ = 200", "D": "μ = 1,102.5 and σ = 210", "E": "μ = 1,102.5 and σ = 212.5"}, "image": null, "answer": "D", "explanation": "新分数 = 1.05×(原分+50)：均值 = 1.05×1050 = 1102.5；线性变换后 SD = 1.05×200 = 210。"}, {"id": 8, "chapter": "第一章 探索性分析", "topic": "单变量数据分析", "stem": "For which of the following variables would it be most\nappropriate to construct a histogram?", "options": {"A": "Television brand", "B": "Insect species", "C": "Gender", "D": "Eye color", "E": "Phone call length"}, "image": null, "answer": "E", "explanation": "通话时长是定量变量，可作直方图；电视品牌、物种、性别、眼睛颜色都是分类变量。"}, {"id": 9, "chapter": "第一章 探索性分析", "topic": "单变量数据分析", "stem": "If the standard deviation of a set of observations is 0, you can\nconclude", "options": {"A": "that a mistake in arithmetic has been made", "B": "that there is no relationship among the observations", "C": "that the average value is 0", "D": "that all observations are the same value", "E": "none of the above"}, "image": null, "answer": "D", "explanation": "标准差为 0 表示没有变异，即所有观测值都相同。"}, {"id": 10, "chapter": "第一章 探索性分析", "topic": "单变量数据分析", "stem": "To which of the histograms below can the following boxplot\ncorrespond?", "options": {"A": "", "B": "", "C": "", "D": "", "E": ""}, "image": "img/q10.png", "answer": "B", "explanation": "箱线图：中位数≈11、Q1≈5、Q3≈50、最大≈60。选项 B（0–10 高柱、10–50 低平、50–60 略高）的 Q3≈49、最大 60、中位数略高于 10，与箱线图吻合；A 无 50 以上数据、Q3≈28。"}, {"id": 11, "chapter": "第一章 探索性分析", "topic": "单变量数据分析", "stem": "Given that the median of a set is 350 and the interquartile range\nis 50, which of the following statements is true?", "options": {"A": "Fifty percent of the data are less than or equal to 350.", "B": "Fifty percent of the data are between 325 and 375.", "C": "Seventy-five percent of the data are less than or equal to 375.", "D": "The mean is between 325 and 375.", "E": "All of the above statements are true."}, "image": null, "answer": "A", "explanation": "中位数 350 表示 50% 的数据不大于 350；IQR=50 并不意味着 Q1=325、Q3=375，故 B、C 不必然成立，均值位置也无法确定。"}, {"id": 12, "chapter": "第一章 探索性分析", "topic": "单变量数据分析", "stem": "The 15 tenure track professors in a university math department\nare paid an average of $90,000 per year, while the 10 adjunct\nprofessors are paid an average of $60,000 per year. What is the\naverage salary among all these math professors?", "options": {"A": "$72,000", "B": "$75,000", "C": "$78,000", "D": "$81,000", "E": "$84,000"}, "image": null, "answer": "C", "explanation": "(15×90000 + 10×60000)/25 = 1950000/25 = 78000 美元。"}, {"id": 13, "chapter": "第一章 探索性分析", "topic": "单变量数据分析", "stem": "Which of the following statements about histograms is true?", "options": {"A": "Two students working with the same set of data may come up\nwith histograms that look different.", "B": "Histograms of categorical variables can pinpoint clusters and\ngaps.", "C": "Unlike other graphs, histogram axes do not need to be labeled.", "D": "Displaying outliers is less problematic when using histograms\nthan when using stemplots.", "E": "Histograms are more widely used than stemplots or dotplots\nbecause histograms display the values of individual\nobservations."}, "image": null, "answer": "A", "explanation": "同一组数据因分组方式不同可以画出不同的直方图，A 正确；直方图只适用定量数据、坐标轴必须标注、单个观测值信息会丢失，其余均错误。"}, {"id": 14, "chapter": "第一章 探索性分析", "topic": "单变量数据分析", "stem": "Which of the following statements about the above two dotplots\nis true?", "options": {"A": "The mean of set A looks to be greater than the mean of set B.", "B": "The mean of set B looks to be greater than the mean of set A.", "C": "Both sets have roughly the same variance.", "D": "The empirical rule applies only to set A.", "E": "The standard deviation of set B is less than 4."}, "image": null, "answer": "E", "explanation": "B 组对称单峰、取值约在 5–15 之间，标准差约 2–2.5＜4；两组均值都约为 10，A 组双峰分布在两端、方差更大。"}, {"id": 15, "chapter": "第一章 探索性分析", "topic": "单变量数据分析", "stem": "Students who apply for graduate school usually must take the\nGRE exam. The following boxplots were constructed from GRE\nquantitative scores (which take values between 130 and 170) of\nmen and women at a state college.\nWhich of the following is a possible boxplot for the combined\nscore of all the students?", "options": {"A": "", "B": "", "C": "", "D": "", "E": ""}, "image": "img/q15.png", "answer": "E", "explanation": "合并后的最小值取两组较小须端（≈141）、最大值取较大须端（≈164），Q1、中位数、Q3 介于两组对应统计量之间；选项 E（须约 141–164、箱约 148–157.5、中位数≈153）最吻合。"}, {"id": 16, "chapter": "第一章 探索性分析", "topic": "单变量数据分析", "stem": "The histograms below give the heights of players in two amateur\nbasketball leagues.\nWhich one of the following statements is true?", "options": {"A": "The distribution of heights in League A has a lower mean and\nlower range than the distribution of heights in League B.", "B": "The distribution of heights in League A has a lower mean and\napproximately the same range as the distribution of heights in\nLeague B.", "C": "The distribution of heights in League A has a higher mean and\nlower range than the distribution of heights in League B.", "D": "The distribution of heights in League A has a higher mean and\nhigher range than the distribution of heights in League B.", "E": "The distribution of heights in League A has a higher mean and\napproximately the same range as the distribution of heights in\nLeague B."}, "image": "img/q16.png", "answer": "B", "explanation": "League A 峰值在 72、League B 在 74，A 的均值更低；两者取值范围都约为 4 英寸（70–74 与 72–76），范围大致相同。"}, {"id": 17, "chapter": "第一章 探索性分析", "topic": "单变量数据分析", "stem": "Given these parallel boxplots, which of the statements below is\nincorrect?", "options": {"A": "The ranges are the same.", "B": "The interquartile ranges are the same.", "C": "Both sets have left and right skews.", "D": "It is possible that set A does not have a symmetric or even a\nroughly symmetric distribution.", "E": "It is possible that set A has 1,000 times as many values as set B."}, "image": "img/q17.png", "answer": "C", "explanation": "每个箱线图只能反映单侧偏态，两图基本对称，不存在同时左偏右偏的情况，C 错误；箱线图不显示分布形状和样本量，D、E 均可能为真。"}, {"id": 18, "chapter": "第一章 探索性分析", "topic": "单变量数据分析", "stem": "Suppose the scores on an exam have a mean of 80 with a standard\ndeviation of 5. If one student has a test result with a z-score of –\n1.4 and a second student has a test result with a z-score of 2.6,\nhow many points higher was the second student’s result than that\nof the first?", "options": {"A": "6", "B": "7", "C": "13", "D": "19", "E": "20"}, "image": "img/q18.png", "answer": "E", "explanation": "分数差 = (z2−z1)×SD = (2.6−(−1.4))×5 = 4×5 = 20 分。"}, {"id": 19, "chapter": "第一章 探索性分析", "topic": "单变量数据分析", "stem": "Each of the high school students in a random sample was given\nfive minutes to solve as many short puzzles as possible. The\nresults by gender are given in the dotplots below.\nWhich of the following is a true statement?", "options": {"A": "One distribution is roughly symmetric; the other is skewed right.", "B": "The difference in their means is less than the difference in their\nmedians.", "C": "The ranges are both 6.", "D": "The standard deviations are roughly the same.", "E": "The range of the entire set of results is equal to the sum of the\nindividual ranges of the two gender specific sets."}, "image": "img/q19.png", "answer": "B", "explanation": "由点图计算：女生均值≈4.42、中位数 5；男生均值≈2.33、中位数 2。均值差≈2.09 小于中位数差 3，B 正确；两组极差都是 5 而非 6。"}, {"id": 20, "chapter": "第一章 探索性分析", "topic": "单变量数据分析", "stem": "A business magazine plans to compare the entry-level salaries\noffered to applicants with and without an MBA degree. Which of\nthe following graphical displays is inappropriate?", "options": {"A": "Side-by-side histograms", "B": "Back-to-back stemplot", "C": "Parallel boxplots", "D": "Scatterplot", "E": "All of the above displays are appropriate."}, "image": "img/q20.png", "answer": "D", "explanation": "散点图用于展示两个定量变量之间的关系，不适合比较有/无 MBA 两组各自的单变量分布；其余三种都合适。"}, {"id": 21, "chapter": "第一章 探索性分析", "topic": "单变量数据分析", "stem": "Given this back-to-back stemplot, which of the following\nstatements is incorrect?", "options": {"A": "The distributions have the same mean.", "B": "The distributions have the same interquartile range.", "C": "The distributions have the same range.", "D": "The distributions have the same standard deviation.", "E": "The distributions have the same variance."}, "image": null, "answer": "A", "explanation": "A 组均值≈377.7、B 组均值≈415.4，均值不同，A 错误；两组极差都是 55（351–406 与 361–416），IQR 也同为 26。"}, {"id": 22, "chapter": "第一章 探索性分析", "topic": "单变量数据分析", "stem": "Which of the following statements about the range is incorrect?", "options": {"A": "The range is a single number, not an interval of values.", "B": "Although the range is affected by outliers, the interquartile range\nis not.", "C": "The interquartile range is the range of the middle half of the\ndata.", "D": "Changing the order from ascending to descending changes the\nsign of the range.", "E": "The range of the sample data can never be greater than the range\nof the population."}, "image": null, "answer": "D", "explanation": "range = max−min 与数据排序顺序无关，不会变号，D 错误；其余关于极差和 IQR 的说法都正确。"}, {"id": 23, "chapter": "第一章 探索性分析", "topic": "单变量数据分析", "stem": "A college statistics professor gives only two exams during the\nsemester, a midterm and a final. The professor grades with z\nscores. On the final exam, a student received a grade of –2.8.\nWhat is the correct interpretation of this grade?", "options": {"A": "The student scored 2.8 points lower on the final than on the\nmidterm.", "B": "The student scored 2.8 points lower than the class average on the\nfinal.", "C": "The student scored 2.8 standard deviations lower on the final\nthan on the midterm.", "D": "The student scored 2.8 standard deviations lower on the final\nthan the class average on the midterm exam.", "E": "The student scored 2.8 standard deviations lower on the final\nthan the class average on the final exam."}, "image": null, "answer": "E", "explanation": "z=−2.8 只针对期末考试本科目分布：该生成绩比期末考试的班级平均低 2.8 个标准差。"}, {"id": 24, "chapter": "第一章 探索性分析", "topic": "单变量数据分析", "stem": "Given the two histograms above, which of the following\nstatements is incorrect?", "options": {"A": "As long as the sample sizes are at least 30, the empirical rule\napplies to both sets.", "B": "Both sets have roughly the same ranges.", "C": "The standard deviation of set A is greater than 5.", "D": "The standard deviation of set B is greater than 5.", "E": "A lower percentage of the values in set A are at least 10 units\nfrom its mean than is the case for set B."}, "image": null, "answer": "A", "explanation": "经验法则要求分布近似正态（钟形），与样本量是否≥30 无关，A 错误；两组范围均约 70–110；A 近似正态 SD≈40/6＞5，B 呈 U 形、数据集中两端 SD 更大；E 亦正确。"}, {"id": 25, "chapter": "第一章 探索性分析", "topic": "单变量数据分析", "stem": "Given the cumulative frequency plot above, what is the value of\nthe mean?", "options": {"A": "The mean is between 75 and 80.", "B": "The mean is between 70 and 75.", "C": "The mean equals 70.", "D": "The mean is less than 70.", "E": "From such a plot, one can determine the median, but nothing can\nbe concluded about the mean."}, "image": null, "answer": "D", "explanation": "累积频率曲线在最右端最陡，说明数据集中在高值端（左偏）：中位数（累积 0.5）约在 x≈70 附近，左偏分布均值小于中位数，故均值小于 70。"}, {"id": 26, "chapter": "第一章 探索性分析", "topic": "单变量数据分析", "stem": "A random sample of scores at a bowling alley one day gives the\nfollowing summary statistics:\nn = 26, ¯x = 132.34, s = 10.18, min = 113, Q1 = 126,\nmed = 134.5, Q3 = 139, and max = 160\nHow many outliers are there?", "options": {"A": "0", "B": "1", "C": "2", "D": "At least 1", "E": "At least 2"}, "image": "img/q26.png", "answer": "D", "explanation": "IQR = 139−126 = 13，上界限 = 139+1.5×13 = 158.5。max = 160 > 158.5 必为离群值；Q3 以上的其他数值未知，可能还有更多，故至少 1 个。"}, {"id": 27, "chapter": "第一章 探索性分析", "topic": "单变量数据分析", "stem": "Which of the following distributions has a mean of 75 and a\nstandard deviation of approximately 5?", "options": {"A": "", "B": "", "C": "", "D": "", "E": ""}, "image": "img/q27.png", "answer": "A", "explanation": "钟形正态曲线两端约在 ±3SD 处趋于零：60 和 90 距中心 75 各 15 = 3×5，即 SD≈5；其余图对应的 SD 分别约 3.3、1.7，或中心不为 75。"}, {"id": 28, "chapter": "第一章 探索性分析", "topic": "单变量数据分析", "stem": "Assuming that weights of sumo wrestlers follow a bell-shaped\ndistribution, how are the weights of the following wrestlers\narranged in ascending order?\nI. A weight with a z-score of 1\nII. A weight with a percentile rank of 80 percent\nIII. A weight at the third quartile", "options": {"A": "I, II, III", "B": "I, III, II", "C": "II, I, III", "D": "III, I, II", "E": "III, II, I"}, "image": null, "answer": "E", "explanation": "钟形分布：Q3 为 75 百分位（z≈0.67），80 百分位对应 z≈0.84，z=1 即 84 百分位；按数值升序为 III, II, I。"}, {"id": 29, "chapter": "第一章 探索性分析", "topic": "单变量数据分析", "stem": "A set has 10 outliers. If every value of this set is increased by 5\npercent, how many outliers will there now be?", "options": {"A": "Fewer than 10", "B": "10", "C": "11", "D": "More than 11", "E": "It is impossible to determine without further information."}, "image": "img/q29.png", "answer": "B", "explanation": "每个值乘 1.05 是线性缩放，Q1、Q3 与 1.5IQR 界限同比例缩放，离群值还是原来那些，个数不变为 10。"}, {"id": 30, "chapter": "第一章 探索性分析", "topic": "单变量数据分析", "stem": "The distribution of the cost of a family’s dine-out meal has a\nmean of $55.30 and a standard deviation of $4.00. If they always\ntip $5 plus 10 percent of the meal, what are the mean and\nstandard deviation of the distribution of tips?", "options": {"A": "Mean = $5.00 and standard deviation = $0.40", "B": "Mean = $5.40 and standard deviation = $4.40", "C": "Mean = $5.53 and standard deviation = $0.40", "D": "Mean = $10.53 and standard deviation = $0.40", "E": "Mean = $10.53 and standard deviation = $4.40"}, "image": null, "answer": "D", "explanation": "小费 = 5 + 0.10×餐费：均值 = 5 + 0.10×55.30 = 10.53 美元；SD = 0.10×4.00 = 0.40 美元。"}, {"id": 31, "chapter": "第一章 探索性分析", "topic": "单变量数据分析", "stem": "Male elk living in the wild have a mean weight of 720 pounds\nwith a standard deviation of 32 pounds. Female elk living in the\nwild have a mean weight of 515 pounds with a standard deviation\nof 20 pounds. What is the weight of a male elk with the same\nstandardized score (z-score) as a female elk with a weight of 500\npounds?", "options": {"A": "696 pounds", "B": "704 pounds", "C": "705 pounds", "D": "735 pounds", "E": "752 pounds"}, "image": null, "answer": "A", "explanation": "雌鹿 z = (500−515)/20 = −0.75；雄鹿重量 = 720 + (−0.75)×32 = 696 磅。"}, {"id": 32, "chapter": "第一章 探索性分析", "topic": "单变量数据分析", "stem": "A psychology student measures the times each of five mice takes\nto solve a maze and reach the food reward. Which of the\nfollowing statistics are probably most useful in describing the\ndistribution of the five times?", "options": {"A": "Mean and standard deviation", "B": "Mean and variance", "C": "Mean and interquartile range", "D": "Median and standard deviation", "E": "Median and interquartile range"}, "image": null, "answer": "E", "explanation": "样本仅 5 只且走迷宫时间通常右偏、可能含离群值，宜用抗性强的中位数和 IQR 描述。"}, {"id": 33, "chapter": "第一章 探索性分析", "topic": "单变量数据分析", "stem": "Which of the following is a true statement?", "options": {"A": "All symmetric bell-shaped curves are normal.", "B": "All symmetric histograms have single peaks.", "C": "All normal curves are bell-shaped and symmetric.", "D": "If the mean equals the median, the distribution is symmetric.", "E": "None of the above are true statements."}, "image": null, "answer": "C", "explanation": "所有正态曲线都是钟形且对称的；钟形对称曲线未必是正态，对称直方图可以双峰，均值等于中位数也不能保证对称。"}, {"id": 34, "chapter": "第一章 探索性分析", "topic": "单变量数据分析", "stem": "The age distribution of 343 students who attended a hip-hop\nconcert is shown below.\nWhich of the following could be the median age?", "options": {"A": "15.8", "B": "16.5", "C": "17.1", "D": "18.2", "E": "18.5"}, "image": "img/q34.png", "answer": "C", "explanation": "各年龄段人数为 18, 25, 42, 65, 93, 100（合计 343）。累积至 16 岁为 85、至 17 岁为 150，中位数为第 172 个值，落在 17–18 组：17 + (172−150)/93 ≈ 17.2，最接近 17.1。"}, {"id": 35, "chapter": "第一章 探索性分析", "topic": "单变量数据分析", "stem": "Given this cumulative plot of the number of Instagram pictures\nsent in a week by the students in an AP Art class and using the\nmost commonly accepted definition of outliers, what numbers of\nInstagram pictures are considered outliers?", "options": {"A": "Between 20 and 25", "B": "Between 20 and 30", "C": "Between 20 and 40", "D": "Between 20 and 25, or between 55 and 60", "E": "Between 20 and 30, or between 50 and 60"}, "image": null, "answer": "A", "explanation": "由累积频率图读出 Q1≈40、Q3≈50，IQR≈10，下界限 ≈ 40−15 = 25：20 到约 25 之间的值低于下界为离群值；上界限 ≈ 65 > 60（最大值），无上侧离群值。"}, {"id": 36, "chapter": "第一章 探索性分析", "topic": "单变量数据分析", "stem": "If a distribution is perfectly symmetric, which of the following\nmust be true?", "options": {"A": "The distribution is uniform.", "B": "The mean and median are equal.", "C": "The interquartile range is equal to twice the standard deviation.", "D": "The range is twice the interquartile range.", "E": "The range is equal to six times the standard deviation."}, "image": null, "answer": "B", "explanation": "分布完全对称时均值与中位数必然相等；对称分布未必均匀，IQR、极差与 SD 之间也没有固定的倍数关系。"}, {"id": 37, "chapter": "第一章 探索性分析", "topic": "单变量数据分析", "stem": "Given the histogram above, what values would be considered\noutliers?", "options": {"A": "Values between 51 and 57", "B": "Values between 55 and 57", "C": "Values between 19 and 20, and values between 50 and 57", "D": "Values between 19 and 25, and values between 45 and 57", "E": "No value is an outlier."}, "image": null, "answer": "B", "explanation": "由图：Q1=30、中位数=35、Q3=40，IQR=10，界限为 30−15=15 和 40+15=55。最小 19 未越下界，55–57 之间的值越上界为离群值。"}, {"id": 38, "chapter": "第一章 探索性分析", "topic": "单变量数据分析", "stem": "On a large campus, 500 students were surveyed about their belief\nin an afterlife. The following table shows their responses\ncategorized by class.\nBelieve in\nAfterlife\nFreshmen\n95\n30\nSophomores\n87\n29\nJuniors\n76\n51\nSeniors\n35\n97\nOf the following graphical displays, which is the most\nappropriate to compare the proportions of students in each class\nwho believe in an afterlife?", "options": {"A": "Back-to-back stemplot", "B": "Parallel boxplots", "C": "Scatterplot", "D": "Segmented bar chart", "E": "Cumulative frequency plot"}, "image": "img/q38.png", "answer": "D", "explanation": "要比较各类别（年级）中相信来世的占比，最合适的是分段条形图（展示每组内部的比例构成）。"}, {"id": 39, "chapter": "第一章 探索性分析", "topic": "单变量数据分析", "stem": "If quartiles Q1 = 25 and Q3 = 45, which of the following must be\ntrue?", "options": {"A": "The median is 35.", "B": "The mean is between 25 and 45.", "C": "The standard deviation is at most 20.", "D": "The range must be greater than 20.", "E": "None must be true."}, "image": null, "answer": "E", "explanation": "Q1=25、Q3=45 只给出 IQR=20：中位数不必是 35，均值、SD 无必然结论，极差可以恰好等于 20（最小值=Q1、最大值=Q3），故都不必然为真。"}, {"id": 40, "chapter": "第一章 探索性分析", "topic": "单变量数据分析", "stem": "A data set includes two outliers, one at each end. If both these\noutliers are removed, which of the following is a possible result?", "options": {"A": "Both the mean and median remain unchanged.", "B": "Both the mean and standard deviation remain unchanged.\nDo Not Believe in\nAfterlife", "C": "Both the median and standard deviation remain unchanged.", "D": "Both the mean and standard deviation increase.", "E": "Both the median and standard deviation decrease."}, "image": null, "answer": "A", "explanation": "可能：如 {0, 50, 50, 100}，删去两端的 0 和 100 后均值仍为 50、中位数仍为 50。删除极端值总会改变标准差，故含 SD 的选项不可能。"}, {"id": 41, "chapter": "第一章 探索性分析", "topic": "单变量数据分析", "stem": "The histogram shown above is of 52 test scores. Which of\nfollowing statements is true?", "options": {"A": "The median score was 75.", "B": "If 60 and below is failing, most students failed.", "C": "More students scored above 80 than below 60.", "D": "More students scored below the median than above the median.", "E": "The mean score is probably greater than the median score."}, "image": null, "answer": "E", "explanation": "各组人数约 20, 8, 8, 8, 8（合计 52）。高峰在 50–60、向右均匀拖尾，分布右偏：均值≈70.4 ＞ 中位数≈68，E 正确；中位数约 68 而非 75，及格者占 62%，80 分以上 16 人少于 60 分以下 20 人。"}, {"id": 42, "chapter": "第一章 探索性分析", "topic": "单变量数据分析", "stem": "The following parallel boxplots show the birth weights during\none month at four hospitals.\nAverage birth weight is considered to be seven pounds (112\nounces). In which hospital were a greater number of newborns\nborn weighing between 102 and 122 ounces?", "options": {"A": "A", "B": "B", "C": "C", "D": "D", "E": "There is insufficient information to answer."}, "image": "img/q42.png", "answer": "E", "explanation": "箱线图只提供五数概括，未给出各医院当月接生总数，人数（而非比例）无法比较，信息不足。"}, {"id": 43, "chapter": "第一章 探索性分析", "topic": "单变量数据分析", "stem": "Counts of the numbers of spam messages received by a\ncompany’s executives during one week are summarized below.\nValues Under\nQ1\nQ1\nMedian\nQ3\n7, 9, 10\n15.5\n20\n21.5\n28, 30, 31\nHow many outliers are there?", "options": {"A": "0", "B": "1", "C": "2", "D": "4", "E": "6"}, "image": "img/q43.png", "answer": "B", "explanation": "IQR = 21.5−15.5 = 6，界限为 15.5−9 = 6.5 和 21.5+9 = 30.5。Q1 以下 7、9、10 均未越界；Q3 以上仅 31 > 30.5，共 1 个离群值。"}, {"id": 44, "chapter": "第一章 探索性分析", "topic": "单变量数据分析", "stem": "Which of the following is a true statement?", "options": {"A": "If the mean and median of a set are equal, then the variance is\nzero.", "B": "If a set has variance zero, then the mean, median, and standard\ndeviation are equal.", "C": "If the population mean and median are equal, then this will also\nbe true for any sample that is randomly selected from the\npopulation.\nValues Over\nQ3", "D": "If the population has variance zero, then the variance of any\nsample is also zero.", "E": "If the variance of a randomly selected sample is zero, then the\npopulation mean and median are equal."}, "image": null, "answer": "D", "explanation": "总体方差为 0 时总体中每个值都相同，任一样本的方差必为 0；B 错误：如 {5,5,5} 方差为 0 但均值/中位数为 5 而 SD 为 0，三者并非相等。"}, {"id": 45, "chapter": "第一章 探索性分析", "topic": "单变量数据分析", "stem": "When there are multiple gaps and clusters, which of the\nfollowing is the best choice to give an overall picture of the\ndistribution?", "options": {"A": "Mean and standard deviation", "B": "Mean and interquartile range", "C": "Boxplot with its five-number summary", "D": "Stemplot or histogram", "E": "None of the above are really helpful in showing gaps and\nclusters."}, "image": null, "answer": "D", "explanation": "存在多个缺口和簇时需要观察整个分布形状，茎叶图或直方图最合适；均值、SD 及五数概括都会掩盖这些特征。"}, {"id": 46, "chapter": "第一章 探索性分析", "topic": "单变量数据分析", "stem": "The mean length of human pregnancies is 266 days with a\nstandard deviation of 16 days. Which of the following is the best\ndescription of the standard deviation?", "options": {"A": "Approximately the median time between the lengths of\nindividual pregnancies and 266 days", "B": "Approximately the mean time between the lengths of individual\npregnancies and 266 days", "C": "Approximately the square root of the mean time between the\nlengths of individual pregnancies and 266 days", "D": "One-third the time between the maximum individual length of\npregnancies and 266", "E": "One-sixth the time between the maximum and minimum\nindividual lengths of pregnancies"}, "image": null, "answer": "B", "explanation": "标准差大致是各个妊娠天数与均值 266 天之间距离的平均水平（典型偏差）。"}, {"id": 47, "chapter": "第一章 探索性分析", "topic": "单变量数据分析", "stem": "In which of the following histograms is the mean greater than the\nmedian?", "options": {"A": "", "B": "", "C": "", "D": "", "E": ""}, "image": "img/q47.png", "answer": "B", "explanation": "B 图高峰在左、向右拖尾（右偏），均值＞中位数；C 图相反（左偏），A 图近似均匀、两者接近。"}, {"id": 48, "chapter": "第一章 探索性分析", "topic": "单变量数据分析", "stem": "Ms. D teaches three sections of AP Statistics with a total of 77\nstudents. Her students did exceptionally well on the final exam.\nSome summary statistics and a histogram of the exam scores are\nshown below.\nMean\nStandard Deviation\nQ1\nQ3\n93.4\n2.6\n92\n95\nUsing the standard definition of outlier, which of the following\ncan be concluded?", "options": {"A": "The mean is greater than the median, and there is only one\noutlier.", "B": "The mean is greater than the median, and there are two outliers.", "C": "The mean is less than the median, and there are no outliers.", "D": "The mean is less than the median, and there is only one outlier.", "E": "The mean is less than the median, and there are two outliers."}, "image": "img/q48.png", "answer": "D", "explanation": "由直方图可读出中位数 = 94 ＞ 均值 93.4；用给定 Q1=92、Q3=95，IQR=3，下界限 = 92−4.5 = 87.5，只有 85 越界，即恰有一个离群值。"}, {"id": 49, "chapter": "第一章 探索性分析", "topic": "单变量数据分析", "stem": "The students from each of two schools were asked how many\nNobel Peace Prize winners they could name. The results are\nshown in the following two histograms.\nWhich school has the greater median with regard to the number\nof Nobel Peace Prize winners students could name, and which\nschool has the greater mean?", "options": {"A": "Greater median: A      Greater mean: A", "B": "Greater median: A      Greater mean: B", "C": "Greater median: B      Greater mean: A", "D": "Greater median: B      Greater mean: B", "E": "Greater median: B      Equal means"}, "image": null, "answer": "A", "explanation": "School A 的柱高度随人数（2→5）递增，多数学生能说出 4–5 人；School B 集中在 0–1 人。故 A 校中位数和均值都更大。"}, {"id": 50, "chapter": "第一章 探索性分析", "topic": "单变量数据分析", "stem": "In 1999, NASA lost the $125 million Mars Climate Orbiter\nspacecraft after a 286-day journey to Mars. Miscalculations due\nto the use of English units instead of metric units apparently sent\nthe craft off course. Suppose a particular measurement from a\nset of measurements in miles has a z-score of 0.85. If all the\nmeasurements are converted to kilometers, what will now be the\nz-score of the particular measurement? (Remember that 1\nkilometer equals 0.621 miles.)", "options": {"A": "0.85", "B": "0.85 miles", "C": "0.85 kilometers", "D": "(0.85)(0.621)", "E": "0.85 + 0.621"}, "image": null, "answer": "A", "explanation": "z 分数是无量纲的相对位置，把所有测量值由英里换算为公里（线性变换）不改变 z 分数，仍为 0.85。"}, {"id": 51, "chapter": "第一章 探索性分析", "topic": "单变量数据分析", "stem": "The prices at which a particular AP Statistics review book are\nsold at 92 online sites are summarized in the table below.\nWhich of the following best describes the distribution of prices?", "options": {"A": "Roughly normal", "B": "Skewed left", "C": "Skewed right", "D": "Skewed both left and right", "E": "Bimodal"}, "image": "img/q51.png", "answer": "C", "explanation": "频数在 12.75 处最高（24），随后向 19.00 缓慢拖尾（5,2,1），分布右偏。"}, {"id": 52, "chapter": "第一章 探索性分析", "topic": "单变量数据分析", "stem": "Given the back-to-back stemplot above, which of the following\nstatements is true?", "options": {"A": "The empirical rule applies to both sets A and B.", "B": "The median of each set is approximately 500 + 550\n2\n.", "C": "The ranges of the two sets are equal.", "D": "The variances of the two sets are approximately the same.", "E": "In one set, the mean and median should be about the same. In the\nother set, the mean appears to be greater than the median."}, "image": null, "answer": "E", "explanation": "A 组集中在低段、右侧拖尾（右偏），均值≈519 ＞ 中位数≈515.5；B 组大致对称，均值≈529 ≈ 中位数 530。两组极差为 51 与 52 并不相等。"}, {"id": 53, "chapter": "第一章 探索性分析", "topic": "单变量数据分析", "stem": "The following histogram displays the top speeds of several roller\ncoasters.\nWhich of the following statements must be true?", "options": {"A": "The minimum and maximum top speeds are 30 mph and 100\nmph, respectively.", "B": "The median top speed is 60.5 mph.", "C": "At least 100 more roller coasters have top speeds between 70 and\n80 mph than between 30 and 40 mph.", "D": "More roller coasters have top speeds between 70 and 80 then\nhave top speeds over 80 mph.", "E": "The same number of roller coasters have top speeds between 50\nand 60 mph as have top speeds between 80 and 90 mph."}, "image": "img/q53.png", "answer": "E", "explanation": "图中 50–60 与 80–90 两根条高度完全相同，故人数必相同；直方图无法给出确切的最小/最大值与中位数，也无法保证数量差达到 100；70–80（高度最高）反而少于 80 以上两组之和。"}, {"id": 54, "chapter": "第一章 探索性分析", "topic": "单变量数据分析", "stem": "Summary statistics for the number of campsites at public parks\nin a large western state are as follows.\nMinimum\nQ1\nMedian\nQ3\nMaximum\nMean\n0\n35\n61\n95\n300\n73.2\n58\nA check of the data shows that 5 had been subtracted from every\nvalue to bring the minimum to 0 for some calculation purpose. If\n5 is added back to every value, what will now be the interquartile\nrange and the standard deviation?", "options": {"A": "IQR = 60 and SD = 58", "B": "IQR = 60 and SD = 63", "C": "IQR = 65 and SD = 58", "D": "IQR = 65 and SD = 63", "E": "IQR = 62.5 and SD = 60.5"}, "image": null, "answer": "A", "explanation": "每个值加 5 是平移变换：IQR 与标准差都不变，仍分别为 60 和 58。"}, {"id": 55, "chapter": "第一章 探索性分析", "topic": "单变量数据分析", "stem": "Below are histograms of two data sets.\nStandard\nDeviation\nWhich of the following statements must be true?", "options": {"A": "The medians of the two data sets must be equal.", "B": "The means of the two data sets must be equal.", "C": "The ranges of the two data sets must be equal.", "D": "The standard deviation of data set S is less than the standard\ndeviation of data set T.", "E": "The standard deviation of data set S is greater than the standard\ndeviation of data set T."}, "image": "img/q55.png", "answer": "E", "explanation": "S 呈 U 形（数据集中在两端），T 单峰居中，S 明显更分散，SD_S ＞ SD_T；两者的均值、中位数和极差都不必然相等。"}, {"id": 56, "chapter": "第一章 探索性分析", "topic": "单变量数据分析", "stem": "A study of phone, e-mail, and texting communication in three\ntime periods results in the following segmented bar chart.\nWhich of the following is greatest?", "options": {"A": "The number of texts and e-mails in 2010.", "B": "The number of e-mails in 2014.", "C": "The number of texts in 2018.", "D": "The above are all equal.", "E": "It is impossible to answer this question without knowing the\nactual numbers of individual communications involved."}, "image": "img/q56.png", "answer": "E", "explanation": "分段条形图只显示各年内部百分比构成，不知道各年通信总量就无法比较具体数量。"}, {"id": 57, "chapter": "第一章 探索性分析", "topic": "单变量数据分析", "stem": "A normal distribution with mean 25 and standard deviation 5 is\nstandardized by subtracting the mean from each value and\ndividing the difference by the standard deviation. What are the\nmean and standard deviation of the resulting distribution?", "options": {"A": "Mean = 0, Standard deviation = 1", "B": "Mean = 5, Standard deviation = 1", "C": "Mean = 5, Standard deviation = 5", "D": "Mean = 25, Standard deviation = 1", "E": "Mean = 25, Standard deviation = 5"}, "image": null, "answer": "A", "explanation": "标准化 z = (x−25)/5 是线性变换：均值为 0，标准差为 1。"}, {"id": 58, "chapter": "第一章 探索性分析", "topic": "单变量数据分析", "stem": "A barber measures hair lengths for a random sample of 100 of\nhis customers and gathers data on the growth rates of their hair.\nThe data are plotted in the histogram below.\nWhich of the values below is closest to the standard deviation of\nthe 100 values?", "options": {"A": "0.008", "B": "0.01", "C": "0.03", "D": "0.08", "E": "1.16"}, "image": "img/q58.png", "answer": "D", "explanation": "数据大致对称地分布在 0.92–1.36（范围约 0.44），近似正态时 SD≈范围/5≈0.08。"}, {"id": 59, "chapter": "第一章 探索性分析", "topic": "单变量数据分析", "stem": "A teacher had two AP Statistics classes. The mean score on the\nAP Exam for one class was 3.2, while the mean score for the other\nclass, which had more students, was 3.8. What must be true about\nthe mean score of all of the teacher’s AP students?", "options": {"A": "The mean score for all students must be 3.5.", "B": "It’s possible that the mean score of all students is 3.5, but it\ndoesn’t have to be.", "C": "The mean score for all students can be anything between 3.2 and\n3.8.", "D": "The mean score for all students is less than 3.5 but greater than\n3.2.", "E": "The mean score for all students is greater than 3.5 but less than\n3.8."}, "image": null, "answer": "E", "explanation": "总均值是两个班均值的加权平均，3.8 的班人数更多，权重超过一半，故总均值必介于 3.5 与 3.8 之间。"}, {"id": 60, "chapter": "第一章 探索性分析", "topic": "单变量数据分析", "stem": "Consider the three distributions below.\nOrder the distributions from least to greatest in terms of the\nproportion of data less than the mean.", "options": {"A": "M, L, N", "B": "N, L, M", "C": "M, N, L", "D": "N, M, L", "E": "L, N, M"}, "image": "img/q60.png", "answer": "A", "explanation": "L 对称（低于均值的比例约 50%）；M 右偏（均值被右侧拖尾拉高，低于均值＜50%）；N 左偏（低于均值＞50%）。由小到大：M, L, N。"}, {"id": 61, "chapter": "第一章 探索性分析", "topic": "单变量数据分析", "stem": "The following statistics summarize the fuel economy in miles per\ngallon (mpg) for 50 different car models in 2018.\nMinimum\nQ1\nMedian\nQ3\nMaximum\nMean\n13.5\n21\n24.5\n30\n48.5\n26\n7.32\nWhich of the following is a true statement?", "options": {"A": "50 percent of the mpg values are between 26 and 48.5.", "B": "25 percent of the mpg values are above 21.", "C": "The distribution of mpg values appears to be roughly bell\nshaped.", "D": "The interquartile range of mpg values is 5.", "E": "There is at least one outlier."}, "image": null, "answer": "E", "explanation": "IQR = 30−21 = 9，上界限 = 30+13.5 = 43.5 ＜ 48.5，至少最大值是离群值；IQR 是 9 而非 5；21 是 Q1（75% 的值大于 21）；均值 26＞中位数 24.5，右偏而非钟形。"}, {"id": 62, "chapter": "第一章 探索性分析", "topic": "单变量数据分析", "stem": "College students’ expenses for academic supplies have a mean of\n$1,100 and a standard deviation of $290. Their expenses for\nentertainment have a mean of $950 and a standard deviation of\n$250. Assuming the expenses for academic supplies and for\nentertainment are independent, what are the mean and standard\ndeviation of the total yearly expenditures by college students for\nthese two items?", "options": {"A": "Mean = $1,025 and standard deviation = $270", "B": "Mean = $1,025 and standard deviation = $383", "C": "Mean = $2,050 and standard deviation = $270", "D": "Mean = $2,050 and standard deviation = $383", "E": "Mean = $2,050 and standard deviation = $540"}, "image": null, "answer": "D", "explanation": "均值 = 1100+950 = 2050；独立时方差相加：SD = √(290²+250²) = √146600 ≈ 383。"}, {"id": 63, "chapter": "第一章 探索性分析", "topic": "单变量数据分析", "stem": "Random samples are taken from three populations, and the\nresulting parallel boxplots of the data follows.\nStandard\nDeviation\nWhich of the following statements is true?", "options": {"A": "One of the sample distributions is roughly a normal distribution.", "B": "All three sample distributions have the same interquartile range.", "C": "All three sample distributions have the same range.", "D": "All three sample medians are between 11 and 15.", "E": "All three of the distributions can reasonably be assumed to be of\nsamples from populations with roughly normal distributions."}, "image": null, "answer": "D", "explanation": "三个中位数约 12、13.5、14.5，都在 11 到 15 之间；三个分布的 IQR 和极差明显不同，箱线图也无法证明样本来自正态总体。"}, {"id": 64, "chapter": "第一章 探索性分析", "topic": "单变量数据分析", "stem": "A histogram of the educational level (in number of years of\nschooling) of the adult population of the United States would\nprobably have which of the following characteristics?", "options": {"A": "A roughly normal population", "B": "Skewness to the right", "C": "Symmetry", "D": "A gap around 12 years", "E": "Clusters around 8, 12, and 16 years"}, "image": null, "answer": "E", "explanation": "受教育年限常在 8、12、16 年附近形成簇（分别对应初中、高中、大学毕业节点）。"}, {"id": 65, "chapter": "第一章 探索性分析", "topic": "单变量数据分析", "stem": "When a set of data has suspect outliers, which of the following\nare preferred measures of central tendency and variability?", "options": {"A": "Mean and standard deviation", "B": "Mean and variance", "C": "Mean and range", "D": "Median and range", "E": "Median and interquartile range\nData were collected as to the magnitudes of earthquakes in a\ncertain region over a long time period."}, "image": null, "answer": "E", "explanation": "数据含可疑离群值时，应使用抗性统计量：中位数和四分位距。"}, {"id": 66, "chapter": "第一章 探索性分析", "topic": "单变量数据分析", "stem": "Based on the boxplot above, which of the following is a\nreasonable conclusion about earthquake magnitudes in that\nregion during that time period?", "options": {"A": "The distribution is roughly normal.", "B": "The distribution is symmetric.", "C": "There were a greater number of earthquakes with magnitudes\nbelow 6 than between 6 and 6.75.", "D": "There were the same number of earthquakes between the median\nand the third quartile magnitudes as there were between the third\nquartile and the maximum magnitudes.", "E": "Over half the earthquakes had magnitudes between 6 and 7.5."}, "image": null, "answer": "E", "explanation": "由图：Q1=6、Q3≈7.46、中位数≈6.8。6 到 7.5 之间包含全部中间 50%（Q1 到 Q3）再加 7.46–7.5 的一小段，故超过一半；6 到 6.75 之间与 6 以下各约 25%，C 不成立。"}, {"id": 67, "chapter": "第一章 探索性分析", "topic": "单变量数据分析", "stem": "Suppose the height distribution of male college basketball\nplayers is approximately normal and varies from 71 to 81 inches,\nwhile the height distribution of female college basketball players\nis approximately normal and varies from 65 to 72 inches. A\nrandom stratified sample of 500 male and 500 female college\nbasketball players is then combined into one large sample of size\n1,000. What is the distribution of this sample?", "options": {"A": "Roughly normal, varying from 65 to 81 inches and centered at\n71.5 inches", "B": "Roughly uniform", "C": "Unimodal and symmetric but not normal", "D": "Skewed", "E": "Bimodal"}, "image": null, "answer": "E", "explanation": "男、女身高两个正态分布中心相差约 8 英寸，各 500 人合并后呈双峰分布。"}, {"id": 68, "chapter": "第一章 探索性分析", "topic": "单变量数据分析", "stem": "A company reports mean salaries cross-classified by position and\ngender.\nMale\nFemale\nJunior Executives\n$45,000\n$48,000\nSenior Executives\n$93,000\n$95,000\nOverall\n?\n?\nIs it possible that males have an overall higher mean salary than\nfemales?", "options": {"A": "No, because 45,000 + 93,000\n2\n<\n48,000 + 95,000\n2", "B": "No, because female junior executives have a higher mean salary\nthan male junior executives and female senior executives have a\nhigher mean salary than male senior executives", "C": "No, because n(48,000 – 45,000) + m(95,000 – 93,000) > 0 for all\nn and m", "D": "Yes, depending on the number of males and females who are\njunior and senior executives", "E": "Yes, because salaries are a numerical, not a categorical, variable\nTWO-VARIABLE DATA ANALYSIS"}, "image": null, "answer": "D", "explanation": "这是辛普森悖论：虽然每个职位女性均值更高，但若男性大多集中在高薪的资深职位、女性大多为初级，总体上男性均值可以更高。"}, {"id": 69, "chapter": "第一章 探索性分析", "topic": "双变量数据分析", "stem": "Large student debt is a growing problem for college graduates,\nwith many graduates seemingly unable ever to get out of debt.\nSuppose the average college debt for graduates is $120,000 with a\nstandard deviation of $40,000, and suppose the average stress\nlevel of college graduates on a 1–100 scale is 30 with a standard\ndeviation of 10. If the correlation between the stress level and\ncollege debt is 0.6, what is the least squares linear regression\nequation for stress level among college graduates based on their\namount of debt (in $1,000)?", "options": {"A": "Predicted stress level = 12 + 0.15(College debt in $1,000)", "B": "Predicted stress level = 113.5 + 0.15(College debt in $1,000)", "C": "Predicted stress level = 30 + 2.4(College debt in $1,000)", "D": "Predicted stress level = 0.25(College debt in $1,000)", "E": "Predicted stress level = 28.2 + 0.015(College debt in $1,000)"}, "image": null, "answer": "A", "explanation": "斜率 b = r·sy/sx = 0.6×10/40 = 0.15；截距 a = 30 − 0.15×120 = 12。"}, {"id": 70, "chapter": "第一章 探索性分析", "topic": "双变量数据分析", "stem": "Suppose every student who attended Saturday review classes\nscored exactly 10 points higher on a final exam than he/she did\non a midterm exam. What would be the correlation between\nscores on the final exam and scores on the midterm exam for\nstudents attending the review classes?", "options": {"A": "0", "B": "Somewhat positive", "C": "0.5", "D": "Nearly 1", "E": "Exactly 1"}, "image": "img/q70.png", "answer": "E", "explanation": "期末成绩 = 期中成绩 + 10，所有点恰好落在一条斜率为 1 的直线上，完全正线性相关，r = 1。"}, {"id": 71, "chapter": "第一章 探索性分析", "topic": "双变量数据分析", "stem": "Paper lengths and final grades are obtained from a random\nsample of term papers handed in to a particular high school\nEnglish teacher. The resulting regression equation is shown.\n\u0000\n\u0000\n\u0000\nGrade = 52.34 + 1.24(Length) with r = 0.18\nWhat percentage of the variation in final grades can be explained\nby the linear regression model of Grade on Length?", "options": {"A": "0.24 percent", "B": "1.24 percent", "C": "3.24 percent", "D": "18 percent", "E": "24 percent"}, "image": null, "answer": "C", "explanation": "可解释变异比例 = r² = 0.18² = 0.0324，即 3.24%。"}, {"id": 72, "chapter": "第一章 探索性分析", "topic": "双变量数据分析", "stem": "In a study of winning percentages in home games versus average\nhome attendance for Big Ten football teams, the resulting\nregression line is shown in the following equation.\n\u0000\n\u0000\n\u0000\nWinning% = 39 + 0.00025(Attendance)\nWhat is the residual if a team has a winning percentage of 55%\nwith an average attendance of 75,000?", "options": {"A": "–3.025", "B": "–2.75", "C": "2.75", "D": "3.025", "E": "57.75"}, "image": null, "answer": "B", "explanation": "预测胜率 = 39 + 0.00025×75000 = 57.75%；残差 = 55 − 57.75 = −2.75。"}, {"id": 73, "chapter": "第一章 探索性分析", "topic": "双变量数据分析", "stem": "Which of the following is an incorrect statement about the least\nsquare regression line?", "options": {"A": "It always passes through the point (¯x, ¯y).", "B": "The slope always has the same sign as the correlation.", "C": "It minimizes the sum of the squares of the residuals.", "D": "The slope is always between –1 and +1.", "E": "The correlation of y versus x is the same as the correlation of x\nversus y."}, "image": null, "answer": "D", "explanation": "回归斜率带单位、随变量尺度变化，不必介于 −1 与 +1 之间，D 错误；A、B、C、E 都是正确性质。"}, {"id": 74, "chapter": "第一章 探索性分析", "topic": "双变量数据分析", "stem": "A linear regression analysis relating teachers’ salaries to years of\nexperience yields:\nˆy = 37.15 + 2.405x\nwhere x is years of experience and y is salary (in $1,000).\nWhich of the following is the most proper conclusion?", "options": {"A": "A starting teacher will earn $37,150, while one with 70 years of\nexperience should earn $205,500.", "B": "Starting teachers average $37,150 with bonuses of $2,405 every\nyear.", "C": "There is a cause-and-effect relationship between teachers’\nsalaries and experience with each extra year of experience\ncorresponding to an extra $2,405 in salary.", "D": "Starting salaries for teachers average $37,150, and each year of\nexperience is associated with an average increase of $2,405.", "E": "There is a high correlation between teachers’ salaries and years\nof experience."}, "image": null, "answer": "D", "explanation": "正确解读：起始（0 年经验）平均工资 37150 美元，每多一年经验平均（关联性）增加 2405 美元；不能断言因果，也不能外推到 70 年。"}, {"id": 75, "chapter": "第一章 探索性分析", "topic": "双变量数据分析", "stem": "What is the correct regression output for the scatterplot below?", "options": {"A": "", "B": "", "C": "", "D": "", "E": ""}, "image": "img/q75.png", "answer": "A", "explanation": "散点呈较强负线性关系（r≈−0.88）：截距约 5.45、斜率约 −0.45、R² 约 76.5%，只有 A 与之相符；C、E 斜率为正，B 系数互换，D 的 R² 过低。"}, {"id": 76, "chapter": "第一章 探索性分析", "topic": "双变量数据分析", "stem": "Suppose a correlation is positive. Given two points from the\nscatterplot, which of the following is/are possible?\nI. The first point has a smaller x-value and a smaller y-value\nthan the second point.\nII. The first point has a larger x-value and a larger y-value than\nthe second point.\nIII. The first point has a smaller x-value and a larger y-value\nthan the second point.", "options": {"A": "I only", "B": "II only", "C": "III only", "D": "I and II only", "E": "I, II, and III"}, "image": null, "answer": "E", "explanation": "正相关只描述整体趋势，个别点可以偏离趋势线：I、II（顺趋势）与 III（逆趋势的点）都可能出现在正相关散点图中。"}, {"id": 77, "chapter": "第一章 探索性分析", "topic": "双变量数据分析", "stem": "Which of the following statements about the correlation\ncoefficient r is incorrect?", "options": {"A": "It is not affected by changes in the measurement units of the\nvariables.", "B": "It itself never has units.", "C": "It gives information about a linear association, not about\ncausation.", "D": "It always takes values between –1 and +1 unless the association\nis nonlinear.", "E": "It is not affected by which variable is explanatory and which is\nresponse."}, "image": null, "answer": "D", "explanation": "无论线性与否，r 总在 −1 与 +1 之间，附加条件除非非线性的说法错误；r 无单位、不解释因果、与解释/响应变量的选取无关。"}, {"id": 78, "chapter": "第一章 探索性分析", "topic": "双变量数据分析", "stem": "The value of a new luxury automobile (in $1,000) versus age (in\nyears) has the following regression analysis.\nWhat is the meaning of –4.78869 in the computer output?", "options": {"A": "The value of the automobile will decrease by 4.78869 thousand\ndollars for every increase of 1 year in the age of the automobile.", "B": "On average, there is a predicted decrease in the value of $1,000\nfor every increase of 4.78869 years in the age of the automobile.", "C": "On average, there is a predicted decrease of 4.78869 thousand\ndollars in the value for every increase of 1 year in the age of the\nautomobile.", "D": "Approximately 4.79 percent of the variability in value can be\nexplained by the linear model of value versus age.", "E": "The standard deviation of the residuals is approximately 4.79."}, "image": "img/q78.png", "answer": "C", "explanation": "斜率 −4.78869：车龄每增加 1 年，二手车价值平均预测减少 4.78869 千美元（即约 4789 美元）。"}, {"id": 79, "chapter": "第一章 探索性分析", "topic": "双变量数据分析", "stem": "Which of the scatterplots below could have resulted in the\nresidual plot shown above? (The y-scales are not the same in the\nscatterplots as in the residual plot.)", "options": {"A": "", "B": "", "C": "", "D": "", "E": "None of these could result in the given residual plot."}, "image": "img/q79.png", "answer": "B", "explanation": "残差呈先正、中负、后正的 U 形，对应散点对直线呈下凸（先陡后缓）的衰减形态，即 B；A 为完全线性会产生随机残差，C 为倒 U 形。"}, {"id": 80, "chapter": "第一章 探索性分析", "topic": "双变量数据分析", "stem": "Consider the scatterplot below of starting salaries and salaries\nafter 5 years of employment for 20 executives at a software\ncompany.\nWhich of the statements below is incorrect?", "options": {"A": "Both the correlation and the slope are positive.", "B": "More executives started making under $50,000 than were making\nunder $50,000 after five years.", "C": "The same number of executives started at $100,000 as were\nmaking $100,000 after five years.", "D": "Most executives who started making under $50,000 were still\nmaking under $50,000 after five years.", "E": "Some executives were making less after five years than they\nmade initially."}, "image": "img/q80.png", "answer": "C", "explanation": "由散点图：起薪为 100 千的约 3 人，而 5 年后薪金为 100 千的只有约 2 人，人数并不相同，C 错误；A、B、D、E 均可由图验证为真。"}, {"id": 81, "chapter": "第一章 探索性分析", "topic": "双变量数据分析", "stem": "Consider the following three scatterplots.\nWhich scatterplot has the greatest correlation coefficient r?", "options": {"A": "I", "B": "II", "C": "III", "D": "They all have the same correlation coefficient.", "E": "The question cannot be answered without additional information."}, "image": "img/q81.png", "answer": "D", "explanation": "三个散点图的点分布形状完全相同，只是坐标刻度作了线性缩放；r 不受变量线性变换的影响，因此三者相同。"}, {"id": 82, "chapter": "第一章 探索性分析", "topic": "双变量数据分析", "stem": "Which of the following statements about influential points is\nincorrect?", "options": {"A": "Removal of an influential point sharply affects the regression\nline.", "B": "Looking at a residual plot is a helpful way of identifying\ninfluential points.", "C": "Determining a regression model with and without a point is an\nexcellent way of identifying influential points.", "D": "Outliers in the x-direction are more likely to be influential points\nthan are outliers in the y-direction.", "E": "None of the above are incorrect statements."}, "image": null, "answer": "B", "explanation": "高杠杆的影响点往往把回归线拉向自己，残差很小，在残差图上不易识别，B 错误；A、C、D 都是正确做法或性质。"}, {"id": 83, "chapter": "第一章 探索性分析", "topic": "双变量数据分析", "stem": "A regression analysis on the relationship between average annual\nprofits of financial companies (in millions of dollars) and the\nyearly number of major security breaches gives the following\ncomputer output.\nIf the analysis was rerun using the number of breaches as the\ndependent variable instead of profits, what would be the\ncorrelation coefficient?", "options": {"A": "–0.318", "B": "0.465", "C": "–0.465", "D": "0.682", "E": "–0.682"}, "image": "img/q83.png", "answer": "E", "explanation": "R-sq = 46.5%，r = ±√0.465 ≈ ±0.682；斜率 −2.71 为负，故 r = −0.682。相关系数与哪个变量作因变量无关。"}, {"id": 84, "chapter": "第一章 探索性分析", "topic": "双变量数据分析", "stem": "Suppose the regression line for a set of data, ˆy = a + 5x, passes\nthrough the point (1, 7). If ¯x and ¯y are the sample means of the xand y-values, respectively, what is ¯y?", "options": {"A": "¯x", "B": "5¯x", "C": "6 + ¯x", "D": "2 + 5¯x", "E": "7 + 5¯x"}, "image": "img/q84.png", "answer": "D", "explanation": "回归线过 (1,7)：7 = a+5，得 a = 2；最小二乘线必过 (x̄, ȳ)，故 ȳ = 2 + 5x̄。"}, {"id": 85, "chapter": "第一章 探索性分析", "topic": "双变量数据分析", "stem": "The number of students who were victims of bullying at a high\nschool during the years 2010–2017 is fitted with a least squares\nregression line. The graph of the residuals and some computer\noutput is as follows.\nHow many students were victims of bullying at this high school\nin the year 2013?", "options": {"A": "47", "B": "48", "C": "52", "D": "53", "E": "58"}, "image": "img/q85.png", "answer": "E", "explanation": "2013 年即 x = 3：预测值 = 11 + 13.9286×3 ≈ 52.8；残差图上 x=3 处残差约 +5，实际人数 ≈ 52.8+5 ≈ 58。"}, {"id": 86, "chapter": "第一章 探索性分析", "topic": "双变量数据分析", "stem": "A simple random sample of 25 AP Statistics students provides\nthe following statistics.\nNumber of hours studying for this class each day: ¯x = 1.8, sx = 0.5\nAverage grade for this class: ¯y = 86.5, sy = 4.2\nCorrelation r = 0.45\nBased on this data, what is the resulting linear regression\nequation?\n\u0000\n\u0000\n\u0000", "options": {"A": "Grade = 52.9 + 18.67(Hours)\n\u0000\n\u0000\n\u0000", "B": "Grade = 79.7 + 3.78(Hours)\n\u0000\n\u0000\n\u0000", "C": "Grade = 83.1 + 1.89(Hours)\n\u0000\n\u0000\n\u0000", "D": "Grade = 85.7 + 0.45(Hours)\n\u0000\n\u0000\n\u0000", "E": "Grade = 86.4 + 0.0536(Hours)"}, "image": null, "answer": "B", "explanation": "b = r·sy/sx = 0.45×4.2/0.5 = 3.78；a = 86.5 − 3.78×1.8 ≈ 79.7。"}, {"id": 87, "chapter": "第一章 探索性分析", "topic": "双变量数据分析", "stem": "A biologist studying robins collected data on the weight and the\negg production for 50 female birds. The correlation is found to be\n0.7. Which of the following is a true statement?", "options": {"A": "On average, a 70 percent increase in weight results in a 49\npercent increase in egg production.", "B": "On average, a 70 percent increase in weight results in a 100\npercent increase in egg production.", "C": "Seventy percent of a robin’s egg production can be explained by\nthe bird’s weight.", "D": "Seventy percent of the variation in robin egg production can be\naccounted for by the linear regression model of egg production\non bird weight.", "E": "Greater egg production tends to be associated with greater bird\nweight."}, "image": null, "answer": "E", "explanation": "r = 0.7 只表明体重与蛋产量呈正向关联（E 正确）；r² = 49% 而非 70%，且相关不表示百分比增长或因果。"}, {"id": 88, "chapter": "第一章 探索性分析", "topic": "双变量数据分析", "stem": "One model for global warming is given by ˆy = 315 + 1.52x with r\n= 0.92, where y is CO2 atmospheric concentration in ppm and x is\nyears since 1960. What is the correct interpretation of the slope?", "options": {"A": "On average, atmospheric CO2 concentration has been rising by\n1.52 ppm per year since 1960.", "B": "The baseline atmospheric CO2 concentration is 315 ppm.", "C": "The regression model explains 84.64 percent of the variation of\natmospheric CO2 concentration over the years since 1960.", "D": "The regression model explains 92 percent of the variation of\natmospheric CO2 concentration over the years since 1960.", "E": "Atmospheric CO2 concentration will top 500 ppm in the year\n2082."}, "image": null, "answer": "A", "explanation": "斜率 1.52：自 1960 年以来大气 CO2 浓度平均每年上升 1.52 ppm；315 是截距，84.64% 是 r²，均非斜率解读。"}, {"id": 89, "chapter": "第一章 探索性分析", "topic": "双变量数据分析", "stem": "Consider the points (–2, 3), (0, 7), (1, 9), (3, 12), and (10, n). What\nshould n be so that the correlation between the x and y values is r\n= 1?", "options": {"A": "25", "B": "26", "C": "27", "D": "A value different from any of the above", "E": "No value for n can make r = 1."}, "image": "img/q89.png", "answer": "E", "explanation": "(−2,3),(0,7),(1,9) 在直线 y = 7+2x 上，但 (3,12) 不在其上（应为 13）；无论 n 取何值五点都无法共线，r 不可能等于 1。"}, {"id": 90, "chapter": "第一章 探索性分析", "topic": "双变量数据分析", "stem": "Suppose the data from two scatterplots, A and B, result in\nidentical least squares regression lines with positive slopes.\nWhich of the following statements is true?", "options": {"A": "The correlation in A equals the correlation in B.", "B": "The sum of the squares of the residuals in A equals the sum of\nthe squares of the residuals in B.", "C": "If the sum of the squares of the residuals in A is greater than the\nsum of the squares of the residuals in B, then the correlation in A\nwill be greater than the correlation in B.", "D": "If the sum of the squares of the residuals in A is greater than the\nsum of the squares of the residuals in B, then the correlation in A\nwill be less than the correlation in B.", "E": "None of the above are true statements."}, "image": null, "answer": "E", "explanation": "回归线相同不能确定 r 或残差平方和：r² = 1−SSE/SST，两个数据集的 SST 可以不同，A–D 都不必然成立。"}, {"id": 91, "chapter": "第一章 探索性分析", "topic": "双变量数据分析", "stem": "Two drivers, one for Uber and one for Lyft, compare their weekly\nmiles driven for 17 consecutive weeks. The following scatterplot\nis the result, where the units for both axes are miles.\nWhich statement below gives the best comparison between the\nweekly miles driven by the two drivers?", "options": {"A": "In all 17 weeks, the Lyft driver drove more miles than the Uber\ndriver.", "B": "In all 17 weeks, the Uber driver drove more miles than the Lyft\ndriver.", "C": "In all but 1 week, the Lyft driver drove more miles than the Uber\ndriver.", "D": "In all but 1 week, the Uber driver drove more miles than the Lyft\ndriver.", "E": "In all but 3 weeks, the Lyft driver drove more miles than the\nUber driver."}, "image": "img/q91.png", "answer": "C", "explanation": "对角线为 y=x：点在上方表示 Lyft＞Uber。17 周中只有约 1 周（约 (800,730)）的点位于线下方，其余各周 Lyft 都开得更多。"}, {"id": 92, "chapter": "第一章 探索性分析", "topic": "双变量数据分析", "stem": "Suppose a study finds that the correlation coefficient relating\noutside temperature in degrees Fahrenheit during the winter to\nthe amount of natural gas a house consumes in cubic feet per day\nis r = –1. Which of the following is a proper conclusion?", "options": {"A": "Lower outside winter temperatures cause a greater usage of\nnatural gas.", "B": "Higher outside winter temperatures cause a greater usage of\nnatural gas.", "C": "There is a 100% cause-and-effect relationship between outside\nwinter temperatures and natural gas usage.", "D": "There is a very strong association between outside winter\ntemperatures and natural gas usage.", "E": "None of the above are proper conclusions."}, "image": null, "answer": "D", "explanation": "r = −1 只说明温度与用气量之间存在极强的（负）线性关联；相关不证明因果，A、B、C 都不当。"}, {"id": 93, "chapter": "第一章 探索性分析", "topic": "双变量数据分析", "stem": "What is the correct scatterplot for the computer output shown\nabove?", "options": {"A": "", "B": "", "C": "", "D": "", "E": ""}, "image": "img/q93.png", "answer": "C", "explanation": "输出：截距 10.3、斜率 −1.734、R²=90.6%。散点应从 (0.5,10) 紧密沿直线降到 (5,1.6)，只有 C 相符；A 的 x 范围到 10 会产生负预测值，B、D 散布太散。"}, {"id": 94, "chapter": "第一章 探索性分析", "topic": "双变量数据分析", "stem": "Suppose the correlation between two variables is –0.28. If each of\nthe y-values is multiplied by –2, which of the following is true\nabout the new scatterplot?", "options": {"A": "It slopes up to the right, and the correlation is –0.28.", "B": "It slopes up to the right, and the correlation is +0.28.", "C": "It slopes down to the right, and the correlation is –0.28.", "D": "It slopes down to the right, and the correlation is –0.56.", "E": "It slopes up to the right, and the correlation is +0.56."}, "image": null, "answer": "B", "explanation": "每个 y 值乘以 −2：斜率与 r 都变号，新散点向右上方倾斜，r = +0.28。"}, {"id": 95, "chapter": "第一章 探索性分析", "topic": "双变量数据分析", "stem": "Consider n pairs of numbers. Suppose ¯x = 3, sx = 2, ¯y = 5, and sy =\n4. Which of the following could be the least squares regression\nline?", "options": {"A": "ˆy = 11 – 2x", "B": "ˆy = –4 + 3x", "C": "ˆy = 17 – 4x", "D": "ˆy = –10 + 5x", "E": "ˆy = 23 – 6x"}, "image": null, "answer": "A", "explanation": "回归线必过 (x̄,ȳ)=(3,5)，各选项均满足；又斜率 = r·sy/sx = r×2，需 |斜率|≤2。只有 A 的斜率 −2（对应 r=−1）可行，其余 |斜率|＞2。"}, {"id": 96, "chapter": "第一章 探索性分析", "topic": "双变量数据分析", "stem": "Which of the following are possible residual plots?", "options": {"A": "I only", "B": "II only", "C": "III only", "D": "I and II only", "E": "I, II, and III"}, "image": null, "answer": "C", "explanation": "OLS 残差之和为 0 且与 x 不相关：全为正值的 I 和呈明显线性趋势的 II 都不可能；弯曲的 III（用直线拟合弯曲数据）是可能出现的残差图。"}, {"id": 97, "chapter": "第一章 探索性分析", "topic": "双变量数据分析", "stem": "Which of the following is a true statement about the correlation\ncoefficient r?", "options": {"A": "A correlation of 0.4 means that 40 percent of the points are\nhighly correlated.", "B": "The unit of measurement for correlation is the y-unit per x-unit.", "C": "Multiplying every y-value by –1 leaves the correlation\nunchanged.", "D": "Perfect correlation, that is, when the points lie exactly on a\nstraight line, results in r = 0.", "E": "The square of the correlation measures the proportion of the y\nvariance that is predictable from the linear regression model."}, "image": null, "answer": "E", "explanation": "r² 表示 y 的方差中可由线性回归解释（预测）的比例；r 没有单位（B 错），y 乘 −1 会变号（C 错），完全线性时 |r|=1（D 错）。"}, {"id": 98, "chapter": "第一章 探索性分析", "topic": "双变量数据分析", "stem": "Which of the following statements about correlation r is\nincorrect?", "options": {"A": "The correlation and the slope of the regression line always have\nthe same sign.", "B": "Correlation r measures the strength and direction of only linear\nassociation.", "C": "Outliers can greatly affect the value of r.", "D": "A correlation of –0.48 and a correlation of +0.48 show the same\ndegree of clustering around the regression line.", "E": "A correlation of 0.48 indicates a relationship that is three times\nas linear as one for which the correlation is 0.16."}, "image": null, "answer": "E", "explanation": "相关不是倍数关系：r=0.48 时可解释方差约 23%，r=0.16 时约 2.6%（约 9 倍而非 3 倍），E 错误；A、B、C、D 都正确。"}, {"id": 99, "chapter": "第一章 探索性分析", "topic": "双变量数据分析", "stem": "A scatterplot of a town’s population versus time indicates a\npossible exponential relationship. A linear regression on y =\nlog(population in 1,000s) against x = years since 2015 gives ˆy =\n2.4 + 0.03x with r = 0.72. Which of the following is a valid\nconclusion?", "options": {"A": "On average, population goes up 0.03 thousand per year.", "B": "The predicted population for year 2025 is approximately 501\nthousand.", "C": "51.84 percent of the variation in population can be explained by\nvariation in time.", "D": "72 percent of the variation in population can be explained by\nvariation in time.", "E": "None of the above are valid conclusions."}, "image": null, "answer": "B", "explanation": "2025 年即 x=10：log(人口) 预测 = 2.4+0.03×10 = 2.7，人口 ≈ 10^2.7 ≈ 501 千；r²=51.84% 解释的是 log(人口) 的变异而非人口本身，A 混淆了对数刻度。"}, {"id": 100, "chapter": "第一章 探索性分析", "topic": "双变量数据分析", "stem": "A scatterplot of log X and log Y shows a strong negative\ncorrelation close to –1. Which of the following is a true\nstatement?", "options": {"A": "The variables X and Y also have a correlation close to –1.", "B": "A scatterplot of the variables X and Y shows a nonlinear pattern.", "C": "The residual plot of the variables X and Y shows a random\npattern.", "D": "The residual plot of the variables log X and log Y shows a strong\nnonrandom pattern.", "E": "None of the above are true."}, "image": null, "answer": "B", "explanation": "log X 与 log Y 高度线性负相关说明 X 与 Y 之间近似幂函数关系（非线性曲线），故 X–Y 散点图呈非线性模式；对原始数据拟合直线的残差会呈现系统模式，而非随机。"}, {"id": 101, "chapter": "第一章 探索性分析", "topic": "双变量数据分析", "stem": "A random sample of 12 cigarettes of different brands is selected.\nThe tar and nicotine content of each is measured and graphed,\nresulting in the scatterplot below.\nIf the point labeled X is removed, which of the following\nstatements would be true about the least squares regression line\nand the correlation coefficient?", "options": {"A": "Both the slope and the correlation would remain the same.", "B": "Both the slope and the correlation would increase.", "C": "Both the slope and the correlation would decrease.", "D": "The slope would increase, and the correlation would decrease.", "E": "The slope would decrease, and the correlation would increase."}, "image": "img/q101_fig.png", "answer": "B", "explanation": "X 明显偏离其他 11 个点构成的紧密上升趋势，是离群点；但其 x 值位于自变量取值范围的中间，删除它对斜率影响很小，故不是强影响点。"}, {"id": 102, "chapter": "第一章 探索性分析", "topic": "双变量数据分析", "stem": "Ten overweight people went on a new diet, and each lost exactly 7\npounds. What is the correlation between weights before the diet\nand weights after the diet?", "options": {"A": "0", "B": "0.5", "C": "1", "D": "This cannot be answered without seeing the actual data.", "E": "This cannot be answered without knowing if the people were\nrandomly picked from the population of all overweight people."}, "image": "img/q102.png", "answer": "C", "explanation": "节食后体重 = 节食前体重 − 7，是完全的正线性关系，故 r = 1。"}, {"id": 103, "chapter": "第一章 探索性分析", "topic": "双变量数据分析", "stem": "A scatterplot with one point labeled X is shown below.\nWith regard to the least square regression line, which of the\nfollowing statements is true?", "options": {"A": "X is an influential point.", "B": "X is an outlier.", "C": "X has the largest residual, in absolute value, of any point on the\nscatterplot.", "D": "There will be no pattern in the residual plot.", "E": "None of the above are true statements."}, "image": "img/q103_fig.png", "answer": "B", "explanation": "数据整体呈倒 U 形曲线趋势，点 X 脱离这一整体模式，属于离群点（outlier）；由于处于 x 中部，对回归线影响不大。"}, {"id": 104, "chapter": "第一章 探索性分析", "topic": "双变量数据分析", "stem": "Consider the following three scatterplots.\nWhich of the following is true about the correlations for the three\nscatterplots?", "options": {"A": "None are close to 0.", "B": "One is approximately 0, one is negative, and one is positive.", "C": "One is approximately 0, and both of the others are positive.", "D": "Two are approximately 0, and the other is +1.", "E": "Two are approximately 0, and the other is close to +1."}, "image": "img/q104_fig.png", "answer": "E", "explanation": "三个散点图中：曲线关系和无明显趋势的图其相关系数都约为 0，只有强正线性关系的图 r 接近 +1 但不等于 +1。"}, {"id": 105, "chapter": "第一章 探索性分析", "topic": "双变量数据分析", "stem": "In a random sample of eight fast food hamburgers, the\nassociation between calories and fat is summarized in the\ncomputer output below.\nWhich of the following is the best approximation of the actual\ncalories for the hamburger in the sample that had 15 grams of\nfat?", "options": {"A": "410", "B": "415", "C": "420", "D": "425", "E": "430"}, "image": "img/full_p61_q105.png", "answer": "C", "explanation": "回归方程 Calories = 211 + 13.9286×Fat，代入 Fat=15：211+13.9286×15≈211+208.9=419.9≈420。"}, {"id": 106, "chapter": "第一章 探索性分析", "topic": "双变量数据分析", "stem": "A recent study on distance (in feet) drivers can see versus age (in\nyears) of drivers resulted in a linear regression model:\nPredicted distance a driver can see = 576.7 – 3.01(Age)\nWhich of the following is the best interpretation of the slope in\ncontext?", "options": {"A": "For each year a driver’s age increases, he or she sees 3.01 feet\nless.", "B": "For each 3.01 years a driver’s age increases, he or she sees 1 foot\nless.", "C": "For each year a driver’s age increases, he or she sees 3.01 feet\nless up to a distance of 576.7 feet.", "D": "For each 3.01 years a driver’s age increases, he or she sees 1 foot\nless on average.", "E": "For each year a driver’s age increases, he or she sees 3.01 feet\nless on average."}, "image": null, "answer": "E", "explanation": "斜率的解释要加“平均”意义：年龄每增加1岁，可视距离平均减少3.01英尺。"}, {"id": 107, "chapter": "第一章 探索性分析", "topic": "双变量数据分析", "stem": "Given the following three scatterplots with correlations r1, r2,\nand r3, respectively, what is the proper ordering of their\ncorrelations?", "options": {"A": "r3 < r1 < r2", "B": "r2 < r1 < r3", "C": "r2 < r3 < r1 < |r2|", "D": "r1 < r3 < |r2|", "E": "r3 < r2 < r1 < |r2|"}, "image": "img/q107_zoom.png", "answer": null, "explanation": "对比三个散点图的相关系数：曲线关系 r≈0；线性且越紧密 |r| 越接近 1，负相关为负值。请结合图判断排序。"}, {"id": 108, "chapter": "第一章 探索性分析", "topic": "双变量数据分析", "stem": "Consider the three points (3, 50), (4, 38), and (5, 14). Given any\nstraight line, we can calculate the sum of the squares of the three\nvertical distances from these points to the line. What is the\nsmallest possible value of this sum?", "options": {"A": "4.9", "B": "8", "C": "9.8", "D": "24", "E": "40"}, "image": "img/q108.png", "answer": "B", "explanation": "最小二乘线：x̄=4, ȳ=34, Sxy=(-1)(16)+0+(1)(-20)=-36, Sxx=2, b=-18, a=34+72=106。预测值52、38、16，残差-2、0、-2，SSE=4+0+4=8。"}, {"id": 109, "chapter": "第一章 探索性分析", "topic": "双变量数据分析", "stem": "Four pairs of data are used in determining the regression line ˆy =\n8 + 2x. If the four values of the independent variable are 17, 29,\n35, and 43, what is the mean of the four values of the dependent\nvariable?", "options": {"A": "31", "B": "62", "C": "70", "D": "256", "E": "The mean cannot be determined from the given information."}, "image": "img/q109.png", "answer": "C", "explanation": "x̄=(17+29+35+43)/4=31，回归线过(x̄,ȳ)，故ȳ=8+2(31)=70。"}, {"id": 110, "chapter": "第一章 探索性分析", "topic": "双变量数据分析", "stem": "Suppose the correlation between two variables is r = 0.16. What\nwill the new correlation be if every value of the x-variable is\ntripled, 0.04 is added to all values of the y-variable, and the two\nvariables are then interchanged?", "options": {"A": "0.16", "B": "0.20", "C": "0.48", "D": "0.52", "E": "0.60"}, "image": null, "answer": "A", "explanation": "对变量做线性变换（乘常数、加常数）以及交换x、y都不改变相关系数，r仍为0.16。"}, {"id": 111, "chapter": "第一章 探索性分析", "topic": "双变量数据分析", "stem": "Which of the following statements about the correlation r is true?", "options": {"A": "When r = 0, there is no relationship between the variables.", "B": "When r = 0.3, 30 percent of the variables are closely related.", "C": "When r = 1, there is a perfect cause-and-effect relationship\nbetween the variables.", "D": "A correlation close to 1 means that a linear model will give the\nbest fit to the data.", "E": "All of the above statements are false."}, "image": null, "answer": "D", "explanation": "r=0只表示没有线性关系（A错）；r不是“相关百分比”（B错）；相关不蕴含因果（C错）；D正确。"}, {"id": 112, "chapter": "第一章 探索性分析", "topic": "双变量数据分析", "stem": "Which of the following statements about residuals in a linear\nregression model is incorrect?", "options": {"A": "The mean of the residuals is always zero.", "B": "The sum of the residuals is always zero.", "C": "The regression line for a residual plot is a horizontal line.", "D": "The standard deviation of the residuals gives a measure of how\nthe points in the scatterplot are spread around the regression line.", "E": "A residual equals the predicted y minus the observed y."}, "image": null, "answer": "E", "explanation": "残差=观测值−预测值，E把定义说反了，是不正确的说法。"}, {"id": 113, "chapter": "第一章 探索性分析", "topic": "双变量数据分析", "stem": "Data on ages (in months) and eBay prices for used cellphones\nresult in a regression line.\n\u0000\n\u0000\n\u0000\nPrice = 500 −22.5(Age)\nGiven that 56.25% of the variation in price is explained by the\nlinear regression model of Price on Age, what is the value of the\ncorrelation coefficient r?", "options": {"A": "–0.75", "B": "–0.5625", "C": "0.5625", "D": "0.75", "E": "There is insufficient information to answer this question."}, "image": null, "answer": "A", "explanation": "r²=0.5625，且斜率为负，故r=−√0.5625=−0.75。"}, {"id": 114, "chapter": "第一章 探索性分析", "topic": "双变量数据分析", "stem": "Studies have shown a strong, positive, linear relationship between\ntemperature and the frequency of a cricket’s chirps. What does\n“strong” mean in this context?", "options": {"A": "The temperature is related to the frequency of a cricket’s chirps.", "B": "For each unit increase in the frequency of cricket chirps, the\npredicted temperature changes by a constant amount.", "C": "The greater the frequency of cricket chirps, the greater the\ntemperature, on average.", "D": "The actual temperatures are very close to the temperatures\npredicted by the linear model.", "E": "The variability in temperatures is related to the variability in the\nfrequency of cricket chirps."}, "image": null, "answer": "D", "explanation": "“强”指实际温度与线性模型预测的温度非常接近，即散点紧密贴合直线。"}, {"id": 115, "chapter": "第一章 探索性分析", "topic": "双变量数据分析", "stem": "A study of family incomes and SAT scores reports a correlation\nof r = +1.07. What should be concluded?", "options": {"A": "Students coming from higher-income families tend to have\nhigher SAT scores than students from lower-income families.", "B": "Students coming from higher-income families have 7 percent\nhigher SAT scores on average than students coming from lowerincome families.", "C": "There is less than a 10 percent relationship between family\nincomes and SAT scores.", "D": "There is a strong positive association between family income\nand SAT scores, but concluding causation would be wrong.", "E": "A mistake in arithmetic was made."}, "image": null, "answer": "E", "explanation": "相关系数必须在−1到1之间，r=1.07说明计算有算术错误。"}, {"id": 116, "chapter": "第一章 探索性分析", "topic": "双变量数据分析", "stem": "An HR officer at a high-pressure company surveys executives as\nto their happiness level and accesses their salaries. The data are\ndisplayed in the scatterplot below.\nDescribe the relationship between happiness level and salary.", "options": {"A": "Negative, weak, linear", "B": "Negative, moderate, linear", "C": "Negative, strong, nonlinear", "D": "Positive, strong, nonlinear", "E": "Positive, weak, nonlinear"}, "image": "img/q116.png", "answer": "E", "explanation": "相关系数 r 必须满足 -1 ≤ r ≤ 1，r=1.07 不可能，说明计算有算术错误。"}, {"id": 117, "chapter": "第一章 探索性分析", "topic": "双变量数据分析", "stem": "Data on w = weight of a car (in pounds) and s = stopping distance\n(in feet) when the car is traveling at 50 mph and brakes are\napplied results in the regression equation ˆs = 35 + 0.03w. Which\nof the following is the best interpretation of the intercept 35 in\nthe equation?", "options": {"A": "For each 1 pound increase in car weight, the stopping distance\nincreases by 35 feet, on average.", "B": "For each 1 foot increase in stopping distance, the car weight is 35\npounds greater, on average.", "C": "The stopping distance is 35 feet when the car weight is 0, on\naverage.", "D": "The stopping distance is 0 feet when the car weight is 35 pounds,\non average.", "E": "The intercept, 35 feet, has no reasonable interpretation because a\nweight of 0 is outside the domain of car weights."}, "image": null, "answer": "E", "explanation": "截距35表示车重为0时的刹车距离，但车重为0超出实际范围，没有合理意义。"}, {"id": 118, "chapter": "第一章 探索性分析", "topic": "双变量数据分析", "stem": "A linear regression analysis of rushing yards and points scored in\na random sample of 25 NFL games results in the following\nequation.\n\u0000\n\u0000\n\u0000\nRushing\n= 10 + 0. 05 (\n)\nPoint\nscored\nA coach would like to predict rushing yards from points scored.\nHe solves the above equation and comes up with the following.\n\u0000\n\u0000\nyards\n\u0000\n\u0000\n\u0000\nPoints\nscored\n= −200 + 20 (\n)\nRushing\nyards\nDid the coach calculate correctly?", "options": {"A": "Yes, because if y = 10 + 0.05x, solving algebraically for x gives x\n= –200 + 20y.", "B": "Yes, because the x- and y-variables are interchangeable in linear\nregression.", "C": "Yes, because\n1\n0.05 = 20 showing that the slopes are reciprocals.", "D": "No, because the equations have different sets of variables.", "E": "This cannot be answered without knowing the value of the\ncorrelation r."}, "image": null, "answer": "D", "explanation": "回归x对y与y对x是两条不同的最小二乘线，不能通过代数变形互相推出；教练需要重新做以 rushing yards 为响应变量的回归。"}, {"id": 119, "chapter": "第一章 探索性分析", "topic": "双变量数据分析", "stem": "A study of airfare prices versus distances gives rise to the\nfollowing scatterplot.\nA least squares regression analysis is performed. Which of the\nfollowing would be apparent from a residual plot of residuals\nversus distance?", "options": {"A": "The variation in airfare is different across the distances.", "B": "There is a positive linear relationship between the residuals and\ndistance.", "C": "The sum of the residuals is less than 0.", "D": "The sum of the residuals is greater than 0.", "E": "The residuals go approximately from 100 to 400."}, "image": "img/q119.png", "answer": "A", "explanation": "散点图中票价随距离增大其离散程度（变异性）也随之增大，残差图会显示不同距离处残差的变异不同。"}, {"id": 120, "chapter": "第一章 探索性分析", "topic": "双变量数据分析", "stem": "In a random sample of 300 men and 200 women, 50 of the men\nand 40 of the women said that they brush their teeth from side to\nside rather than up and down. If there is no difference between\nthe proportions of men and women who brush their teeth from\nside to side rather than up and down, how many men and women\nin the sample would be expected to brush their teeth from side to\nside rather than up and down?", "options": {"A": "45 men and 45 women", "B": "50 men and 40 women", "C": "54 men and 36 women", "D": "150 men and 100 women", "E": "250 men and 160 women"}, "image": null, "answer": "C", "explanation": "合并比例=(50+40)/500=0.18；期望男性=300×0.18=54，女性=200×0.18=36。"}, {"id": 121, "chapter": "第一章 探索性分析", "topic": "双变量数据分析", "stem": "A random sample of 200 people was anonymously surveyed as to\nwhether they sneak food into movie theaters rather than pay the\nhigh price of snacks. 120 were teenagers, and 80 were adult. 150\nanswered “yes,” they have snuck in food, and 50 answered “no.”\nAmong those surveyed, there was independence between\nteenager/adult and whether or not they snuck food into movie\ntheaters. Which of the following tables shows this conclusion?", "options": {"A": "Yes\nNo\nTotal\nTeenagers\n75\n45\n120\nAdults\n75\n5\n80\nTotal\n150\n50\n200", "B": "Yes\nNo\nTotal\nTeenagers\n80\n40\n120\nAdults\n70\n10\n80\nTotal\n150\n50\n200", "C": "Yes\nNo\nTotal\nTeenagers\n90\n30\n120\nAdults\n60\n20\n80\nTotal\n150\n50\n200", "D": "Yes\nNo\nTotal\nTeenagers\n110\n10\n120\nAdults\n40\n40\n80\nTotal\n150\n50\n200", "E": "Yes\nNo\nTotal\n100\n20\n120\nTeenagers\nAdults\n50\n30\n80\nTotal\n150\n50\n200"}, "image": "img/q121.png", "answer": "C", "explanation": "独立时“是”的比例=150/200=0.75；青少年120×0.75=90，成人80×0.75=60，对应C表。"}, {"id": 122, "chapter": "第一章 探索性分析", "topic": "双变量数据分析", "stem": "In the following table, what value of n results in a table showing\nperfect independence?", "options": {"A": "30", "B": "50", "C": "70", "D": "75", "E": "100"}, "image": "img/q122.png", "answer": "D", "explanation": "完全独立意味着每行“Yes”的比例相同：n/120 = 50/80 = 0.625，解得 n = 75。"}, {"id": 123, "chapter": "第一章 探索性分析", "topic": "双变量数据分析", "stem": "A random sample of 200 movies were cross-classified by genre\nand rating. The following table gives the individual counts.\nRating\nG\nPG\nPG-13\nR\nTotal\nAction\n5\n20\n15\n5\n45\nComedy\n10\n25\n10\n5\n50\nDocumentary\n25\n10\n3\n2\n40\nDrama\n20\n15\n17\n13\n65\nTotal\n60\n70\n45\n25\n200\nOf the movies in the sample that were classified as PG or PG-13,\nwhat proportion of them were either action or drama?", "options": {"A": "45 + 65\n200", "B": "70+ 45\n200", "C": "20+ 15\n70 + 45", "D": "20+ 15 + 15 + 17\n200", "E": "20+ 15 + 15 + 17\n70 + 45"}, "image": "img/q123.png", "answer": "E", "explanation": "PG或PG-13共70+45=115部，其中action或drama=20+15+15+17=67，比例=(20+15+15+17)/(70+45)。"}, {"id": 124, "chapter": "第一章 探索性分析", "topic": "双变量数据分析", "stem": "For a class project, a student surveyed 125 cars in a high school\nparking lot. The student recorded whether each car had a student\nor a staff tag and whether each was an American or a foreign\nmodel car. Of the 50 American cars, 20 had staff tags. Given that\nwhether the car has a student or a staff tag and whether it is an\nAmerican or a foreign model are independent, how many of the\nforeign cars had student tags?", "options": {"A": "20", "B": "30", "C": "45", "D": "50", "E": "75"}, "image": "img/q124.png", "answer": "C", "explanation": "P(员工牌)=20/50=0.4；外国车75辆中期望有员工牌30辆，学生牌=75−30=45。"}, {"id": 125, "chapter": "第一章 探索性分析", "topic": "双变量数据分析", "stem": "Men and women were sampled as to whether, in general, they feel\n“always rushed,” “sometimes rushed,” or “almost never rushed.”\nA summary of those responses are given in the following table.\nMen\nWomen\nTotal\nAlways\n44\n95\n139\nSometimes\n79\n69\n148\nAlmost never\n22\n28\n50\nTotal\n145\n192\n337\nBased on this table, which of the following statements is\nincorrect?", "options": {"A": "Most men feel “sometimes rushed.”", "B": "Most women feel “always rushed.”", "C": "Men are more likely than women to say that they are “almost\nnever rushed.”", "D": "More men than women say they are “sometimes rushed.”", "E": "The proportion of people who are either “sometimes rushed” or\n“almost never rushed” is 1 −\n139\n337 .\nMC Collecting and Producing Data\nAnswers for Chapter 2 are on pages 252–262.\nSAMPLING STRATEGIES"}, "image": "img/q125.png", "answer": "B", "explanation": "女性中“总是匆忙”95/192≈49%不到半数，B说法不正确；其余说法均正确。"}, {"id": 126, "chapter": "第二章 数据收集", "topic": "抽样方法", "stem": "An online retailer wishes to survey its customers. The decision is\nmade to select randomly 50 customers who pay with credit\ncards, 25 customers who pay with debit cards, and 10 customers\nwho pay with checks. This procedure is an example of which type\nof sampling?", "options": {"A": "Cluster", "B": "Convenience", "C": "Simple random", "D": "Stratified", "E": "Systematic"}, "image": null, "answer": "D", "explanation": "按付款方式（信用卡/借记卡/支票）分组后在各组内随机抽取，是分层抽样。"}, {"id": 127, "chapter": "第二章 数据收集", "topic": "抽样方法", "stem": "Which of the following is a true statement about sampling?", "options": {"A": "Data obtained when conducting a census are always more\naccurate than data obtained from a sample, no matter how\ncareful the design of the sampling method.", "B": "The sampling frame is the population of interest.", "C": "Sampling error implies an error, possibly very small but still an\nerror, on the part of the surveyor.", "D": "Careful analysis of a given sample will indicate whether or not\nit is random.", "E": "The clusters in cluster sampling should all look pretty much\nalike.\nCHAPTER\n2"}, "image": null, "answer": "E", "explanation": "整群抽样要求各群尽可能相似（群内异质、群间同质），E正确；其余说法均有误。"}, {"id": 128, "chapter": "第二章 数据收集", "topic": "抽样方法", "stem": "Sampling error occurs", "options": {"A": "when samples are too small", "B": "when interviewers make mistakes resulting in bias", "C": "when interviewers use judgment instead of random choice in\npicking the sample", "D": "because a sample statistic is used to estimate a population\nparameter", "E": "in all of the above cases"}, "image": null, "answer": "D", "explanation": "抽样误差指用样本统计量估计总体参数所产生的固有误差，任何抽样都存在。"}, {"id": 129, "chapter": "第二章 数据收集", "topic": "抽样方法", "stem": "A town has one high school, which buses students from urban,\nsuburban, and rural communities. Which of the following\nsampling techniques is most recommended in studying high\nschool students’ attitudes toward college versus trade school\nafter high school graduation?", "options": {"A": "Cluster", "B": "Simple random", "C": "Stratified", "D": "Systematic", "E": "Voluntary response"}, "image": null, "answer": "C", "explanation": "学生来自城市/郊区/农村不同层，按居住地分层抽样最能保证各层代表性。"}, {"id": 130, "chapter": "第二章 数据收集", "topic": "抽样方法", "stem": "A company wishes to survey what people think about a new\nproduct it plans to market. The company decides to sample\nrandomly from their large employee database as this includes\nphone numbers and addresses. This procedure is an example of\nwhich type of sampling?", "options": {"A": "Cluster", "B": "Convenience", "C": "Simple random", "D": "Stratified", "E": "Systematic\nEach of the 31 National Hockey League teams carries a 20person roster. A sample of 62 players (10% of all 620 players)\nwill be selected to undergo drug tests. To do this, each team is\ninstructed to put the names of the 20 players into a hat and\nrandomly draw 2 names. Will this method result in a simple\nrandom sample of the 620 hockey players?"}, "image": null, "answer": "C", "explanation": "从员工数据库的名单中随机抽取，每个成员机会相同，属于简单随机抽样。"}, {"id": 131, "chapter": "第二章 数据收集", "topic": "抽样方法", "stem": "", "options": {"A": "Yes, because each player has the same chance of being selected", "B": "Yes, because each team is equally represented", "C": "Yes, because this is an example of stratified sampling, which is a\nspecial case of simple random sampling", "D": "No, because the teams are not chosen randomly", "E": "No, because not each group of 62 players has the same chance of\nbeing selected"}, "image": null, "answer": "E", "explanation": "每队固定抽2人，并非每个62人组合都有相同被抽中的机会，故不是SRS（属分层样本）。"}, {"id": 132, "chapter": "第二章 数据收集", "topic": "抽样方法", "stem": "The United States Tennis Association (USTA) plans to survey 50\nof the 1,000 top-rated tennis players in the United States. A list\nof the players by rank is made available, and a random number\nbetween 1 and 20 is picked. The sample consists of the person\nthat number down the list together with every 20th person after\nthat. This procedure is an example of which type of sampling?", "options": {"A": "Cluster", "B": "Convenience", "C": "Simple random", "D": "Stratified", "E": "Systematic"}, "image": null, "answer": "E", "explanation": "随机起点后每隔20个取一人，是系统抽样。"}, {"id": 133, "chapter": "第二章 数据收集", "topic": "抽样方法", "stem": "A travel agent plans to survey passengers taking the crossCanada train trip from Vancouver to Toronto. She is considering\ntwo sampling methods.\nMethod 1: From a boarding list, randomly choose 15\npassengers traveling first class (who have roomettes for\nsleeping) and 35 passengers traveling coach (who have\nreclining chairs for sleeping).\nMethod 2: Randomly select one of the cars (each holds 50\npassengers), and survey all the passengers in that car.\nIdentify each sampling method.", "options": {"A": "Method 1 is cluster sampling, and Method 2 is stratified\nsampling.", "B": "Method 1 is cluster sampling, and Method 2 is systematic\nsampling.", "C": "Method 1 is stratified sampling, and Method 2 is cluster\nsampling.", "D": "Method 1 is stratified sampling, and Method 2 is simple random\nsampling.", "E": "Method 1 is simple random sampling, and Method 2 is cluster\nsampling."}, "image": null, "answer": "C", "explanation": "方法1按舱位（层）分别随机抽取是分层抽样；方法2随机选一节车厢调查所有人是整群抽样。"}, {"id": 134, "chapter": "第二章 数据收集", "topic": "抽样方法", "stem": "A polling company conducts a weekly survey to determine the\nproportion of voters who support the direction Congress is\ntaking. For the coming year, the company decides to double the\nsample size. The main benefit of this is to", "options": {"A": "eliminate sampling error", "B": "decrease population variability", "C": "reduce undercoverage bias", "D": "reduce nonresponse bias", "E": "decrease the standard deviation of the sampling distribution"}, "image": null, "answer": "E", "explanation": "样本量加倍使抽样分布的标准差按1/√n减小，即降低估计量的变异；不能消除抽样误差或偏差。"}, {"id": 135, "chapter": "第二章 数据收集", "topic": "抽样方法", "stem": "The athletic office at a large university is thinking of allowing\nwomen to try out for the baseball team and decides to survey\nstudent opinion with a stratified sample based on gender. Which\nof the following will achieve the desired sampling plan?", "options": {"A": "Using a list of all students on campus, randomly select a starting\nname and every 10th name thereafter. Separate the groups by\ngender until the desired sample size is attained.", "B": "Using a numbered list of all students on campus and a random\nnumber generator, select numbers between 1 and the size of the\nstudent body until the desired sample size is reached. A repeat\nof a selected number is disregarded. Separate selected students\nby gender.", "C": "Select all students from a campus dormitory that is\nrepresentative of all students on campus. Separate students by\ngender.", "D": "Select separate random samples of females and males based on\nthe proportions of females and males on campus.", "E": "Select a random sample of students attending the next baseball\ngame. Separate the students by gender."}, "image": null, "answer": "D", "explanation": "按性别分层并按比例在各层内分别随机抽取，正是分层抽样方案。"}, {"id": 136, "chapter": "第二章 数据收集", "topic": "抽样方法", "stem": "To gauge patron use of city libraries, all patrons who check out\nbooks from randomly selected libraries will be surveyed. This\nprocedure is an example of which type of sampling?", "options": {"A": "Cluster", "B": "Convenience", "C": "Simple random", "D": "Stratified", "E": "Systematic"}, "image": null, "answer": "A", "explanation": "随机抽取若干图书馆并调查其中所有读者，是以图书馆为群的整群抽样。"}, {"id": 137, "chapter": "第二章 数据收集", "topic": "抽样方法", "stem": "An IRS agent is given an assignment to choose and audit the tax\nreturns of 260 companies claiming large refunds. She has an\nassistant list all companies whose name begins with A, assigns\neach a number, and uses a random number generator to pick 10\nof these numbers and thus 10 companies. She proceeds to use the\nsame procedure for each letter of the alphabet and combines the\nresults into a group of 260. Which of the following is a true\nstatement?", "options": {"A": "Her procedure makes use of chance.", "B": "Her procedure results in a simple random sample.", "C": "Each company has an equal probability of being selected.", "D": "The company named Amazon probably has a higher probability\nof being selected than the company named Xerox.", "E": "This is an example of a systematic sample."}, "image": null, "answer": "A", "explanation": "每个字母下都用随机数抽取，程序使用了机会；但各字母公司数不同，不是SRS，各公司被抽中概率也不相等。"}, {"id": 138, "chapter": "第二章 数据收集", "topic": "抽样方法", "stem": "A government pollster is planning to survey randomly 50 voters\nin each of a state’s 102 counties regarding a proposed change in\nthe state income tax code. Why is stratification used here?", "options": {"A": "Stratification helps remove bias when estimating proportions, in\nthis case the proportion of voters favoring the change in code.", "B": "Stratification helps remove bias when estimating means, in this\ncase the mean number of voters favoring the change in code.", "C": "Stratification helps remove bias when estimating variances, in\nthis case the variance in the number of voters favoring the\nchange in code.", "D": "Stratification helps reduce sampling variability.", "E": "Stratification is generally the procedure of choice when\nefficiency and cost is of paramount interest."}, "image": null, "answer": "D", "explanation": "按县分层的主要目的是降低抽样变异性（减小估计量的方差），不是消除偏差。"}, {"id": 139, "chapter": "第二章 数据收集", "topic": "抽样方法", "stem": "Which of the following statements about sampling error is\nincorrect?", "options": {"A": "Sampling error is generally smaller when the sample size is\nlarger.", "B": "Sampling error concerns natural variation among samples, is\nalways present, and can be described using probability.", "C": "Sampling error is greater when working with higher confidence\nlevels.", "D": "Sampling error is unrelated to bias.", "E": "Sampling error can be eliminated only if a survey is both\nextremely well designed and extremely well conducted."}, "image": null, "answer": "E", "explanation": "抽样误差是样本本身的固有变异性，除非普查否则无法消除，E说法不正确。"}, {"id": 140, "chapter": "第二章 数据收集", "topic": "抽样方法", "stem": "A cybersecurity analyst wishes to survey her client base of 63\ncompanies as to their vulnerability to a new computer virus. She\nhas 63 business cards, all of the identical size, from her contacts\nin the companies. The analyst decides to drop all of the business\ncards into a small box, shake them up, and reach in to pick 5\ncards for her sample. This procedure is an example of which\ntype of sampling?", "options": {"A": "Cluster", "B": "Convenience", "C": "Simple random", "D": "Stratified", "E": "Systematic"}, "image": null, "answer": "C", "explanation": "把63张名片混匀后抽取，每个5张样本组合机会相同，是简单随机抽样。"}, {"id": 141, "chapter": "第二章 数据收集", "topic": "抽样方法", "stem": "The Environmental Protection Agency (EPA) is concerned about\na region near an old industrial waste dump and plans to take soil\nsamples to test for toxic chemicals. The EPA is considering using\neither vertical or horizontal strips with regard to sampling, as\nshown in the figures below.\nWhat are the two most appropriate sampling techniques?", "options": {"A": "Stratified sample with vertical strips or cluster sample with\nvertical strips", "B": "Stratified sample with vertical strips or cluster sample with\nhorizontal strips", "C": "Stratified sample with horizontal strips or cluster sample with\nvertical strips", "D": "Stratified sample with horizontal strips or cluster sample with\nhorizontal strips", "E": "None of the above are pairs where both techniques are\nappropriate."}, "image": "img/q141.png", "answer": "B", "explanation": "竖条贯穿整个区域可作层（分层），横条作为整群（随机选若干横条整条调查），B组合合适。"}, {"id": 142, "chapter": "第二章 数据收集", "topic": "抽样方法", "stem": "A study is made on whether a particular review book helps\nstudents achieve higher scores on the AP Statistics Exam. In\ncomparing the records of 200 students, half of whom purchased\nthe review book, it is noted that the average AP Statistics score is\nhigher for those 100 students who purchased the book. Which of\nthe following are true statements?\nI. Although this study indicates a relation, it does not prove\ncausation.\nII. There could well be a confounding variable responsible for\nthe seeming relationship.\nIII. Self-selection here makes drawing a conclusion difficult.", "options": {"A": "I only", "B": "I and II only", "C": "I and III only", "D": "II and III only", "E": "I, II, and III"}, "image": null, "answer": "E", "explanation": "观察性研究中购买与否是自选择，不能证明因果（I），可能有混杂变量（II），自选择使结论困难（III），三条都真。"}, {"id": 143, "chapter": "第二章 数据收集", "topic": "抽样方法", "stem": "Which of the following would best help avoid response bias?", "options": {"A": "Proper use of randomization", "B": "A larger sample size", "C": "A smaller sample size", "D": "A longer survey", "E": "Careful wording of the questions"}, "image": null, "answer": "E", "explanation": "慎重措辞、避免诱导性问题最能防止反应偏差；随机化或加大样本不能消除措辞引起的偏差。"}, {"id": 144, "chapter": "第二章 数据收集", "topic": "抽样方法", "stem": "A buffet restaurant features a salad bar, a chicken and beef\nentree station, a seafood station, a pizza station, a vegetable\nstation, and a dessert bar, with a total of 235 items. What kind of\nsampling procedure would be recommended for use by a visiting\nrestaurant evaluator?", "options": {"A": "A simple random sample", "B": "A stratified sample", "C": "A cluster sample", "D": "A systematic sample", "E": "A convenience sample"}, "image": null, "answer": "B", "explanation": "各站点/餐台相当于不同层，从每层中抽取若干菜品比较合适，推荐分层抽样。"}, {"id": 145, "chapter": "第二章 数据收集", "topic": "抽样方法", "stem": "A dietary supplement manufacturer wants to test consistency of\nthe krill oil content of omega-3 supplements produced in one\nfactory. The company decides to select one bottle of capsules\nrandomly from each day’s production run at the factory and\nanalyze every capsule in this bottle as to krill oil content. What\ntype of sampling is this?", "options": {"A": "Cluster", "B": "Convenience", "C": "Simple random", "D": "Stratified", "E": "Systematic"}, "image": null, "answer": "A", "explanation": "随机抽取整瓶并检测瓶内每一粒胶囊，是把瓶子作为群的整群抽样。"}, {"id": 146, "chapter": "第二章 数据收集", "topic": "抽样方法", "stem": "A study is made to determine whether taking multiple AP classes\nin high school eventually leads to a higher 4-year graduation rate\nfrom college. In comparing the college records of 250 students,\nhalf of whom took multiple AP courses in high school, it is\ndetermined that a higher proportion of the students who took the\nAP courses graduated from college in four years than did\nstudents who had not taken these courses. Based on this study,\nstudents are encouraged to take multiple AP courses. Which of\nthe following is an incorrect statement?", "options": {"A": "This an observational study, not an experiment.", "B": "Self-selection here makes drawing conclusions such as\nencouraging more AP enrollment difficult.", "C": "Although this study indicates a relation, it does prove causation.", "D": "There could well be a confounding variable responsible for the\nseeming relationship.", "E": "A more meaningful study would be to compare a simple random\nsample (SRS) from each of the two groups of 125 students."}, "image": null, "answer": "C", "explanation": "观察性研究只能说明关联，不能证明因果，C说“确实证明因果关系”是错误的。"}, {"id": 147, "chapter": "第二章 数据收集", "topic": "抽样方法", "stem": "A survey with regard to using park funds for building a new\nskateboard facility resulted in the following table.\n21–\n30\n31–\n40\n41–\n50\n51–\n60\n61–\n70\n71–\n80\nTotal\nAge\nFor\n35\n22\n18\n15\n5\n1\n96\nAgainst\n20\n33\n37\n40\n50\n54\n234\nWhich of the following sampling strategies was most likely used?", "options": {"A": "Simple random", "B": "Cluster", "C": "Stratified", "D": "Systematic", "E": "Proportional"}, "image": "img/q147.png", "answer": "D", "explanation": "各年龄组总人数都是55人，说明对各年龄段按等间隔系统（或等额）抽取，最可能为系统抽样。"}, {"id": 148, "chapter": "第二章 数据收集", "topic": "抽样方法", "stem": "To survey the opinions of people attending a Lincoln Center\nballet, a surveyor plans to select every 30th theatergoer as he or\nshe exits at the conclusion. Will this method result in a simple\nrandom sample?", "options": {"A": "Yes, because each theatergoer has the same chance of being\nselected", "B": "Yes, but only if everyone leaves by the same exit", "C": "Yes, because the 29 out of 30 theatergoers who are not selected\nwill form a control group", "D": "Yes, because this is an example of systematic sampling, which is\na special case of simple random sampling", "E": "No, because not every sample of the intended size has an equal\nchance of being selected"}, "image": null, "answer": "E", "explanation": "系统抽样不是SRS的特例：并非每个同样大小的样本都有相同的被抽中机会。"}, {"id": 149, "chapter": "第二章 数据收集", "topic": "抽样方法", "stem": "To conduct a survey on television viewing preferences, a\nresearcher opens a telephone book to a random page, closes his\neyes, puts his finger down on the page, and then reads off the\nnext 80 names. Which of the following is not a true statement?", "options": {"A": "The survey incorporates chance.", "B": "The procedure will not give a simple random sample.", "C": "The procedure results in a systematic sample.", "D": "The procedure could easily result in selection bias.", "E": "The use of a phone book will result in undercoverage bias."}, "image": null, "answer": "C", "explanation": "先随机定起点再依次读80个名字，不是“每隔k个”的系统抽样，C说法不正确；其余均为真。"}, {"id": 150, "chapter": "第二章 数据收集", "topic": "抽样方法", "stem": "The annual AP national conference is attended by a large\nnumber of AP teachers from all AP subject areas. At one of these\nconferences, a random sample of AP Statistics teachers at the\nconference was selected and surveyed as to the proportion of\ntheir students who go on to do well in college. To what population\ncan the result be generalized?", "options": {"A": "All students", "B": "All students taking an AP course", "C": "All students taking AP Statistics", "D": "Students of all AP teachers who attended the conference", "E": "Students of all AP Statistics teachers who attended the\nconference"}, "image": null, "answer": "E", "explanation": "样本来自与会AP统计教师，只能推广到所有参加该会议的AP统计教师的学生。"}, {"id": 151, "chapter": "第二章 数据收集", "topic": "抽样方法", "stem": "Sampling error is", "options": {"A": "the result of bias", "B": "the mean of a sample statistic", "C": "the standard deviation of a sample statistic", "D": "the standard error of a sample statistic", "E": "the difference between a population parameter and an estimate\nof that parameter\nBIAS IN SAMPLING"}, "image": null, "answer": "E", "explanation": "抽样误差是总体参数与其估计值之间的差异，任何抽样估计都不可避免。"}, {"id": 152, "chapter": "第二章 数据收集", "topic": "抽样偏差", "stem": "A typical method of telephone survey is to call a random number\n(with a random number generator). If there is no answer, call\nanother number. If someone answers, check if the person is at\nleast 18. If not, call another number; if yes, proceed with the\nsurvey. There are many concerns with this methodology. Which\nof the following is not a concern?", "options": {"A": "People living in large households may be underrepresented.", "B": "People with multiple phones may be overrepresented.", "C": "People with unlisted numbers may be underrepresented.", "D": "People with children under 18 years old may be\nunderrepresented.", "E": "People with caller ID and recording machines may be\nunderrepresented."}, "image": null, "answer": "C", "explanation": "随机拨号同样能拨到未登记号码，所以“未登记号码者代表性不足”不是问题；其余各项都是问题。"}, {"id": 153, "chapter": "第二章 数据收集", "topic": "抽样偏差", "stem": "Two surveys using different sampling methods were both aimed\nat determining the extent of illegal drug use on a college campus.\nThe first method was able to obtain responses from only a small\npercentage of those selected to be surveyed. The second method\nreceived a very high rate of response; however, the answers to\nthe question on drug use indicated a much smaller use of drugs\non campus than the health counselors knew to be true. What\ntype of bias did each sampling method illustrate?", "options": {"A": "The first method illustrates response bias; the second method\nillustrates nonresponse bias.", "B": "The first method illustrates nonresponse bias; the second\nmethod illustrates response bias.", "C": "The first method illustrates selection bias; the second method\nillustrates nonresponse bias.", "D": "The first method illustrates selection bias; the second method\nillustrates response bias.", "E": "The first method illustrates nonresponse bias; the second method\nillustrates selection bias."}, "image": null, "answer": "B", "explanation": "第一种是很多人不回应——无应答偏差；第二种是回应不真实——反应偏差。"}, {"id": 154, "chapter": "第二章 数据收集", "topic": "抽样偏差", "stem": "42 percent of Americans have blood type A. In a random sample\nof 200 Americans, 40 percent had blood type A. What is the most\nlikely explanation for this difference between the observed and\nexpected percents?", "options": {"A": "Selection bias", "B": "Confounding between those who have and do not have blood\ntype A", "C": "Experimental design error", "D": "Incomplete sampling frame", "E": "Sampling variability"}, "image": null, "answer": "E", "explanation": "随机样本的统计量与总体参数的差异由抽样变异（抽样误差）自然造成。"}, {"id": 155, "chapter": "第二章 数据收集", "topic": "抽样偏差", "stem": "Pollsters are becoming more and more concerned about the\naccuracy of telephone surveys because of nonresponse bias.\nWhich of the following best explains their concern?", "options": {"A": "The attitude of the surveyor might cause the respondent to hang\nup.", "B": "The wording of the questions might influence the way people\nrespond.", "C": "If people are uncomfortable with the questions, they might not\nanswer truthfully.", "D": "Not everyone has phones, so certain groups of people—for\nexample, the homeless—will be missed in telephone surveys.", "E": "With caller ID and answering machines, many people simply\nchoose to not participate in telephone surveys."}, "image": null, "answer": "E", "explanation": "来电显示和录音电话使许多人干脆不接听、不参与，这正是无应答偏差。"}, {"id": 156, "chapter": "第二章 数据收集", "topic": "抽样偏差", "stem": "A school district with 25 elementary schools is considering\nhaving the school day begin and end one hour later. They are\nconsidering four methods of surveying parents.\nMethod 1: Randomly select one of the 25 schools, and\ncontact every parent with children in that school by mail.\nMethod 2: Send a survey home with every elementary\nschool student, and ask parents to fill it out and return it\nwithin a week.\nMethod 3: Randomly select 10 parents from each\nelementary school, send them a survey, follow up with a\nphone call if they don’t return the survey, and follow up\nwith a home visit if they don’t return the survey or answer\nthe phone.\nMethod 4: Make a public announcement on a local\ntelevision news program asking people to post their opinions\nto an online website.\nHow many of the above methods clearly illustrate some form of\nbias?", "options": {"A": "0", "B": "1", "C": "2", "D": "3", "E": "4"}, "image": "img/q156.png", "answer": "D", "explanation": "方法1（只抽一所学校）、方法2（自愿寄回）和方法4（自愿网络回应）明显有偏差；方法3分层加追踪访问设计良好，故3个有偏差。"}, {"id": 157, "chapter": "第二章 数据收集", "topic": "抽样偏差", "stem": "An assembly line produces 1,000 circuit boards a day. As a\nquality check, a sample of 50 boards are checked. One day\nunbeknownst to everyone, the first 600 boards off the assembly\nline are fine but the last 400 are defective. Which sampling\nmethod would have best picked up on the fraction of defective\nboards produced that day?", "options": {"A": "A simple random sample, randomly picking 50 of the 1,000\nboards", "B": "A systematic sample, randomly picking one of the first 20\nboards and then testing it and every 20th board from that point\non", "C": "A convenience sample, randomly picking one of the first 951\nboards and then testing it and the following 49 boards", "D": "Go through the boards one at a time, flipping a coin for each\nboard, and testing the first 50 boards for which the coin comes\nup “heads”", "E": "All of the above methods involve randomization, so all are\nequally likely to have picked up on the fraction of defective\nboards produced that day"}, "image": null, "answer": "B", "explanation": "每20块检测一块的系统抽样会均匀覆盖全天生产，最容易同时抽到正常和缺陷批次。"}, {"id": 158, "chapter": "第二章 数据收集", "topic": "抽样偏差", "stem": "A home economist is interested in estimating the proportion of\npeople who make their beds in the morning, and she plans to\ninterview a random sample of 500 people. If instead she\nincreases the sample size to 750, what effect will this have on\nbias and on the variance of the estimator?", "options": {"A": "The bias and the variance will remain the same.", "B": "Both the bias and the variance will decrease.", "C": "The bias will decrease, while the variance will remain the same.", "D": "The bias will remain the same, while the variance will decrease.", "E": "The bias will increase, while the variance will decrease."}, "image": null, "answer": "D", "explanation": "随机抽样下偏差不随n改变，但增大样本量会降低估计量的方差。"}, {"id": 159, "chapter": "第二章 数据收集", "topic": "抽样偏差", "stem": "What fault do all of these sampling designs have in common?\nI. The Wall Street Journal plans to make a prediction for the\nRepublican nominees for open Senate seats based on a\nsurvey of its readers.\nII. An Internet site asks viewers to vote on their choice for\n“Television Series of the Year.”\nIII. A college teacher randomly picks a sample of his students\nand interviews them concerning how much they feel they\nhave learned during the academic semester.", "options": {"A": "None of the designs satisfactorily controls for sampling error.", "B": "All the designs confuse association with cause and effect.", "C": "All the designs have errors that can lead to strong bias.", "D": "All the designs make improper use of stratification.", "E": "None of the designs makes use of chance in selecting a sample."}, "image": null, "answer": "C", "explanation": "三个设计分别有读者调查偏差、自愿回应偏差和方便样本偏差，都会导致很强的偏差。"}, {"id": 160, "chapter": "第二章 数据收集", "topic": "抽样偏差", "stem": "A researcher plans a study to examine the depth of belief in faith\nhealing (the power of prayer in curing illnesses) among the adult\npopulation. She interviews a simple random sample of 50 adults\nleaving church one Sunday morning. All but two of them agree\nto participate in the survey, consisting of a series of neutrally\nworded questions. Which of the following is a true statement?", "options": {"A": "Selection bias makes this a poorly designed survey.", "B": "The high response rate makes this a well-designed survey.", "C": "The use of neutral wording makes this a well-designed survey.", "D": "The proper use of chance as evidenced by the simple random\nsample and neutral wording makes this a well-designed survey.", "E": "The large sample, n ≥ 30, helps makes this a well-designed\nsurvey."}, "image": null, "answer": "A", "explanation": "只在教堂门口抽样研究信仰治疗，存在严重的抽样（选择）偏差，设计很差。"}, {"id": 161, "chapter": "第二章 数据收集", "topic": "抽样偏差", "stem": "Which of the following statements is incorrect?", "options": {"A": "Convenience samples often lead to undercoverage bias.", "B": "There is no way to fix the results if a biased sampling method\nwas employed.", "C": "Questionnaires with nonneutral wording are likely to have\nresponse bias.", "D": "Voluntary response samples often underrepresent people with\nstrong opinions.", "E": "Nonresponse bias should be avoided because those who do not\nrespond might have different views from those who do respond."}, "image": null, "answer": "D", "explanation": "自愿回应样本倾向于高估（而非低估）持有强烈意见者的比例，D说法错误；其余正确。"}, {"id": 162, "chapter": "第二章 数据收集", "topic": "抽样偏差", "stem": "Two wordings for a questionnaire on whether Guamanians want\nindependence from the U.S. are as follows.\nI. Would you vote for sovereignty for Guam?\nII. Would you support a Guam separate from the United\nStates?\nOne of these questions showed 32 percent support for\nindependence while the other showed 38 percent support. Which\nquestion produced which result and why?", "options": {"A": "The first question showed the 38 percent support because of lack\nof randomization in choice of subjects as evidenced by the\nwording of the questions.", "B": "The first question showed the 32 percent support because of a\nplacebo effect.", "C": "The first question showed the 38 percent support due to lack of\nblocking.", "D": "The first question showed the 32 percent support because of\nresponse bias due to the wording of the question.", "E": "The first question showed the 38 percent support because of\nresponse bias due to the wording of the question."}, "image": null, "answer": "E", "explanation": "措辞引起的反应偏差：“主权”措辞较正面，第一问得到较高的38%支持率。"}, {"id": 163, "chapter": "第二章 数据收集", "topic": "抽样偏差", "stem": "When conducting surveys, bias refers to which of the following?", "options": {"A": "Lack of a control group", "B": "Difficulty in concluding cause and effect", "C": "Confounding variables", "D": "An example of sampling error", "E": "A tendency to favor the selection of certain members of a\npopulation"}, "image": null, "answer": "E", "explanation": "偏差指抽样系统性地偏向于选中或有利于总体中某些成员的倾向。"}, {"id": 164, "chapter": "第二章 数据收集", "topic": "抽样偏差", "stem": "To find out a town’s average family size, a researcher interviews\na random sample of parents at a school’s PTA meeting. The\naverage family size in the 50-family sample is 3.72. Is this\nestimate probably too low or too high?", "options": {"A": "Too low, because of undercoverage bias", "B": "Too low, because convenience samples underestimate average\nresults", "C": "Too high, because of undercoverage bias", "D": "Too high, because convenience samples overestimate average\nresults", "E": "Too high, because voluntary response samples overestimate\naverage results"}, "image": null, "answer": "C", "explanation": "在PTA会场抽样遗漏了不参加PTA的（通常子女较少的）家庭，覆盖不全使平均家庭规模估计偏高。"}, {"id": 165, "chapter": "第二章 数据收集", "topic": "抽样偏差", "stem": "Which of the following is a true statement about sampling?", "options": {"A": "There is no such thing as a “bad sample.”", "B": "Sampling techniques that use probability techniques effectively\neliminate bias.", "C": "Sampling techniques that allow the surveyor to choose\nparticipants with care and precision go a long way in controlling\nbias.", "D": "If bias is present in a sampling procedure, it can be overcome by\ndramatically increasing the sample size.", "E": "When choosing a sample, sample size is more important than the\nfraction of the population that is surveyed."}, "image": null, "answer": "E", "explanation": "样本质量取决于样本量绝对数而非抽样比例；有偏的方法加大样本也无法消除偏差（D错）。"}, {"id": 166, "chapter": "第二章 数据收集", "topic": "抽样偏差", "stem": "In general, for a survey to yield usable results,", "options": {"A": "sampling error must be avoided", "B": "a sample size of at least n = 30 is necessary", "C": "researchers must carefully choose people who they think are\nrepresentative of the population", "D": "researchers must be careful in the way questions are worded", "E": "a census should be strived for as it is the only truly accurate\nmethodology"}, "image": null, "answer": "D", "explanation": "问卷措辞不当会引入反应偏差，调查必须慎重措辞；其余说法都不成立。"}, {"id": 167, "chapter": "第二章 数据收集", "topic": "抽样偏差", "stem": "A researcher planning a survey of high school mathematics\nteachers in Illinois has faculty lists for each of the 97 high school\ndistricts in the state. The procedure is to obtain a simple random\nsample of teachers from each of these districts rather than\ngrouping all the districts together and obtaining a sample from\nthe entire group. Which of the following is not a true statement\nabout the resulting stratified sample?", "options": {"A": "It is more susceptible to bias than a simple random sample.", "B": "It is easier and more cost-effective than a simple random\nsample.", "C": "It gives comparative information that a simple random sample\nwouldn’t give.", "D": "It recognizes that opinions of teachers in rural districts may\ndiffer from those in urban districts.", "E": "All of the above are true statements."}, "image": null, "answer": "A", "explanation": "分层随机样本并不比简单随机样本更容易有偏差（各层内均随机抽取），A是不正确的说法。"}, {"id": 168, "chapter": "第二章 数据收集", "topic": "抽样偏差", "stem": "A national magazine aimed at college students asks its\nsubscribers if they would have chosen their current college if\nthey had to make the choice over again. Of the 12,000 or so\nresponses, 75 percent said no. What does this show?", "options": {"A": "The survey is meaningless because of voluntary response bias.", "B": "No meaningful conclusion is possible without knowing\nsomething more about the characteristics of the subscribers.", "C": "The survey would have been more meaningful if it had picked a\nrandom sample of the 12,000 subscribers who responded.", "D": "The survey would have been more meaningful if it had used a\ncontrol group.", "E": "This was a legitimate sample, randomly drawn from the\nsubscribers and of sufficient size to allow the conclusion that\nmost of the subscribers would have chosen a different college if\nthey had to make the choice over again."}, "image": null, "answer": "A", "explanation": "这是自愿回应调查，持有不满意见者更倾向回应，结果因自愿回应偏差而无意义。"}, {"id": 169, "chapter": "第二章 数据收集", "topic": "抽样偏差", "stem": "Internet surveys have been increasing dramatically. Which of the\nfollowing is not true about such surveys?", "options": {"A": "They allow for the use of multimedia elements not available to\nother survey modes.", "B": "There is less response bias than associated with interviewer\nadministered modes.", "C": "There is less nonresponse and undercoverage bias.", "D": "They have a low cost compared with most other types of\nsurveys.", "E": "It is convenient for respondents to take the surveys at their own\ntime and own pace.\nEXPERIMENTS AND OBSERVATIONAL\nSTUDIES"}, "image": null, "answer": "C", "explanation": "网络调查的无应答和覆盖不全（不是人人上网）偏差更严重，C是不真实的说法。"}, {"id": 170, "chapter": "第二章 数据收集", "topic": "实验与观察研究", "stem": "An advantage to using observational studies as opposed to\nexperiments is that", "options": {"A": "observational studies are generally cheaper to conduct", "B": "concluding cause and effect is generally easier from\nobservational studies", "C": "observational studies are generally not subject to bias", "D": "observational studies involve the use of randomization", "E": "observational studies can make use of stratification"}, "image": null, "answer": "A", "explanation": "观察性研究通常不需施加处理、成本较低；实验才便于推断因果。"}, {"id": 171, "chapter": "第二章 数据收集", "topic": "实验与观察研究", "stem": "Two studies are run to compare the number of doctor visits of\nlow-income families enrolled in the Children’s Health Insurance\nProgram (CHIP) to those receiving cash subsidies for health\ncare. The first study interviews 75 families who have been in\neach government program for at least five years, while the\nsecond randomly assigns 75 families to each program and\ninterviews them after five years. Which of the following is a true\nstatement?", "options": {"A": "Both studies are observational studies because of the time\nperiod involved.", "B": "Both studies are observational studies because there are no\ncontrol groups.", "C": "The first study is an observational study, while the second is an\nexperiment.", "D": "The first study is an experiment, while the second is an\nobservational study.", "E": "Both studies are experiments because in each, families are\nreceiving treatments (due to either CHIP or cash)."}, "image": null, "answer": "C", "explanation": "第一项只观察已选择项目的家庭（观察研究）；第二项随机分配处理，是实验。"}, {"id": 172, "chapter": "第二章 数据收集", "topic": "实验与观察研究", "stem": "Which of the following is most useful in establishing cause-andeffect relationships?", "options": {"A": "A complete census", "B": "A least squares regression line showing high correlation", "C": "A simple random sample (SRS)", "D": "A well-designed, well-conducted observational study\nincorporating chance to ensure a representative sample", "E": "A controlled experiment"}, "image": null, "answer": "E", "explanation": "只有对照实验（随机分配处理）才能建立因果关系。"}, {"id": 173, "chapter": "第二章 数据收集", "topic": "实验与观察研究", "stem": "Some medical investigations have indicated that nondrinkers are\nmore likely to develop dementia than drinkers. Assuming these\ninvestigations were carefully carried out, what is the most\nreasonable conclusion?", "options": {"A": "These were probably observational studies, and so no conclusion\nabout drinking providing protection against dementia is proper.", "B": "Given that these investigations were carefully carried out, they\nprobably were experiments. Thus they do show that drinking\nprovides protection against dementia (although it may have\nother undesired outcomes).", "C": "Without information about the use or nonuse of randomization,\nno conclusion about cause and effect is possible.", "D": "Without the use of blinding—for example, having all\nparticipants drink something (alcohol or a harmless placebo)—\nno conclusion is proper.", "E": "No matter how carefully the investigations were carried out, no\nconclusion is possible without knowing the sample sizes."}, "image": null, "answer": "A", "explanation": "此类医学调查几乎都是观察性研究，只能提示关联，不能得出饮酒预防痴呆的因果结论。"}, {"id": 174, "chapter": "第二章 数据收集", "topic": "实验与观察研究", "stem": "A critical difference between experiments and observational\nstudies is that", "options": {"A": "experiments are free to choose subjects from an entire\npopulation while an observational study considers only a\nrandom sample", "B": "tests of significance can be used on data collected from\nexperiments but not on data from observational studies", "C": "observational studies make use of randomization, while\nexperiments do not", "D": "experiments are generally more cost-effective and time\neffective than observational studies", "E": "an experiment often suggests a causal relationship, while an\nobservational study only suggests an association"}, "image": null, "answer": "E", "explanation": "实验通过随机分配处理可提示因果关系，观察性研究只能提示关联。"}, {"id": 175, "chapter": "第二章 数据收集", "topic": "实验与观察研究", "stem": "Which of the following is a true statement?", "options": {"A": "The purposes behind random sampling and random assignment\nare different.", "B": "In well-designed observational studies, responses are\nsystematically influenced during the collection of data.", "C": "In well-designed experiments, the treatments result in responses\nthat are as similar as possible.", "D": "A well-designed experiment always has a single treatment but\nmay test that treatment at different levels.", "E": "Randomized block design refers to deciding randomly which\nblocks receive which treatments."}, "image": null, "answer": "A", "explanation": "随机抽样目的是便于对总体推断，随机分配目的是平衡混杂以推断因果，两者目的不同。"}, {"id": 176, "chapter": "第二章 数据收集", "topic": "实验与观察研究", "stem": "In which of the following studies are cause-and-effect conclusions\n(rather than simple association conclusions) probably\nreasonable?", "options": {"A": "Studies showing that drivers who speed more than 25 percent\nabove the speed limit have higher mortality rates in accidents\nthan those who do not speed.", "B": "Studies showing that college students who regularly use illegal\ndrugs have lower GPAs than students who don’t.", "C": "Studies showing that teenagers using a particular skin cream\ntend to have fewer skin problems than teenagers not using that\ncream.", "D": "Studies noting weights and ages at death tend to show that\nunderweight people live longer than overweight people.", "E": "Studies noting that children born to parents who are nonsmokers\nand nondrinkers tend to engage in less risky behavior as adults."}, "image": null, "answer": "C", "explanation": "只有护肤霜研究最可能通过随机对照实验进行，其他各项均为观察性研究，存在大量混杂。"}, {"id": 177, "chapter": "第二章 数据收集", "topic": "实验与观察研究", "stem": "Suppose you wish to compare the average age of math/science\nteachers to the average age of English/social studies teachers in\nyour high school. Which is the most appropriate technique for\ngathering the needed data?", "options": {"A": "Census", "B": "Sample survey", "C": "Experiment", "D": "Observational study", "E": "None of these methods is appropriate."}, "image": null, "answer": "A", "explanation": "比较本校两类教师的平均年龄，教师总数不大，直接普查全体最合适。"}, {"id": 178, "chapter": "第二章 数据收集", "topic": "实验与观察研究", "stem": "Does “an apple a day keep the doctor away?” A study is\nproposed to pick a random sample of 100 healthy adults, divide\nthem into two groups based on who eats apples regularly and\nwho doesn’t, follow them for five years, and calculate the mean\nnumber of doctor visits of each group. Which of the following is\na correct statement?", "options": {"A": "This is an observational study because no treatment is applied.", "B": "This is an experiment, but one design fault is that the two groups\nmight be different sizes.", "C": "This is an experiment where eating apples is the treatment but\nthere is no control.", "D": "This is an experiment, but no causation can be concluded\nbecause there was no random assignment.", "E": "This is an experiment, but no causation can be concluded\nbecause of confounding variables."}, "image": null, "answer": "D", "explanation": "按是否吃苹果分组并非随机分配，即使称为实验也不能得出因果结论，因为缺少随机分配。"}, {"id": 179, "chapter": "第二章 数据收集", "topic": "实验与观察研究", "stem": "Personnel entering the armed forces undergo extensive medical\ntesting, and these records are a valuable source of data. Medical\nresearchers compared vitamin D blood level records of military\npersonnel who later developed multiple sclerosis (MS) with the\nvitamin D blood level records of military personnel who never\ndeveloped MS. What can be said about this study?", "options": {"A": "This is an observational study, and the subjects are military\npersonnel. Two variables are considered, vitamin D blood levels\nand whether or not the subject develops MS.", "B": "This is an experiment where the treatments are the different\nlevels of vitamin D in the blood.", "C": "This is an experiment where the response variable is whether or\nnot the subject develops MS.", "D": "This is an experiment where the subjects who do not develop\nMS act as a control group.", "E": "This is an experiment. However, causation cannot be concluded\nbecause there is no random assignment."}, "image": null, "answer": "A", "explanation": "研究者只是比较历史记录，没有施加处理，是观察性研究；受试者是军人，两个变量为维生素D水平和是否患MS。"}, {"id": 180, "chapter": "第二章 数据收集", "topic": "实验与观察研究", "stem": "In a study of medical patches to help prevent the urge to smoke,\n500 heavy smokers (at least two packs per day) were randomly\nsorted into two groups. The smokers in one group were given a\nnew nicotine patch, while smokers in the other group were given\na similar-looking patch containing no medication. The subjects\ndid not know who received the drug. In the weeks to follow, all\n500 smokers showed a similar reduction in the urge to smoke.\nThis is an example of which of the following?", "options": {"A": "The effect of a treatment unit", "B": "The placebo effect", "C": "The control group effect", "D": "Sampling error", "E": "Voluntary response bias"}, "image": null, "answer": "B", "explanation": "两组都出现相似的烟瘾下降，含药物的贴片并无额外效果，说明是安慰剂效应。"}, {"id": 181, "chapter": "第二章 数据收集", "topic": "实验与观察研究", "stem": "One hundred patients suffering from severe back pain are\nrandomly selected from hospital records. Half the patients are\ntold to listen to classical music and sit in the dark the next time\nthey experience severe pain. The remaining patients are told to\nuse neither of these possible remedies. Participants then report\nback as to relief, if any. Faults of this experimental design\ninclude all of the following except which response?", "options": {"A": "Lack of randomization", "B": "Confounding variables", "C": "Lack of blinding", "D": "Unclear factor levels", "E": "Measurement of response variable"}, "image": null, "answer": "A", "explanation": "患者是随机选取并分组的，不缺随机化；缺双盲、因子水平不清、自我报告测量等才是缺陷。"}, {"id": 182, "chapter": "第二章 数据收集", "topic": "实验与观察研究", "stem": "Which of the following are most important in minimizing the\nplacebo effect?", "options": {"A": "Randomization and blinding", "B": "Randomization and a control", "C": "Replication and randomization", "D": "Replication and blinding", "E": "Blinding and a control"}, "image": null, "answer": "E", "explanation": "采用双盲和使用对照组（安慰剂）最能控制安慰剂效应。"}, {"id": 183, "chapter": "第二章 数据收集", "topic": "实验与观察研究", "stem": "Which of the following is a true statement about blocking?", "options": {"A": "The paired (matched pairs) comparison design is a special case\nof blocking.", "B": "Blocking is a useful procedure when there are certain attributes,\nwhich are not under study, that may affect the outcomes.", "C": "Blocking is to experiment design as stratification is to sampling\ndesign.", "D": "By controlling certain variables, blocking can make conclusions\nmore specific.", "E": "All of the above are true statements about blocking."}, "image": null, "answer": "E", "explanation": "配对设计是分组的特例（A），分组用于控制可能影响结果的属性（B），分组之于实验如分层之于抽样（C），都能使结论更具体（D），全对。"}, {"id": 184, "chapter": "第二章 数据收集", "topic": "实验与观察研究", "stem": "Two procedures are to be compared in the treatment of autism:\nthe use of antipsychotic medication versus family therapy. The\nexperimental design is to create homogeneous blocks with\nrespect to the severity of the developmental disorder. How\nshould randomization be used for a randomized block design?", "options": {"A": "Within each block, randomly pick half the patients to receive\neach procedure.", "B": "Randomly pick half of all patients to receive each procedure, but\nthen analyze the results separately by blocks.", "C": "Randomly choose which blocks will receive which procedure.", "D": "Randomly choose half the blocks to receive each procedure for a\ngiven time period. Then for the same time period, switch the\nprocedure in each block and compare the results.", "E": "For ethical reasons, allow families to choose which procedure\nthey prefer taking, but then randomly assign patients to the\nblocks."}, "image": null, "answer": "A", "explanation": "随机区组设计中，应在每个区组内部把病人随机平分到两种处理。"}, {"id": 185, "chapter": "第二章 数据收集", "topic": "实验与观察研究", "stem": "Before playing a soccer game, players rested for 2, 4, or 6 hours.\nHalf of each group was given an energy drink before starting the\ngame. Determine the number of factors, levels for each factor,\nand number of treatments.", "options": {"A": "1 factor with 2 levels; 5 treatments", "B": "2 factors, one with 1 level and one with 2 levels; 3 treatments", "C": "2 factors, one with 2 levels and one with 3 levels; 5 treatments", "D": "2 factors, one with 2 levels and one with 3 levels; 6 treatments", "E": "3 factors, each with 2 levels; 6 treatments"}, "image": null, "answer": "D", "explanation": "两个因子：休息时间（3水平）与能量饮料（2水平），处理组合=3×2=6种。"}, {"id": 186, "chapter": "第二章 数据收集", "topic": "实验与观察研究", "stem": "A medical researcher is running an experiment on the treatment\nof carotid artery stenosis with an invasive procedure (stenting or\nsurgery) and a blood thinner (Plavix or aspirin). She has 40\nvolunteers, half men and half women, all suffering from\nsymptomatic carotid artery stenosis. The researcher randomly\nselects 10 men and 10 women for treatment with stenting and\nPlavix, while the remaining volunteers are treated with surgery\nand aspirin. Of the following, which is the most important\nobservation about this procedure?", "options": {"A": "The variables—invasive procedure and blood thinner—are\nconfounded.", "B": "The variables—gender and invasive procedure—are confounded.", "C": "The variables—gender and blood thinner—are confounded.", "D": "No variables are confounded.", "E": "There is a hidden confounding variable."}, "image": null, "answer": "A", "explanation": "支架总是配Plavix、手术总是配阿司匹林，两种处理完全捆绑，侵入性操作与血液稀释剂混杂。"}, {"id": 187, "chapter": "第二章 数据收集", "topic": "实验与观察研究", "stem": "A sports product agency measures average feet per second for\nballs hit by high school baseball players using each of four\ndifferent brand bats (Easton, Louisville Slugger, Rawlings, and\nDeMarini). Which of the following is true?", "options": {"A": "There are four explanatory variables and one response variable.", "B": "There is one explanatory variable with four levels of response.", "C": "Feet per second is the only explanatory variable, but there are\nfour response variables corresponding to the different brands.", "D": "There are four levels of a single explanatory variable.", "E": "Each explanatory level has an associated level of response."}, "image": null, "answer": "D", "explanation": "球棒品牌是单个因子，四个品牌即该因子的四个水平。"}, {"id": 188, "chapter": "第二章 数据收集", "topic": "实验与观察研究", "stem": "In a random survey of 30 students at an elementary school, those\nwith greater weight appear to have higher math levels. Of the\nfollowing, which is the most important conclusion about this\nobservation?", "options": {"A": "Parents interested in their child’s math level should feed their\nchildren more.", "B": "Generalizing from a single elementary school and a single study\nis unreasonable.", "C": "The sample size is too small for any reasonable conclusion.", "D": "There is a confounding variable.", "E": "As long as the sample was a simple random sample (SRS) from\namong all students at the school, a cause-and-effect conclusion\nis valid."}, "image": null, "answer": "D", "explanation": "年龄/年级是混杂变量，同时影响体重和数学水平，不能由此得出因果结论。"}, {"id": 189, "chapter": "第二章 数据收集", "topic": "实验与观察研究", "stem": "A sociologist and musician team wants to estimate the\nproportion of adults who sing in the shower. They go to a\nBroadway musical and a Lincoln Center concert and then\nrandomly pick 25 adults from each event to survey. If the team\nmembers want to be able to generalize their findings, what are\nthe appropriate sample and population?", "options": {"A": "The sample is the 50 musical/concert attendees, and the\npopulation is all adults.", "B": "The sample is the 50 musical/concert attendees, and the\npopulation is all adults who attend Broadway musicals or\nLincoln Center concerts.", "C": "The sample is the 50 musical/concert attendees, and the\npopulation is all adults who enjoy music.", "D": "The sample is all adults who attend Broadway musicals or\nLincoln Center concerts, and the population is all adults.", "E": "The sample is all adults who attend Broadway musicals or\nLincoln Center concerts, and the population is all adults who\nenjoy music."}, "image": null, "answer": "B", "explanation": "样本是两场合随机抽取的50名观众，总体只能是去百老汇音乐剧或林肯中心音乐会的成人。"}, {"id": 190, "chapter": "第二章 数据收集", "topic": "实验与观察研究", "stem": "Which of the following best explains why researchers try to\nguard against confounding when designing experiments?", "options": {"A": "Confounding can conflict with randomization.", "B": "Confounding can negate the benefits of blinding.", "C": "Confounding can lead to bias.", "D": "Confounding can lead to uncertainty as to which variable is\ncausing an effect.", "E": "Confounding can make it more difficult to separate subjects into\ntreatment and control groups."}, "image": null, "answer": "D", "explanation": "混杂变量使研究者无法确定是哪个变量真正导致了观测到的效应。"}, {"id": 191, "chapter": "第二章 数据收集", "topic": "实验与观察研究", "stem": "A psychologist takes 50 volunteers suffering from acrophobia to\nthe top of the Empire State Building and instructs them to stay\nthere for 12 straight hours in an effort to cure their fear of\nheights. He contacts them a week later, and 60 percent claim to\nno longer have a fear of heights. Which of the following is a true\nstatement?", "options": {"A": "Because the subjects were volunteers, their desire to be cured\ncould be a confounding variable.", "B": "With 60 percent claiming a cure after a single treatment, this is\nstatistically significant.", "C": "With this large of a sample, n = 50 ≥ 30, the positive\nconclusions can be generalized to the general population of\npeople suffering from acrophobia.", "D": "Bringing the subjects to the top of the Empire State Building is a\ntreatment, so this is an experiment and positive conclusions are\njustified.", "E": "Because the subjects were volunteers, positive conclusions can\nbe generalized only to other volunteers.\nA nutritionist conducts a study on serving size. As each attendee\narrived at an ice cream social, they were handed either a 12ounce or an 18-ounce bowl depending upon whether the next\ndigit in a random digit table was odd or even. The nutritionist\nthen noted how much ice cream each attendee put into his or her\nbowl. All attendees participated. The nutritionist compared the\nmean helping of ice cream taken by those given 12-ounce bowls\nversus those given 18-ounce bowls. What method of testing is the\nnutritionist using?"}, "image": null, "answer": "A", "explanation": "志愿者强烈的治愈愿望（自选择）与治疗效应混杂，是真实的混杂变量，结论不可靠。"}, {"id": 192, "chapter": "第二章 数据收集", "topic": "实验与观察研究", "stem": "", "options": {"A": "A census because all attendees participated", "B": "An observational study because the nutritionist did not tell\nanyone how much ice cream to take but, rather, simply observed", "C": "An experiment with a blocked design, blocked on size of bowl", "D": "An experiment with a completely randomized design", "E": "An experiment with a matched-pair design"}, "image": null, "answer": "D", "explanation": "每位到场者被随机分配到12盎司或18盎司碗两种处理之一，是完全随机化设计实验。"}, {"id": 193, "chapter": "第二章 数据收集", "topic": "实验与观察研究", "stem": "A winemaker is undecided as to which of two grape varieties, A\nor B, to use in the production of malbec. He runs a test with a\nrandom sample of wine connoisseurs. The winemaker randomly\nassigns each participant to one of four groups and then has taste\ntests as follows.\nTaste First\nTaste Second\nGroup\n1\nVariety A, Bottle\nlabeled “10”\nGroup\n2\nVariety B, Bottle\nlabeled “20”\nGroup\n3\nVariety B, Bottle\nlabeled “10”\nGroup\nVariety A, Bottle\nlabeled “20”\nVariety B, Bottle\nlabeled “20”\nVariety A, Bottle\nlabeled “10”\nVariety A, Bottle\nlabeled “20”\nVariety B, Bottle\nlabeled “10”\n4\nWhich of the following explains the use of random assignment\nhere?", "options": {"A": "The connoisseurs might be partial to whichever wine they taste\nfirst. So groups 1 and 4 will first taste the malbec made from\ngrape variety A, while groups 2 and 3 will first taste the malbec\nmade from grape variety B.", "B": "The connoisseurs might subconsciously be partial to the bottle\nlabeling, “10” and “20.” So groups 1 and 2 will taste from\nbottles labeled “10” with variety A grapes and bottles labeled\n“20” with variety B grapes. Groups 3 and 4 will taste from\nbottles labeled “10” with variety B grapes and bottles labeled\n“20” with variety A grapes.", "C": "Random assignment in this context is needed to minimize the\neffect of confounding variables, the most obvious of which are\nthe order of tasting and the labels on the bottles.", "D": "All three of the above statements explain the use of random\nassignment here.", "E": "This study is really an observational study, so random\nassignment does not apply."}, "image": null, "answer": "D", "explanation": "A、B、C分别说明随机分配平衡了品尝顺序和标签偏好的影响、从而减少混杂，三条都成立。"}, {"id": 194, "chapter": "第二章 数据收集", "topic": "实验与观察研究", "stem": "Twenty volunteers with chronic backache agreed to try yoga\nexercises for a month, at the end of which 45 percent reported a\nlower level of back pain. An influential yoga instructor\nannounced on his TV program that yoga exercises are better at\nrelieving back pain than not doing anything. Which of the\nfollowing best explains why the results of the study do not\nsupport the yoga instructor’s TV claim?", "options": {"A": "The subjects were volunteers.", "B": "The subjects self-reported pain levels.", "C": "The sample was small.", "D": "There was no control group.", "E": "There was no randomization."}, "image": null, "answer": "D", "explanation": "没有对照组，无法知道自然缓解等效应，不能得出瑜伽优于“什么都不做”的结论。"}, {"id": 195, "chapter": "第二章 数据收集", "topic": "实验与观察研究", "stem": "A randomized block design will be used in an experiment to test\nwhether or not having alcoholic drinks affects drug effectiveness\nfor people taking different medications. Which of the following\nrelates to the composition of the blocks?", "options": {"A": "Subjects should be randomly assigned to the blocks.", "B": "Subjects in the same block should all receive the same treatment\n(alcohol or no alcohol).", "C": "Subjects in the same block should be as similar as possible (all\nshould be taking the same medication).", "D": "Subjects in the same block should be as different as possible (all\nthe medications under study should be represented by subjects in\neach block).", "E": "Participants in each block should be blinded as to which\nmedication they are taking."}, "image": null, "answer": "C", "explanation": "区组内应尽量相似（服用同一种药物），即按用药类型组成同质区组。"}, {"id": 196, "chapter": "第二章 数据收集", "topic": "实验与观察研究", "stem": "A high school manager runs a study on whether players can hit a\nball farther with an aluminum or a wood bat. For 10 games, as\neach player comes to bat, the manager reads off the next digit\nfrom a random digit table. If the digit is odd, the player uses an\naluminum bat. If the digit is even, the player uses a wood bat.\nWhat best describes the manager’s study?", "options": {"A": "An observational study", "B": "A completely randomized design", "C": "A matched-pair design", "D": "A randomized block design with two blocks", "E": "A flawed experiment because the two types of bat can be\nconfounded"}, "image": null, "answer": "B", "explanation": "每次击球独立地按随机数字随机分配球棒类型，是完全随机化设计。"}, {"id": 197, "chapter": "第二章 数据收集", "topic": "实验与观察研究", "stem": "What is the purpose of blocking in experimental design?", "options": {"A": "To control the level of an experiment", "B": "To be a first step in randomization", "C": "To reduce bias", "D": "To reduce variation", "E": "To provide a substitute for a control group"}, "image": null, "answer": "D", "explanation": "分组的目的是把已知来源的变异隔离出来，从而降低随机变异、提高精度。"}, {"id": 198, "chapter": "第二章 数据收集", "topic": "实验与观察研究", "stem": "A botanist tests three different strengths of a new fertilizer on\nthe growth of tomato plants. Which of the following is a true\nstatement?", "options": {"A": "There are three explanatory variables and one response variable.", "B": "There is one explanatory variable with three levels of response\nvariable.", "C": "Growth is the only explanatory variable, but there are three\nresponse variables corresponding to the three strengths.", "D": "There are three levels of a single explanatory variable.", "E": "Each explanatory variable has an associated level of response."}, "image": null, "answer": "D", "explanation": "肥料强度是单一因子的三个水平，响应变量是番茄生长。"}, {"id": 199, "chapter": "第二章 数据收集", "topic": "实验与观察研究", "stem": "Which of the following is a true statement about the design of\nmatched pair experiments?", "options": {"A": "Randomization is unnecessary in matched pair designs.", "B": "Blocking is one form of matched pair design.", "C": "Each subject might receive both treatments.", "D": "Stratification into two equal-sized strata is an example of\nmatched pairs.", "E": "Each pair of subjects receives identical treatment, and the\ndifferences in their responses are noted."}, "image": null, "answer": "C", "explanation": "配对实验中每个受试者可先后接受两种处理（随机化顺序），故随机化仍需要。"}, {"id": 200, "chapter": "第二章 数据收集", "topic": "实验与观察研究", "stem": "Some researchers believe that drinking a glass of red wine every\nday can raise the level of HDL, the “good” cholesterol. A study is\nperformed by randomly selecting half of a group of volunteers to\ndrink a glass of red wine every day while the rest are instructed\nnot to drink any red wine. Is this an experiment or an\nobservational study?", "options": {"A": "An experiment with a single factor", "B": "An observational study with comparison and randomization", "C": "An experiment with a control group and blinding", "D": "An observational study with no bias", "E": "An experiment with blocking\nMC Probability\nAnswers for Chapter 3 are on pages 262–277.\nBASIC PROBABILITY RULES"}, "image": null, "answer": "A", "explanation": "随机分配喝红酒与否是施加处理，故为单因子实验；但无盲法，C不成立。"}, {"id": 201, "chapter": "第三章 概率", "topic": "基本概率规则", "stem": "The following is from a particular region’s mortality table.\nAge\n0\n20\n40\n60\n80\nNumber\nSurviving\n10,000\n9,800\n9,350\n8,600\n5,100\nWhat is the probability that a 20-year-old will survive to be 60?", "options": {"A": "1,400\n9,800", "B": "1,400\n10,000", "C": "8,600\n9,800", "D": "8,600\n10,000", "E": "9,800\n10,000"}, "image": null, "answer": "C", "explanation": "条件概率：20岁活着的人共9800人，其中活到60岁的8600人，故P=8600/9800。"}, {"id": 202, "chapter": "第三章 概率", "topic": "基本概率规则", "stem": "The pattern on the back of an insect causes an entomologist to\nsuspect that she has found a rare subspecies of the insect. In the\nrare subspecies, 96 percent have the pattern. In the common\nCHAPTER\n3\nsubspecies, only 3 percent have the pattern. The rare subspecies\naccounts for 0.2 percent of the population. Which expression is\nused to calculate the probability that a bug having this pattern is\nrare?", "options": {"A": "(0.002)(0.96)", "B": "1 – (0.002)(0.96)", "C": "(0.002)(0.96)\n(0.998)(0.03)", "D": "(0.002)(0.96)\n(0.002)(0.96) + (0.998)(0.03)", "E": "(0.998)(0.03)\n(0.002)(0.96) + (0.998)(0.03)"}, "image": null, "answer": "D", "explanation": "贝叶斯公式：P(稀有|有花纹)=(0.002)(0.96)/[(0.002)(0.96)+(0.998)(0.03)]。"}, {"id": 203, "chapter": "第三章 概率", "topic": "基本概率规则", "stem": "A student calculates the probabilities of the four outcomes of an\nexperiment to be 0, 0.25, 0.80, and –0.05. The proper conclusion\nis that", "options": {"A": "the sum of the individual probabilities is 1", "B": "one of the outcomes will never occur", "C": "one of the outcomes will occur over 50 percent of the time", "D": "all of the above are true", "E": "the student made an error"}, "image": null, "answer": "E", "explanation": "概率不能为负，−0.05无效，说明该学生的概率赋值有误（虽然其余陈述在算术上成立，但赋值本身无效）。"}, {"id": 204, "chapter": "第三章 概率", "topic": "基本概率规则", "stem": "There are two games involving flipping a fair coin. In the first\ngame, you win a prize if you can throw between 40% and 60%\nheads. In the second game, you win a prize if you can throw more\nthan 60% heads. For each game, would you rather flip the coin\n15 times or 150 times?", "options": {"A": "15 times for each game", "B": "150 times for each game", "C": "15 times for the first game, and 150 times for the second game", "D": "150 times for the first game, and 15 times for the second game", "E": "The outcomes of the games do not depend on the number of\nflips."}, "image": null, "answer": "D", "explanation": "投掷次数越多，正面比例越集中在50%附近：游戏1要求40%~60%之间，次数多更有利，选150次；游戏2要求超过60%，次数少更容易偏离50%，选15次。"}, {"id": 205, "chapter": "第三章 概率", "topic": "基本概率规则", "stem": "Which of the following is not a probability density function?", "options": {"A": "f (x) = 1, 0 ≤ x ≤ 1", "B": "f (x) = 0.05, 0 ≤ x ≤ 20\n0.3,\n0 ≤x ≤2\n0.2,\n2 < x ≤4", "C": "f(x) = {", "D": "f (x) = 2x, 0 ≤ x ≤ 1", "E": "f (x) = 4x, 0 ≤ x ≤ 0.25"}, "image": null, "answer": "E", "explanation": "∫[0,0.25] 4x dx = 2(0.25)^2 = 0.125 ≠ 1，不满足密度函数总面积为1；A、B、C、D的面积均为1。"}, {"id": 206, "chapter": "第三章 概率", "topic": "基本概率规则", "stem": "Suppose that during any weekend afternoon, the probabilities\nthat you receive a text message is 0.94, an e-mail is 0.75, and\nboth a text and an e-mail is 0.705. Are receiving a text message\nand receiving an e-mail independent events?", "options": {"A": "Yes, because (0.94)(0.75) = 0.705", "B": "No, because (0.94)(0.75) = 0.705", "C": "Yes, because 0.94 > 0.75 > 0.705", "D": "No, because 0.5(0.94 + 0.75) ≠ 0.705", "E": "There is insufficient information to answer this question."}, "image": null, "answer": "A", "explanation": "(0.94)(0.75)=0.705 恰好等于P(两者)，满足P(A∩B)=P(A)P(B)，故独立。"}, {"id": 207, "chapter": "第三章 概率", "topic": "基本概率规则", "stem": "Suppose you toss a fair coin five times and it comes up tails\nevery time. Which of the following is a true statement?", "options": {"A": "By the law of large numbers, the next toss is more likely to be\nheads rather than another tail.", "B": "By the properties of conditional probability, the next toss is\nmore likely to be tails given that five tosses in a row have been\ntails.", "C": "Coins actually do have memories, and thus what comes up on\nthe next toss is influenced by the past tosses.", "D": "The law of large numbers tells how many tosses will be\nnecessary before the percentages of heads and tails are again in\nbalance.", "E": "None of the above are true statements."}, "image": null, "answer": "E", "explanation": "硬币无记忆，每次仍以0.5概率正面；大数定律不预测短期补偿，也不说明何时恢复平衡，A、B、C、D均不成立。"}, {"id": 208, "chapter": "第三章 概率", "topic": "基本概率规则", "stem": "Suppose P(X) = 0.36 and P(Y) = 0.41. If P(X ∣ Y) = 0.27, what is\nP(Y ∣ X)?", "options": {"A": "(0.36)(0.41)\n0.27", "B": "(0.36)(0.27)\n0.41", "C": "(0.27)(0.41)\n0.36", "D": "0.27\n(0.36)(0.41)", "E": "0.27\n0.36+0.41"}, "image": null, "answer": "C", "explanation": "P(Y|X)=P(X∩Y)/P(X)=[P(X|Y)P(Y)]/P(X)=(0.27)(0.41)/0.36。"}, {"id": 209, "chapter": "第三章 概率", "topic": "基本概率规则", "stem": "Body temperatures of healthy humans are roughly normal. If 10\npercent of people have temperatures above 37.333°C and if 20\npercent have temperatures below 36.781°C, what is the mean of\nthis distribution?", "options": {"A": "36.965°C", "B": "37.000°C", "C": "37.057°C", "D": "37.149°C", "E": "The mean cannot be calculated from the given information."}, "image": "img/q209.png", "answer": "B", "explanation": "由1.282σ=37.333−μ和0.842σ=μ−36.781，得σ=0.552/2.124≈0.26，μ=37.333−1.282(0.26)≈37.000。"}, {"id": 210, "chapter": "第三章 概率", "topic": "基本概率规则", "stem": "Given the probabilities P", "options": {"E": "0.64−0.25\n1.25", "A": "0.25\n0.64", "B": "0.64 – 0.25", "C": "0.25 + 0.64", "D": "0.64−0.25\n0.75"}, "image": null, "answer": "B", "explanation": "互斥时P(E∪F)=P(E)+P(F)，故P(F)=0.64−0.25=0.39。"}, {"id": 211, "chapter": "第三章 概率", "topic": "基本概率规则", "stem": "Given the probabilities P", "options": {"E": "0.73−0.35\n1.35", "A": "0.35\n0.73", "B": "0.73 – 0.35", "C": "0.35 + 0.73", "D": "0.73−0.35\n0.65"}, "image": null, "answer": "D", "explanation": "独立时P(E∪F)=P(E)+P(F)−P(E)P(F)：0.73=0.35+F−0.35F，解得F=(0.73−0.35)/0.65≈0.585。"}, {"id": 212, "chapter": "第三章 概率", "topic": "基本概率规则", "stem": "If P", "options": {"A": "0.144", "B": "0.626", "C": "0.770", "D": "0.856", "E": "There is insufficient information to answer this question."}, "image": null, "answer": "B", "explanation": "独立时P(A∪B)=0.32+0.45−(0.32)(0.45)=0.77−0.144=0.626。"}, {"id": 213, "chapter": "第三章 概率", "topic": "基本概率规则", "stem": "A marksman hits exactly one of the first two clay pigeons\nthrown. From that point on, the probability that he makes the\nnext shot is equal to the proportion of shots made up to that\npoint. If he takes two more shots, what is the probability he ends\nup making a total of exactly two shots?", "options": {"A": "1\n4", "B": "1\n3", "C": "1\n2", "D": "2\n3", "E": "3\n4"}, "image": null, "answer": "C", "explanation": "此刻命中率=1/2，再打两枪恰中一枪的概率=2(1/2)(1/2)=1/2。"}, {"id": 214, "chapter": "第三章 概率", "topic": "基本概率规则", "stem": "In wake of the 2016 Ebola crisis, a rapid test was developed that\ntested positive in 99.5 percent of patients with Ebola but gave a\nfalse positive in 3 percent of healthy people. Suppose 0.1 percent\nof the population in a particular area has Ebola. If a person in\nthis area tests positive, what is the probability he or she has\nEbola?", "options": {"A": "0.001\n0.995", "B": "(0.001)(0.995)\n(0.001)(0.995)+(0.999)(0.03)", "C": "(0.999)(0.03)\n(0.001)(0.995)+(0.999)(0.03)", "D": "(0.995)(0.03)\n(0.001)(0.995)+(0.999)(0.03)", "E": "(0.001)(0.999)\n(0.001)(0.995)+(0.999)(0.03)"}, "image": null, "answer": "B", "explanation": "贝叶斯：P(埃博拉|阳性)=(0.001)(0.995)/[(0.001)(0.995)+(0.999)(0.03)]≈0.032。"}, {"id": 215, "chapter": "第三章 概率", "topic": "基本概率规则", "stem": "Given two events, E and F, such that P", "options": {"E": "There is not enough information to answer this question.", "A": "independent and mutually exclusive", "B": "independent but not mutually exclusive", "C": "mutually exclusive but not independent", "D": "neither independent nor mutually exclusive"}, "image": null, "answer": "B", "explanation": "P(E∩F)=0.420+0.350−0.623=0.147=(0.420)(0.350)，满足独立性；交集大于0故不互斥。"}, {"id": 216, "chapter": "第三章 概率", "topic": "基本概率规则", "stem": "Which of the following is not a valid discrete probability\ndistribution for the set {x1, x2, x3}?", "options": {"A": "P(x1) = 0, P(x2) = 1, P(x3) = 0", "B": "P(x1) = 1\n3 , P(x2) = 1\n3 , P(x3) = 1\n3", "C": "P(x1) = 1\n6 , P(x2) = 1\n3 , P(x3) = 1\n2", "D": "P(x1) = 2\n5 , P(x2) = 4\n5 , P(x3) = −1\n5", "E": "All of the above are valid probability distributions."}, "image": null, "answer": "D", "explanation": "P(x3)=−1/5为负概率，违反概率非负性，不是有效分布。"}, {"id": 217, "chapter": "第三章 概率", "topic": "基本概率规则", "stem": "Suppose that when taking a multiple-choice exam and when\nstudents have no idea what an answer is, 45 percent will guess, 35\npercent will choose answer", "options": {"C": "0.20\n0.55", "A": "0.20", "B": "0.20\n0.45", "D": "0.20\n0.80", "E": "1 −0.20\n0.80"}, "image": null, "answer": "C", "explanation": "不猜的人占0.35+0.20=0.55，条件概率P(选最长答案|不猜)=0.20/0.55。"}, {"id": 218, "chapter": "第三章 概率", "topic": "基本概率规则", "stem": "Given that 51.0 percent of the U.S. population are female and\nthat 5.9 percent of the population are over 75 years of age, can\nwe conclude that (0.510)(0.059) = 3.01 percent of the population\nare women older than 75?", "options": {"A": "Yes, by the multiplication rule", "B": "Yes, by conditional probabilities", "C": "Yes, by the law of large numbers", "D": "No, because the events are not independent", "E": "No, because the events are not mutually exclusive"}, "image": null, "answer": "D", "explanation": "乘法规则要求独立，性别与年龄(是否超过75岁)显然不独立，不能直接相乘。"}, {"id": 219, "chapter": "第三章 概率", "topic": "基本概率规则", "stem": "In a set of 10 boxes, 7 boxes each contain 3 red and 2 blue\nmarbles, while the remaining boxes each contain 1 red and 4\nblue marbles. A player randomly picks a box and then randomly\npicks a marble from that box. She wins if she ends up with a red\nmarble. If she plays 4 times, what is the probability she wins\nexactly twice?", "options": {"A": "4(0.48)2(0.52)2", "B": "6(0.48)2(0.52)2", "C": "4(0.7)2(0.3)2", "D": "6(0.8)2(0.2)2", "E": "4(0.32)2(0.68)2"}, "image": null, "answer": "B", "explanation": "P(红)=(0.7)(3/5)+(0.3)(1/5)=0.48；4次恰胜2次=C(4,2)(0.48)^2(0.52)^2=6(0.48)^2(0.52)^2。"}, {"id": 220, "chapter": "第三章 概率", "topic": "基本概率规则", "stem": "Suppose E and F are independent events with P", "options": {"E": "P(E or F) = 0.775", "A": "P(F) = 0.4", "B": "P(F) = 0.6", "C": "P(E or F) = 0.375", "D": "P(E or F) = 0.625"}, "image": null, "answer": "D", "explanation": "独立时P(F)=0.15/0.4=0.375；P(E或F)=0.4+0.375−0.15=0.625。"}, {"id": 221, "chapter": "第三章 概率", "topic": "基本概率规则", "stem": "Suppose that for a certain midwestern city, in any given year the\nprobability of a tornado hitting is 0.15, the probability of\nflooding from the river running through the city is 0.08, and the\nprobability of both a tornado and flooding is 0.02. What is the\nprobability of flooding given that a tornado hits?", "options": {"A": "0.02\n0.08", "B": "0.02\n0.15", "C": "0.08\n0.15", "D": "0.02\n(0.15)(0.08)", "E": "(0.02)(0.08)\n0.15"}, "image": null, "answer": "B", "explanation": "条件概率P(水灾|龙卷风)=P(两者)/P(龙卷风)=0.02/0.15。"}, {"id": 222, "chapter": "第三章 概率", "topic": "基本概率规则", "stem": "A dentist compiles the following summary data from interviews\nof her patients.\n55% floss once a day, and these patients have a 0.04\nprobability of a cavity each year.\n30% floss twice a day, and these patients have a 0.01\nprobability of a cavity each year.\n15% don’t floss, and these patients have a 0.10 probability\nof a cavity each year.\nWhat is the probability that one of the dentist’s patients flosses\nand has a cavity for any given year?", "options": {"A": "0.04 + 0.01", "B": "0.04 + 0.01 + 0.10", "C": "(0.55 + 0.30)(0.04 + 0.01)", "D": "(0.55)(0.04) + (0.30)(0.01)", "E": "(0.55)(0.04)+(0.30)(0.01)\n(0.55)(0.04)+(0.30)(0.01)+(0.15)(0.10)"}, "image": null, "answer": "D", "explanation": "全概率公式：(0.55)(0.04)+(0.30)(0.01)=0.025。"}, {"id": 223, "chapter": "第三章 概率", "topic": "基本概率规则", "stem": "A game show offers the contestant who wins the grand prize a\nchoice of three boxes, one containing 4 red chips and 1 white\nchip, one containing 2 red chips and 2 white chips, and one\ncontaining 1 red chip and 3 white chips. The contestant\nrandomly picks a box and with eyes closed, reaches in and picks\na chip. A white chip earns $5,000 and a red chip earns $10,000.\nThe contestant then tosses a fair die. If it comes up “6,” the prize\nis doubled. Does the contestant have more than an even chance of\nwalking away with exactly $10,000?", "options": {"A": "Yes, because 23\n45 > 1\n2", "B": "No, because 22\n45 < 1\n2", "C": "Yes, because 31\n60 > 1\n2", "D": "No, because 29\n60 < 1\n2", "E": "The contestant has a 0.5 chance of walking away with exactly\n$10,000."}, "image": null, "answer": "A", "explanation": "P(白)=(1/3)(1/5+2/4+3/4)=29/60，P(红)=31/60；恰好得$10,000=(31/60)(5/6)+(29/60)(1/6)=184/360=23/45>1/2。"}, {"id": 224, "chapter": "第三章 概率", "topic": "基本概率规则", "stem": "Nine-tenths of adults change their jobs at least once. The reasons\nfor changing and what they change to are as shown in the\nfollowing tables.\nChange in\nCareer\nInterests\nReason\nProbability\n0.55\n0.10\n0.20\n0.15\nChange To\nRelated Field\nNew Field\nUnemployed\nHigher\nOther\nReason\nSalary\nFired\nProbability\n0.40\n0.55\n0.05\nAssuming independence of all variables, what is the probability\nthat an adult decides to change a job, based on higher salary, and\nmove into a new field?", "options": {"A": "0.0495", "B": "0.055", "C": "0.585", "D": "0.595", "E": "0.65"}, "image": "img/q224.png", "answer": "A", "explanation": "0.9(换工作)×0.10(因更高薪水)×0.55(进入新领域)=0.0495，独立性允许直接相乘。"}, {"id": 225, "chapter": "第三章 概率", "topic": "基本概率规则", "stem": "Suppose two events, S and T, have the nonzero probabilities p\nand q, respectively. Which of the following is impossible?", "options": {"A": "p + q > 1", "B": "p – q < 0", "C": "p\nq > 1", "D": "S and T are both independent and mutually exclusive.", "E": "S and T are neither independent nor mutually exclusive."}, "image": null, "answer": "D", "explanation": "若S、T互斥且概率非零，则P(S∩T)=0≠pq，与独立矛盾，故既独立又互斥不可能。"}, {"id": 226, "chapter": "第三章 概率", "topic": "基本概率规则", "stem": "A computer security company puts in bids for a state contract\nand for a federal contract. The company’s chief financial officer\nbelieves that the probabilities of being awarded the state and\nfederal grants are 0.32 and 0.15, respectively. If the probability\nof receiving both grants is 0.048 and if these three probabilities\nare correct, which of the following statements is true?", "options": {"A": "Receiving the two grants are independent and mutually\nexclusive events.", "B": "Receiving the two grants are independent but not mutually\nexclusive events.", "C": "Receiving the two grants are mutually exclusive but not\nindependent events.", "D": "Receiving the two grants are not mutually exclusive and not\nindependent events.", "E": "None of the above statements can be known for sure until the\nprobability of receiving at least one grant is known."}, "image": null, "answer": "B", "explanation": "0.048=(0.32)(0.15)成立→独立；P(两者)=0.048>0→不互斥。"}, {"id": 227, "chapter": "第三章 概率", "topic": "基本概率规则", "stem": "According to a college’s records, 46 percent of its students live in\nthe dorms, 65 percent have meal contracts, and 33 percent do\nboth. For a randomly selected student, what is the probability\nthat he/she either lives in a dorm or has a meal contract, but not\nboth?", "options": {"A": "0.13", "B": "0.22", "C": "0.32", "D": "0.45", "E": "0.78"}, "image": null, "answer": "D", "explanation": "只取其一：(0.46−0.33)+(0.65−0.33)=0.13+0.32=0.45。"}, {"id": 228, "chapter": "第三章 概率", "topic": "基本概率规则", "stem": "The distribution of jelly beans in a large assorted bag is shown in\nthe following table.\nVery\nCherry\nVanilla\nFlavor\nProbability\n0.4\n0.2\n0.2\n0.1\n0.1\nIf two jelly beans are picked at random, what is the probability\nthey are different flavors?", "options": {"A": "0.2", "B": "0.26", "C": "0.37", "D": "0.74", "E": "0.63\nGreen\nApple\nLicorice\nPeach"}, "image": "img/q228.png", "answer": "D", "explanation": "P(同味)=Σp^2=0.16+0.04+0.04+0.01+0.01=0.26，不同味=1−0.26=0.74。"}, {"id": 229, "chapter": "第三章 概率", "topic": "基本概率规则", "stem": "A medical researcher examines the records of cases where a\nchild comes in to a pediatrician complaining of a sore throat and\nthe next day a sibling comes in also complaining of a sore throat.\nSuppose 20 percent of children with sore throats test positive for\nstrep throat. If a child tests positive for strep, there is a 0.7\nprobability that his/her sibling who comes in the next day will\nalso test positive for strep. If a child tests negative for strep,\nthere is a 0.1 probability that his/her sibling who comes in the\nnext day will test positive for strep. Let S be the random variable\nfor the number of children in such pairs who test positive for\nstrep. What is the probability distribution for S?", "options": {"A": "x\n0\n1\n2\nP(x)\n1/3\n1/3\n1/3", "B": "x\n0\n1\n2\nP(x)\n0.5\n0.25\n0.25", "C": "x\n0\n1\n2\nP(x)\n0.5\n0.33\n0.17", "D": "x\n0\n1\n2\nP(x)\n0.72\n0.14\n0.14", "E": "x\n0\n1\n2\nP(x)\n0.8\n0.06\n0.14"}, "image": null, "answer": "D", "explanation": "P(S=0)=0.8×0.9=0.72；P(S=1)=0.2×0.3+0.8×0.1=0.14；P(S=2)=0.2×0.7=0.14。"}, {"id": 230, "chapter": "第三章 概率", "topic": "基本概率规则", "stem": "85 percent of people wear seat belts. The probability of serious\ninjury in an accident is 8 percent for those wearing seat belts and\n36 percent for those not wearing seat belts. What is the\nprobability of serious injury in an accident?", "options": {"A": "0.054", "B": "0.068", "C": "0.122", "D": "0.318", "E": "0.44"}, "image": null, "answer": "C", "explanation": "全概率：0.85×0.08+0.15×0.36=0.068+0.054=0.122。"}, {"id": 231, "chapter": "第三章 概率", "topic": "基本概率规则", "stem": "At many colleges, many nonmath majors choose to take at least\none math/computer science course. Suppose 16 percent take\ncomputer science, 53 percent take statistics, and 62 percent take\nat least one of these two offerings. What is the probability a\nrandomly chosen nonmath major takes both a computer science\nclass and a statistics class?", "options": {"A": "(0.16)(0.53)", "B": "0.16 + 0.53 – 0.62", "C": "0.62 – (0.16)(0.53)", "D": "(0.62 – 0.16)(0.62 – 0.53)", "E": "(0.16)(0.53)\n0.62"}, "image": null, "answer": "B", "explanation": "P(两者)=P(CS)+P(统计)−P(至少一门)=0.16+0.53−0.62=0.07。"}, {"id": 232, "chapter": "第三章 概率", "topic": "基本概率规则", "stem": "The following are parts of the probability distributions for the\nrandom variables X and Y.\nIf X and Y are independent and the joint probability P(X = 3, Y =\n2) = 0.1, what is P(Y = 4)?", "options": {"A": "0.1", "B": "0.2", "C": "0.3", "D": "0.4", "E": "0.5"}, "image": null, "answer": "C", "explanation": "X的概率和为1得P(X=3)=0.5；独立故P(Y=2)=0.1/0.5=0.2；P(Y=4)=1−0.2−0.2−0.3=0.3。"}, {"id": 233, "chapter": "第三章 概率", "topic": "基本概率规则", "stem": "A research firm is successful in contacting 65 percent of the\nhouseholds randomly selected for telephone surveys. What is the\nprobability that the firm is successful in contacting a household\ngiven that it was unsuccessful in contacting the previous two\nhouseholds?", "options": {"A": "0.65", "B": "(0.35)2(0.65)", "C": "3(0.35)2(0.65)", "D": "1 – (0.35)2(0.65)", "E": "1 – [(0.35)3 + (0.35)2(0.65)]\nBINOMIAL AND GEOMETRIC\nPROBABILITIES"}, "image": null, "answer": "A", "explanation": "各户联系相互独立，之前两次失败不影响下一次，概率仍为0.65。"}, {"id": 234, "chapter": "第三章 概率", "topic": "二项与几何分布", "stem": "Suppose only 70 percent of claims on a particular news program\nare accurate. If four independent claims are made during one\nprogram, what is the probability that less than half are\naccurate?", "options": {"A": "6(0.7)2(0.3)2", "B": "(0.3)4 + 4(0.7)(0.3)3", "C": "(0.3)4 + 4(0.7)(0.3)3 + 6(0.7)2(0.3)2", "D": "1 – [(0.3)4 + 4(0.7)(0.3)3]", "E": "1 – [(0.7)4 + 4(0.3)(0.7)3]"}, "image": null, "answer": "B", "explanation": "不到一半准确即4条中0条或1条准确：(0.3)^4+4(0.7)(0.3)^3。"}, {"id": 235, "chapter": "第三章 概率", "topic": "二项与几何分布", "stem": "Which of the following statements is incorrect?", "options": {"A": "The histogram of a binomial distribution with p = 0.5 is always\nsymmetric no matter the value of n, the number of trials.", "B": "The histogram of a binomial distribution with p = 0.1 is\nunimodal.", "C": "The histogram of a binomial distribution with p = 0.1 is skewed\nto the left.", "D": "The histogram of a binomial distribution with p = 0.01 looks\nmore and more symmetric, the larger the value of n.", "E": "The histogram of a binomial distribution with p = 0.001 looks\nmore and more like a normal distribution the larger the value of\nn."}, "image": null, "answer": "C", "explanation": "p=0.1的二项分布峰值偏左、尾部拖向右侧（右偏），说左偏是错误的。"}, {"id": 236, "chapter": "第三章 概率", "topic": "二项与几何分布", "stem": "An inspection procedure at a manufacturing plant involves\npicking three items at random and then accepting the whole lot\nif at least two of the three items are in perfect condition. If in\nreality 91 percent of the whole lot is perfect, what is the\nprobability that the lot will be accepted?", "options": {"A": "(0.91)2", "B": "(0.91)2 + (0.91)3", "C": "3(0.91)2 + (0.91)3", "D": "3(0.09)(0.91)2 + (0.91)3", "E": "1 – (0.91)3"}, "image": null, "answer": "D", "explanation": "至少2件完美：C(3,2)(0.91)^2(0.09)+(0.91)^3=3(0.09)(0.91)^2+(0.91)^3≈0.971。"}, {"id": 237, "chapter": "第三章 概率", "topic": "二项与几何分布", "stem": "In a recent poll, only 6 percent of the American public say they\nhave significant confidence in Congress. In a random sample of\nfive people, what is the probability that at least one has\nsignificant confidence in Congress?", "options": {"A": "(0.06)(0.94)4", "B": "5(0.06)(0.94)4", "C": "5(0.06)(0.94)4 + (0.94)5", "D": "1 – (0.94)5", "E": "1 – [5(0.06)(0.94)4 + (0.94)5]"}, "image": null, "answer": "D", "explanation": "至少一人=1−无人=1−(0.94)^5≈0.266。"}, {"id": 238, "chapter": "第三章 概率", "topic": "二项与几何分布", "stem": "Suppose we have a random variable X where the probability\nassociated with the value k is (\n)(0.28)k(0.72)15−k for k = 0, …\n, 15. What is the mean of X?", "options": {"A": "0.28", "B": "0.72", "C": "4.2", "D": "10.8", "E": "None of the above"}, "image": null, "answer": "C", "explanation": "这是n=15、p=0.28的二项分布，均值np=15×0.28=4.2。"}, {"id": 239, "chapter": "第三章 概率", "topic": "二项与几何分布", "stem": "It is estimated that 20 percent of all backseat passengers don’t\nwear seat belts. In a random sample of 5 backseat passengers,\nwhat is the probability that at least two don’t wear seat belts?", "options": {"A": "1 – (0.2)5", "B": "1 – [(0.8)5 + 5(0.2)(0.8)4]", "C": "10(0.2)2(.8)3", "D": "10(0.2)3(0.8)2 + 5(0.2)4(0.8) + (0.2)5\n15\nk", "E": "10(0.2)2(0.8)3 + 5(0.2)(0.8)4 + (0.8)5"}, "image": null, "answer": "B", "explanation": "至少2人=1−(恰0人+恰1人)=1−[(0.8)^5+5(0.2)(0.8)^4]。"}, {"id": 240, "chapter": "第三章 概率", "topic": "二项与几何分布", "stem": "You win a game if you toss a fair die with 5 coming up in exactly\n1/6 of the tosses. Would you rather flip 12 times or 120 times?", "options": {"A": "12 times because 0.296 > 0.0973", "B": "12 times because of the central limit theorem", "C": "120 times because 120C20 > 12C2", "D": "120 times because of the law of large numbers", "E": "Your chance of winning is the same with 12 or 120 flips."}, "image": null, "answer": "A", "explanation": "12次需恰2个5：66(1/6)^2(5/6)^10≈0.296；120次需恰20个，≈0.097；故选12次更有利。"}, {"id": 241, "chapter": "第三章 概率", "topic": "二项与几何分布", "stem": "A polygraph test (lie detector) will indicate a lie 5 percent of the\ntime when a person is telling the truth and 98 percent of the time\nwhen a person is lying. Suppose three suspects, all innocent and\ntelling the truth, are given polygraph tests. What is the\nprobability that at least one of them is falsely accused of lying?", "options": {"A": "(0.02)(0.98)2", "B": "3(0.02)(0.98)2", "C": "3(0.05)(0.95)2", "D": "1 – (0.95)3", "E": "1 – (0.98)3"}, "image": null, "answer": "D", "explanation": "无辜者被误判说谎概率0.05，至少一人被误判=1−(0.95)^3≈0.143。"}, {"id": 242, "chapter": "第三章 概率", "topic": "二项与几何分布", "stem": "Suppose we have a binomial random variable where the\nprobability of exactly five successes is ( )p5(0.41)8. What is the\nmean of the distribution?", "options": {"A": "2.05", "B": "2.95", "C": "3.28", "D": "5.33", "E": "7.67\nn\n5"}, "image": null, "answer": "E", "explanation": "指数中失败数为8，故n=13、p=1−0.41=0.59，均值np=13×0.59=7.67。"}, {"id": 243, "chapter": "第三章 概率", "topic": "二项与几何分布", "stem": "For which of the following is a binomial an appropriate model?", "options": {"A": "The number of 5s in 20 flips of an unfair die weighted so that\neven numbers come up twice as often as odd number", "B": "The number of baskets in 20 shots where the probability of\nmaking the basket is either 0.92 or 0.34 depending upon whether\nit is a layup or a jump shot", "C": "The number of tosses of a fair coin before tails appear on three\nconsecutive tosses", "D": "The number of rainy days in a given week", "E": "The binomial is not appropriate for any of the above."}, "image": null, "answer": "A", "explanation": "A满足二项条件：次数固定20、每次P(5)恒定、各次独立；B概率不定，C是等待型问题，D各天降雨不独立且p不恒定。"}, {"id": 244, "chapter": "第三章 概率", "topic": "二项与几何分布", "stem": "Suppose the Census Bureau reports a city as having 35 percent\nAfrican-American residents. If 12 people are randomly selected\nfor a jury, what is the probability that fewer than two of the\nchosen people are African-American?", "options": {"A": "(0.35)12 + 12(0.65)(0.35)11", "B": "(0.65)12 + (0.35)(0.65)11", "C": "1 – (0.65)12", "D": "1 – [(0.35)12 + 12(0.65)(0.35)11]", "E": "1 – [(0.65)12 + 12(0.35)(0.65)11]"}, "image": null, "answer": "B", "explanation": "少于2人即0或1人：(0.65)^12+12(0.35)(0.65)^11。"}, {"id": 245, "chapter": "第三章 概率", "topic": "二项与几何分布", "stem": "A blind taste test is conducted with 6 volunteers. Each is\npresented with three small cups of water. For each subject, either\ntwo of the cups are tap water and the other is bottled water or\ntwo of the cups are bottled water and the other is tap water. Each\nvolunteer guesses about which of his/her three cups of water is\ndifferent from the other two. Suppose 4 volunteers give the\ncorrect answer. Could this have happened by chance if they all\nwere blind guessing? What is the probability of at least 4 correct\nanswers if the volunteers were all simply guessing?", "options": {"A": "0.0178", "B": "0.0823", "C": "0.1001", "D": "0.8999", "E": "0.9822"}, "image": null, "answer": "C", "explanation": "瞎猜正确率1/3，n=6：P(X≥4)=15(1/3)^4(2/3)^2+6(1/3)^5(2/3)+(1/3)^6=73/729≈0.1001。"}, {"id": 246, "chapter": "第三章 概率", "topic": "二项与几何分布", "stem": "Which of the following graphs represents a binomial distribution\nwith n = 10 and p = 0.3?", "options": {"A": "", "B": "", "C": "", "D": "", "E": ""}, "image": "img/q246.png", "answer": "D", "explanation": "n=10、p=0.3的分布均值np=3且右偏，对应峰在3附近、右侧拖尾的直方图D。"}, {"id": 247, "chapter": "第三章 概率", "topic": "二项与几何分布", "stem": "A rapid Zika antibody test can be performed in minutes and, if\npositive, can be followed up by a more time-consuming, more\naccurate test. Suppose the rapid test results in positive in 2\npercent of the population and the more accurate test shows that\n94 percent of those who test positive in the rapid test actually\nhave Zika. Assuming that those testing negative in the rapid test\ndo not have Zika, what is the probability that in a random\nsample of three people, none of them have Zika?", "options": {"A": "0.054", "B": "0.831", "C": "0.885", "D": "0.945", "E": "0.9812"}, "image": null, "answer": "D", "explanation": "P(有Zika)=0.02×0.94=0.0188，三人都没有Zika=(0.9812)^3≈0.945。"}, {"id": 248, "chapter": "第三章 概率", "topic": "二项与几何分布", "stem": "A person has a 7 percent chance of winning the daily office\nlottery. What is the probability she first wins on the third day?\n3\n1", "options": {"A": "( )(0.07)2(0.93)", "B": "( )(0.07)(0.93)2\n3\n2", "C": "(0.07)2(0.93)", "D": "(0.07)(0.93)2", "E": "None of the above gives the correct probability."}, "image": null, "answer": "D", "explanation": "几何分布首次成功在第3天：(0.93)^2(0.07)。"}, {"id": 249, "chapter": "第三章 概率", "topic": "二项与几何分布", "stem": "A manufacturer knows that 15 percent of the widgets coming off\nthe assembly line have a minor defect. If an inspector keeps\ninspecting units until he comes upon one with the defect, what is\nthe probability he will have to inspect at most four widgets?", "options": {"A": "0.1275", "B": "0.2775", "C": "0.3859", "D": "0.4780", "E": "0.5563"}, "image": null, "answer": "D", "explanation": "至多检查4件=1−前4件都无缺陷=1−(0.85)^4≈0.478。"}, {"id": 250, "chapter": "第三章 概率", "topic": "二项与几何分布", "stem": "It has been estimated that 78.6 percent of all e-mail is spam. On\na random day, what is the probability that the first spam e-mail\nyou receive is the fourth e-mail of the day?", "options": {"A": "(0.214)3", "B": "(0.214)3(0.786)", "C": "(0.214)(0.786)3", "D": "4(0.214)3(0.786)", "E": "1 – (0.214)3(0.786)\nRANDOM VARIABLES"}, "image": null, "answer": "B", "explanation": "几何分布第4封才是垃圾邮件：(0.214)^3(0.786)。"}, {"id": 251, "chapter": "第三章 概率", "topic": "随机变量", "stem": "Which of the following are true statements?\nI. By the law of large numbers, the mean of a random variable\nwill get closer and closer to a specific value.\nII. The standard deviation of a random variable is never\nnegative.\nIII. The standard deviation of a random variable is 0 only if the\nrandom variable takes a lone single value.", "options": {"A": "I and II only", "B": "I and III only", "C": "II and III only", "D": "I, II, and III", "E": "None of the above gives the complete set of true responses."}, "image": null, "answer": "D", "explanation": "I（大量重复后均值趋于稳定值）、II（标准差恒非负）、III（仅取单一值时标准差才为0）均正确。"}, {"id": 252, "chapter": "第三章 概率", "topic": "随机变量", "stem": "A carnival game has three prizes with the following\nprobabilities:\nPrize ($)\n0\n25\n50\nProbability\n0.8\n0.16\n0.04\nWhat is the variance of the prize variable?\n3 [(0 −6)2 + (25 −6)2 + (50 −6)2]", "options": {"A": "1", "B": "(25 – 6)2(0.16) + (50 – 6)2(0.04)", "C": "(0 – 6)2(0.8) + (25 – 6)2(0.16) + (50 – 6)2(0.04)\n3 [(0 −25)2 + (25 −25)2 + (50 −25)2]", "D": "1", "E": "(0 – 25)2(0.8) + (25 – 25)2(0.16) + (50 – 25)2(0.04)"}, "image": "img/q252.png", "answer": "C", "explanation": "均值μ=0(0.8)+25(0.16)+50(0.04)=6，方差=Σ(x−6)^2·P(x)，即C的表达式。"}, {"id": 253, "chapter": "第三章 概率", "topic": "随机变量", "stem": "An insurance company charges $750 annually for flood\ninsurance policies for those living in designated flood zones. The\npolicy specifies the company will pay $25,000 for major flood\ndamage and $5,000 for minor flood damage. If the probability of\na storm with minor flood damage during the year is 0.04 and of a\nstorm with major flood damage is 0.01, how much can the\ninsurance company expect to make on 25 policies?", "options": {"A": "$     300", "B": "$     450", "C": "$  7,500", "D": "$11,250", "E": "$18,750"}, "image": null, "answer": "C", "explanation": "每单期望赔付=25000(0.01)+5000(0.04)=450，每单利润=750−450=300，25单共25×300=$7,500。"}, {"id": 254, "chapter": "第三章 概率", "topic": "随机变量", "stem": "Box A has 3 $20 bills and a single $100 bill. Box B has 100 $10\nbills and 300 $50 bills. Box C has 40 $1 bills. You can have all of\nBox C or blindly pick one bill out of either Box A or Box B.\nWhich choice offers the greatest expected winning?", "options": {"A": "Box A", "B": "Box B", "C": "Box C", "D": "Either Box A or Box B, but not Box C", "E": "All boxes offer the same expected winning."}, "image": null, "answer": "E", "explanation": "Box A期望=(3×20+100)/4=40；Box B=(100×10+300×50)/400=40；Box C=40，三者期望相同。"}, {"id": 255, "chapter": "第三章 概率", "topic": "随机变量", "stem": "An Ivy League college has an acceptance rate of 8 percent. In a\nrandom sample of 50 applications, what is the expected number\nof applicants that will be turned down?", "options": {"A": "50(0.08)", "B": "50(0.92)", "C": "50(0.08)(0.92)", "D": "√50(0.08)(0.92)", "E": "√(0.08)(0.92)\n50"}, "image": null, "answer": "B", "explanation": "被拒人数~B(50, 0.92)，期望=50×0.92=46人。"}, {"id": 256, "chapter": "第三章 概率", "topic": "随机变量", "stem": "The number of condos a real estate agent sells monthly has the\nfollowing probability distribution:\nNumber of Condos\n0\n1\n2\n3\nProbability\n0.37\n0.28\n0.25\n0.10\nThe agent averages a commission of $6,500 per sale. What is the\nexpected monthly commission from selling condos?", "options": {"A": "4 × 6,500(0.37 + 0.28 + 0.25 + 0.10)", "B": "6,500[0(0.37) + 1(0.28) + 2(0.25) + 3(0.10)]", "C": "4 × 6,500[0(0.37) + 1(0.28) + 2(0.25) + 3(0.10)]", "D": "6, 500( 0.37+0.28+0.25+0.10\n4\n)", "E": "6,500\n4\n[0(0.37) + 1(0.28) + 2(0.25) + 3(0.10)]"}, "image": null, "answer": "B", "explanation": "期望佣金=6500×E(销量)=6500[0(0.37)+1(0.28)+2(0.25)+3(0.10)]=6500×0.93。"}, {"id": 257, "chapter": "第三章 概率", "topic": "随机变量", "stem": "A company has a choice of three investment schemes. Option I\ngives a sure $50,000 return on investment. Option II gives a 50%\nchance of returning $70,000 and a 50% chance of returning\nnothing. Option III gives a 10% chance of returning $90,000 and\na 90% chance of returning nothing. Which option should the\ncompany choose?", "options": {"A": "Option I if it wants to maximize expected return.", "B": "Option II if it needs at least $60,000 to pay off an overdue loan.", "C": "Option III if it needs at least $80,000 to pay off an overdue loan.", "D": "All of the above answers are correct.", "E": "Because of chance, it really doesn’t matter which option the\ncompany chooses."}, "image": null, "answer": "D", "explanation": "期望收益I(50k)>II(35k)>III(9k)，A对；需至少60k时II有最大机会(50%得70k)，B对；需至少80k时只有III可能(10%得90k)，C对；故三条全对。"}, {"id": 258, "chapter": "第三章 概率", "topic": "随机变量", "stem": "Two fair coins are tossed. If both land on heads, the player wins\n$5. If exactly one lands on heads, the player wins $2. If it costs $3\nto play, what is the player’s expected outcome after six games?", "options": {"A": "Loss of $0.75", "B": "Loss of $1.50", "C": "Loss of $4.50", "D": "Win of $2.25", "E": "Win of $13.50"}, "image": null, "answer": "C", "explanation": "每局期望赢=0.25×5+0.5×2=2.25，扣成本3后每局−0.75，6局共−$4.50。"}, {"id": 259, "chapter": "第三章 概率", "topic": "随机变量", "stem": "The number of days a juror serves on a grand jury can be\nconsidered a random variable. The table below shows the\nrelative frequency distribution for the number of days someone\nserves on a federal grand jury.\nNumber of Days\n1\n2\n3\n4\n5\nRelative Frequency\n0.3\n0.4\n0.15\n0.1\n0.05\nFederal jurors are paid $40 per day. Based on the above\ndistribution, what is the mean amount of pay federal jurors\nreceive for their service?", "options": {"A": "$    2.20", "B": "$  17.60", "C": "$  40", "D": "$  88", "E": "$200"}, "image": "img/q259.png", "answer": "D", "explanation": "E(天数)=1(0.3)+2(0.4)+3(0.15)+4(0.1)+5(0.05)=2.2天，2.2×40=$88。"}, {"id": 260, "chapter": "第三章 概率", "topic": "随机变量", "stem": "A carnival game charges 25 cents per minute while a player is\ntrying to win a prize. The player will receive prizes of different\nvalues. However, it is very difficult to win, and prizes are\nactually independent of the number of minutes played. The\naverage number of minutes a player tries before giving up has a\nmean of 8 and a standard deviation of 2. The average value of the\nprize a player receives has a value of $1.75 with a standard\ndeviation of $0.50. What are the expected value and standard\ndeviation for playing this game for the player?", "options": {"A": "Expected value = –$0.25 and standard deviation = $0.56", "B": "Expected value = –$0.25 and standard deviation = $0.71", "C": "Expected value = $1.50 and standard deviation = $0.56", "D": "Expected value = $1.50 and standard deviation = $0.71", "E": "Expected value = $1.50 and standard deviation = $0.75"}, "image": null, "answer": "B", "explanation": "净收益=奖品−0.25×分钟：E=1.75−0.25×8=−0.25；SD=√((0.25²)(2²)+0.5²)=√0.5≈0.71。"}, {"id": 261, "chapter": "第三章 概率", "topic": "随机变量", "stem": "The four sides of a tetrahedron are labeled 1 through 4. For a\ncertain carnival game, the player wins the number of points on\nthe face-down side when the tetrahedron is tossed. Unbeknownst\nto the player, the tetrahedron is weighted so that the side with a 1\nis twice as likely to land facedown as any of the other sides,\nwhich all have equal probabilities. What are the mean and\nvariance of the number of points a player should expect from\none toss of the polyhedron?", "options": {"A": "Mean is 2.5 and variance is 1.118", "B": "Mean is 2.5 and variance is 1.291", "C": "Mean is 2.2 and variance is 1.166", "D": "Mean is 2.2 and variance is 1.36", "E": "Mean is 2.2 and variance is 1.4"}, "image": null, "answer": "D", "explanation": "P(1)=0.4、P(2)=P(3)=P(4)=0.2：均值=2.2；E(X²)=0.4+0.2(4+9+16)=6.2，方差=6.2−2.2²=1.36。"}, {"id": 262, "chapter": "第三章 概率", "topic": "随机变量", "stem": "Given two independent random variables, X with mean 28.1 and\nstandard deviation 3.4, and Y with mean 23.7 and standard\ndeviation 2.9, which of the following is a true statement?", "options": {"A": "The mean of X – Y is 51.8.", "B": "The median of X – Y is 4.4.", "C": "The range of X – Y is 51.8.", "D": "The standard deviation of X – Y is 6.3.", "E": "The variance of X – Y is 19.97."}, "image": null, "answer": "E", "explanation": "var(X−Y)=3.4²+2.9²=11.56+8.41=19.97正确；但未说X、Y正态，X−Y的中位数未必等于均值4.4，B不必然成立。"}, {"id": 263, "chapter": "第三章 概率", "topic": "随机变量", "stem": "Suppose X and Y are random variables with µx = 45, σx = 12, µy =\n42, and σy = 5. Given that X and Y are independent, what is the\nstandard deviation of the random variable X – Y?", "options": {"A": "√7", "B": "√17", "C": "7", "D": "13", "E": "169"}, "image": "img/q263.png", "answer": "D", "explanation": "SD(X−Y)=√(12²+5²)=√169=13。"}, {"id": 264, "chapter": "第三章 概率", "topic": "随机变量", "stem": "Given a random variable X taking three possible values, x1, x2,\nx3, which of the following is a true statement?", "options": {"A": "x1 + x2 + x3 = 1", "B": "E(X) = 1\n3 ∑xi\n3 ∑(xi −¯x)2", "C": "var(X) = 1", "D": "E(X + c) = E(X) + c", "E": "var(aX) = a var(X)"}, "image": null, "answer": "D", "explanation": "E(X+c)=E(X)+c是期望的线性性质；A、B、C均错，E应为var(aX)=a²var(X)。"}, {"id": 265, "chapter": "第三章 概率", "topic": "随机变量", "stem": "A store sells metallic, printed, and glow-in-the-dark fidget\nspinners for $11.98, $1.47, and $5.40, respectively. If metallic,\nprinted, and glow-inthe-dark fidget spinners represent 10\npercent, 60 percent, and 30 percent, respectively, of the fidget\nspinner sales, what is the expected monetary value of one day’s\nsales if 200 fidgets are sold?", "options": {"A": "11.98(0.10) + 1.47(0.60) + 5.40(0.30)", "B": "200[3(11.98 +1.47 + 5.40)]", "C": "200[11.98(0.10) + 1.47(0.60) + 5.40(0.30)]", "D": "200( 11.98+1.47+5.40\n3\n)", "E": "11.98(0.10)+1.47(0.60)+5.40(0.30)\n200"}, "image": null, "answer": "C", "explanation": "每个期望价格=11.98(0.10)+1.47(0.60)+5.40(0.30)=3.70元，卖出200个即乘200。"}, {"id": 266, "chapter": "第三章 概率", "topic": "随机变量", "stem": "Suppose X and Y are random variables with E(X) = 29, var(X) =\n7, E(Y) = 35, and var(Y) = 9. What are the expected value and\nvariance of the random variable X + Y?", "options": {"A": "E(X + Y) = 32, var(X + Y) = 8", "B": "E(X + Y) = 64, var(X + Y) = 8", "C": "E(X + Y) = 64, var(X + Y) = 16", "D": "E(X + Y) = 64, var(X + Y) = √72 + 92", "E": "There is insufficient information to answer this question."}, "image": null, "answer": "E", "explanation": "E(X+Y)=29+35=64可求，但var(X+Y)=7+9+2Cov(X,Y)，题目未说明独立或给出协方差，方差无法确定。"}, {"id": 267, "chapter": "第三章 概率", "topic": "随机变量", "stem": "Suppose X and Y are independent random variables with E(X) =\n34, var(X) = 4, E(Y) = 23, and var(Y) = 3. What is the variance of\nthe random variable X – Y?", "options": {"A": "4 – 3", "B": "4 + 3", "C": "42 – 32", "D": "42 + 32", "E": "√42 + 32"}, "image": null, "answer": "B", "explanation": "独立时var(X−Y)=var(X)+var(Y)=4+3=7（方差相加，不是标准差相减）。"}, {"id": 268, "chapter": "第三章 概率", "topic": "随机变量", "stem": "Suppose X and Y are independent random variables, both with\nnormal distributions. If X has a mean of 45 with a standard\ndeviation of 4 and if Y has a mean of 35 with a standard\ndeviation of 3, what is the probability that a randomly generated\nvalue of X is greater than a randomly generated value of Y?", "options": {"A": "P(z > –2)", "B": "P(z > –1)", "C": "P(z > 0)", "D": "P(z > 1)", "E": "P(z > 2)"}, "image": null, "answer": "A", "explanation": "X−Y~N(10, √(4²+3²)=5)，P(X>Y)=P(X−Y>0)=P(z>(0−10)/5)=P(z>−2)≈0.977。"}, {"id": 269, "chapter": "第三章 概率", "topic": "随机变量", "stem": "The random variable X has a mean of 15 and a standard\ndeviation of 10. The random variable Y is defined by Y = 5 + 3X.\nWhat are the mean and standard deviation of Y?", "options": {"A": "The mean is 45, and the standard deviation is 30.", "B": "The mean is 45, and the standard deviation is 35.", "C": "The mean is 50, and the standard deviation is 10.", "D": "The mean is 50, and the standard deviation is 30.", "E": "The mean is 50, and the standard deviation is 35."}, "image": null, "answer": "D", "explanation": "Y=5+3X：均值=5+3×15=50，标准差=|3|×10=30。"}, {"id": 270, "chapter": "第三章 概率", "topic": "随机变量", "stem": "A random variable X has a mean of 25 and a standard deviation\nof 8. The random variable X – Y has a mean of 9 and a standard\ndeviation of 10. Assuming X and Y are independent, what are the\nmean and standard deviation of the random variable Y?", "options": {"A": "μY = 16 and σY = 2", "B": "μY = 16 and σY = 6", "C": "μY = 16 and σY = 12.8", "D": "μY = 34 and σY = 2", "E": "μY = 34 and σY = 6"}, "image": null, "answer": "B", "explanation": "μY=25−9=16；var(X−Y)=8²+var(Y)=10²，得var(Y)=36，σY=6。"}, {"id": 271, "chapter": "第三章 概率", "topic": "随机变量", "stem": "Bowler A bowls an average of 225 with a standard deviation of\n15. Bowler B bowls an average of 190 with a standard deviation\nof 20. Together they form a team with an average total score of\n225 + 190 = 415. Under what conditions is the standard deviation\nof their total score equal to √152 + 202 = 25?", "options": {"A": "No conditions are required. The standard deviation is 25.", "B": "No matter what conditions are given, the standard deviation is\ndifferent from 25.", "C": "As long as they consistently bowl their averages, the standard\ndeviation is 25.", "D": "The standard deviation is 25 only if their scores are independent.", "E": "The standard deviation is 25 only if their scores are mutually\nexclusive."}, "image": null, "answer": "D", "explanation": "只有两人各自得分相互独立时，方差才能相加，SD(总分)=√(15²+20²)=25。"}, {"id": 272, "chapter": "第三章 概率", "topic": "随机变量", "stem": "The four sides of a tetrahedron are labeled 1 through 4. Let X be\nthe random variable whose values are the numbers that end up\nface down when the tetrahedron is tossed. Assume this is a fair\ntetrahedron, that is, all sides are equally likely to end up face\ndown. The mean of X is 2.5, and its variance is 5\n4 . Let Y be the\nrandom variable whose values are the differences between the\nface-down values of the first and second rolls of the tetrahedron\nif it is rolled twice. What is the standard deviation of Y?", "options": {"A": "√5\n4", "B": "√5\n4 + √5\n4", "C": "√5\n4 + 5\n4", "D": "√5\n4 −5\n4", "E": "5\n4 + 5\n4"}, "image": null, "answer": "C", "explanation": "Y=X₁−X₂（两次独立），var(Y)=5/4+5/4，SD(Y)=√(5/4+5/4)=√(5/2)。"}, {"id": 273, "chapter": "第三章 概率", "topic": "随机变量", "stem": "The variance of a set of data is 5. If every value in the set is\nmultiplied by 4 and then added to 100, what is the new variance?", "options": {"A": "5", "B": "20", "C": "80", "D": "120", "E": "180\nNORMAL PROBABILITIES"}, "image": "img/q273.png", "answer": "C", "explanation": "y=4x+100是线性变换：方差乘a²=16，新方差=5×16=80（加100不影响方差）。"}, {"id": 274, "chapter": "第三章 概率", "topic": "正态概率", "stem": "Diet Plan A advertises an average monthly weight loss of 10\npounds with a standard deviation of 3 pounds. Diet Plan B claims\nan average monthly weight loss of 12 pounds with a standard\ndeviation of 1.8 pounds. Assuming both assertions are correct\nand assuming roughly normal distributions, which diet plan is\nmore likely to result in a monthly weight loss of over 15 pounds?", "options": {"A": "Diet Plan A is more likely to result in a monthly weight loss of\nover 15 pounds because of its greater standard deviation.", "B": "Diet Plan B is more likely to result in a monthly weight loss of\nover 15 pounds because of its greater mean.", "C": "For both plans, the probability of a weight loss over 15 pounds is\n0.04779.", "D": "For both plans, the probability of a weight loss over 15 pounds\nis 0.95221.", "E": "The problem cannot be solved from the information given."}, "image": null, "answer": "C", "explanation": "两者的z都=(15−10)/3=(15−12)/1.8≈1.67，超过15磅的概率均为0.04779，可能性相同。"}, {"id": 275, "chapter": "第三章 概率", "topic": "正态概率", "stem": "Which of the following are true statements?\nI. The area under a normal curve is always equal to 1, no\nmatter the mean and standard deviation.\nII. The smaller the standard deviation of a normal curve, the\nhigher and narrower the graph is.\nIII. Normal curves with different means are centered around\ndifferent numbers.", "options": {"A": "I and II only", "B": "I and III only", "C": "II and III only", "D": "I, II, and III", "E": "None of the above gives the complete set of true responses."}, "image": null, "answer": "D", "explanation": "正态曲线面积恒为1；σ越小曲线越高越窄；均值决定中心位置，三条全部正确。"}, {"id": 276, "chapter": "第三章 概率", "topic": "正态概率", "stem": "A cell phone takes an average of 11 minutes to move through an\nassembly line. If the standard deviation is 2 minutes and if the\ndistribution is roughly normal, what is the probability that a cell\nphone will take over 12 minutes to move through the assembly\nline?", "options": {"A": "P(z > 12−11\n2\n)", "B": "P(z > 12−11\n)\n2\n√2", "C": "P(z > 11−12\n2\n)", "D": "P(z > 11−12\n)\n2\n√2", "E": "2P(t > 11−12\n2\n)"}, "image": null, "answer": "A", "explanation": "z=(12−11)/2=0.5，所求为P(z>(12−11)/2)。"}, {"id": 277, "chapter": "第三章 概率", "topic": "正态概率", "stem": "A bowler’s scores are approximately normally distributed with a\nmean of 210. What is the standard deviation if 30 percent of her\nscores are below 200?", "options": {"A": "200−210\n−0.5244", "B": "200−200\n−0.5244", "C": "200−210\n0.5244", "D": "200−210\n0.4756", "E": "210−200\n0.4756"}, "image": "img/q277.png", "answer": "A", "explanation": "30%低于200对应z=−0.5244：−0.5244=(200−210)/σ，故σ=(200−210)/(−0.5244)≈19.1。"}, {"id": 278, "chapter": "第三章 概率", "topic": "正态概率", "stem": "Which of the following is a true statement?", "options": {"A": "The area under the standard normal curve between 0 and 2 is\ntwice the area between 0 and 1.", "B": "The area under the standard normal curve between 0 and 2 is\nhalf the area between –2 and 2.", "C": "For the standard normal curve, the interquartile range is\napproximately 3.", "D": "For the standard normal curve, approximately 1 out of 1,000\nvalues are greater than 10.", "E": "The 68-95-99.7 rule applies only to normal curves where the\nmean and standard deviation are known."}, "image": null, "answer": "B", "explanation": "标准正态0到2的面积0.4772恰为−2到2面积0.9544的一半，B正确；A、C、D、E均不成立（IQR≈1.35，P(z>10)远小于0.001）。"}, {"id": 279, "chapter": "第三章 概率", "topic": "正态概率", "stem": "The starting national average salary for a computer security\nspecialist is $58,760. Assuming a roughly normal distribution\nand a standard deviation of $6,500, what is the probability that a\nrandomly chosen computer security specialist will start with a\nsalary between $50,000 and $60,000?", "options": {"A": "P( 50,000\n6,500 < z < 60,000\n6,500 )", "B": "2P(z > 60,000−50,000\n6,500\n)", "C": "P( 58,760−50,000\n6,500\n< z < 58,760−60,000", "D": "P( 58,760−50,000\n< z < 58,760−60,000\n6,500\n√n", "E": "P( 50,000−58,760\n6,500\n< z < 60,000−58,760"}, "image": null, "answer": "E", "explanation": "两端标准化：P((50000−58760)/6500<z<(60000−58760)/6500)=P(−1.35<z<0.19)。"}, {"id": 280, "chapter": "第三章 概率", "topic": "正态概率", "stem": "Populations P1 and P2 are roughly normally distributed and\nhave identical means. However, the standard deviation of P1 is\nhalf the standard deviation of P2. What can be said about the\npercentage of observations falling within one standard deviation\nof the mean for each population?", "options": {"A": "The percentage for P1 is twice the percentage for P2.", "B": "The percentage for P1 is greater, but not twice as great, as the\npercentage for P2.", "C": "The percentage for P2 is twice the percentage for P1.\n6,500\n)\n)\n6,500\n√n\n6,500\n)", "D": "The percentage for P2 is greater, but not twice as great, as the\npercentage for P1.", "E": "The percentages are identical."}, "image": null, "answer": "E", "explanation": "任何正态分布在均值±1个标准差内恒约为68%，与标准差大小无关。"}, {"id": 281, "chapter": "第三章 概率", "topic": "正态概率", "stem": "A college library determines that books are checked out for an\naverage of 8 days with a standard deviation of 2 days. Assuming\na roughly normal distribution, what is the shortest time interval\nfor which two-thirds of the books are out?", "options": {"A": "0 to 8.9 days", "B": "6.1 to 7.1 days", "C": "6.1 to 8 days", "D": "6.1 to 9.9 days", "E": "7.1 to 14 days"}, "image": null, "answer": "A", "explanation": "时间不能为负，含三分之二的最短区间从0起：invNorm(2/3)≈0.43，x=8+0.43×2≈8.9天，即0到8.9天。"}, {"id": 282, "chapter": "第三章 概率", "topic": "正态概率", "stem": "A set of employee commuting distances from work has a roughly\nnormal distribution with a mean of 12 miles and a standard\ndeviation of 3.5 miles. If a randomly selected employee\ncommutes over 10 miles, what is the probability she commutes\nunder 15 miles?", "options": {"A": "0.521", "B": "0.545", "C": "0.647", "D": "0.716", "E": "0.727"}, "image": null, "answer": "E", "explanation": "条件概率=[P(10<X<15)]/P(X>10)=(0.804−0.284)/(1−0.284)≈0.520/0.716≈0.727。"}, {"id": 283, "chapter": "第三章 概率", "topic": "正态概率", "stem": "Which of the following statements is incorrect?", "options": {"A": "In all normal distributions, the mean and median are equal.", "B": "Bell-shaped curves may not have normal distributions.", "C": "Virtually all the area under a normal curve is within three\nstandard deviations of the mean, no matter the particular mean\nand standard deviation.", "D": "A normal distribution is completely determined by two\nnumbers, its mean and its standard deviation.", "E": "Standardized scores (z-scores) always have a normal distribution\nno matter the original distribution."}, "image": null, "answer": "E", "explanation": "只有原分布本身近似正态时其z分数才近似正态，任意分布标准化后不一定正态，E错误。"}, {"id": 284, "chapter": "第三章 概率", "topic": "正态概率", "stem": "A trucking firm determines that the miles per gallon (mpg)\nachieved by trucks in its fleet are roughly normally distributed\nwith a standard deviation of 2.5 mpg. What is the mean mpg if\n75 percent of the trucks achieve better than 13.2 mpg?", "options": {"A": "13.2 + 0.3255(2.5)", "B": "13.2 + 0.6745(2.5)", "C": "13.2 + 0.7500(2.5)", "D": "13.2 – 0.6745(2.5)", "E": "13.2 – 0.7500(2.5)"}, "image": null, "answer": "B", "explanation": "75%高于13.2→13.2为25分位数，对应z=−0.6745，μ=13.2+0.6745×2.5≈14.9。"}, {"id": 285, "chapter": "第三章 概率", "topic": "正态概率", "stem": "The mean yearly medical expenses (including insurance\npayments) for individuals in a large city is $15,300 with a\nstandard deviation of $3,600. Assuming a roughly normal\ndistribution, what is the probability that two randomly chosen\nindividuals in the city both have yearly medical expenses over\n$20,000?", "options": {"A": "2P(z > 20,000−15,300\n3,600\n)\n2", "B": "[P(z > 20,000−15,300\n3,600\n)]\n2 P(z > 20,000−15,300\n3,600\n)", "C": "1", "D": "P(z > 20,000−15,300\n√3,600\n)", "E": "P(z > 20,000−15,300\n)\n3,600\n√2"}, "image": "img/q285.png", "answer": "B", "explanation": "两人独立：[P(z>(20000−15300)/3600)]²=[P(z>1.31)]²≈(0.095)²≈0.009。"}, {"id": 286, "chapter": "第三章 概率", "topic": "正态概率", "stem": "The monthly rental paid per person for college students living\noff campus has a roughly normal distribution with a mean of\n$275 and a standard deviation of $40. Ninety percent of the\nrentals are greater than what amount?", "options": {"A": "$275 – 1.282($40)", "B": "$275 – 1.645($40)", "C": "$275 – 1.96($40)", "D": "$275 + 1.282($40)", "E": "$275 + 1.96($40)"}, "image": null, "answer": "A", "explanation": "90%租金高于该值→该值为10%分位：275−1.282×40≈$223.7。"}, {"id": 287, "chapter": "第三章 概率", "topic": "正态概率", "stem": "Suppose women’s foot lengths are roughly normally distributed\nwith a mean of 20 cm and a standard deviation of 4 cm, while\nmen’s foot lengths are roughly normally distributed with a mean\nof 26 cm and a standard deviation of 5 cm. If a policeman\nmeasures a footprint to be 22.5 cm, is there a greater probability\nthat it belongs to a man or to a woman?", "options": {"A": "Man, because 22.5 – 20 < 26 – 22.5", "B": "Man, because 2.5\n4 < 3.5\n5", "C": "Woman, because 22.5 – 20 < 26 – 22.5", "D": "Woman, because 2.5\n4 < 3.5\n5", "E": "This cannot be answered without knowing if we can assume\nindependence."}, "image": null, "answer": "D", "explanation": "女：z=(22.5−20)/4=0.625；男：z=(22.5−26)/5=−0.7。0.625<0.7，该长度更接近女性均值，更可能是女性。"}, {"id": 288, "chapter": "第三章 概率", "topic": "正态概率", "stem": "A couple is looking to purchase their first house. In the\nneighborhood in which they are interested, home prices are\nroughly normally distributed with a mean of $275,000 and a\nstandard deviation of $35,000. They ask the realtor to show them\nonly homes under $300,000. What percentage of the homes\nshown to them will be over $250,000?", "options": {"A": "0.312", "B": "0.475", "C": "0.525", "D": "0.688", "E": "0.763"}, "image": null, "answer": "D", "explanation": "条件概率=P(250<X<300)/P(X<300)=(0.762−0.238)/0.762≈0.688。"}, {"id": 289, "chapter": "第三章 概率", "topic": "正态概率", "stem": "Among 2,500 subjects signing up for a program to stop smoking,\nthe distribution of their daily number of cigarettes was roughly\nnormal with a mean of 27.1 and a standard deviation of 4.6.\nWhich expression below represents the 60th percentile of the\ndistribution?", "options": {"A": "27.1 – (0.25)(4.6)", "B": "27.1 – (0.40)(4.6)", "C": "27.1 – (0.60)(4.6)", "D": "27.1 + (0.25)(4.6)", "E": "27.1 + (0.40)(4.6)"}, "image": "img/q289.png", "answer": "D", "explanation": "60分位对应z≈0.25：27.1+0.25×4.6≈28.3。"}, {"id": 290, "chapter": "第三章 概率", "topic": "正态概率", "stem": "The women’s 500 m skating times are roughly normally\ndistributed with a mean of 40.44 seconds. The z-score of China’s\nPeiyu Jin’s time of 38.69 seconds is –1.944. What percent of all\nwomen’s 500 m skating times are above 42 seconds?", "options": {"A": "1.56", "B": "1.75", "C": "4.15", "D": "98.25", "E": "95.85"}, "image": null, "answer": "C", "explanation": "σ=(40.44−38.69)/1.944≈0.90；z=(42−40.44)/0.90≈1.73，上尾面积≈4.15%。"}, {"id": 291, "chapter": "第三章 概率", "topic": "正态概率", "stem": "The number of miles that a highway construction team can lay\non good-weather days is roughly normally distributed with a\nmean of 3 and a standard deviation of 0.35. The number of miles\nthat a highway construction team can lay on bad-weather days is\nroughly normally distributed with a mean of 1.9 and a standard\ndeviation of 0.4. If the probability of good weather is 0.7 (and of\nbad weather is 0.3), what is the probability of laying at least 2.5\nmiles on a random day?", "options": {"A": "0.06681", "B": "0.49511", "C": "0.64638", "D": "0.6664", "E": "0.92344"}, "image": null, "answer": "D", "explanation": "全概率：0.7×P(z≥−0.5/0.35)+0.3×P(z≥0.6/0.4)=0.7(0.9234)+0.3(0.0668)≈0.6664。"}, {"id": 292, "chapter": "第三章 概率", "topic": "正态概率", "stem": "A distribution of scores is approximately normal with a mean of\n28 and a standard deviation of 3.4. Which of the following\nequations should be used to find a score x with 20 percent of the\nscores above it?", "options": {"A": "x−28\n3.4\n= 0.80", "B": "x−28\n√3.4 = 0.80", "C": "x−28\n3.4\n= 0.84", "D": "x−28\n√3.4 = 0.84", "E": "x−28\n3.42 = 0.84"}, "image": null, "answer": "C", "explanation": "上方20%对应z=0.84：(x−28)/3.4=0.84，x≈30.9。"}, {"id": 293, "chapter": "第三章 概率", "topic": "正态概率", "stem": "A large data set is approximately normally distributed. What is\nthe proper order, from smallest to largest, of l, m, and n, where\nl = the value with a z-score of –0.6\nm = the value of the first quartile\nn = the value of the 30th percentile", "options": {"A": "l < m < n\n0.50\n)\n∣∣∣∣", "B": "m < l < n", "C": "m < n < l", "D": "l < n < m", "E": "n < l < m"}, "image": null, "answer": "B", "explanation": "z=−0.6对应约27.4%分位，故按数值排序：Q1(25%)<l(27.4%)<n(30%)，即m<l<n。"}, {"id": 294, "chapter": "第三章 概率", "topic": "正态概率", "stem": "A city’s average winter low temperature is 28°F with a standard\ndeviation of 10°F, while the city’s average summer low\ntemperature is 66°F with a standard deviation of 6°F. In which\nseason is it more unusual to have a day with a low temperature\nof 51°F?", "options": {"A": "Winter, because 51 – 28 = 23 is greater than 66 – 51 = 15", "B": "Winter, because 10 is greater than 6", "C": "Winter, because 51 sounds like a more unusual temperature for\nwinter than summer", "D": "Summer, because 51−66\n6\nis more than 51−28\n10", "E": "Summer, because 28+66\n2\n= 47 < 51\nSAMPLING DISTRIBUTIONS"}, "image": null, "answer": "D", "explanation": "冬季z=(51−28)/10=2.3，夏季z=(51−66)/6=−2.5；夏季|z|更大，更异常。"}, {"id": 295, "chapter": "第三章 概率", "topic": "抽样分布", "stem": "The owner of a bagel shop, who is the father of an AP Statistics\nstudent, advertises that the price of a dozen bagels on any given\nday will be randomly picked using a normal distribution with a\nmean of $10.00 and a standard deviation of $0.50. If a customer\nbuys a dozen bagels on each of five days, what is the probability\nthat he will pay a total exceeding $52?", "options": {"A": "P(z > 10.40−10.00\n0.50\n)", "B": "P(z > 52.00−50.00\n0.50\n)", "C": "2P(z > 52.00−50.00", "D": "P(z > 10.40−10.00\n√5 )\n)\n( 0.50", "E": "P(z > 52.00−50.00\n√5 )\n)\n( 0.50"}, "image": null, "answer": "E", "explanation": "5天总价均值=5×10=50，SD=0.5×√5，故P(z>(52−50)/(0.5√5))。"}, {"id": 296, "chapter": "第三章 概率", "topic": "抽样分布", "stem": "6.7 percent of women graduate college with STEM degrees. In a\nsimple random sample (SRS) of 1,000 women, what is the\nprobability that more than 8 percent will graduate college with a\nSTEM degree?", "options": {"A": "0.033", "B": "0.05", "C": "0.067", "D": "0.10", "E": "0.933"}, "image": null, "answer": "B", "explanation": "p帽的SD=√(0.067×0.933/1000)≈0.0079，z=(0.08−0.067)/0.0079≈1.64，概率≈0.05。"}, {"id": 297, "chapter": "第三章 概率", "topic": "抽样分布", "stem": "Which of the following statements is incorrect?", "options": {"A": "The larger the sample is, the larger the spread is in the sampling\ndistribution.", "B": "Provided that the population size is significantly greater than the\nsample size, the spread of a sampling distribution does not\ndepend on the population size.", "C": "Bias has to do with the center, not the spread, of a sampling\ndistribution.", "D": "Sample distribution and sampling distribution refer to different\nthings.", "E": "The larger the sample is, the closer the sample distribution\ngenerally becomes to the population distribution."}, "image": null, "answer": "A", "explanation": "样本越大，抽样分布的离散度越小（SD=σ/√n），A说样本越大spread越大，错误。"}, {"id": 298, "chapter": "第三章 概率", "topic": "抽样分布", "stem": "Suppose 15 percent of the mines in a particular Alaska region\nwill strike gold. In a random sample of 200 mines in this region,\nwhat is the probability that more than 18 percent will strike\ngold?", "options": {"A": "P(z >\n0.18−0.15\n√200(0.15)(0.85) )", "B": "P(z >\n0.18−0.15\n√200(0.5)(0.5) )", "C": "P(z >\n0.18−0.15\n(0.15)(0.85)/√200 )", "D": "P(z >\n0.18−0.15\n200\n)\n√(0.15)(0.85)", "E": "P(z > 0.18−0.15\n200\n)\n√(0.5)(0.5)"}, "image": null, "answer": "D", "explanation": "比例的抽样分布SD=√((0.15)(0.85)/200)≈0.0252，对应D的表达式。"}, {"id": 299, "chapter": "第三章 概率", "topic": "抽样分布", "stem": "Which of the following statements is incorrect?", "options": {"A": "The sampling distribution of ¯x has a mean equal to the\npopulation mean μ even if the sample size n is small.", "B": "The sampling distribution of ¯x has a standard deviation of σ\n√n\neven if the population is not normally distributed and even if n\nis small.", "C": "The sampling distribution of ¯x is normal, no matter what n is, if\nthe population has a normal distribution.", "D": "When n is large, the sampling distribution of ¯x is approximately\nnormal even if the population is not normally distributed.", "E": "Even if the observations are not independent, the central limit\ntheorem applies as long as n is large enough."}, "image": null, "answer": "E", "explanation": "CLT要求观察值相互独立（或为总体的10%以下抽样），不独立时不适用，E错误；A、B、C、D均正确。"}, {"id": 300, "chapter": "第三章 概率", "topic": "抽样分布", "stem": "It is known that 73 percent of the employees at one factory are\nwomen, while 61 percent of the employees of a second factory are\nwomen. In a simple random sample (SRS) of 115 employees from\nthe first factory and an independent SRS of 80 employees from\nthe second, what is the probability that the difference between\nthe percentages of women picked (first factory minus second) is\nmore than 10 percent?", "options": {"A": "P(z >\n0.10−0.12\n√(0.5)(0.5)(\n1\n115 + 1\n80 ) )", "B": "P(z >\n0.10−0.12\n√(0.67)(0.33)(\n1\n115 + 1\n80 ) )", "C": "P(z >\n0.10−0.12\n80\n)\n√(0.73)(0.27)\n115\n+ (0.61)(0.39)", "D": "P(z >\n0.10−0.12\n(0.73)(0.27)\n)\n√115\n+ (0.61)(0.39)\n√80", "E": "P(z >\n0.10−0.12\n115+80 )(1−84+49\n115+80 )(\n1\n115 + 1\n80 ) )\n√( 84+49"}, "image": null, "answer": "C", "explanation": "两比例差的均值=0.73−0.61=0.12，SD=√((0.73)(0.27)/115+(0.61)(0.39)/80)，对应C。"}, {"id": 301, "chapter": "第三章 概率", "topic": "抽样分布", "stem": "A population is normally distributed with a mean of 37.\nConsider all samples of size 8. The variable ¯x−37", "options": {"A": "has a normal distribution.", "B": "has a t-distribution with df = 8", "C": "has a t-distribution with df = 7", "D": "has neither a normal distribution nor a t-distribution", "E": "has either a normal distribution or a t-distribution depending on\nthe characteristics of the population standard deviation\ns\n√8"}, "image": null, "answer": "A", "explanation": "总体正态时,样本均值 x̄ 本身服从正态分布(除以常数 σ/√8 后仍是正态),与 t 分布无关(t 分布用于以 s 代替 σ 的标准化统计量)。"}, {"id": 302, "chapter": "第三章 概率", "topic": "抽样分布", "stem": "Which of the following statements is incorrect?", "options": {"A": "Sample statistics are used to make inferences about population\nparameters.", "B": "Statistics from smaller samples have more variability than those\nfrom larger samples.", "C": "Parameters are fixed, while statistics vary depending on which\nsample is chosen.", "D": "As the sample size n becomes larger, the sample distribution\nbecomes closer to a normal distribution.", "E": "All of the above are true statements."}, "image": null, "answer": "D", "explanation": "随着 n 增大变得接近正态的是样本统计量的抽样分布,而不是样本数据的分布;其余各项均为真。"}, {"id": 303, "chapter": "第三章 概率", "topic": "抽样分布", "stem": "Which of the following is a true statement?", "options": {"A": "The sampling distribution of ˆp has a mean that can vary from\nthe population proportion p by approximately 1.96 standard\ndeviations.", "B": "The sampling distribution of ˆp has a standard deviation equal to\n√np(1 −p).", "C": "The sampling distribution of ˆp is considered close to normal\nprovided that n ≥ 30.", "D": "The sample proportion is a random variable with a probability\ndistribution.", "E": "All of the above are true statements."}, "image": null, "answer": "D", "explanation": "p̂ 是随样本变化的随机变量,具有概率分布(抽样分布);A、B、C 的均值/标准差/正态条件说法均不对。"}, {"id": 304, "chapter": "第三章 概率", "topic": "抽样分布", "stem": "Which of the following is a true statement?", "options": {"A": "The sampling distribution of the difference ¯x1 −¯x2 has a mean\nequal to the difference of the population means.", "B": "The sampling distribution of the difference ¯x1 −¯x2 has a\nstandard deviation equal to the sum of the population standard\ndeviations.", "C": "The sampling distribution of the difference ¯x1 −¯x2 has a\nstandard deviation equal to the difference of the population\nstandard deviations.", "D": "The sample sizes must be equal in two-sample inference.", "E": "As long as the sample sizes are large enough (for example, each\nat least 30), the samples in two-sample inference do not have to\nbe independent."}, "image": null, "answer": "A", "explanation": "x̄1−x̄2 的抽样分布均值等于总体均值之差;标准差公式是平方和再开方而非相加或相减,两样本须独立且样本数不必相等。"}, {"id": 305, "chapter": "第三章 概率", "topic": "抽样分布", "stem": "The calories in butterscotch hard candies are normally\ndistributed with a mean of 23.1 and a standard deviation of 0.6.\nIn a random sample of 10 butterscotch candies, what is the\nprobability that the total calories are between 228 and 232?", "options": {"A": "P( 228−231\n0.6\n< z < 232−231\n0.6\n)", "B": "P( 228−231\n6\n< z < 232−231\n6\n)", "C": "P( 22.8−23.1\n0.6\n< z < 23.2−23.1\n0.6\n)", "D": "P( 22.8−23.1\n< z < 23.2−23.1\n0.6\n√10\n0.6\n√10", "E": "P( 228−231\n< z < 232−231\n0.6\n√10\n0.6\n√10"}, "image": null, "answer": "E", "explanation": "10 颗糖总热量均值 10×23.1=231,标准差 0.6√10,故 z=(228−231)/(0.6√10) 到 (232−231)/(0.6√10),对应 E。"}, {"id": 306, "chapter": "第三章 概率", "topic": "抽样分布", "stem": "Which of the following are unbiased estimators for the\ncorresponding population parameters?\nI. Sample means\nII. Sample proportions\nIII. Difference of sample means\nIV. Difference of sample proportions", "options": {"A": "None are unbiased.", "B": "I and II only", "C": "I and III only", "D": "III and IV only\n)\n)", "E": "All are unbiased."}, "image": null, "answer": "E", "explanation": "样本均值、样本比例以及两个均值之差、两个比例之差都是相应总体参数的无偏估计量。"}, {"id": 307, "chapter": "第三章 概率", "topic": "抽样分布", "stem": "Which one of these histograms represents the sampling\ndistribution of ˆp for p = 0.6 and n = 150?", "options": {"A": "", "B": "", "C": "", "D": "", "E": ""}, "image": "img/q307.png", "answer": "D", "explanation": "p̂ 的均值 0.6,标准差 √(0.6×0.4/150)≈0.04;选中心在 0.60、范围约 0.52–0.68(±2 个标准差)的正态直方图,即 D。"}, {"id": 308, "chapter": "第三章 概率", "topic": "抽样分布", "stem": "Which of the following statements is true?", "options": {"A": "The mean of the set of sample means varies inversely as the\nsquare root of the size of the samples.", "B": "The variance of the set of sample means varies directly as the\nsize of the samples and inversely as the variance of the original\npopulation.", "C": "The standard deviation of the set of sample means varies\ndirectly as the standard deviation of the original population and\ninversely as the square root of the size of the samples.", "D": "The larger the sample size, the larger the variance of the set of\nsample means.", "E": "One must double the sample size in order to cut the standard\ndeviation of ¯x in half."}, "image": null, "answer": "C", "explanation": "x̄ 的标准差 σ/√n 与总体标准差成正比、与样本量的平方根成反比;要把标准差减半需样本量增至 4 倍,故 E 错。"}, {"id": 309, "chapter": "第三章 概率", "topic": "抽样分布", "stem": "Surveys have shown that 30 percent of people refuse to use\npublic toilets. A random sample of 200 people is selected, and the\nproportion of those people who refuse to use public toilets is\nnoted. This is repeated 100 times, and a dotplot is constructed of\nthe 100 sample proportions. Which of the following best\ndescribes the standard deviation of the data in the dotplot?", "options": {"A": "√100(0.3)(0.7)", "B": "√200(0.3)(0.7)", "C": "√300(0.3)(0.7)", "D": "√(0.3)(0.7)\n100", "E": "√(0.3)(0.7)\n200"}, "image": null, "answer": "E", "explanation": "单次抽 200 人的样本比例标准差 √(0.3×0.7/200);重复 100 次只是产生 100 个 p̂,点图数据的离散度仍由该公式描述。"}, {"id": 310, "chapter": "第三章 概率", "topic": "抽样分布", "stem": "Past surveys have shown that 9 percent of high school students\nand 12 percent of college students skip breakfast. A polling\nagency picks independent random samples of 100 high school\nstudents and of 100 college students. The agency surveys each\ngroup as to the proportion of students who skip breakfast. The\ndifference in proportions is noted. This is repeated 400 times,\nand a dotplot is constructed of the 400 differences of\nproportions. Which of the following best describes the standard\ndeviation of the data in the dotplot?", "options": {"A": "√100(0.09)(0.91) + 100(0.12)(0.88)", "B": "√400(0.09)(0.91) + 400(0.12)(0.88)", "C": "√(0.09)(0.91)\n100\n+ (0.12)(0.88)\n100", "D": "√(0.09)(0.91)\n400\n+ (0.12)(0.88)\n400", "E": "√(0.105)(0.895)(\n1\n100 +\n1\n100 )"}, "image": null, "answer": "C", "explanation": "两个独立样本比例之差的标准差为 √(0.09×0.91/100 + 0.12×0.88/100);重复次数 400 与离散度无关。"}, {"id": 311, "chapter": "第三章 概率", "topic": "抽样分布", "stem": "The distribution of weights of “18-ounce” boxes of corn flakes is\ngiven by the following histogram.\nThe distribution has a mean of 18.96 ounces with a standard\ndeviation of 2.31 ounces. If 50 random samples of 12 boxes each\nare picked and if the mean weight of each sample is found, which\nof the following is most likely to represent the distribution of the\nsample means?", "options": {"A": "", "B": "", "C": "", "D": "", "E": "None of the above gives a reasonable histogram for the sampling\ndistribution."}, "image": "img/q311_fig.png", "answer": "D", "explanation": "中心极限定理：n=12 的样本均值抽样分布近似正态，均值仍为 18.96，标准差 2.31/√12≈0.67，故选关于 19 对称且离散最小的直方图 D。"}, {"id": 312, "chapter": "第三章 概率", "topic": "抽样分布", "stem": "Which of the sample sizes n and population proportions p below\nwould result in the greatest standard deviation for the sampling\ndistribution of ˆp?", "options": {"A": "n = 50 and p = 0.1", "B": "n = 50 and p = 0.4", "C": "n = 250 and p = 0.1", "D": "n = 250 and p = 0.6", "E": "n = 250 and p = 0.95"}, "image": "img/q312.png", "answer": "B", "explanation": "σp̂=√(p(1−p)/n),n 越小越大,p 越接近 0.5 越大;n=50、p=0.4 时 √(0.24/50)=0.069 最大,选 B。"}, {"id": 313, "chapter": "第三章 概率", "topic": "抽样分布", "stem": "The sampling distribution of the sample mean is close to the\nnormal distribution", "options": {"A": "only if the parent population is unimodal, is not badly skewed,\nand does not have outliers", "B": "no matter the distribution of the parent population or what the\nvalue of n", "C": "if n is large, no matter the distribution of the parent population", "D": "if the standard deviation of the parent population is known", "E": "only if both n is large and the parent population has a normal\ndistribution"}, "image": null, "answer": "C", "explanation": "中心极限定理:只要 n 足够大,不论总体分布如何,x̄ 的抽样分布都近似正态。"}, {"id": 314, "chapter": "第三章 概率", "topic": "抽样分布", "stem": "Suppose that 48 percent of high school students and 44 percent\nof college students would answer yes to the question “Do you\nenjoy Drake’s music?” In a simulation, a random sample of 100\nhigh school students and 100 college students is selected. The\ndifference in sample proportions of those who answered yes, ˆp\nhigh school – ˆp college, is calculated. The procedure is repeated\n10,000 times. Which of the histograms below is most likely to\nresult as the simulated sampling distribution of the difference in\nproportions?", "options": {"A": "", "B": "", "C": "", "D": "", "E": ""}, "image": "img/q314.png", "answer": "D", "explanation": "差 p̂hs−p̂col 的均值 0.48−0.44=0.04,标准差 √(0.48×0.52/100+0.44×0.56/100)≈0.07;选中心在 0.04、近似正态的直方图 D。"}, {"id": 315, "chapter": "第三章 概率", "topic": "抽样分布", "stem": "The body temperatures of healthy elderly adults follow a skewed\nleft distribution with a mean of 37°C and a standard deviation of\n0.4°C. A random sample of 100 healthy adults is selected, and\ntheir mean body temperature is recorded. This is repeated 500\ntimes, and a dotplot is constructed of the 500 sample means.\nWhich of the following best describes the dotplot?", "options": {"A": "Skewed left with a mean of 37°C and a standard deviation of\n0.0179°C", "B": "Skewed left with a mean of 37°C and a standard deviation of\n0.04°C", "C": "Skewed left with a mean of 37°C and a standard deviation of\n0.4°C", "D": "Approximately normal with a mean of 37°C and a standard\ndeviation of 0.0179°C", "E": "Approximately normal with a mean of 37°C and a standard\ndeviation of 0.04°C"}, "image": null, "answer": "E", "explanation": "n=100 时由 CLT,500 个样本均值近似正态,均值 37,标准差 0.4/√100=0.04。"}, {"id": 316, "chapter": "第三章 概率", "topic": "抽样分布", "stem": "Which of the following is a biased estimator?", "options": {"A": "Sampling distribution of proportions", "B": "Sampling distribution of means", "C": "Sampling distribution of slopes", "D": "Sampling distribution of maxima", "E": "All of the above are unbiased."}, "image": null, "answer": "D", "explanation": "样本最大值是低估(有偏)的估计量;比例、均值、回归斜率的样本估计都是无偏的。"}, {"id": 317, "chapter": "第三章 概率", "topic": "抽样分布", "stem": "A simulation is conducted by using 25 fair dice whose faces are\nnumbered 1 through 6, tossing them all at once, and averaging\nthe 25 numbers showing face up. This is repeated 400 times.\nWhich of these best describes the distribution being simulated?", "options": {"A": "A sampling distribution of a sample proportion with μˆp = 1\nand σˆp = √(1/6)(5/6)\n25", "B": "A sampling distribution of a sample proportion with μˆp = 1\nand σˆp = √(1/6)(5/6)\n400", "C": "A sampling distribution of a sample mean with\nμ¯x = 3.5 and σ¯x = 1.708", "D": "A sampling distribution of a sample mean with\nμ¯x = 3.5 and σ¯x = 1.708\n√25", "E": "A sampling distribution of a sample mean with\nμ¯x = 3.5 and σ¯x = 1.708\n√400"}, "image": null, "answer": "D", "explanation": "模拟的是 25 个骰子点数的样本均值:μ=3.5,σx̄=1.708/√25(骰子点数标准差 1.708,1.708/√25≈0.342)。"}, {"id": 318, "chapter": "第三章 概率", "topic": "抽样分布", "stem": "Cycle times for express washing for Brand A dishwashers have a\nmean of 42.5 minutes with a standard deviation of 0.6 minutes,\nwhile times for the same cycle in Brand B dishwashers have a\nmean of 41.2 minutes with a standard deviation of 0.5 minutes.\nBrand A and Brand B dishwashers are each sent through an\n6\n6\nexpress washing 100 times. What is the probability that the\nmean difference (A minus B) is greater than 1 minute?", "options": {"A": "P(z >\n1−1.3\n√0.62+0.52 )", "B": "P(z >\n1−1.3\n100\n2+ 0.5\n100\n2 )\n√0.6", "C": "P(z >\n1−1.3\n0.6\n√100 +\n0.5\n√100\n)", "D": "P(z >\n1−1.3\n100\n) )\n√0.62+0.52\n(", "E": "P(z >\n1−1.13\n100+100 ) )\n√0.62+0.52\n("}, "image": null, "answer": "B", "explanation": "均值差 42.5−41.2=1.3,标准误 √(0.6²/100+0.5²/100);P(x̄A−x̄B>1)=P(z>(1−1.3)/√(0.6²/100+0.5²/100)),对应 B。"}, {"id": 319, "chapter": "第三章 概率", "topic": "抽样分布", "stem": "The mean waist size for American men is 39.7 inches with a\nstandard deviation of 3.8 inches. In a random sample of 200 men,\nwhat is the probability that the mean waist size is less than 35\ninches?", "options": {"A": "P(z < 35)", "B": "P(z < 35−39.7\n3.8\n)", "C": "P(z < 35−39.7\n(\n3.8\n√200 ) )", "D": "P(z < 35−39.7\n200 ) )\n( √38", "E": "P(z < 35−39.7\n200 ) )\n( √3.8"}, "image": null, "answer": "C", "explanation": "样本均值标准误 3.8/√200,故 P(x̄<35)=P(z<(35−39.7)/(3.8/√200)),选 C。"}, {"id": 320, "chapter": "第三章 概率", "topic": "抽样分布", "stem": "Based on past studies, a credit card company believes that 1.5\npercent of adults receiving a new credit card offer in the mail\nwill accept it. If the company is correct, what is the probability\nthat less than 1 percent of adults in a random sample of 400\nadults receiving the offer will accept it?", "options": {"A": "P(z <\n0.01−0.015\n√400(0.01)(0.99) )", "B": "P(z <\n0.01−0.015\n√400(0.015)(0.985) )", "C": "P(z <\n0.01−0.015\n400\n)\n√(0.01)(0.99)", "D": "P(z <\n0.01−0.015\n400\n)\n√(0.015)(0.985)", "E": "P(z < 0.01−0.015\n400\n)\n√(0.5)(0.5)"}, "image": null, "answer": "D", "explanation": "σp̂=√(0.015×0.985/400),z=(0.01−0.015)/√(0.015×0.985/400),对应 D。"}, {"id": 321, "chapter": "第三章 概率", "topic": "抽样分布", "stem": "Suppose that the average age of MLB players is 26.8 with a\nstandard deviation of 3.8, while the average age of NFL players\nis 25.6 with a standard deviation of 2.9. In random samples of 35\nMLB players and 30 NFL players, what is the probability that\nthe mean age of the MLB players is greater than the mean age of\nthe NFL players?", "options": {"A": "0.8158", "B": "0.8324", "C": "0.9253", "D": "0.9584", "E": "0.9860"}, "image": null, "answer": "C", "explanation": "均值差 1.2,标准误 √(3.8²/35+2.9²/30)≈0.832,z=1.442,P≈0.9253。"}, {"id": 322, "chapter": "第三章 概率", "topic": "抽样分布", "stem": "Child development scientists gathered data from 65 randomly\nselected babies and calculated the mean age in weeks at which\nthe babies began to crawl. Is the sample mean ¯x an unbiased\nestimator for µ, the mean age at which all babies begin to crawl?", "options": {"A": "No, because some sample means do not equal the population\nmean", "B": "No, because there is no reason to assume that the ages at which\nall babies begin to crawl is normally distributed", "C": "No, not unless the sample size n is very large", "D": "No, not unless the population standard deviation is known", "E": "Yes, because for random samples, the mean of the sample means\nis the population mean"}, "image": null, "answer": "E", "explanation": "随机抽样下所有可能样本均值的平均等于总体均值 μ,故 x̄ 是 μ 的无偏估计,与正态性、n 或是否已知 σ 无关。"}, {"id": 323, "chapter": "第三章 概率", "topic": "抽样分布", "stem": "Which of the following statements is incorrect?", "options": {"A": "Like the normal, t-distributions are always symmetric.", "B": "Like the normal, t-distributions are always bell-shaped.", "C": "Like the normal, t-distributions are always unimodal.", "D": "The t-distributions have less spread than the normal, that is, they\nhave less probability in the tails and more in the center than the\nnormal.", "E": "For larger values of df, degrees of freedom, the t-distributions\nlook more like the normal distribution."}, "image": null, "answer": "D", "explanation": "t 分布尾部更厚(更分散),而不是更集中;其余关于对称、钟形、单峰及 df 越大越接近正态的说法都对。"}, {"id": 324, "chapter": "第三章 概率", "topic": "抽样分布", "stem": "Which of the following statements about the chi-square\ndistribution is incorrect?", "options": {"A": "There is a separate χ2 curve for each df value", "B": "The area under every χ2 curve is the same.", "C": "For small df, the distribution is skewed to the right; however, for\nlarge df, it becomes more symmetric and bell-shaped.", "D": "For 1 or 2 degrees of freedom. the histogram peak occurs at 0.\nFor 3 or more degrees of freedom, the peak is at df – 2.", "E": "Just like for the t-distribution, the degrees of freedom for χ2\ndistributions depend upon the sample size."}, "image": null, "answer": "E", "explanation": "卡方分布的自由度取决于类别数或表格行列数,并非像 t 分布那样由样本量决定;其余各项说法正确。"}, {"id": 325, "chapter": "第三章 概率", "topic": "抽样分布", "stem": "Which of the following is a true statement about t-distributions?", "options": {"A": "The greater the number of degrees of freedom, the narrower the\ntails.", "B": "The smaller the number of degrees of freedom, the closer the\ncurve is to the normal curve.", "C": "Thirty degrees of freedom gives the normal curve.", "D": "The shape of a t-distribution depends on the degrees of freedom,\nwhich depend on the population size.", "E": "The probability that z > 1.96 in a normal distribution is greater\nthan the probability that t > 1.96 in a t-distribution with df = 30.\nMC Statistical Inference\nAnswers for Chapter 4 are on pages 277–300.\nCONFIDENCE INTERVALS FOR PROPORTIONS"}, "image": null, "answer": "A", "explanation": "df 越大 t 曲线尾部越窄、越接近正态;t 分布自由度取决于样本量而非总体大小;t 尾部比正态厚,故 P(t>1.96)>P(z>1.96)。"}, {"id": 326, "chapter": "第四章 统计推断", "topic": "比例的置信区间", "stem": "In a random sample of 1,500 adults with library cards, 525 said that their favorite books\nto read in bed are mysteries. What is a 95% confidence interval estimate of the\nproportion of all adults with library cards whose favorite books to read in bed are\nmysteries?", "options": {"A": "0.35 ± 1.96 (0.5)(0.5)\n√1,500", "B": "0.35 ± 1.96√(0.5)(0.5)\n1,500", "C": "0.35 ± 1.96 (0.35)(0.65)\n√1,500", "D": "0.35 ± 1.96√(0.35)(0.65)\n1,500", "E": "0.35 ± 1.96√1, 500(0.35)(0.65)"}, "image": null, "answer": "D", "explanation": "p̂=525/1500=0.35,95% CI 为 0.35±1.96√(0.35×0.65/1500),对应 D。"}, {"id": 327, "chapter": "第四章 统计推断", "topic": "比例的置信区间", "stem": "A 2018 survey of 1,470 adult Americans found that 29 percent of the American adult\npopulation would be willing to pay higher taxes if the government offered “Medicare for\nall.” Which of the following best describes what is meant by the poll having a margin of\nerror of ±3 percent?", "options": {"A": "It means that 3 percent of those surveyed refused to participate in the poll.", "B": "It would not be unexpected for 3 percent of the population to agree readily to the higher\ntaxes.", "C": "Between 382 and 470 of the 1,470 adults surveyed responded that they would be willing to\npay higher taxes in return for “Medicare for all.”", "D": "If a similar survey of 1,470 American adults was taken weekly, a 3 percent change in each\nweek’s results would not be unexpected.", "E": "It is likely that between 26 percent and 32 percent of American adults would be willing to\npay higher taxes in return for “Medicare for all.”"}, "image": null, "answer": "E", "explanation": "边际误差 ±3% 意为 95% 置信区间 29%±3%,即有 95% 把握认为总体比例在 26% 到 32% 之间。"}, {"id": 328, "chapter": "第四章 统计推断", "topic": "比例的置信区间", "stem": "The probability that a “walk-on” makes an intercollegiate team at a particular D-1\nuniversity is 17%. If one takes a simple random sample (SRS) of walk-ons and constructs\nCHAPTER\n4\na confidence interval estimate of the acceptance rate, which of the following statements is\ntrue?", "options": {"A": "The center of the interval would be 17%.", "B": "The interval would contain 17%.", "C": "A 99% confidence interval estimate would contain 17%.", "D": "All of the above are true statements.", "E": "None of the above are true statements."}, "image": null, "answer": "E", "explanation": "区间中心是样本比例而非 17%;任何一次区间都可能不包含 17%,即使 99% 置信也不保证,故三项都不必然成立。"}, {"id": 329, "chapter": "第四章 统计推断", "topic": "比例的置信区间", "stem": "A telephone survey of 750 registered voters showed that 330 favored legalizing medical\nmarijuana. At what confidence level can we say that between 40% and 48% of the\nelectorate favor this legalization?", "options": {"A": "P(0.40 < z 0.48)", "B": "P(\n0.40−0.44\n√750(0.44)(0.56) < z <\n0.48−0.44\n√750(0.44)(0.56) )", "C": "P(\n0.40−0.44\n√(0.5)(0.5)/750 < z <\n0.48−0.44\n√(0.5)(0.5)/750 )", "D": "P(\n0.40−0.44\n√(0.40)(0.60)/750 < z <\n0.48−0.44\n√(0.48)(0.52)/750 )", "E": "P(\n0.40−0.44\n√(0.44)(0.56)/750 < z <\n0.48−0.44\n√(0.44)(0.56)/750 )"}, "image": null, "answer": "E", "explanation": "p̂=330/750=0.44,标准差 √(0.44×0.56/750),置信度为 P((0.40−0.44)/SE<z<(0.48−0.44)/SE),对应 E。"}, {"id": 330, "chapter": "第四章 统计推断", "topic": "比例的置信区间", "stem": "In a random survey of 530 students, 28 percent said that they had experienced bullying at\nsome time. With what degree of confidence can the pollster say that 28% ± 3% of\nstudents have experienced bullying?", "options": {"A": "12.4%", "B": "28%", "C": "87.6%", "D": "93.8%", "E": "95%"}, "image": null, "answer": "C", "explanation": "SE=√(0.28×0.72/530)=0.0195,z=0.03/0.0195=1.54,置信度≈2Φ(1.54)−1≈0.876,即 87.6%。"}, {"id": 331, "chapter": "第四章 统计推断", "topic": "比例的置信区间", "stem": "An online news magazine periodically gives viewers an opportunity to record\nelectronically their agreement or disagreement with some viewpoint or commentary. On\none such occasion, 250 out of 800 respondents agreed with a statement that the most\npractical way of meeting a potential spouse is through online dating sites. The immediate\nonline calculation concluded that 31.25% of the viewers, with a margin of error of ±3.2%,\nagreed with the statement. The fine print below stated that the calculation was made with\n95% confidence. What is the proper conclusion?", "options": {"A": "We are 95% confident that the proportion of viewers who believe that online dating sites\nare the most practical way of meeting a potential spouse is between 0.280 and 0.345.", "B": "Without knowing whether both np and n(1 – p) are ≥ 10, the calculation is inappropriate.", "C": "Without knowing whether or not the 800 respondents represent less than 10% of the entire\npopulation of viewers, the calculation is inappropriate.", "D": "The z-distribution was used when the t-distribution should have been used, so the\ncalculation is inappropriate.", "E": "The data set was not a simple random sample, so the calculation is inappropriate."}, "image": "img/q331.png", "answer": "E", "explanation": "这是网上自愿回应样本,不是简单随机样本,无法用标准置信区间公式对总体做推断。"}, {"id": 332, "chapter": "第四章 统计推断", "topic": "比例的置信区间", "stem": "There are 25,000 high school students in an extended metropolitan region. As each\nschool’s students came in to register for classes, guidance counselors were instructed to\nuse a calculator to pick a random number between 1 and 100. If the number 37 was\npicked, the student was included in a survey. For one of the many survey questions, 86\npercent of the students said they couldn’t live without instant messaging. Are all\nconditions met for constructing a confidence interval of the proportion of this region’s\nteenagers who believe they couldn’t live without instant messaging?", "options": {"A": "No, there is no guarantee that a representative random sample is chosen.", "B": "No, np and n(1 – p) are not both greater than 10.", "C": "No, the sample size is not less than 10% of the population.", "D": "No, there is no reason to assume that the population has a normal distribution.", "E": "Yes, all conditions are met and a confidence interval can be constructed."}, "image": null, "answer": "A", "explanation": "各校报到注册的学生不等于全区青少年的随机样本(且存在分批注册带来的覆盖偏差),不能保证代表性随机样本。"}, {"id": 333, "chapter": "第四章 统计推断", "topic": "比例的置信区间", "stem": "For a given large sample size, which of the following gives the smallest margin of error\nwhen calculating a confidence interval for a population proportion?", "options": {"A": "91% confidence with ˆp = 0.18", "B": "93% confidence with ˆp = 0.18", "C": "95% confidence with ˆp = 0.18", "D": "91% confidence with ˆp = 0.34", "E": "93% confidence with ˆp = 0.34"}, "image": null, "answer": "A", "explanation": "边际误差 ∝ z*√(p̂(1−p̂));p̂=0.18 时 √(0.18×0.82)=0.384 小于 p̂=0.34 时的 0.474,且 91% 的 z* 最小,故 A 最小。"}, {"id": 334, "chapter": "第四章 统计推断", "topic": "比例的置信区间", "stem": "We are interested in the proportion p of people who drive for Uber in a large city. Four\npercent of a simple random sample of 890 people say they drive for Uber. What is the\nmidpoint for a 98% confidence interval estimate of p?", "options": {"A": "0.01", "B": "0.49", "C": "0.5", "D": "p", "E": "None of these are correct."}, "image": "img/q334.png", "answer": "E", "explanation": "98% 置信区间的中点就是样本比例 p̂=0.04,不在选项中(置信水平只影响宽度),故选 E。"}, {"id": 335, "chapter": "第四章 统计推断", "topic": "比例的置信区间", "stem": "If all else is held constant, how does an interval change when constructing a 97%\nconfidence interval rather than a 93% confidence interval?", "options": {"A": "The interval width increases by 4%.", "B": "The interval width increases by 20%.", "C": "The interval width increases by 35.8%.", "D": "The interval width decreases by 20%.", "E": "This question cannot be answered without knowing the sample size."}, "image": null, "answer": "B", "explanation": "z*0.985≈2.17,z*0.965≈1.81,宽度比 2.17/1.81≈1.20,即增宽约 20%。"}, {"id": 336, "chapter": "第四章 统计推断", "topic": "比例的置信区间", "stem": "In a random sample of men, a 95% confidence interval for the proportion who say they\nhave ever spent a night in jail is (0.095, 0.115). In a random sample of women, the\nproportion who say they have ever spent a night in jail is ˆp = 0.10. If the two sample sizes\nare the same, how does a 95% confidence interval for the women’s proportion compare to\nthat of the men’s?", "options": {"A": "The women’s interval has the same width and a lower point estimate.", "B": "The women’s interval is narrower and has a higher point estimate.", "C": "The women’s interval is narrower and has a lower point estimate.", "D": "The women’s interval is wider and has a higher point estimate.", "E": "The women’s interval is wider and has a lower point estimate."}, "image": null, "answer": "C", "explanation": "男区间中心 0.105、ME=0.01;女 p̂=0.10,同 n 时 1.96√(0.10×0.90/n) < 1.96√(0.105×0.895/n),故女区间更窄且点估计更低。"}, {"id": 337, "chapter": "第四章 统计推断", "topic": "比例的置信区间", "stem": "A restaurant evaluator reports that 22 percent of all restaurant meals include french\nfries. A 95% confidence interval is given by (0.202, 0.238). Which of the following is a\ncorrect interpretation of the confidence level?", "options": {"A": "We are 95 percent confident that the true proportion of all restaurant meals that include\nfrench fries is between 0.202 and 0.238.", "B": "There is a 0.95 probability that the true proportion of all restaurant meals that include\nfrench fries is between 0.202 and 0.238.", "C": "95 percent of all random samples of the same size chosen from the population result in\nconfidence intervals that contain 0.22.", "D": "95 percent of all random samples of the same size chosen from the population result in\nconfidence intervals that contain the true proportion of all restaurant meals that include\nfrench fries.", "E": "95 percent of all random samples of the same size chosen from the population have sample\nproportions in the 0.202 to 0.238 interval."}, "image": null, "answer": "D", "explanation": "置信水平的解释:所有同样大小的随机样本中约有 95% 得到的置信区间包含真实总体比例(不是包含 0.22,也不是关于这个特定区间的概率)。"}, {"id": 338, "chapter": "第四章 统计推断", "topic": "比例的置信区间", "stem": "In a random sample of 800 married couples, 240 reported that the husband did the family\nlaundry. What is a 90% confidence interval estimate of the proportion of all husbands\nwho do the family laundry?", "options": {"A": "0.3 ± 1.645 √240(0.3)(0.7)", "B": "0.3 ± 1.645 √800(0.3)(0.7)", "C": "0.3 ± 196 √800(0.3)(0.7)", "D": "0.3 ± 1.645 √(0.3)(0.7)\n240", "E": "0.3 ± 1.645 √(0.3)(0.7)\n800\nA pollster plans to obtain a random sample of 250 adults from which he will construct a\n99% confidence interval for the proportion of all adults who believe that global warming\nis caused by human activity. Which of the following statements must be true?"}, "image": null, "answer": "E", "explanation": "p̂=240/800=0.3,90% CI 为 0.3±1.645√(0.3×0.7/800),对应 E。"}, {"id": 339, "chapter": "第四章 统计推断", "topic": "比例的置信区间", "stem": "", "options": {"A": "The population proportion will be in the confidence interval.", "B": "The probability that he will construct a confidence interval containing the sample\nproportion is 0.99.", "C": "The probability that he will construct a confidence interval containing the population\nproportion is 0.99.", "D": "There is a 0.99 probability that the sample proportion will equal the population proportion.", "E": "None of the above are true statements."}, "image": null, "answer": "B", "explanation": "任何置信区间都必然包含样本比例 p̂(它是区间中心),故 B 必真;C 说的是构造过程包含总体比例的概率为 0.99,是重复抽样下的解释而非这次的具体概率。"}, {"id": 340, "chapter": "第四章 统计推断", "topic": "比例的置信区间", "stem": "In a random sample of 1,500 adults, 46 percent answered that they feel the death penalty\nis applied fairly by the courts. A margin of error of 2 percent is reported. Which of the\nfollowing statements is appropriate?", "options": {"A": "The sample is large, the sample is random, and the margin of error is relatively small. So\nit is reasonable to conclude that 46 percent of all adults feel the death penalty is applied\nfairly by the courts.", "B": "Although n = 1,500 may seem large, it is actually very small in comparison to the number\nof all adults. So no conclusion can be drawn with any confidence.", "C": "The sample proportion plus the margin of error is less than 0.50. So there is evidence that\nless than half of all adults feel the death penalty is applied fairly by the courts.", "D": "No treatments are applied. So this is not an experiment, and cause and effect cannot be\nconcluded.", "E": "0.02 < 0.05, so there is sufficient evidence for any reasonable conclusion."}, "image": null, "answer": "C", "explanation": "区间上限 0.46+0.02=0.48<0.50,有证据认为不足一半的成年人认为死刑执行公平。"}, {"id": 341, "chapter": "第四章 统计推断", "topic": "比例的置信区间", "stem": "Factors involved in the creation of a confidence interval include the sample size, level of\nconfidence, and margin of error. Which of the following is incorrect?", "options": {"A": "For a fixed margin of error, larger samples provide greater confidence.", "B": "For a given confidence level, halving the margin of error requires a sample twice as large.", "C": "For a given sample size, reducing the margin of error means lower confidence.", "D": "For a given sample size, higher confidence means a larger margin of error.", "E": "For a fixed margin of error, smaller samples mean lower confidence."}, "image": null, "answer": "B", "explanation": "边际误差与 1/√n 成正比,要把它减半需要 4 倍的样本量而不是 2 倍,故 B 错误。"}, {"id": 342, "chapter": "第四章 统计推断", "topic": "比例的置信区间", "stem": "In a survey of a random sample of 450 South Carolinians, 75 percent say they enjoy hot\npepper sauce on their food. If the margin of error is 2.3 percent, what is the level of\nconfidence?", "options": {"A": "67%", "B": "74%", "C": "87%", "D": "90%", "E": "93%\nThere have been various reports about restaurants and fish markets mislabeling seafood.\nA consumer advocacy magazine sponsored a study in which 190 pieces of seafood (out of\nan estimated 12,000 pieces sold each day in the region of interest) were purchased and\nanalyzed. Laboratory results concluded that 22 percent were mislabeled. What is a 97%\nconfidence interval for the proportion of all seafood servings/packages that were\nmislabeled?"}, "image": null, "answer": "C", "explanation": "z=0.023/√(0.75×0.25/450)=0.023/0.0204≈1.13,置信度≈2Φ(1.13)−1≈0.87,即 87%。"}, {"id": 343, "chapter": "第四章 统计推断", "topic": "比例的置信区间", "stem": "", "options": {"A": "0.22 ± 2.17 √(0.22)(0.78)\n190", "B": "0.22 ± 1.88√(0.22)(0.78)\n190", "C": "0.22 ± 2.17√(0.5)(0.5)\n190", "D": "0.22 ± 1.88√(0.22)(0.78)\n12,000", "E": "0.22 ± 2.17√(0.22)(0.78)\n12,000"}, "image": null, "answer": "A", "explanation": "97% 的 z*=2.17,CI 为 0.22±2.17√(0.22×0.78/190)(总体 12000 远大于样本,分母用 190)。"}, {"id": 344, "chapter": "第四章 统计推断", "topic": "比例的置信区间", "stem": "Is climate change causing more severe storms? In a 2017 pre–Hurricane Irma survey, 486\nout of 1,080 adults answered in the affirmative. In a 2017 post–Hurricane Irma survey,\n546 out of 1,050 answered affirmatively. Establish a 90% confidence interval estimate of\nthe difference (preminus post-hurricane) between the proportions of adults pre–\nHurricane Irma and post–Hurricane Irma who believe climate change is causing more\nsevere storms.", "options": {"A": "(0.45 − 0.52) ± 1.645√(0.45)(0.55)\n1,080\n+ (0.52)(0.48)", "B": "(0.45 − 0.52) ± 1.645( (0.45)(0.55)\n√1,050\n)\n√1,080\n+ (0.52)(0.48)", "C": "(0.45 − 0.52) ± 1.96√(0.45)(0.55)\n1,080\n+ (0.52)(0.48)\n1,050", "D": "(0.45 − 0.52) ± 1.96( (0.45)(0.55)\n√1,050\n)\n√1,080\n+ (0.52)(0.48)\n√(0.45)(0.55)\n√(0.52)(0.48)", "E": "(0.45 − 0.52) ± 2.576(\n1,080\n+"}, "image": null, "answer": "A", "explanation": "p̂pre=0.45,p̂post=0.52,90% 用 z=1.645,且置信区间用未合并的各比例标准误,对应 A。"}, {"id": 345, "chapter": "第四章 统计推断", "topic": "比例的置信区间", "stem": "In a random sample of 500 high school students, 350 said they had TVs in their bedrooms.\nIn a random sample of 500 middle school students, 325 said they had TVs in their\nbedrooms. Which of the following represents a 99% confidence interval estimate for the\ndifference (high school minus middle school) between the proportions of high school\nstudents and middle school students who have TVs in their bedrooms?\n1,050\n1,050\n)", "options": {"A": "(0.7 − 0.65) ± 1.96√(0.7)(0.3)\n500\n+ (0.65)(0.35)\n500", "B": "(0.7 − 0.65) ± 2.326√(0.7)(0.3)\n500\n+ (0.65)(0.35)\n500", "C": "(0.7 − 0.65) ± 2.576√(0.7)(0.3)\n500\n+ (0.65)(0.35)\n500", "D": "(0.7 − 0.65) ± 2.326√(0.675)(0.325)(\n1\n500 +\n1\n500 )", "E": "(0.7 − 0.65) ± 2.576√(0.675)(0.325)(\n1\n500 +\n1\n500 )"}, "image": null, "answer": "B", "explanation": "99% 的 z*=2.326,两比例差的置信区间用各自(未合并)p̂ 计算标准误:0.05±2.326√(0.7×0.3/500+0.65×0.35/500)。"}, {"id": 346, "chapter": "第四章 统计推断", "topic": "比例的置信区间", "stem": "A statistician is developing a method to compare the quotient of proportions from two\npolitical polling companies. He tests this method by simulating 5,000 samples from an\nappropriate population and calculating a 95% confidence interval from each using his\nnew method. If his new method works as intended, 4,750 of the confidence intervals from\nthe simulation will capture what?", "options": {"A": "The difference of sample proportions", "B": "The quotient of sample proportions", "C": "The quotient of sample proportions divided by an appropriate standard deviation", "D": "The difference of population proportions", "E": "The quotient of population proportions"}, "image": null, "answer": "E", "explanation": "95% 的区间中约 95%(即 5000 中的 4750)会捕获被估计的总体参数——这里是两个总体比例之商。"}, {"id": 347, "chapter": "第四章 统计推断", "topic": "比例的置信区间", "stem": "In a survey of randomly selected Americans over the age of 65, 40 percent of 756 men and\n49 percent of 825 women suffered from some form of arthritis. What is a 96% confidence\ninterval for the difference in the proportions of senior men and senior women who have\nthis disease, and does it indicate sufficient evidence that the proportions are not equal?", "options": {"A": "The confidence interval is (–0.138, –0.042). This does not indicate sufficient evidence that\nthe proportions aren’t equal because 0 is not in the interval.", "B": "The confidence interval is (–0.141, –0.039). This does not indicate sufficient evidence that\nthe proportions aren’t equal because 0 is not in the interval.", "C": "The confidence interval is (–0.138, –0.042). This does indicate sufficient evidence that the\nproportions aren’t equal because 0 is not in the interval.", "D": "The confidence interval is (–0.141, –0.039). This does indicate sufficient evidence that the\nproportions aren’t equal because 0 is not in the interval.", "E": "The confidence interval is (–0.138, –0.042). This does not indicate sufficient evidence that\nthe proportions aren’t equal because the entire interval is less than 0.\nCONFIDENCE INTERVALS FOR MEANS"}, "image": null, "answer": "D", "explanation": "差 −0.09,SE=√(0.40×0.60/756+0.49×0.51/825)≈0.0249,96% 的 z*=2.054,ME≈0.051,CI≈(−0.141,−0.039);不含 0,说明两比例有显著差异。"}, {"id": 348, "chapter": "第四章 统计推断", "topic": "均值的置信区间", "stem": "Two confidence interval estimates from the same sample are (52.7, 58.3) and (51.8, 59.2).\nOne estimate is at the 95% level, while the other is at the 99% level. Which is which?", "options": {"A": "(51.8, 59.2) is the 95% level.", "B": "(51.8, 59.2) is the 99% level.", "C": "This question cannot be answered without knowing the sample size.", "D": "This question cannot be answered without knowing the sample standard deviation.", "E": "This question cannot be answered without knowing both the sample size and the sample\nstandard deviation."}, "image": null, "answer": "B", "explanation": "置信水平越高临界值越大、区间越宽;同一样本中心相同,(51.8,59.2) 更宽,是 99% 的区间。"}, {"id": 349, "chapter": "第四章 统计推断", "topic": "均值的置信区间", "stem": "When making an inference about a population mean, which of the following suggests the\nuse of z-scores rather than t-scores?", "options": {"A": "The absence of outliers", "B": "The population is normal", "C": "The sample size is under 30", "D": "The absence of strong skew", "E": "The population standard deviation is known"}, "image": null, "answer": "E", "explanation": "只有当总体标准差 σ 已知(且总体正态或 n 大)时才用 z;否则用 t。"}, {"id": 350, "chapter": "第四章 统计推断", "topic": "均值的置信区间", "stem": "Under what conditions would it be meaningful to construct a confidence interval estimate\nwhen the data consist of the entire population?", "options": {"A": "If the population size is small (n < 30)", "B": "If the population size is large (n ≥ 30)", "C": "If a higher level of confidence is desired", "D": "If the population is truly random", "E": "Never"}, "image": null, "answer": "E", "explanation": "若数据就是整个总体,参数可直接算出,不存在抽样误差,构造置信区间毫无意义。"}, {"id": 351, "chapter": "第四章 统计推断", "topic": "均值的置信区间", "stem": "The number of cloudy days per month (days in which the sun is never seen) in a\nnorthwestern town is noted for a random sample of 60 months with ¯x = 23.4 and s = 3.7.\nWith what degree of confidence can we assert that the mean number of cloudy days per\nmonth in this town is between 22.4 and 24.4?", "options": {"A": "48%", "B": "90%", "C": "95%", "D": "96%", "E": "99%"}, "image": null, "answer": "D", "explanation": "SE=3.7/√60=0.478,ME=1,t=1/0.478≈2.09,df=59 时 P(|t|<2.09)≈0.959,约 96%。"}, {"id": 352, "chapter": "第四章 统计推断", "topic": "均值的置信区间", "stem": "Four statistics majors receiving actuarial job offers after graduation will receive the\nfollowing starting salaries: $54,000, $61,000, $48,000, and $57,000. If all assumptions are\nmet, what is a 95% confidence interval for the population mean for all statistics majors\nreceiving actuarial job offers?", "options": {"A": "55,000 ± 1.96( 5,477\n√3 )", "B": "55,000 ± 2.776( 5,477\n√3 )", "C": "55,000 ± 3.182( 5,477\n√3 )", "D": "55,000 ± 2.776( 5,477\n√4 )", "E": "55,000 ± 3.182( 5,477\n√4 )"}, "image": null, "answer": "C", "explanation": "s=5477 是用 n−1=3 算的,n=4 时 t*(df=3,95%)=3.182,CI 为 55000±3.182(5477/√4)。"}, {"id": 353, "chapter": "第四章 统计推断", "topic": "均值的置信区间", "stem": "Two 95% confidence interval estimates are obtained: I (34.5, 40.5) and II (36.3, 44.2).\na.   If the sample sizes are the same, which has the larger standard deviation?\nb.   If the sample standard deviations are the same, which has the larger sample size?", "options": {"A": "a. I, b. I", "B": "a. I, b. II", "C": "a. II, b. I", "D": "a. II, b. II", "E": "More information is needed to answer these questions."}, "image": null, "answer": "C", "explanation": "宽度 I=6、II=7.9;a. 同 n 时越宽说明标准差越大,选 II;b. 同标准差时越宽说明 n 越小,故 II 的 n 更小、I 的 n 更大。"}, {"id": 354, "chapter": "第四章 统计推断", "topic": "均值的置信区间", "stem": "Suppose (59, 72) is a 99% confidence interval estimate for a population mean µ. Which of\nthe following is a true statement?", "options": {"A": "Confidence level cannot be interpreted until after data are obtained.", "B": "There is a 0.99 probability that μ is between 59 and 72.", "C": "The probability that μ is in any particular confidence interval can be any value between 0\nand 1.", "D": "If 100 random samples of the given size are picked and a 99% confidence interval\nestimate is calculated from each, μ will be in 99 of the resulting intervals.", "E": "If 99% confidence intervals are calculated from all possible samples of the given size, μ\nwill be in 99% of these intervals."}, "image": null, "answer": "E", "explanation": "正确解释:所有可能样本构造的 99% 区间中约有 99% 包含 μ;D 说恰好 99 个,不对(是期望/比例,不保证恰好)。"}, {"id": 355, "chapter": "第四章 统计推断", "topic": "均值的置信区间", "stem": "A principal is informed that among the 376 seniors in her high school, the average SAT\nMath score is 615 with a standard deviation of 28.5. With what margin of error is the\nmean SAT Math score of these students known?", "options": {"A": "0", "B": "28.5", "C": "28.5\n√615", "D": "1.96( 28.5\n√615 )", "E": "None of the above give the correct answer."}, "image": "img/q355.png", "answer": "A", "explanation": "这是全体 376 名学生的普查数据,均值 615 已知,没有抽样变异,边际误差为 0。"}, {"id": 356, "chapter": "第四章 统计推断", "topic": "均值的置信区间", "stem": "In a random sample of 18 high school students’ backpacks, the average weight was 14.5\npounds with a standard deviation of 3.9 pounds. Assuming all conditions for inference are\nmet, what is a 96% confidence interval for the mean weight of all high school students’\nbackpacks?", "options": {"A": "14.5 ± 2.054 3.9\n√18", "B": "14.5 ± 2.205 3.9\n√18", "C": "14.5 ± 2.214 3.9\n√18", "D": "14.5 ± 2.224 3.9\n√18", "E": "14.5 ± 2.235 3.9\n√18"}, "image": null, "answer": "D", "explanation": "n=18,df=17,96% 双侧 t*=2.224,CI 为 14.5±2.224(3.9/√18)。"}, {"id": 357, "chapter": "第四章 统计推断", "topic": "均值的置信区间", "stem": "To determine the average spent on books and lab supplies during a year in college, a\nsimple random sample of 50 students is interviewed, showing a mean of $970 with a\nstandard deviation of $310. Which of the following is the best interpretation of a 95%\nconfidence interval estimate for the average spent on books and lab supplies during a\nyear in college?", "options": {"A": "95% of college students spend between $882 and $1,058 on books and lab supplies yearly.", "B": "95% of college students spend a mean dollar amount on books and lab supplies yearly that\nis between $882 and $1,058.", "C": "We are 95% confident that college students spend between $882 and $1,058 on books and\nlab supplies yearly.", "D": "We are 95% confident that college students spend a mean dollar amount between $882 and\n$1,058 on books and lab supplies yearly.", "E": "We are 95% confident that in the chosen sample, the mean dollar amount spent on books\nand lab supplies yearly by college students is between $882 and $1,058."}, "image": null, "answer": "D", "explanation": "置信区间估计的是总体均值(平均花费),不是个体:有 95% 把握认为全体大学生每年在书籍实验用品上的平均花费在 882 到 1058 美元之间。"}, {"id": 358, "chapter": "第四章 统计推断", "topic": "均值的置信区间", "stem": "A survey was conducted involving 75 of the 26,000 families living in a town. In 2017, the\naverage amount of medical expenses paid per family in the sample was $1,950 with a\nstandard deviation of $640. What is a 95% confidence interval estimate for the total\nmedical expenses paid by all families in the town?", "options": {"A": "26,000[1,950 ± 1.993(640)]", "B": "1,950 ± 1.993( 640\n√75 )", "C": "26,000[1,950 ± 1.993( 640\n√75 )", "D": "26,000[1,950 ± 1.993( 640\n√74 )", "E": "26,000[1,950 ± 1.96( 640\n√75 )"}, "image": null, "answer": "C", "explanation": "先用 t(df=74,95%)=1.993 得均值的 CI:1950±1.993(640/√75),再乘以 26000 个家庭得总支出区间。"}, {"id": 359, "chapter": "第四章 统计推断", "topic": "均值的置信区间", "stem": "A home economist wants to estimate the mean temperature of the typical shower. In a\nrandom sample of 16 people, the mean temperature of their showers was 101°F with a\nstandard deviation of 2.4°F. Assuming all conditions of inference were met, what is a 95%\nconfidence interval estimate for the mean typical shower temperature for all people?", "options": {"A": "101 ± 1.96( 2.4\n4 )", "B": "101 ± 1.96( √2.4\n4\n)", "C": "101 ± 2.131( √2.4\n16 )", "D": "101 ± 2.131( 2.4\n4 )", "E": "101 ± 2.131( 2.4\n16 )"}, "image": null, "answer": "D", "explanation": "n=16,df=15,t*(95%)=2.131,CI 为 101±2.131(2.4/4)=101±2.131(2.4/√16)。"}, {"id": 360, "chapter": "第四章 统计推断", "topic": "均值的置信区间", "stem": "A national education journal states that high school students get an average of 7.6 hours\nof sleep per night. A guidance counselor thinks that her school’s students might have a\ndifferent average and collects data from a random sample of these students. The data are\nused to test the hypotheses H0: µ = 7.6, Ha: µ ≠ 7.6. The results of the test are shown in\nthe table below.\nSample Mean\nStandard Error\ndf\nt\nP\n7.4\n0.1091\n20\n–1.833\n0.0817\nAssuming all conditions for inference are met, which of the following gives a 90%\nconfidence interval for µ?", "options": {"A": "7.4 ± 0.0411", "B": "7.4 ± 0.0436", "C": "7.4 ± 0.1091", "D": "7.4 ± 0.1882", "E": "7.4 ± 0.2000"}, "image": "img/q360.png", "answer": "D", "explanation": "df=20,90% 的 t*=1.725,CI 为 7.4±1.725×0.1091=7.4±0.1882。"}, {"id": 361, "chapter": "第四章 统计推断", "topic": "均值的置信区间", "stem": "A random sample of 60 high school students took part in a study where they ate helpings\nof pizza and then, four hours later, had their LDL cholesterol levels measured. The 95%\nconfidence interval for the mean LDL cholesterol level for all high school students four\nhours after eating pizza was calculated to be (103, 120). Which of the following is a true\nstatement?", "options": {"A": "95 percent of all high school students will have LDL cholesterol levels between 103 and\n120 four hours after eating pizza.", "B": "Approximately 95 percent of all random samples of size 60 of high school students will\nresult in 95% confidence intervals containing 111.5 for the mean LDL cholesterol level of\nall high school students four hours after eating pizza.", "C": "Approximately 95 percent of all random samples of size 60 of high school students will\nresult in 95% confidence intervals containing the true mean population LDL cholesterol\nlevel for the mean LDL cholesterol level of all high school students four hours after eating\npizza.", "D": "The probability is 0.95 that a randomly selected high school student will have an LDL\ncholesterol level between 103 and 120 four hours after eating pizza.", "E": "We are 95 percent confident that the mean LDL cholesterol level of these 60 students four\nhours after eating pizza is between 103 and 120."}, "image": null, "answer": "C", "explanation": "置信区间含义:所有大小为 60 的随机样本中约 95% 所得区间包含真实的总体平均 LDL 水平。"}, {"id": 362, "chapter": "第四章 统计推断", "topic": "均值的置信区间", "stem": "A 90% confidence interval for the mean time, in minutes, for high school students to\nfinish a video game is determined to be (35.2, 73.4). Which of the following is the best\ninterpretation of the interval?", "options": {"A": "Ten percent of players will take either less than 35.2 minutes or more than 73.4 minutes.", "B": "The probability that a randomly selected player will take between 35.2 and 73.4 minutes is\n0.90.", "C": "For 90 percent of players, their mean finishing time is between 35.2 and 73.4 minutes.", "D": "If this study was repeated many times, the resulting confidence interval will contain the\ntrue mean finishing time 90 percent of the time.", "E": "We are 90 percent confident that the true mean finishing time is between 35.2 and 73.4\nminutes."}, "image": null, "answer": "E", "explanation": "标准解释:我们有 90% 的把握认为真实平均通关时间在 35.2 到 73.4 分钟之间(D 也接近,但 E 是最直接的区间解释,最佳答案为 E)。"}, {"id": 363, "chapter": "第四章 统计推断", "topic": "均值的置信区间", "stem": "Americans and Japanese were surveyed as to the ideal number of children a family\nshould have. For 500 Americans, the mean was 1.95 with a standard deviation of 0.65. For\n450 Japanese, the mean was 1.05 with a standard deviation of 0.40. Assuming all\nconditions for inference are met, what is a 90% confidence interval for the difference in\npopulation means?", "options": {"A": "0.9 ± 1.96√(1.95)2\n500\n+ (1.05)2\n450", "B": "0.9 ± 1.645( 1.65\n√500 +\n0.4\n√450 )", "C": "0.9 ± 1.645√(0.65)2\n500\n+ (0.4)2\n450", "D": "0.9 ± 1.96( 0.65\n√500 +\n0.4\n√450 )", "E": "0.9 ± 3.29( 0.65\n√500 +\n0.4\n√450 )"}, "image": null, "answer": "C", "explanation": "两均值差 0.9,90% 的 z=1.645,SE=√(0.65²/500+0.40²/450),对应 C。"}, {"id": 364, "chapter": "第四章 统计推断", "topic": "均值的置信区间", "stem": "A dietician plans to compare the average number of minutes spent eating each day for\nFrench and Americans. The 95% confidence interval estimate of the difference (French –\nAmericans) is (47, 73). Which of the following is the most reasonable conclusion?", "options": {"A": "The mean number of minutes daily spent eating among the French is 73\n47 times the mean\nspent by Americans.", "B": "The mean number of minutes daily spent eating among the French is 73 minutes, while the\nmean among Americans is 47 minutes.", "C": "The probability that the mean numbers of minutes spent eating are different is 0.95.", "D": "The probability that the difference in minutes spent eating is greater than 47 minutes is\n0.95.", "E": "We should be 95 percent confident that the difference in mean minutes spent eating is\nbetween 47 and 73 minutes."}, "image": null, "answer": "E", "explanation": "95% 置信区间 (47,73) 的含义:有 95% 把握认为法国人与美国人每日就餐时间平均相差(法国−美国)在 47 到 73 分钟之间。"}, {"id": 365, "chapter": "第四章 统计推断", "topic": "均值的置信区间", "stem": "In a national sleep study, a random sample of adults was contacted. These subjects were\nasked the number of hours they slept per night and whether or not they exercised\nregularly. Of the 240 adults who exercised regularly, the mean number of hours of sleep\nper night was 8.2 with a standard deviation of 0.4. Of the 347 adults who did not exercise\nregularly, the mean number of hours of sleep per night was 7.9 with a standard deviation\nof 0.5. Which of the following is the most appropriate standard error for a confidence\ninterval of the difference in nightly sleep hours between exercisers and nonexercisers?", "options": {"A": "√(8.2)2\n240 + (7.9)2\n347", "B": "8.2\n√240 +\n7.9\n√347", "C": "√(0.4)2\n240 + (0.5)2\n347", "D": "0.4\n√240 +\n0.5\n√347", "E": "√(0.4)2+(0.5)2\n240+347"}, "image": null, "answer": "C", "explanation": "两独立均值差的标准误为 √(s1²/n1+s2²/n2)=√(0.4²/240+0.5²/347)。"}, {"id": 366, "chapter": "第四章 统计推断", "topic": "均值的置信区间", "stem": "A botanist chooses a random sample of 10 maple trees and an independent random\nsample of 10 elm trees. She measures the diameters of the trees and then calculates the\nmean and standard deviation of the diameters for each sample. Assume the distribution\nof diameter sizes for maples and for elms are both normal. What is the appropriate\nmethod for constructing a two-sample confidence interval of the difference in the mean\ndiameters of maples and elms?", "options": {"A": "A z-interval is appropriate because both distributions are normal.", "B": "A z-interval is appropriate because both sample standard deviations are known.", "C": "A z-interval is appropriate because the central limit theorem applies.", "D": "A t-interval is appropriate because the population standard deviations are unknown.", "E": "A t-interval is appropriate because the sample sizes are both below 30."}, "image": null, "answer": "D", "explanation": "总体标准差未知、用样本标准差 s 估计时,应使用两样本 t 区间(虽然总体正态保证 t 方法有效,但理由是 σ 未知)。"}, {"id": 367, "chapter": "第四章 统计推断", "topic": "均值的置信区间", "stem": "Suppose a two-sample t-test for H0: µ1 – µ2 = 0 and Ha: µ1 – µ2 > 0 results in a P-value of\n0.04. Which of the statements below must be true?\nI. A 90% confidence interval for the difference in means will contain 0.\nII. A 95% confidence interval for the difference in means will contain 0.\nIII. A 99% confidence interval for the difference in means will contain 0.", "options": {"A": "I only", "B": "I and II only", "C": "I, II, and III", "D": "III only", "E": "II and III only"}, "image": "img/q367.png", "answer": "D", "explanation": "单侧检验 P=0.04:在 α=0.05 下显著 ⇔ 90%、95% 区间不含 0;在 α=0.01 下不显著 ⇔ 99% 区间含 0,故只有 III。"}, {"id": 368, "chapter": "第四章 统计推断", "topic": "均值的置信区间", "stem": "A nutritionist analyzes two types of hot dogs, all-beef and all-chicken. A random sample\nof 10 all-beef hot dogs showed a mean of 9.9 mg of fat with a standard deviation of 1.5\nmg. A random sample of 8 all-chicken hot dogs showed a mean of 7.5 mg of fat with a\nstandard deviation of 1.1 mg. What is the standard error of the difference in fat content\n(beef – chicken) between the sample means?", "options": {"A": "√9.92\n10 + 7.52\n8", "B": "9.9\n√10 −7.5\n√8", "C": "√1.52\n10 −1.12\n8", "D": "√1.52\n10 + 1.12\n8", "E": "1.5\n√10 + 1.1\n√8"}, "image": null, "answer": "D", "explanation": "差的标准误为 √(1.5²/10+1.1²/8)。"}, {"id": 369, "chapter": "第四章 统计推断", "topic": "均值的置信区间", "stem": "An experiment is conducted to test the effectiveness of a new sleep aid. Twenty volunteers\nare randomly assigned to two groups, with one group receiving the sleep aid while the\nother group receives a similar-looking placebo. The next day, the mean hours of sleep for\neach group is calculated and a two-sample t-test for the difference of means is performed.\nWhich of the following is a necessary assumption?", "options": {"A": "The expected number of hours of sleep for each person is at least 5.", "B": "The sample sizes are greater than or equal to 10 percent of the population sizes.", "C": "The 10 volunteers randomly assigned to the sleep aid group are paired with the 10\nvolunteers assigned to the placebo group.", "D": "The distributions of hours of sleep in each population is approximately normal.", "E": "Each group of 10 should have 5 successes and 5 failures.\nCONFIDENCE INTERVALS FOR SLOPES"}, "image": null, "answer": "D", "explanation": "两样本 t 检验(独立组、n 小)需要假定两个总体睡眠小时数的分布近似正态。"}, {"id": 370, "chapter": "第四章 统计推断", "topic": "斜率的置信区间", "stem": "Below is the computer out put for a regression analysis involving starting salary (in\n$1,000) and college GPA in a random sample of 25 graduates.\nWhat is a 90% confidence interval for the slope of the regression line?", "options": {"A": "11.8204 ± 1.645(1.848)", "B": "11.8204 ± 1.711(1.848)", "C": "11.8204 ± 1.714(1.848)", "D": "11.8204 ± 1.645( 1.848\n√25 )", "E": "11.8204 ± 1.711( 1.848\n√25 )"}, "image": "img/q370.png", "answer": "C", "explanation": "df=n−2=23,90% 的 t*=1.714,斜率 CI 为 11.8204±1.714×1.848。"}, {"id": 371, "chapter": "第四章 统计推断", "topic": "斜率的置信区间", "stem": "An insurance adjustor is interested in the age of homes and average wind and flood\ndamage from hurricanes. Data from 20 randomly selected homes generate the following\ncomputer output:\nWhich of the following gives a 96% confidence interval for the slope of the regression\nline?", "options": {"A": "9,718 ± 2.054", "B": "9,718 ± 2.197", "C": "9,718 ± 2.214( 7,208\n√20 )", "D": "15,675 ± 2.197( 15,720\n√20 )", "E": "15,675 ± 2.214(1,678)"}, "image": "img/q371.png", "answer": "E", "explanation": "n=20,df=18,96% 的 t*=2.214;斜率 15675、SE 1678,CI 为 15675±2.214(1678)。"}, {"id": 372, "chapter": "第四章 统计推断", "topic": "斜率的置信区间", "stem": "A study is made relating life expectancy (in days) of a laptop battery as a function of\nprice of the battery. Data from a sample of 13 laptops generates the following computer\noutput.\nWhich of the following gives a 90% confidence interval for the slope of the regression\nline?", "options": {"A": "410.997 ± 1.771(67.79)", "B": "410.997 ± 1.796(67.79)", "C": "5.52979 ± 1.645(74.29)", "D": "5.52979 ± 1.771(1.135)", "E": "5.52979 ± 1.796(1.135)"}, "image": null, "answer": "A", "explanation": "n=13,df=11,90% 的 t*=1.796,斜率 CI 为 410.997±1.796×67.79。"}, {"id": 373, "chapter": "第四章 统计推断", "topic": "斜率的置信区间", "stem": "A college Office of Alumni Relations gathers data and performs a linear regression\nanalysis on donation gifts versus salary of alumni. The resulting computer output (where\nsalary is in $1,000) is shown below.\nWhat is a 95% confidence interval of the slope interpreted in context?", "options": {"A": "We are 95 percent confident that for each $1,000 more in salary that an alumnus earns,\nhe/she will donate $10.29 more.", "B": "We are 95 percent confident that for each $1,000 more in salary that an alumnus earns,\nhe/she will donate between $8.57 and $12.01 more.", "C": "We are 95 percent confident that for each $1,000 more in salary that an alumnus earns, it is\npredicted that he/she will donate between $8.57 and $12.01 more on average.", "D": "89.8 percent of the variability in gift donations is explained by the linear model.", "E": "89.8 percent of the variability in gift donations is explained by variability in salary."}, "image": "img/q373.png", "answer": "C", "explanation": "斜率 10.29、SE 0.44,95% CI 约 (8.57,12.01):每多挣 1000 美元薪水,预测平均多捐 8.57 至 12.01 美元(C 强调平均/predicted)。"}, {"id": 374, "chapter": "第四章 统计推断", "topic": "斜率的置信区间", "stem": "A regression analysis of the prices of textbooks versus page lengths yields the following\nequation.\nPredicted price = –3.35 + 0.15(Pages)\nA 92% confidence interval of the slope is (0.115, 0.185). What is a correct interpretation\nof this interval in context?", "options": {"A": "Every additional page will raise the price $0.15.", "B": "The probability is 0.92 that, on average, each additional page will raise the price $0.15.", "C": "92 percent of all random samples of textbooks will give a regression slope between 0.115\nand 0.185.", "D": "We are 92 percent confident that each additional page will raise the price $0.15.", "E": "We are 92 percent confident that each additional page will raise the price between $0.115\nand $0.185 on average."}, "image": null, "answer": "E", "explanation": "斜率置信区间的解释:有 92% 把握认为每增加一页,书价平均提高 0.115 到 0.185 美元。"}, {"id": 375, "chapter": "第四章 统计推断", "topic": "斜率的置信区间", "stem": "The 96% confidence interval for the slope of a regression line is (–0.142, 1.036). Which of\nthe following is a true statement?", "options": {"A": "The sample slope is b = 0.", "B": "The sample slope is b = 0.589.", "C": "The sum of the residuals is positive.", "D": "The mean of the residuals is positive.", "E": "The correlation coefficient r is positive.\nDETERMINING SAMPLE SIZES"}, "image": null, "answer": "E", "explanation": "样本斜率是区间中心 (−0.142+1.036)/2≈0.447>0,而 r 与 b 同号,故 r 为正;其余选项无法由区间得出(B 的 0.589 不是中点)。"}, {"id": 376, "chapter": "第四章 统计推断", "topic": "确定样本量", "stem": "In general, how does halving the sample size change the confidence interval size?", "options": {"A": "It doubles the interval size.", "B": "It halves the interval size.", "C": "It multiplies the interval size by 1.414.", "D": "It divides the interval size by 1.414.", "E": "This question cannot be answered without knowing the sample size."}, "image": null, "answer": "C", "explanation": "边际误差与 1/√n 成正比,n 减半则乘以 √2≈1.414,区间变宽为原来 1.414 倍。"}, {"id": 377, "chapter": "第四章 统计推断", "topic": "确定样本量", "stem": "A hospital administrator wishes to determine the mean number of admissions per day to\nwithin ±0.15 at a 94% confidence level. What sample size should be chosen if it is known\nthat the standard deviation is 0.56?", "options": {"A": "8", "B": "38", "C": "49", "D": "50", "E": "54"}, "image": "img/q377.png", "answer": "D", "explanation": "n=(1.881×0.56/0.15)²=(7.02)²=49.3,向上取整为 50。"}, {"id": 378, "chapter": "第四章 统计推断", "topic": "确定样本量", "stem": "A survey is to be taken to estimate the proportion of voters who favor stem cell research.\nAmong the following proposed sample sizes, which is the smallest that will still guarantee\na margin of error of at most 0.045 for a 98% confidence interval?", "options": {"A": "26", "B": "200", "C": "400", "D": "600", "E": "700"}, "image": "img/q378.png", "answer": "E", "explanation": "用最保守 p=0.5:n=(2.326×0.5/0.045)²=(25.84)²=667.7,需至少 668,选项中最小满足的是 700。"}, {"id": 379, "chapter": "第四章 统计推断", "topic": "确定样本量", "stem": "Which of the following would result in the narrowest confidence interval?", "options": {"A": "Small sample size and 93% confidence", "B": "Small sample size and 97% confidence", "C": "Large sample size and 93% confidence", "D": "Large sample size and 97% confidence", "E": "This cannot be answered without knowing an appropriate standard deviation."}, "image": null, "answer": "C", "explanation": "区间宽度随样本量增大而变窄、随置信水平提高而变宽,故大样本+较低置信水平(93%)最窄。"}, {"id": 380, "chapter": "第四章 统计推断", "topic": "确定样本量", "stem": "A researcher plans to investigate the difference between the proportion of children of\ndrug abusers and the proportion of children of nondrug abusers who experiment with\ndrugs while in high school. Which of the following will give the sample size n that should\nbe taken (same number for each group) to be 95 percent certain of knowing the difference\nto within ±0.025?", "options": {"A": "1.645( 0.5\n√n ) ≤0.025", "B": "1.645( 0.5√2\n√n ) ≤0.025", "C": "1.96( 0.5\n√n ) ≤0.025", "D": "1.96( 0.5√2\n√n ) ≤0.025", "E": "2.576( 0.5\n√n ) ≤0.025"}, "image": null, "answer": "D", "explanation": "95%、两组各 n、用 p=0.5:ME=1.96√(0.5×0.5/n+0.5×0.5/n)=1.96(0.5√2/√n)≤0.025。"}, {"id": 381, "chapter": "第四章 统计推断", "topic": "确定样本量", "stem": "A concerned-scientists action group wishes to learn the proportion of high school students\nwho believe that humans are the principal cause of observed global warming. From a past\nstudy, the group knows that it will have to poll 200 people for the desired level of\nconfidence. If the group wants to keep the same level of confidence but divide the margin\nof error by 3, how many people will it have to poll?", "options": {"A": "23", "B": "68", "C": "300", "D": "600", "E": "1,800"}, "image": "img/q381.png", "answer": "E", "explanation": "边际误差与 1/√n 成正比,要除以 3 则 n 需乘以 9:200×9=1800。"}, {"id": 382, "chapter": "第四章 统计推断", "topic": "确定样本量", "stem": "A guidance counselor wishes to know the difference in GPAs between students who\nexercise regularly and those who don’t. Suppose the standard deviation of each group is\nknown to be 0.93. Which of the following will give the sample size n that should be taken\n(same number for each group) to be 99 percent certain of knowing the difference to\nwithin ±0.1 on the GPA scale?", "options": {"A": "2.326 0.93\n√n ≤0.1", "B": "2.576 0.93\n√n ≤0.1", "C": "2.326√0.932\nn\n+ 0.932\nn\n≤0.1", "D": "2.576√0.932\nn\n+ 0.932\nn\n≤0.1", "E": "2.576 0.5\n√n ≤0.1"}, "image": null, "answer": "D", "explanation": "99%、两均值差:ME=2.576√(0.93²/n+0.93²/n)≤0.1,对应 D。"}, {"id": 383, "chapter": "第四章 统计推断", "topic": "确定样本量", "stem": "Two samples, S and T, of sizes 50 and 200, respectively, are obtained from the same\npopulation and result in the same sample proportion. In calculating a confidence interval\nfor p from each sample, which of the following is a true statement about the margins of\nerror obtained for each confidence interval?", "options": {"A": "Since the sample proportions are equal, the margins of error are equal.", "B": "The margin of error using S is 2 times the margin of error using T.", "C": "The margin of error using S is 4 times the margin of error using T.", "D": "The margin of error using T is 2 times the margin of error using S.", "E": "The margin of error using T is 4 times the margin of error using S."}, "image": null, "answer": "B", "explanation": "同 p̂ 下边际误差 ∝ 1/√n;n=50 是 n=200 的 1/4,故 S 的边际误差是 T 的 √4=2 倍。"}, {"id": 384, "chapter": "第四章 统计推断", "topic": "确定样本量", "stem": "The presence of mercury in fish is a growing concern, especially now that people are\nbeing encouraged to eat more fish for the omega-3 content. Past studies of New Jersey\ncoastal fish indicated mercury above 0.5 parts per million, a level that could pose a\nhuman health concern. A new study is being planned. Assuming the old study’s standard\ndeviation of 0.1 parts per million is still accurate, what size sample of fish should be\ncaught for a 95% confidence interval for mercury with a margin of error no more than\n0.008 parts per million?", "options": {"A": "25", "B": "123", "C": "423", "D": "601", "E": "15,007"}, "image": "img/q384.png", "answer": "D", "explanation": "n=(1.96×0.1/0.008)²=24.5²=600.25,向上取整为 601。"}, {"id": 385, "chapter": "第四章 统计推断", "topic": "确定样本量", "stem": "An administrator at the National Council of Teachers is interested in the proportion of\nteachers who believe they will be able to retire comfortably when they reach the age of 60.\nWhich of the following will give the minimum sample size that needs to be surveyed to be\n98 percent confident of the true proportion to within ±3.5 percent?", "options": {"A": "2.054√(0.98)(0.02)\nn\n≤0.035", "B": "2.054 0.5\n√n ≤0.035", "C": "2.326√(0.98)(0.02)\nn\n≤0.035", "D": "2.326 0.5\n√n ≤0.035", "E": "2.576√(0.035)(0.965)\nn\n≤0.035"}, "image": null, "answer": "D", "explanation": "无先前估计时用 p=0.5、98% 的 z=2.326:ME=2.326(0.5/√n)≤0.035。"}, {"id": 386, "chapter": "第四章 统计推断", "topic": "确定样本量", "stem": "How fast can you download movies? We want to estimate the mean download time of new\nsoftware being heavily advertised. Suppose the standard deviation of download times is\nknown to be approximately 8 minutes. Which of the following gives the number of trial\ndownloads we should run to have 90 percent confidence in our answer with a margin of\nerror of at most 5 minutes?", "options": {"A": "1.645 8\n√n ≤5", "B": "1.645 8(0.5)\n√n\n≤5", "C": "1.645 8√2\n√n ≤5", "D": "1.96 8\n√n ≤5", "E": "1.96 8√2\n√n ≤5"}, "image": null, "answer": "A", "explanation": "σ=8 已知,90% 的 z=1.645:ME=1.645(8/√n)≤5,对应 A。"}, {"id": 387, "chapter": "第四章 统计推断", "topic": "确定样本量", "stem": "A cardiologist notices that 20 percent of her patients who complain of chest pain need\nstents. She plans to establish a confidence interval estimate at the 96 percent level with a\nmargin of error of 3 percent. Of the following, which is the smallest sample size that she\ncan use?", "options": {"A": "25", "B": "350", "C": "600", "D": "800", "E": "1,000\nLOGIC OF SIGNIFICANCE TESTING"}, "image": "img/q387.png", "answer": "D", "explanation": "n=(2.054²×0.2×0.8)/0.03²=0.675/0.0009≈750,需至少751;n=600时ME=2.054√(0.16/600)=0.0335>0.03,选项中最小满足的是800。"}, {"id": 388, "chapter": "第四章 统计推断", "topic": "显著性检验逻辑", "stem": "Which of the following is a true statement?", "options": {"A": "A well-planned test of significance should result in a statement that either the null\nhypothesis is true or that it is false.", "B": "The null hypothesis is one-sided and expressed using either < or > if there is interest in\ndeviations in only one direction.", "C": "When a true parameter value is further from the hypothesized value, it becomes easier to\nreject the alternative hypothesis.", "D": "Tests of significance (hypothesis tests) are designed to measure the strength of evidence\nagainst the null hypothesis.", "E": "Increasing the sample size makes it more difficult to conclude that an observed distance\nbetween observed and hypothesized values is significant."}, "image": null, "answer": "D", "explanation": "显著性检验的目的是衡量反对原假设证据的强度,不会证明原假设真或假;样本量越大越容易得出显著结论。"}, {"id": 389, "chapter": "第四章 统计推断", "topic": "显著性检验逻辑", "stem": "Which of the following is a true statement?", "options": {"A": "If the P-value is 0.16, the probability that the null hypothesis is correct is 0.16.", "B": "The larger the P-value is, the more evidence there is against the null hypothesis.", "C": "If the P-value is small enough, we can conclude that the alternative hypothesis is true.", "D": "It is always wrong to use sample statistics in stating hypotheses.", "E": "It is helpful to examine your data before deciding whether to use a one-sided or a two\nsided hypothesis test."}, "image": null, "answer": "D", "explanation": "假设是关于总体参数的,用样本统计量(如x̄)陈述假设永远是错的;单双侧应在看数据前确定,故其余皆错。"}, {"id": 390, "chapter": "第四章 统计推断", "topic": "显著性检验逻辑", "stem": "Which of the following is a true statement?", "options": {"A": "The P-value of a test is the probability of obtaining a result as extreme as or more extreme\nthan the one obtained when assuming the null hypothesis is true.", "B": "If the P-value for a test is 0.014, the probability that the null hypothesis is true is 0.014.", "C": "When the null hypothesis is rejected, it is because the null hypothesis is not true.", "D": "A very large P-value provides convincing evidence that the null hypothesis is true.", "E": "The larger the P-value is, the greater the evidence of something statistically significant."}, "image": null, "answer": "A", "explanation": "P值定义:在原假设为真的前提下,得到与观测结果同样极端或更极端结果的概率。"}, {"id": 391, "chapter": "第四章 统计推断", "topic": "显著性检验逻辑", "stem": "Which of the following is a true statement?", "options": {"A": "If a population parameter is known, there is no reason to run a hypothesis test on that\npopulation parameter.", "B": "The P-value can be negative or positive depending on whether the sample statistic is less\nthan or greater than the claimed value of the population parameter in the null hypothesis.", "C": "The P-value is based on a specific test statistic, so the P-value must be chosen before an\nexperiment is conducted.", "D": "If a P-value is larger than a specified value α, the data are statistically significant at that\nlevel.", "E": "The P-value is a probability calculated when assuming that the alternative hypothesis is\ntrue."}, "image": null, "answer": "A", "explanation": "若总体参数已知,就无需再做关于该参数的假设检验;P值非负、P>α表示不显著、且是在H0为真下计算的。"}, {"id": 392, "chapter": "第四章 统计推断", "topic": "显著性检验逻辑", "stem": "The mean miles per gallon (mpg) of a certain model car is 32.8. Concerned that a\nproduction process change might have lowered that efficiency, an inspector tests a\nrandom sample of six cars, calculating a mean of 31.9 mpg with a t-score of –2.16 and a Pvalue of 0.0416. Which of the following is the most reasonable conclusion?", "options": {"A": "95.84% of the cars produced under the new process will have an mpg under 31.9.", "B": "95.84% of the cars produced under the new process will have an mpg under 32.8.", "C": "4.16% of the cars produced under the new process will have an mpg over 32.8.", "D": "There is evidence at the 5% significance level to conclude that the new process is\nproducing cars with a mean mpg under 31.9.", "E": "There is evidence at the 5% significance level to conclude that the new process is\nproducing cars with a mean mpg under 32.8."}, "image": null, "answer": "E", "explanation": "单侧检验P=0.0416<0.05,在5%水平上有证据认为新工艺生产的汽车平均mpg低于32.8(原假设值)。"}, {"id": 393, "chapter": "第四章 统计推断", "topic": "显著性检验逻辑", "stem": "Which of the following is a true statement?", "options": {"A": "A P-value is a conditional probability.", "B": "The P-value is the probability that the null hypothesis is true.", "C": "A P-value is the probability the null hypothesis is true given a particular observed\nstatistic.", "D": "The P-value is the same as the power of a hypothesis test.", "E": "A large P-value is evidence against the null hypothesis because it says that observed\nresults are unlikely to occur when the null hypothesis is true."}, "image": null, "answer": "A", "explanation": "P值是在H0为真条件下的条件概率,不是H0为真的概率,也不等于检验功效。"}, {"id": 394, "chapter": "第四章 统计推断", "topic": "显著性检验逻辑", "stem": "A small retail store employs five men and five women. When comparing the mean years\nof service of these men and women, which of the following is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "A two-sample z-test of population means", "B": "A two-sample t-test of population means", "C": "A one-sample z-test on a set of differences", "D": "A one-sample z-test on a set of differences", "E": "None of the above are appropriate."}, "image": null, "answer": "B", "explanation": "男、女是两个独立小组的均值比较,总体标准差未知,应使用两样本t检验。"}, {"id": 395, "chapter": "第四章 统计推断", "topic": "显著性检验逻辑", "stem": "In a simple random sample (SRS) of 625 families who do not live near any chemical\nplant, 10 had children with leukemia. In an SRS of 412 families living near chemical\nplants, 15 had children with leukemia. A 90% confidence interval of the difference is\nreported to be –0.020 ± 0.017. Which of the following is a proper conclusion?", "options": {"A": "The interval is invalid because it does not contain 0.", "B": "The interval is invalid because probabilities cannot be negative.", "C": "Families who live near chemical plants are approximately 2.0 percent more likely to have\nchildren with leukemia than are families who do not live near chemical plants.", "D": "90 percent of families living near chemical plants are approximately 2.0 percent more\nlikely to have children with leukemia than are families who do not live near chemical\nplants.", "E": "None of the above are proper conclusions."}, "image": null, "answer": "C", "explanation": "区间(−0.037,−0.003)不含0,说明住在化工厂附近的家庭孩子患白血病的比例高约2%(差异显著)。"}, {"id": 396, "chapter": "第四章 统计推断", "topic": "显著性检验逻辑", "stem": "For which of the following is it appropriate to use a t-distribution with 12 degrees of\nfreedom?\nI. Constructing a confidence interval from an SRS with n = 11.\nII. Doing a hypothesis test H0: µ1 – µ2 = 0 and Ha: µ1 – µ2 > 0, where n1 = 12 and n2 = 12.\nIII. Doing a χ2-test of independence where the contingency table has 4 rows and 3\ncolumns.", "options": {"A": "I only", "B": "II only", "C": "III only", "D": "I, II, and III", "E": "None are appropriate."}, "image": null, "answer": "E", "explanation": "I:n=11时df=10;II:两样本合并df=22(或保守11);III:独立性卡方df=(4−1)(3−1)=6——都不是12。"}, {"id": 397, "chapter": "第四章 统计推断", "topic": "显著性检验逻辑", "stem": "Are generic batteries just as good as brand-name batteries? A consumer group runs a test\non random samples of 20 brand-name alkaline AA batteries and 20 generic alkaline AA\nbatteries. The group lets all the batteries run continuously and notes the number of hours\nwhen they die. Which of the following shows the proper set of hypotheses?", "options": {"A": "H0: ¯xname = ¯xgeneric and Ha: ¯xname ≠ ¯xgeneric", "B": "H0: ¯xname = ¯xgeneric and Ha: ¯xname > ¯xgeneric", "C": "H0: ¯xname = ¯xgeneric and Ha: ¯xname < ¯xgeneric", "D": "H0: ¯xdifference = 0 and Ha: ¯xdifference ≠ 0", "E": "None of the above show the proper set of hypotheses."}, "image": null, "answer": "E", "explanation": "假设应针对总体参数(μ)而非样本统计量(x̄),前四个选项都用x̄陈述,均不当。"}, {"id": 398, "chapter": "第四章 统计推断", "topic": "显著性检验逻辑", "stem": "A driving instructor claims that the probability of passing the driving test on the first try\nafter finishing his course is 0.85. A reporter believes this claim is high and plans to survey\na random sample of people who took the instructor’s class. Assuming the sample size will\nbe large, what statistical test will be most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "z-test of a proportion", "B": "z-test for difference of two proportions", "C": "chi-square test of homogeneity of proportions", "D": "t-test of mean of a proportion", "E": "t-test for a slope where a proportion is interpreted as a slope"}, "image": null, "answer": "A", "explanation": "检验单一比例0.85、大样本,用单样本比例z检验(单侧)。"}, {"id": 399, "chapter": "第四章 统计推断", "topic": "显著性检验逻辑", "stem": "The P-value for a two-sided t-test is 0.10. If the test had been one-sided, what would the Pvalue have been?", "options": {"A": "0.05", "B": "0.10", "C": "0.20", "D": "0.95", "E": "It depends on the direction of the alternative hypothesis."}, "image": null, "answer": "A", "explanation": "若单侧方向与观测效应方向一致,单侧P值是双侧的一半:0.10/2=0.05。"}, {"id": 400, "chapter": "第四章 统计推断", "topic": "显著性检验逻辑", "stem": "Medical researchers keep trying different molecular variations of a drug. They test each\nof these variations for effectiveness in relieving pain after minor surgeries. At the 5%\nsignificance level, 2 out of 75 of these variations show some statistical significance. Which\nof the following should be concluded?", "options": {"A": "A sample size of 75 is not large enough for any serious medical conclusion.", "B": "2\n75 = 0.0267 < 0.05, so there is sufficient evidence to conclude that the drug is effective in\nrelieving pain after minor surgeries.", "C": "Although no conclusion can be made about the drug in general, positive conclusions can\nbe made about 2 of the 75 molecular variations in their effectiveness in relieving pain\nafter minor surgeries.", "D": "Pain relief is very susceptible to the placebo effect, so no conclusion is reasonable from\nthis study without knowing whether or not random assignment and blinding with one\ngroup receiving a placebo was part of the procedure.", "E": "There is insufficient evidence that any of the molecular variations are effective in\nrelieving pain after minor surgeries because at the 5% significance level, statistically\nsignificant results in 2 out of 75 tests could simply be due to chance variability."}, "image": null, "answer": "E", "explanation": "在5%水平做75次检验,即使无效也会有约3到4次假显著,2次显著可能只是随机波动,故不足以证明任何变体有效。"}, {"id": 401, "chapter": "第四章 统计推断", "topic": "显著性检验逻辑", "stem": "One of the conditions in performing a one-sample t-test is that the sample size be less\nthan 10 percent of the population size. What is the reason for this condition?", "options": {"A": "This allows for the application of the central limit theorem.", "B": "This allows for using the sample standard deviation as an approximation to the population\nstandard deviation.", "C": "This guarantees an unbiased sample.", "D": "If the sample is not too large when compared to the population, the dependence among\nobservations is negligible even though we sample without replacement.", "E": "This guarantees a large enough sample for the test procedure."}, "image": null, "answer": "D", "explanation": "10%条件是针对无放回抽样：当样本相对总体很小时，观测之间的相关性可忽略，独立性条件近似成立。"}, {"id": 402, "chapter": "第四章 统计推断", "topic": "显著性检验逻辑", "stem": "Suppose a two-sided hypothesis test results in a P-value of 0.32. If a one-sided test is\nconducted on the same data, which of the following would be true about the possible\nvalues of the resulting P-value?", "options": {"A": "The only possible value is 0.16.", "B": "The only possible value is 0.32.", "C": "The only possible value is 0.64.", "D": "The only possible values are 0.16 and 0.84.", "E": "The only possible values are 0.32 and 0.68."}, "image": null, "answer": "D", "explanation": "双侧P=0.32；同一数据做单侧检验时，若样本方向与备择一致则P减半为0.16，若方向相反则为1-0.16=0.84，故可能值为0.16或0.84。"}, {"id": 403, "chapter": "第四章 统计推断", "topic": "显著性检验逻辑", "stem": "A politician is concerned about whether he has a problem with support from women\nvoters. A large number of polls are taken by various polling organizations, each\nsurveying independent random samples of 500 male voters and 500 female voters. The\ndifference in the proportion of male support and proportion of female support is\ncalculated for each poll. If there is actually no difference in support for the politician\nbetween male and female voters, which of the following results would be anticipated?\nI. If for each poll tests are run at the 5% significance level with the hypotheses H0: pM =\npW versus H0: pM > pW, the P-value will be less than 0.05 for approximately 5 percent\nof the tests.\nII. There will be approximately equal numbers of polls showing higher support levels\nfrom males and higher support levels from females.\nIII. If for each poll 95% confidence intervals for the difference in proportions, pM – pW ,\nare calculated, approximately 95 percent of the confidence intervals will contain 0.", "options": {"A": "I only", "B": "II only", "C": "III only", "D": "I and II only", "E": "I, II, and III\nHYPOTHESIS TESTS FOR PROPORTIONS"}, "image": null, "answer": "E", "explanation": "H0为真时P值近似均匀分布，故约5%的检验P<0.05（I对）；抽样对称，两类支持更高的人数约各半（II对）；95%置信区间约95%含0（III对）。"}, {"id": 404, "chapter": "第四章 统计推断", "topic": "比例的假设检验", "stem": "A truant officer believes that the percentage of students skipping school during the World\nSeries is even greater than the previously claimed 7 percent. She conducts a hypothesis\ntest on a random 200 students and finds 23 guilty of truancy. Is this strong evidence\nagainst the 0.07 claim?", "options": {"A": "Yes, because the P-value is 0.0062", "B": "Yes, because the P-value is 2.5", "C": "No, because the P-value is only 0.0062", "D": "No, because the P-value is over 0.10", "E": "There is insufficient information to reach a conclusion."}, "image": null, "answer": "A", "explanation": "p̂=23/200=0.115，z=(0.115−0.07)/√(0.07×0.93/200)≈2.49，单侧P≈0.0062<0.05，是拒绝0.07的强证据。"}, {"id": 405, "chapter": "第四章 统计推断", "topic": "比例的假设检验", "stem": "New York State law limits the decibel level of truck horns. A major truck manufacturer\nclaims that only 35 percent of trucks on the road are even capable of a horn decibel level\nat or over the legal limit. However, a congressional investigator believes the true\npercentage is greater and runs a hypothesis test at the 5% significance level. If 57 out of a\nsimple random sample (SRS) of 150 trucks have horns capable of decibel blasts over the\nlegal limit, what is the appropriate test statistic?", "options": {"A": "z =\n0.38−0.35\n√150(0.35)(1−0.35)", "B": "z =\n0.38−0.35\n√150(0.38)(1−0.38)", "C": "z =\n0.38−0.35\n√(0.35)(1−0.35)\n150", "D": "z =\n0.38−0.35\n√(0.38)(1−0.38)\n150", "E": "z = 1.96\n0.38−0.35\n√150(0.35)(1−0.35)"}, "image": null, "answer": "C", "explanation": "检验统计量必须用原假设的p=0.35算标准误：z=(0.38−0.35)/√(0.35×0.65/150)。"}, {"id": 406, "chapter": "第四章 统计推断", "topic": "比例的假设检验", "stem": "It is claimed that 54 percent of lost remote controls are stuck between sofa cushions. A\nreporter tests this claim by checking a simple random sample (SRS) of 500 people who\nlost remote controls, and 280 of them reported finding the remote controls between sofa\ncushions. With H0: p = 0.54 and Ha: p ≠ 0.54, what is the P-value?", "options": {"A": "P(z >\n0.56−0.54\n√500(0.54)(1−0.54) )", "B": "2P(z >\n0.56−0.54\n√500(0.54)(1−0.54) )", "C": "P(z >\n0.56−0.54\n500\n)\n√(0.54)(1−0.54)", "D": "2P(z >\n0.56−0.54\n500\n)\n√(0.54)(1−0.54)", "E": "2P(z >\n0.56−0.54\n√500(0.56)(1−0.56) )"}, "image": null, "answer": "D", "explanation": "Ha为双侧(≠)，P值=2P(z>(0.56−0.54)/√(0.54×0.46/500))，分母用假设值0.54并乘2。"}, {"id": 407, "chapter": "第四章 统计推断", "topic": "比例的假设检验", "stem": "Two students using the same data perform different significant tests. The first student\nperforms the test H0: p = 0.76 with Ha: p ≠ 0.76, while the second student performs the\ntest H0: p = 0.76 with Ha: p < 0.76. Although both use the α = 0.01 level of significance, the\nfirst student claims there is not enough evidence to reject H0, while the second student\nsays there is enough evidence to reject H0. Which of the following could have been the\nvalue for the test statistic?", "options": {"A": "z = –2.6", "B": "z = –2.4", "C": "z = –0.01", "D": "z = 0.76", "E": "z = 2.6"}, "image": null, "answer": "B", "explanation": "单侧(左尾)1%拒绝需z≤−2.326；双侧不拒绝需单侧P>0.005即z>−2.576。z=−2.4（单侧P≈0.0082，双侧P≈0.0164）两者都满足。"}, {"id": 408, "chapter": "第四章 统计推断", "topic": "比例的假设检验", "stem": "A hypothesis test is being run to see if there is significant evidence that fewer than 75\npercent of elementary school children regularly eat cereal. In a random sample of 500\nelementary school children, 66 percent say they regularly eat cereal. What is the test\nstatistic for the appropriate test?", "options": {"A": "0.66−0.75\n√(0.66)(0.34)\n500", "B": "0.66−0.75\n√(0.75)(0.25)\n500", "C": "0.66−0.75\n√(0.5)(0.5)\n500", "D": "0.75−0.66\n√(0.66)(0.34)\n500", "E": "0.75−0.66\n√(0.75)(0.25)\n500"}, "image": null, "answer": "B", "explanation": "z=(0.66−0.75)/√(0.75×0.25/500)，分母必须用原假设p=0.75。"}, {"id": 409, "chapter": "第四章 统计推断", "topic": "比例的假设检验", "stem": "Five years ago, a claim stated, “22 percent of American adults are functionally illiterate.”\nFor her dissertation, a graduate student tests the hypothesis that this percent has gone\ndown, where H0: p = 0.22 and Ha: p < 0.22. An appropriate z-test gives a P-value of 0.18.\nIs there sufficient evidence at the 10% significance level to say that the proportion of\nAmerican adults who are functionally illiterate is now less than 22 percent?", "options": {"A": "Yes, because 0.18 < 0.22", "B": "Yes, because 0.18 > 0.10", "C": "Yes, because 0.10 < 0.22", "D": "No, because 0.18 < 0.22", "E": "No, because 0.18 > 0.10"}, "image": null, "answer": "E", "explanation": "P=0.18>α=0.10，不能拒绝H0，没有充分证据说明比例已低于22%。"}, {"id": 410, "chapter": "第四章 统计推断", "topic": "比例的假设检验", "stem": "A test of the hypotheses H0: p = 0.4 versus Ha: p ≠ 0.4 was conducted using a sample of\nsize n = 175. The test statistic was z = 1.15. What was the P-value of the test?", "options": {"A": "0.125", "B": "0.25", "C": "0.4", "D": "0.5", "E": "0.75"}, "image": null, "answer": "B", "explanation": "双侧P=2P(z>1.15)=2×0.1251≈0.25。"}, {"id": 411, "chapter": "第四章 统计推断", "topic": "比例的假设检验", "stem": "A survey of 850 Europeans reveals that 725 believe that Atlantic bluefin tuna are an\nendangered animal and should have protections from the fishing industry. In Japan,\nbluefin tuna are considered a sushi delicacy. In a survey of 720 Japanese, only 216 believe\nthat bluefin tuna are endangered and should be protected. To test at the 5% significance\nlevel whether or not the data are significant evidence that the proportion of Japanese who\nbelieve that tuna need protection is less than the proportion of Americans with this belief,\na student notes that 216\n720 = 0.3 and sets up the following: H0: p = 0.3 and Ha: p > 0.3,\nwhere p is the proportion of Europeans who believe that bluefin tuna need protection.\nWhich of the following is a true statement?", "options": {"A": "The student has set up a correct hypothesis test.", "B": "Given the large sample sizes, a 1% significance level would be more appropriate.", "C": "A two-sided test would be more appropriate.", "D": "Given that 725+216\n850+720 = 0.60, Ha: p > 0.60 would be more appropriate.", "E": "A two-population difference in proportions hypothesis test would be more appropriate."}, "image": null, "answer": "E", "explanation": "欧洲人和日本人是两个不同总体，应做两总体比例差的假设检验，而非以0.3为参数的单样本检验。"}, {"id": 412, "chapter": "第四章 统计推断", "topic": "比例的假设检验", "stem": "In a random survey of 500 college STEM majors, 315 said they were more interested in\nbeing challenged than in receiving high grades. In a random survey of 400 college\nbusiness majors, 220 said they were more interested in being challenged than in receiving\nhigh grades. Is there sufficient evidence to show that the proportion of STEM majors who\nare more interested in being challenged than in receiving high grades is greater than the\n⎜\n⎟\n⎜\n⎟\nproportion of business majors who are more interested in being challenged than in\nreceiving high grades?", "options": {"A": "Because 0.63 > 0.55, there is strong evidence that the proportion of STEM majors who are\nmore interested in being challenged is greater than that of business majors.", "B": "Because 0.0075 < 0.01, there is very strong evidence that the proportion of STEM majors\nwho are more interested in being challenged is greater than that of business majors.", "C": "Because 0.01 < 0.0329 < 0.05, there is strong evidence that the proportion of STEM majors\nwho are more interested in being challenged is greater than that of business majors.", "D": "There is insufficient evidence that the proportion of STEM majors who are more\ninterested in being challenged is greater than that of business majors.", "E": "There is insufficient information to determine whether the proportion of STEM majors\nwho are more interested in being challenged is greater than that of business majors."}, "image": null, "answer": "B", "explanation": "合并p̂=535/900≈0.594，z=(0.63−0.55)/√(0.594×0.406×(1/500+1/400))≈2.43，单侧P≈0.0075<0.01，证据非常强。"}, {"id": 413, "chapter": "第四章 统计推断", "topic": "比例的假设检验", "stem": "In a well-known basketball study, it was reported that Larry Bird hit a second free throw\nin 48 out of 53 attempts after the first free throw was missed and hit a second free throw\nin 251 of 285 attempts after the first free throw was made. Suppose an appropriate test is\nperformed to determine whether there is sufficient evidence to say that the probability\nthat Bird will make a second free throw is different depending on whether or not he made\nthe first free throw. What is the P-value of the appropriate test?", "options": {"A": "P(z >\n53+285 )(1−48+251\n53+285 )( 1\n53 +\n1\n285 ) )\n48\n53 −251\n285\n√( 48+251", "B": "2P(z >\n53+285 )(1−48+251\n53+285 )( 1\n53 +\n1\n285 ) )\n48\n53 −251\n285\n√( 48+251\n⎛\n⎞\n48\n53 −251\n285", "C": "P\nz >\n⎝\n⎠\n√\n48\n53 (1−48\n53 )\n251\n285 (1−251\n285 )\n53\n+\n285\n⎛\n⎞\n48\n53 −251\n285", "D": "2P\nz >\n⎝\n⎠\n√\n48\n53 (1−48\n53 )\n251\n285 (1−251\n285 )\n53\n+\n285", "E": "P(z >\n53 +\n1\n285 ) )\n48\n53 −251\n285\n√(0.5)(1−0.5)( 1"}, "image": null, "answer": "B", "explanation": "双侧两比例检验需用合并比例(48+251)/(53+285)：P=2P(z>(48/53−251/285)/√(p̄(1−p̄)(1/53+1/285)))。"}, {"id": 414, "chapter": "第四章 统计推断", "topic": "比例的假设检验", "stem": "A poll of 800 moviegoers showed 560 liked a new action film, while a poll of 600\nmoviegoers showed 450 liked the sequel. In a hypothesis test on whether a higher\nproportion of moviegoers like the sequel than like the original, what is the test statistic?", "options": {"A": "z =\n0.7−0.75\n√(0.7)(1−0.7)/800+(0.75)(1−0.75)/600", "B": "z =\n0.7−0.75\n√(0.7214)(1−0.7214)(1/800+1/600)", "C": "z =\n0.7−0.75\n√800(0.7)(1−0.7)+600(0.75)(1−0.75)", "D": "z =\n0.7−0.75\n√(0.725)(1−0.725)\n700", "E": "z =\n0.7−0.75\n√700(0.725)(1−0.725)"}, "image": null, "answer": "B", "explanation": "合并p̂=(560+450)/(800+600)=0.7214，z=(0.7−0.75)/√(0.7214×0.2786×(1/800+1/600))。"}, {"id": 415, "chapter": "第四章 统计推断", "topic": "比例的假设检验", "stem": "In independent random samples of 600 men and 500 women, 80 percent of the men and 76\npercent of the women say they are satisfied with their physical attractiveness. Is there\nsufficient evidence to say that the proportion of men who are satisfied with their physical\nattractiveness is greater than the proportion of women who are satisfied with their\nphysical attractiveness?", "options": {"A": "Yes, because the difference in sample proportions, 0.04, is less than 0.05", "B": "Yes, because the probability of observing a difference at least as large as the sample\ndifference, if the two population proportions are the same, is less than 0.05", "C": "Yes, because the probability of observing a difference at least as large as the sample\ndifference, if the two population proportions are the same, is greater than 0.05", "D": "No, because the probability of observing a difference at least as large as the sample\ndifference, if the two population proportions are the same, is less than 0.05", "E": "No, because the probability of observing a difference at least as large as the sample\ndifference, if the two population proportions are the same, is greater than 0.05"}, "image": null, "answer": "E", "explanation": "合并p̂=860/1100≈0.782，z=0.04/0.025≈1.60，单侧P≈0.055>0.05，无充分证据说明男性比例更高。"}, {"id": 416, "chapter": "第四章 统计推断", "topic": "比例的假设检验", "stem": "Are more immigrants coming to America or to Canada? In random surveys of 3,500\nAmericans and 2,800 Canadians, 525 of the Americans and 474 of the Canadians say they\nwere born in a different country. A test is conducted with H0: p1 = p2 versus Ha: p1 ≠ p2.\nWhat is the conclusion at the 5% significance level?", "options": {"A": "There is not sufficient evidence that the proportion of all Americans who say they were\nborn in another country is different from the proportion of all Canadians who say they\nwere born in another country because ˆp =\n525+474\n3,500+2,800 = 0.159 > 0.05", "B": "There is not sufficient evidence that the proportion of all Americans who say they were\nborn in another country is different from the proportion of all Canadians who say they\nwere born in another country because the P-value is greater than 0.05.", "C": "There is sufficient evidence that the proportion of all Americans who say they were born\nin another country is different from the proportion of all Canadians who say they were\nborn in another country because the P-value is positive.", "D": "There is sufficient evidence that the proportion of all Americans who say they were born\nin another country is different from the proportion of all Canadians who say they were\nborn in another country because the P-value is less than 0.05.", "E": "There is sufficient evidence that the proportion of all Americans who say they were born\nin another country is different from the proportion of all Canadians who say they were\nborn in another country because the P-value is greater than 0.05."}, "image": null, "answer": "D", "explanation": "合并p̂=999/6300，z=(0.15−0.169)/0.00926≈−2.08，双侧P≈0.037<0.05，有充分证据说明两国比例不同。"}, {"id": 417, "chapter": "第四章 统计推断", "topic": "比例的假设检验", "stem": "In a well-known study, two booths were set up, one on each side of a college campus. One\nbooth sold fine Swiss chocolates for 5 cents each and Hershey chocolate kisses for a penny\neach. The second booth sold the fine Swiss chocolates for 4 cents each and gave away the\nHershey chocolate kisses for free. Both booths limited each student to picking one piece\nof chocolate. A higher proportion of students bought the Swiss chocolates at the first\nbooth, while a higher proportion of students took the free Hershey chocolate kisses at the\nsecond booth. Which of the following test procedures should be used to test for statistical\nsignificance?", "options": {"A": "A one-sample z-test of proportions because the student body is a single population", "B": "A two-sample z-test for a difference of proportions", "C": "A one-sample t-test of the mean difference of proportions", "D": "A matched pair t-test comparing one booth to the other", "E": "A t-test for the slope of a regression line of proportions sold at one booth versus\nproportions sold at the other booth"}, "image": null, "answer": "B", "explanation": "比较两个独立售货亭（两组不同学生）购买瑞士巧克力的比例，应用两样本比例差的z检验。"}, {"id": 418, "chapter": "第四章 统计推断", "topic": "比例的假设检验", "stem": "A random sample of 120 AP students was asked if they would prefer to have review\nsessions after school rather than on Saturdays. 67 students expressed a preference for\nafter-school sessions, while 53 expressed a preference for Saturday sessions. A twosample z-test for the difference of proportions using ˆp =\n67\n120 and  ˆp =\n53\n120 results in a Pvalue of 0.035. What was the error in this analysis?", "options": {"A": "A two-sided test should have been performed rather than the one-sided test that was\nperformed.", "B": "A one-sample z-test should have been performed rather than the two-sample test.", "C": "A chi-square test of independence would have been the proper test.", "D": "It is not clear whether or not the 10% rule was violated.", "E": "There was no error."}, "image": null, "answer": "B", "explanation": "67+53=120，两组是同一样本的互补部分并不独立；正确做法是对单一样本做比例的单样本z检验（p̂=67/120）。"}, {"id": 419, "chapter": "第四章 统计推断", "topic": "比例的假设检验", "stem": "A hypothesis test comparing two population proportions results in a P-value of 0.028.\nWhich of the following is a proper conclusion?", "options": {"A": "The probability that the null hypothesis is true is 0.028.", "B": "The probability that the alternative hypothesis is true is 0.028.", "C": "The difference in sample proportions is 0.028.", "D": "The difference in population proportions is 0.028.", "E": "None of the above are proper conclusions.\nHYPOTHESIS TESTS FOR MEANS"}, "image": null, "answer": "E", "explanation": "P值既不是假设为真的概率，也不是样本或总体比例差本身，以上结论都不正确。"}, {"id": 420, "chapter": "第四章 统计推断", "topic": "均值的假设检验", "stem": "An efficiency expert is interested in comparing the mean time taken for breaks by\nemployees in areas with access to the Internet and those in areas that do not have this\naccess. She interviews a simple random sample (SRS) of 10 employees with access to the\nInternet and an SRS of 10 without access. The efficiency expert then proceeds to run a t\ntest to compare the mean time taken for breaks in each group. Which of the following is a\nnecessary assumption?", "options": {"A": "The population standard deviations from each group are known.", "B": "The population standard deviations from each group are unknown.", "C": "The population standard deviations from each group are equal.", "D": "The population of break times from each group is normally distributed.", "E": "The samples must be independent samples and for each sample np and n(1 – p), must both\nbe at least 10."}, "image": null, "answer": "D", "explanation": "每组n=10样本较小，两样本t检验需要两组 break times 总体近似正态分布。"}, {"id": 421, "chapter": "第四章 统计推断", "topic": "均值的假设检验", "stem": "A high school coach claims that the average pulse rate of those trying out for sports is\n62.4 beats per minute (bpm). The AP Statistics instructor suspects this is a made-up\nnumber and runs a hypothesis test on a simple random sample (SRS) of 32 students\ntrying out for sports, calculating a mean of 65.0 bpm with a standard deviation of 10.3\nbpm. What is the P-value?", "options": {"A": "(z > 65.0−62.4\n√32 ) )\n( 10.3", "B": "2P(z > 65.0−62.4\n√32 ) )\n( 10.3", "C": "P(t > 65.0−62.4\n√32 ) ) with df = 31\n( 10.3", "D": "2P(t > 65.0−62.4\n√32 ) ) with df = 31\n( 10.3", "E": "P(t > 65.0−62.4\n10.3\n) with df = 32"}, "image": null, "answer": "D", "explanation": "教练“怀疑是编造的数字”为双侧检验：P=2P(t>(65.0−62.4)/(10.3/√32))，自由度df=n−1=31。"}, {"id": 422, "chapter": "第四章 统计推断", "topic": "均值的假设检验", "stem": "In a one-sided hypothesis test for the mean for a random sample of size 20, the t-score of\nthe sample mean is 2.615 in the direction of the alternative. Is this significant at the 5%\nlevel? At the 1% level?", "options": {"A": "Significant at the 1% level but not at the 5% level", "B": "Significant at the 5% level but not at the 1% level", "C": "Significant at both the 1% and 5% levels", "D": "Significant at neither the 1% nor 5% levels", "E": "Cannot be determined from the given information"}, "image": null, "answer": "C", "explanation": "df=19单侧临界值t.01=2.539、t.05=1.729，2.615>2.539，故在1%和5%水平都显著。"}, {"id": 423, "chapter": "第四章 统计推断", "topic": "均值的假设检验", "stem": "A confidence interval estimate is determined from the summer earnings of a simple\nrandom sample (SRS) of n students. All other things being equal, which of the following\nresults in a larger margin of error?", "options": {"A": "A lesser confidence level", "B": "A smaller sample standard deviation", "C": "A smaller sample size", "D": "Introducing bias into the sampling", "E": "Introducing blinding into an experiment"}, "image": null, "answer": "C", "explanation": "边际误差=临界值×s/√n；置信度减小或s减小都会使其变小，只有样本量变小会使边际误差变大。"}, {"id": 424, "chapter": "第四章 统计推断", "topic": "均值的假设检验", "stem": "A spokesperson for the National Council of Teachers of Mathematics (NCTM) states that\nmiddle school math teachers spend an average of $1,250 per year of their own funds on\nclassroom materials. A member of a Board of Education believes that the real figure is\nlower. So he interviews 12 randomly chosen middle school math teachers and comes up\nwith a mean of $1,092 and a standard deviation of $308 spent out of pocket by middle\nschool math teachers during the past academic year for classroom materials. Where is\nthe P-value?", "options": {"A": "Below 0.01", "B": "Between 0.01 and 0.025", "C": "Between 0.025 and 0.05", "D": "Between 0.05 and 0.10", "E": "Over 0.10"}, "image": null, "answer": "D", "explanation": "t=(1092−1250)/(308/√12)≈−1.78，df=11单侧(左尾)，t.05(11)=1.796略大于1.78，P介于0.05与0.10之间。"}, {"id": 425, "chapter": "第四章 统计推断", "topic": "均值的假设检验", "stem": "A critic of public education claims that during their four years of high school, students\nread an average of only 38 books. An English teacher plans to sample 100 high school\ngraduates randomly as to the number of books read during their high school years and\nwill reject the critic’s claim if the sample mean exceeds 40 books. If the critic’s claim is\nwrong and the true mean is 43 books, what is the probability that the random sample will\nlead to a mistaken failure to reject the critic’s claim? Assume that the standard deviation\nin number of books read from the sample is 12 books.", "options": {"A": "P(t <\n43−38\n(\n12\n√100 ) )", "B": "P(t >\n40−38\n(\n12\n√100 ) )", "C": "P(t <\n40−43\n(\n12\n√100 ) )", "D": "P(t >\n40−43\n(\n12\n√100 ) )", "E": "P(t >\n43−38\n(\n12\n√100 ) )\nA dietician claims that a new weight loss program will result in an average loss of 9\npounds in the first month. The program developer believes that the average weight loss in\nthe first month will be greater than this. The program developer runs a test on a random\nsample of 64 overweight volunteers. What conclusion is reached if the sample mean loss\nis 9.55 pounds with a standard deviation of 3.00 pounds?"}, "image": null, "answer": "C", "explanation": "第二类错误概率=P(未拒绝H0|μ=43)=P(x̄<40|μ=43)=P(t<(40−43)/(12/√100))。"}, {"id": 426, "chapter": "第四章 统计推断", "topic": "均值的假设检验", "stem": "", "options": {"A": "The P-value is less than 0.001, indicating very strong evidence against the 9-pound claim.", "B": "The P-value is 0.01, indicating strong evidence against the 9-pound claim.", "C": "The P-value is 0.07, indicating some evidence against the 9-pound claim.", "D": "The P-value is 0.18, indicating very little evidence against the 9-pound claim.", "E": "The P-value is 0.43, indicating no evidence against the 9-pound claim."}, "image": null, "answer": "C", "explanation": "t=(9.55−9)/(3/√64)=1.47，df=63单侧P≈0.07，有一定证据但未达0.05。"}, {"id": 427, "chapter": "第四章 统计推断", "topic": "均值的假设检验", "stem": "According to Major League Baseball (MLB) official rules, the average weight of\nmanufactured baseballs should be 5.25 ounces. An MLB inspector weighs a simple\nrandom sample (SRS) of five baseballs, obtaining weights of 5.3, 5.15, 5.15, 5.2, and 5.25\nounces. The inspector then runs an appropriate hypothesis test. Which of the following\ngives the P-value of this test?", "options": {"A": "P(t < 5.21−5.25\n√5 ) ) with df = 4\n( 0.0652", "B": "2P(t < 5.21−5.25\n√5 ) ) with df = 4\n( 0.0652", "C": "P(t < 5.21−5.25\n√5 ) ) with df = 5\n( 0.0652", "D": "2P(t < 5.21−5.25\n√5 ) ) with df = 5\n( 0.0652", "E": "0.5P(t < 5.21−5.25\n√5 ) ) with df = 5\n( 0.0652"}, "image": null, "answer": "B", "explanation": "x̄=5.21，s=0.0652，检验“重量是否不同于5.25”为双侧：P=2P(t<(5.21−5.25)/(0.0652/√5))，df=4。"}, {"id": 428, "chapter": "第四章 统计推断", "topic": "均值的假设检验", "stem": "An editor for a college magazine claims that the mean cost for 4-year tuition at private\ncolleges is $258,000. A high school guidance counselor checks a random sample of 10\nstudents who attended private colleges for four years and found the mean cost for tuition\nalone was $245,000 with a standard deviation of $38,000. What is the test statistic in\nperforming a hypothesis test of H0: µ = 258,000 and Ha: µ ≠ 258,000?", "options": {"A": "t = 245,000−258,000\n√38,000\n10−1", "B": "t = 2 245,000−258,000\n√38,000\n10", "C": "t = 2 245,000−258,000\n( 38,000\n√10−1 )", "D": "t = 245,000−258,000\n( 38,000\n√10 )", "E": "t = 245,000−258,000\n( 38,000\n√10 )"}, "image": null, "answer": "D", "explanation": "单样本t统计量=t=(245000−258000)/(38000/√10)。"}, {"id": 429, "chapter": "第四章 统计推断", "topic": "均值的假设检验", "stem": "In testing the null hypothesis H0: µ = 51 against the alternative hypothesis H0: µ > 51, a\nsample from a normal population has a mean of 52.1 with a corresponding t-score of 2.2\nand a P-value of 0.0377. Which of the following is not a reasonable conclusion?", "options": {"A": "If the null hypothesis is assumed to be true, the probability of obtaining a sample mean as\nextreme as or more extreme than 52.1 is only 0.0377.", "B": "In all samples using the same sample size and the same sampling technique, the null\nhypothesis will be wrong 3.77 percent of the time.", "C": "There is sufficient evidence to reject the null hypothesis at the 5% significance level.", "D": "If the population standard deviation s had been known, the z-score of 2.2 would have\nresulted in a smaller P-value.", "E": "If the alternative hypothesis had been two-sided, the same t-score would have resulted in a\nP-value of 0.0754."}, "image": null, "answer": "B", "explanation": "“在所有样本中原假设会有3.77%的时间是错的”是对P值的错误解读；A、C、D、E的说法均合理。"}, {"id": 430, "chapter": "第四章 统计推断", "topic": "均值的假设检验", "stem": "A cell phone salesperson claims that the average teen processes 3,700 texts per month. A\nteacher believes this claim is high and randomly samples 30 teens. What conclusion is\nreached if the sample mean is 3,490 with a standard deviation of 1,120?", "options": {"A": "There is sufficient evidence to prove the salesperson’s claim is true.", "B": "There is sufficient evidence to prove the salesperson’s claim is false.", "C": "The teacher has sufficient evidence to reject the salesperson’s claim.", "D": "The teacher does not have sufficient evidence to reject the salesperson’s claim.", "E": "There are not sufficient data to reach any conclusion."}, "image": null, "answer": "D", "explanation": "t=(3490−3700)/(1120/√30)≈−1.03，df=29，P≈0.15>0.05，教师没有足够证据拒绝销售员的说法。"}, {"id": 431, "chapter": "第四章 统计推断", "topic": "均值的假设检验", "stem": "The claim is made that the students at Lake Wobegon High School are smarter than other\nhigh school students. To test this, the principal gathers a simple random sample (SRS) of\n30 students and finds their mean IQ is 104 with a standard deviation of 7.5. What is the\ntest statistic in performing a hypothesis test of H0: µ = 100 and Ha: µ > 100 (average IQ is\n100)?", "options": {"A": "t = 104−100\n7.5", "B": "t = 104−100\n√7.5\n30", "C": "t = 104−100\n√7.5\n29", "D": "t = 104−100\n( 7.5\n√30 )", "E": "t = 104−100\n( 7.5\n√29 )"}, "image": null, "answer": "D", "explanation": "t=(104−100)/(7.5/√30)，标准误为s/√n。"}, {"id": 432, "chapter": "第四章 统计推断", "topic": "均值的假设检验", "stem": "A t-test is being conducted at the 5% significance level with H0: µ = 20 and Ha: µ ≠ 20. If\na 95 percent t-interval constructed from the same data set contains the value 20, which of\nthe following can be concluded about the t-test?", "options": {"A": "The P-value is less than 0.05, and there is sufficient evidence to reject H0.", "B": "The P-value is greater than 0.05, and there is sufficient evidence to reject H0.", "C": "The P-value is less than 0.05, and there is not sufficient evidence to reject H0.", "D": "The P-value is greater than 0.05, and there is not sufficient evidence to reject H0.", "E": "Without knowing the sample standard deviation, none of the above can be concluded."}, "image": null, "answer": "D", "explanation": "95%置信区间包含20等价于双侧5%检验不拒绝H0：P>0.05，无充分证据拒绝H0。"}, {"id": 433, "chapter": "第四章 统计推断", "topic": "均值的假设检验", "stem": "A test of the hypotheses H0: µ = 10 versus Ha: µ ≠ 10 was conducted using a sample of size\nn = 6. The test statistic was t = 1.641. What was the P-value of the test?", "options": {"A": "0.0760", "B": "0.0809", "C": "0.1008", "D": "0.1519", "E": "0.1617"}, "image": null, "answer": "E", "explanation": "df=5的双侧检验：P=2P(t>1.641)≈2×0.0809≈0.1617。"}, {"id": 434, "chapter": "第四章 统计推断", "topic": "均值的假设检验", "stem": "Suppose in a small town with a single Walmart, it is known that customers spend an\naverage of $75 per visit. A new Target is opening, and its sales manager believes that the\nnew store is taking in a higher mean amount per customer visit than Walmart. To test the\nTarget manager’s belief, which hypotheses should be used?", "options": {"A": "H0: the mean amount spent at Target is equal to $75\nHa: the mean amount spent at Target is less than $75", "B": "H0: the mean amount spent at Target is equal to $75\nHa: the mean amount spent at Target is greater than $75", "C": "H0: the mean amount spent at Target is less than $75\nHa: the mean amount spent at Target is greater than $75", "D": "H0: the mean amount spent at Walmart is equal to $75\nHa: the mean amount spent at Walmart is less than $75", "E": "H0: the mean amount spent at Walmart is equal to $75\nHa: the mean amount spent at Walmart is greater than $75\nA spokesperson for the airline industry states that the mean number of pieces of lost\nluggage per 1,000 passengers is 3.09. A consumer agency believes the true figure is higher\nand runs an appropriate hypothesis test that results in a P-value of 0.075. What is an\nappropriate conclusion?"}, "image": null, "answer": "B", "explanation": "经理相信Target均值更高，故H0: μ_Target=75，Ha: μ_Target>75（原假设含等号）。"}, {"id": 435, "chapter": "第四章 统计推断", "topic": "均值的假设检验", "stem": "", "options": {"A": "Because 0.075 > 0.05, there is sufficient evidence at the 5% significance level to conclude\nthat the mean number of pieces of lost luggage per 1,000 passengers is greater than 3.09.", "B": "Because 0.075 > 0.05, there is sufficient evidence at the 5% significance level to conclude\nthat the mean number of pieces of lost luggage per 1,000 passengers is less than 3.09.", "C": "Because 0.075 < 0.10, there is sufficient evidence at the 10% significance level to\nconclude that the mean number of pieces of lost luggage per 1,000 passengers is less than\n3.09.", "D": "Because 0.075 < 0.10, there is not sufficient evidence at the 10% significance level to\nconclude that the mean number of pieces of lost luggage per 1,000 passengers is greater\nthan 3.09.", "E": "Because 0.05 < 0.075 < 0.10, there is sufficient evidence at the 10% significance level but\nnot at the 5% significance level to conclude that the mean number of pieces of lost\nluggage per 1,000 passengers is greater than 3.09."}, "image": null, "answer": "E", "explanation": "0.075>0.05在5%水平不显著；0.075<0.10在10%水平显著，且方向是“大于3.09”。"}, {"id": 436, "chapter": "第四章 统计推断", "topic": "均值的假设检验", "stem": "A researcher believes that a new diet should improve weight gain in laboratory mice. The\naverage weight gain for 18 mice on the new diet is 4.2 ounces with a standard deviation of\n0.4 ounces. The average weight gain for 16 control mice on the old diet is 3.8 ounces with\na standard deviation of 0.3 ounces. Where is the P-value?", "options": {"A": "Below 0.01", "B": "Between 0.01 and 0.025", "C": "Between 0.025 and 0.05", "D": "Between 0.05 and 0.10", "E": "Over 0.10"}, "image": null, "answer": "A", "explanation": "t=(4.2−3.8)/√(0.4²/18+0.3²/16)≈3.32，df≈31，单侧P≈0.001<0.01。"}, {"id": 437, "chapter": "第四章 统计推断", "topic": "均值的假设检验", "stem": "A teacher is interested in comparing the mean writing speeds of teenagers using cursive\nversus those printing. A simple random sample (SRS) of 50 students is chosen. The\nstudents are timed copying the same paragraph, once in cursive and once in printing. For\neach student, a coin flip decides which method he/she must use first. Which of the\nfollowing is a proper test?", "options": {"A": "Test of difference in two population means", "B": "Test of difference in two population proportions", "C": "One-sample test on differences of paired data", "D": "Chi-square goodness-of-fit test", "E": "Chi-square test for homogeneity"}, "image": null, "answer": "C", "explanation": "每个学生用两种方法各测一次（配对数据），应对差值做单样本配对t检验。"}, {"id": 438, "chapter": "第四章 统计推断", "topic": "均值的假设检验", "stem": "An entomologist believes that a certain species of large beetles decreased in size between\ntwo time periods. He plans to sample the remains of 45 fossilized beetles randomly from\neach period and reject any equality claim if the mean size in the second time period\nsample is at least 1 centimeter less than the mean size from the first period sample. If the\nstandard deviation in size in all time periods for this species of beetle is known to be 1.8\ncentimeters, what is the probability the anthropologist will commit a Type I error and\nmistakenly reject a correct null hypothesis of equality?", "options": {"A": "P(z >\n1−0\n45 + 1.82\n45\n)\n√1.82", "B": "P(t >\n1−0\n45 + 1.82\n45\n) with df = 44\n√1.82", "C": "P(t >\n1−0\n45 + 1.82\n45\n) with df = 88\n√1.82", "D": "P(z >\n1−0\n√45 ) )\n( 1.8", "E": "P(t >\n1−0\n( 1.8\n√45 ) ) with df = 44"}, "image": null, "answer": "A", "explanation": "σ=1.8已知用z：α=P(x̄1−x̄2>1|μ1=μ2)=P(z>1/√(1.8²/45+1.8²/45))≈P(z>2.64)≈0.004。"}, {"id": 439, "chapter": "第四章 统计推断", "topic": "均值的假设检验", "stem": "College students majoring in computer science and interested in pursuing careers in\ncomputer security were randomly put into either Python or PowerShell classes. In a\nstandard security certification exam, 230 students who took Python scored an average of\n76.5 with a standard deviation of 7.4, while 185 students who took PowerShell averaged\n78.8 with a standard deviation of 5.6. Is there sufficient evidence of a difference in the test\nresults? What is the test statistic for H0: µ1 – µ2 = 0 and Ha: µ1 – µ2 ≠ 0?", "options": {"A": "t =\n76.5−78.8\n√(7.4)2\n230 + (5.6)2\n185", "B": "t = 2\n76.5−78.8\n√(7.4)2\n230 + (5.6)2\n185", "C": "t =\n76.5−78.8\n(\n7.4\n√230 +\n5.6\n√185 )", "D": "t = 2\n76.5−78.8\n(\n7.4\n√230 +\n5.6\n√185 )", "E": "t = 76.5−78.8\n(\n6.5\n√207.5 )"}, "image": null, "answer": "A", "explanation": "两样本t统计量=t=(76.5−78.8)/√(7.4²/230+5.6²/185)。"}, {"id": 440, "chapter": "第四章 统计推断", "topic": "均值的假设检验", "stem": "To test whether husbands or wives have greater manual agility, a simple random sample\n(SRS) of 75 married couples is chosen. All 150 people are given the Illinois Agility Run\nTest, a commonly used fitness test of agility, and their scores are recorded. What is the\nproper conclusion at a 5% significance level if a two-sample hypothesis test, H0: µ1 – µ2 =\n0, Ha: µ1 – µ2 ≠ 0, results in a P-value of 0.038?", "options": {"A": "The observed difference between husbands and wives is significant.", "B": "The observed difference is not significant.", "C": "A conclusion is not possible without knowing the mean scores of the husbands and of the\nwives.", "D": "A conclusion is not possible without knowing both the mean and standard deviation of the\nscores of the husbands and of the wives.", "E": "A two-sample hypothesis test should not be used in this example."}, "image": null, "answer": "E", "explanation": "75对夫妻的数据是天然配对的（每对两人），不独立，应使用配对t检验而非两样本检验。"}, {"id": 441, "chapter": "第四章 统计推断", "topic": "均值的假设检验", "stem": "A random sample of college students tries out two different strategies of card counting at\nblackjack. The data give the following table.\nStrategy\nSample Size\nMean Winnings\nStandard Deviation\nA\n20\n131\n12\nB\n15\n140\n15\nAssuming all conditions of inference are met, and performing a two-sample t-test with\nH0: µ1 – µ2 = 0, is there evidence of a significant difference in outcomes at the 5%\nsignificance level?", "options": {"A": "P < 0.05, so reject H0", "B": "P < 0.05, so fail to reject H0", "C": "P > 0.05, so reject H0", "D": "P > 0.05, so fail to reject H0", "E": "The 5% significance level is inappropriate for gambling experiments."}, "image": "img/q441.png", "answer": "D", "explanation": "t=(131−140)/√(144/20+225/15)≈−1.91，df≈26，双侧P≈0.067>0.05，不拒绝H0，无显著差异。"}, {"id": 442, "chapter": "第四章 统计推断", "topic": "均值的假设检验", "stem": "To compare online prices between Amazon and Walmart, a consumer-oriented research\norganization picks 50 basic items and checks the prices of these items online at Amazon\nand Walmart. Which test should be used to determine if the prices are different at the\ntwo sites?", "options": {"A": "χ2-test for goodness-of-fit", "B": "χ2-test for independence", "C": "Two-sample z-test", "D": "Two-sample t-test", "E": "Matched pairs t-test"}, "image": null, "answer": "E", "explanation": "同一50件商品在两个网站各查一次价格，属配对设计，应对价格差做配对t检验。"}, {"id": 443, "chapter": "第四章 统计推断", "topic": "均值的假设检验", "stem": "The prescription drugs Coumadin and Plavix are both blood thinners known to increase\nclotting time. In one double-blind study, Coumadin outperformed Plavix. The 95%\nconfidence interval estimate of the difference in mean clotting time (prothrombin time)\nincrease was (2.1, 3.7) seconds. Which of the following is a reasonable conclusion?", "options": {"A": "Plavix raises clotting time an average of 2.1 seconds, while Coumadin raises clotting time\nan average of 3.7 seconds.", "B": "There is a 0.95 probability that Coumadin will outperform Plavix in raising clotting time\nfor any given individual.", "C": "There is a 0.95 probability that Coumadin will outperform Plavix by at least 2.1 seconds in\nraising the clotting time for any given individual.", "D": "We should be 95% confident that Coumadin will outperform Plavix as the drug that raises\na clotting time.", "E": "None of the above."}, "image": null, "answer": "D", "explanation": "差的置信区间(2.1,3.7)完全为正，故有95%的置信认为Coumadin比Plavix更能提高凝血时间；A、B、C都对置信区间做了错误解读。"}, {"id": 444, "chapter": "第四章 统计推断", "topic": "均值的假设检验", "stem": "Material thickness plays a vital part in the quality of soccer balls. Researchers plan to\ntest two layers versus five layers of lining between the cover and the bladder of soccer\nballs. Two identical-looking soccer balls, one with two internal layers and the other with\nfive, are each kicked and given scores ranging from 1–10 by 12 professional soccer\nplayers. A score of 10 is given for a top-quality feel to the ball. A score of 1 is given for a\npoor-quality feel to the ball. For each player, a coin flip decides which ball is to be kicked\nfirst. What type of study and what type of inference test are appropriate here?", "options": {"A": "Observational study and t-test of difference of means", "B": "Matched pairs design and one-sample t-test for a mean difference", "C": "Matched pairs design and two-sample t-test for difference of population means", "D": "Completely randomized design and one-sample t-test for a mean difference", "E": "Completely randomized design and two-sample t-test for difference of population means"}, "image": null, "answer": "B", "explanation": "每个球员踢两种球、掷硬币决定先后，是配对（区组）设计，推断用单样本均值差的配对t检验。"}, {"id": 445, "chapter": "第四章 统计推断", "topic": "均值的假设检验", "stem": "An experiment was performed to determine whether a waitress would receive larger tips\nif she gave her name when greeting customers. A sample of waitresses was randomly\nassigned to two groups. One group was instructed to give their names when greeting\ncustomers, while the other group was instructed not to give their names. After one week,\nthe mean tip received for each group was calculated. The P-value of this one-sided test\nwas 0.065. Consider the three factors: original sample size, two sample standard\ndeviations, and magnitude of the difference of the two sample means. Which of the\nfollowing would have resulted in a smaller P-value?", "options": {"A": "The magnitude of the difference of the two sample means and the two sample standard\ndeviations remains the same, but the original sample size is smaller.", "B": "The original sample size remains the same, but the magnitude of the difference of the two\nsample means is larger and the two sample standard deviations are smaller.", "C": "The original sample size and the magnitude of the difference of the two sample means\nremain the same, but the two sample standard deviations are larger.", "D": "The original sample size and the two sample standard deviations remain the same, but the\nmagnitude of the difference of the two sample means is smaller.", "E": "The original sample size remains the same, but the magnitude of the difference of the two\nsample means is smaller and the two sample standard deviations are larger."}, "image": null, "answer": "B", "explanation": "t=均值差/SE；均值差更大、标准差更小都使|t|增大、P值变小（样本量不变）。"}, {"id": 446, "chapter": "第四章 统计推断", "topic": "均值的假设检验", "stem": "In which of the following is a matched pairs t-test not appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Heights of twins for 100 randomly selected pairs of twins", "B": "Heights of 100 randomly selected children when they are age 10 and again when they are\nage 11", "C": "Heights of both spouses of 100 randomly selected married couples", "D": "Heights of both people in pairs formed by 100 randomly selected people", "E": "Heights of 100 randomly selected children from England and from France in pairs by\nmatching family income."}, "image": null, "answer": "D", "explanation": "随机凑成的人对之间没有自然联系，配对无意义，配对t检验不适用；其余情形都有天然或有意配对。"}, {"id": 447, "chapter": "第四章 统计推断", "topic": "均值的假设检验", "stem": "A doctors’ association gathers data on weights from a random sample of elementary\nschool children who are allowed to watch TV all week and from an independent random\nsample of elementary school children who are allowed to watch TV only on weekends.\nThe data are summarized in the table below.\nTV All Week\nLimited TV\nNumber in Sample\n175\n160\nMean Weight\n75.4\n71.3\nStandard Deviation\n9.8\n8.2\nWhat is the t-statistic for an appropriate test of whether elementary school children who\nwatch TV all week have a higher mean weight than elementary school children who watch\nTV only on weekends: H0: µ1 – µ2 = 0 and Ha: µ1 – µ2 > 0?", "options": {"A": "t =\n75.4−71.3\n(\n9.8\n√175 +\n8.2\n√160 )", "B": "t =\n9.8−8.2\n√75.42\n175 + 71.32\n160", "C": "t =\n9.8−8.2\n√9.82\n175 + 8.22\n160", "D": "t =\n75.4−71.3\n√9.82\n175 + 8.22\n160", "E": "t =\n75.4−71.3\n√9.82+8.22\n175+160"}, "image": "img/q447.png", "answer": "D", "explanation": "两样本t统计量=t=(75.4−71.3)/√(9.8²/175+8.2²/160)。"}, {"id": 448, "chapter": "第四章 统计推断", "topic": "均值的假设检验", "stem": "A sports equipment manufacturer is considering producing one of two new golf tees. The\nmanufacturer chooses 60 sports stores. The manufacturer randomly assigns 30 stores to\nsell a prototype of one of the tees and the other 30 stores to sell a prototype of the other\ntee. After a period of time, the mean number of sales in each group of 30 stores is\ncalculated. A statistician concludes that the difference in means is statistically significant.\nWhich of the following is the appropriate conclusion?", "options": {"A": "It is reasonable to conclude that the difference in sales is caused by the difference in tees\nbecause the tees were randomly assigned to stores.", "B": "It is reasonable to conclude that the difference in sales is caused by the difference in tees\nbecause the sample size was ≥30.", "C": "It is not reasonable to conclude that the difference in sales is caused by the difference in\ntees because the 60 stores were not randomly selected.", "D": "It is not reasonable to conclude that the difference in sales is caused by the difference in\ntees because there was no control group for comparison.", "E": "It is not reasonable to conclude that the difference in sales is caused by the difference in\ntees because this was an observational study, not an experiment."}, "image": null, "answer": "A", "explanation": "发球座被随机分配到各店（实验+随机分配），可以合理推断销售差异由发球座引起；因果推断不要求随机抽样。"}, {"id": 449, "chapter": "第四章 统计推断", "topic": "均值的假设检验", "stem": "A car simulator was used to compare the effect on reaction time between DWI (driving\nwhile intoxicated) and DWT (driving while texting). A total of 20 volunteers were\ninstructed to drive at 60 mph and then hit the brakes in response to the sudden image of a\nchild darting into the road. One day, each driver was tested for stopping distance while\ndriving while texting. Another day, each driver was tested after consuming a quantity of\nalcohol. For each driver, which test was done on the first day was decided by a coin toss.\nWhich of the following is an appropriate test and hypotheses to determine if there is a\ndifference in stopping distances between DWI and DWT?", "options": {"A": "A two-sample t-test with H0: μDWI = μDWT and Ha: μDWI ≠ μDWT", "B": "A two-sample t-test with H0: μDWI ≠ μDWT and Ha: μDWI = μDWT", "C": "A two-sample t-test with H0: μDWI = μDWT and Ha: μDWI > μDWT", "D": "A matched pairs t-test with H0: μDiff = 0 and Ha: μDiff ≠ 0", "E": "A matched pairs t-test with H0: μDiff = 0 and Ha: μDiff > 0"}, "image": null, "answer": "D", "explanation": "同一20名司机在DWI和DWT两种条件下各测一次（掷硬币定顺序），用配对t检验；问“是否有差异”用双侧Ha: μDiff≠0。"}, {"id": 450, "chapter": "第四章 统计推断", "topic": "均值的假设检验", "stem": "A sports statistician is interested in whether the mean number of home runs hit in\nAmerican League stadiums (with designated hitters batting instead of pitchers) is greater\nthan the mean number hit in National League stadiums (where pitchers do bat). A\nrandom sample of games is analyzed as to the number of home runs. Computer output\nfor a random sample of American and National League games is as follows.\nDo the confidence interval and t-test lead to the same conclusion, and was this expected?", "options": {"A": "With 0.0386 < 0.05 and the entire confidence interval > 0, both lead to the conclusion that\nthere is sufficient evidence that the mean number of home runs hit in American League\ngames is greater than the mean number hit in National League games. It should be\nexpected that both lead to the same conclusion.", "B": "With 0.0386 < 0.05 and the entire confidence interval > 0, both lead to the conclusion that\nthere is sufficient evidence that the mean number of home runs hit in American League\ngames is greater than the mean number hit in National League games. It is possible that\nthis confidence interval and t-test contradict each other.", "C": "With 0.0086 < 0.0386 < 0.2374, both lead to the conclusion that there is sufficient\nevidence that the mean number of home runs hit in American League games is greater\nthan the mean number hit in National League games. It should be expected that both lead\nto the same conclusion.", "D": "With 0.0386 < 0.05 and 0.05 < 0.2374, they lead to opposite conclusions, with the t-test\nshowing a significant difference and the confidence interval not. This is not unexpected\nbecause these are two different approaches.", "E": "With 0.0386 < 0.05 and 0.05 < 0.2374, they lead to opposite conclusions with the t-test\nshowing a significant difference and the confidence interval not. This is unexpected as\nonly one conclusion at the given significance level is possible."}, "image": "img/q450.png", "answer": "A", "explanation": "P=0.0386<0.05且95%CI(0.0086,0.2374)不含0，都表明AL均值显著更高；双侧检验与95%置信区间本应给出一致结论。"}, {"id": 451, "chapter": "第四章 统计推断", "topic": "均值的假设检验", "stem": "In an often-quoted study reported in the British Medical Journal, researchers examined\nthe number of people admitted to the ER for auto accidents on Friday the 6th of several\nmonths versus on Friday the 13th of several months. Is there evidence that more people\nare admitted, on average, on Friday the 13th? Some data are summarized below.\nNumber of ER Admissions from Auto Accidents\nOct\n1989\nJul\n1990\nSep\n1991\nDec\n1991\nMar\n1992\nNov\n1992\nFriday 6th\n9\n6\n11\n11\n3\n5\nFriday\n13th\n13\n12\n14\n10\n4\n12\nLet µ1 = the mean number of ER admissions from auto accidents on all Fridays that fall\non the 6th\nLet µ2 = the mean number of ER admissions from auto accidents on all Fridays that fall\non the 13th\nLet µD = the mean of the differences (the 6th minus the 13th) in the number of ER\nadmissions from auto accidents on all Fridays that fall on the 6th and 13th.\nWhat is the appropriate test and alternative hypothesis?", "options": {"A": "Two-sample t-test with Ha: μ1 ≠ μ2", "B": "Two-sample t-test with Ha: μ1 < μ2", "C": "Two-sample t-test with Ha: μ1 > μ2", "D": "Match paired t-test with Ha: μD < 0", "E": "Match paired t-test with Ha: μD > 0"}, "image": "img/q451.png", "answer": "D", "explanation": "同一月份的两个周五天然配对；若13日入院更多，则差值(6日−13日)的均值μD<0，故配对t检验且Ha: μD<0。"}, {"id": 452, "chapter": "第四章 统计推断", "topic": "均值的假设检验", "stem": "A study compared the math scores on a standardized test for homeschooled and publicschooled high school students. The scores of the eight students in each group are shown in\nthe following table.\nHome-Schooled\n78\n65\n70\n83\n96\n66\n70\n81\nPublic-Schooled\n75\n74\n78\n89\n91\n70\n75\n89\nAssume the groups were simple random samples and all conditions for inference were\nmet. What is the appropriate test to determine if there is a significant difference in the\naverage test scores of the two groups?", "options": {"A": "Matched pair t-test for means", "B": "Two-sample t-test for means", "C": "Chi-square test of independence", "D": "Chi-square goodness-of-fit test", "E": "Linear regression t-test\nTYPE I AND II ERRORS, POWER"}, "image": "img/q452.png", "answer": "B", "explanation": "在家教育与公立学校两组独立样本比较平均分，用两样本t检验（不是配对，学生不同）。"}, {"id": 453, "chapter": "第四章 统计推断", "topic": "第一/二类错误与功效", "stem": "An industry spokesperson claims that the mean income of entry-level employees is\n$29,500 with a standard deviation of $2,500. A reporter plans to test this claim through\ninterviews with a random sample of 40 entry-level employees. If the reporter finds a\nsample mean more than $500 less than the claimed $29,500, she will dispute the\nspokesperson’s claim. What is the probability that the reporter will commit a Type I\nerror?", "options": {"A": "P(t < 29,000−29,500\n√40 )\n) with df = 40 −1\n( 2,500", "B": "P(z < 29,000−29,500\n√40 )\n)\n( 2,500", "C": "P(t < 29,000−29,500\n2,500\n) with df = 40 −1", "D": "P(z < 29,000−29,500\n2,500\n)", "E": "This cannot be calculated without knowing the sample standard deviation."}, "image": null, "answer": "B", "explanation": "σ=2500已知用z：α=P(x̄<29000|μ=29500)=P(z<(29000−29500)/(2500/√40))≈P(z<−1.26)≈0.10。"}, {"id": 454, "chapter": "第四章 统计推断", "topic": "第一/二类错误与功效", "stem": "When leaving for school on an overcast morning, you make a judgment on the null\nhypothesis: The weather will remain dry. What would the results be of Type I and Type II\nerrors?", "options": {"A": "Type I error: carry an umbrella and it rains\nType II error: carry no umbrella, but weather remains dry", "B": "Type I error: get drenched\nType II error: carry no umbrella, but weather remains dry", "C": "Type I error: get drenched\nType II error: carry an umbrella, and it rains.", "D": "Type I error: get drenched\nType II error: needlessly carry around an umbrella", "E": "Type I error: needlessly carry around an umbrella\nType II error: get drenched"}, "image": null, "answer": "E", "explanation": "H0=天将保持干燥：第一类错误=天晴却拒绝（白白带伞）；第二类错误=实际下雨却未拒绝（没带伞被淋湿）。"}, {"id": 455, "chapter": "第四章 统计推断", "topic": "第一/二类错误与功效", "stem": "Consider a hypothesis test with H0: µ = 127 and Ha: µ < 127. Which of the following\nchoices of significance level and sample size results in the greatest power of the test when\nµ = 125?", "options": {"A": "α = 0.05, n = 30", "B": "α = 0.01, n = 30", "C": "α = 0.05, n = 45", "D": "α = 0.01, n = 45", "E": "There is no way of answering without knowing the strength of the given power."}, "image": null, "answer": "C", "explanation": "显著性水平α越大、样本量n越大，功效越大；故α=0.05且n=45功效最大。"}, {"id": 456, "chapter": "第四章 统计推断", "topic": "第一/二类错误与功效", "stem": "An assembly line machine is supposed to turn out fidget spinners with spin times of 4\nminutes. Each day, a simple random sample (SRS) of five fidget spinners are pulled and\ntheir spin times measured. If their mean is under 3.5 minutes, the machinery is stopped\nand an engineer is called to make adjustments before production is resumed. The quality\ncontrol procedure may be viewed as a hypothesis test with H0: µ = 4.0 and Ha: µ < 4.0.\nWhat would a Type II error result in?", "options": {"A": "A warranted halt in production to adjust the machinery", "B": "An unnecessary stoppage of the production process", "C": "Continued production of fidget spinners with low spin times", "D": "Continued production of fidget spinners with the proper spin times", "E": "Continued production of fidget spinners that may or may not have the proper spin times."}, "image": null, "answer": "C", "explanation": "第二类错误=μ实际<4.0（自转时间偏短）却未拒绝H0，即继续生产转速偏低的产品。"}, {"id": 457, "chapter": "第四章 统计推断", "topic": "第一/二类错误与功效", "stem": "Given an experiment with H0: µ = 31, Ha: µ > 31, and a possible correct value of 32,\nwhich of the following will increase with an increase in the sample size n?", "options": {"A": "The probability of a Type I error", "B": "The probability of a Type II error", "C": "The power of the test", "D": "The significance level α", "E": "1 – power"}, "image": null, "answer": "C", "explanation": "n增大使标准误减小，检验更易检出真实差异，功效增大；α不变，β减小。"}, {"id": 458, "chapter": "第四章 统计推断", "topic": "第一/二类错误与功效", "stem": "A quality inspector plans to test a sample of 50 bottles of water with a claimed pH level of\n6.0. If she finds the mean pH to be less than 5.5, she will have the bottling machinery\nstopped and the water sources inspected. From previous analysis, it is known that the\nstandard deviation in pH level among bottles is 0.75. What is the probability that the\ninspector will commit a Type I error and mistakenly stop a machine when the pH level of\nthe water really is the claimed 6.0?", "options": {"A": "P(z < 5.5−6.0\n0.75 )", "B": "P(z < 5.5−6.0\n√50 ) )\n( 0.75", "C": "P(t < 5.5−6.0\n0.75 ) with df = 50 −1", "D": "P(t < 5.5−6.0\n√50 ) ) with df = 50 −1\n( 0.75", "E": "P(t < 5.5−6.0\n√50 ) ) with df = 50\n( 0.75"}, "image": null, "answer": "B", "explanation": "σ=0.75已知用z：α=P(x̄<5.5|μ=6.0)=P(z<(5.5−6.0)/(0.75/√50))。"}, {"id": 459, "chapter": "第四章 统计推断", "topic": "第一/二类错误与功效", "stem": "Which of the following is incorrect?", "options": {"A": "The power of a test concerns its ability to detect a true alternative hypothesis.", "B": "The significance level of a test is the probability of rejecting a true null hypothesis.", "C": "The probability of a Type I error plus the probability of a Type II error always equals 1.", "D": "Power equals 1 minus the probability of failing to reject a false null hypothesis.", "E": "Anything that makes a null hypothesis harder to reject increases the probability of\ncommitting a Type II error."}, "image": null, "answer": "C", "explanation": "第一类和第二类错误概率之和一般不等于1（β随真实参数值变化而α固定），该说法错误；其余均正确。"}, {"id": 460, "chapter": "第四章 统计推断", "topic": "第一/二类错误与功效", "stem": "Suppose H0: p = 0.4, Ha: p < 0.4, and against the alternative p = 0.3, the power is 0.8.\nWhich of the following is a valid conclusion?", "options": {"A": "The probability of committing a Type I error is 0.1.", "B": "If p = 0.3 is true, the probability of failing to reject H0 is 0.2.", "C": "The probability of committing a Type II error is 0.7.", "D": "All of the above are valid conclusions.", "E": "None of the above are valid conclusions."}, "image": null, "answer": "B", "explanation": "功效=1−β，故对p=0.3时β=0.2，即在p=0.3为真时未能拒绝H0的概率为0.2。"}, {"id": 461, "chapter": "第四章 统计推断", "topic": "第一/二类错误与功效", "stem": "Which of the following is a true statement?", "options": {"A": "A Type I error is a conditional probability.", "B": "A Type II error results if one incorrectly assumes the data are normally distributed.", "C": "Types I and II errors are caused by mistakes, however small, by the person conducting the\ntest.", "D": "The probability of a Type II error does not depend on the probability of a Type I error.", "E": "In conducting a hypothesis test, it is possible to make both a Type I and Type II error\nsimultaneously."}, "image": null, "answer": "A", "explanation": "α=P(拒绝H0|H0为真)是条件概率，正确；β不是“误设分布”、与α有关，且两类错误不可能同时发生。"}, {"id": 462, "chapter": "第四章 统计推断", "topic": "第一/二类错误与功效", "stem": "Which of the following statements is incorrect?", "options": {"A": "The significance level of a test is the probability of a Type II error.", "B": "Given a particular alternative, the power of a test against that alternative is 1 minus the\nprobability of the Type II error associated with that alternative.", "C": "If the significance level remains fixed, increasing the sample size reduces the probability\nof a Type II error.", "D": "If the significance level remains fixed, increasing the sample size raises the power.", "E": "With the sample size held fixed, increasing the significance level decreases the probability\nof a Type II error."}, "image": null, "answer": "A", "explanation": "显著性水平是第一类错误（而非第二类错误）的概率，该说法错误；其余关于功效与β的陈述均正确。"}, {"id": 463, "chapter": "第四章 统计推断", "topic": "第一/二类错误与功效", "stem": "A manufacturer of surgical gowns (to protect surgeons from contamination) periodically\nchecks a sample of its product and performs a major shutdown and inspection if any\nleaks are detected. Similarly, a manufacturer of blankets periodically checks the sizes of\nits blankets coming off an assembly line and halts production if measurements are\nsufficiently off target. In both situations, we have the null hypothesis that the production\nequipment is performing satisfactorily. For each situation, which is the more serious\nconcern, a Type I or Type II error?", "options": {"A": "Surgical gown producer: Type I error\nBlanket manufacturer: Type I error", "B": "Surgical gown producer: Type I error\nBlanket manufacturer: Type II error", "C": "Surgical gown producer: Type II error\nBlanket manufacturer: Type I error", "D": "Surgical gown producer: Type II error\nBlanket manufacturer: Type II error", "E": "This is impossible to answer without making an expected value judgment between human\nlife and accurate blanket sizes."}, "image": null, "answer": "C", "explanation": "手术衣：漏检污染隐患（第二类：设备不合格却继续生产）更严重；毯子：尺寸稍偏不严重，误停正常生产线（第一类）代价更大。"}, {"id": 464, "chapter": "第四章 统计推断", "topic": "第一/二类错误与功效", "stem": "If all other variables remain constant, which of the following will not increase the power\nof a hypothesis test?", "options": {"A": "Increasing the sample size", "B": "Increasing the significance level", "C": "Increasing the probability of a Type II error", "D": "Decreased variability in the data", "E": "An increased difference between the true and the hypothesized parameters"}, "image": null, "answer": "C", "explanation": "功效=1−β，增大第二类错误概率会降低功效；其余选项都会提高功效。"}, {"id": 465, "chapter": "第四章 统计推断", "topic": "第一/二类错误与功效", "stem": "A company that produces bungee cords continually monitors the strength of the cords. If\nthe mean strength from a sample drops below a specified level, the production process is\nhalted and the machinery inspected. Which of the following would result from a Type I\nerror?", "options": {"A": "Halting the production process when sufficient customer complaints are received", "B": "Halting the production process when the bungee cord strength is below specifications", "C": "Halting the production process when the bungee cord strength is within specifications", "D": "Allowing the production process to continue when the bungee cord strength is below\nspecifications", "E": "Allowing the production process to continue when the bungee cord strength is within\nspecifications"}, "image": "img/q465.png", "answer": "C", "explanation": "H0=强度符合规格，第一类错误=规格合格却错误地停机检修。"}, {"id": 466, "chapter": "第四章 统计推断", "topic": "第一/二类错误与功效", "stem": "Given that the power of a significance test against a particular alternative is 97 percent,\nwhich of the following is true?", "options": {"A": "The probability of mistakenly rejecting a true null hypothesis is less than 3 percent.", "B": "The probability of mistakenly rejecting a true null hypothesis is 3 percent.", "C": "The probability of mistakenly rejecting a true null hypothesis is greater than 3 percent.", "D": "The probability of mistakenly failing to reject the false null hypothesis is 3 percent.", "E": "The probability of mistakenly failing to reject the false null hypothesis is different from 3\npercent."}, "image": null, "answer": "D", "explanation": "功效97%意味着对某特定备择，错误地未能拒绝假的原假设的概率β=3%。"}, {"id": 467, "chapter": "第四章 统计推断", "topic": "第一/二类错误与功效", "stem": "A company will market a new hybrid luxury smartphone only if it can sell the phone for\nmore than $900 (otherwise there is not enough profit to make the venture worthwhile).\nThe company does a random survey of 80 potential customers and runs a hypothesis test\nwith H0: µ = 900 and Ha: µ > 900. What would be the consequences of Type I and Type II\nerrors?", "options": {"A": "Type I error: produce a nonprofitable smartphone\nType II error: fail to produce a profitable smartphone", "B": "Type I error: fail to produce a profitable smartphone\nType II error: produce a nonprofitable smartphone", "C": "Type I error: fail to produce a nonprofitable smartphone\nType II error: produce a profitable smartphone", "D": "Type I error: fail to produce a profitable smartphone\nType II error: produce a profitable smartphone", "E": "Type I error: produce a nonprofitable smartphone\nType II error: fail to produce a nonprofitable smartphone"}, "image": null, "answer": "A", "explanation": "第一类错误：实际μ=900（无利可图）却拒绝H0而生产无利润手机；第二类错误：实际μ>900（有利可图）却未生产。"}, {"id": 468, "chapter": "第四章 统计推断", "topic": "第一/二类错误与功效", "stem": "A Human Resource manager suspects that employees are abusing the company’s sick day\npolicy and plans to investigate this issue. The hypotheses under consideration are the\nfollowing:\nH0: employees are not abusing the company’s sick day policy\nHa: employees are abusing the policy by taking sick days when they are not actually\nsick\nWhat is the power of this test?", "options": {"A": "The probability of mistakenly accusing employees of abusing the sick day policy when in\nfact they aren’t abusing the policy", "B": "The probability of mistakenly not accusing employees who are abusing the sick day policy", "C": "The probability of correctly not accusing employees who are not abusing the sick day\npolicy", "D": "The probability of correctly accusing employees who are abusing the sick day policy", "E": "The probability that the test will come to a correct decision on whether or not employees\nare abusing the sick day policy"}, "image": null, "answer": "D", "explanation": "功效=正确拒绝H0的概率，即正确地指控确实在滥用病假政策的员工。"}, {"id": 469, "chapter": "第四章 统计推断", "topic": "第一/二类错误与功效", "stem": "A hypothesis test is to be performed at a significance level of α = 0.10. What is the effect\non the probability of committing a Type I error if the sample size is increased?", "options": {"A": "The probability of committing a Type I error decreases.", "B": "The probability of committing a Type I error increases.", "C": "The probability of committing a Type I error remains the same.", "D": "This cannot be determined without knowing the relevant standard deviation.", "E": "This cannot be determined without knowing if a Type II error is committed."}, "image": null, "answer": "C", "explanation": "α已固定为0.10，第一类错误概率就是α，与样本量无关（n增大只减小β）。"}, {"id": 470, "chapter": "第四章 统计推断", "topic": "第一/二类错误与功效", "stem": "A TV manufacturer claims that 75 percent of purchasers understand the setup\ninstructions. The sales department of a TV superstore disagrees and decides to do a largesample z-test for a population proportion at the 5% significance level. The null\nhypothesis that the proportion of TV purchasers who understand the instructions is 75\npercent is tested against the alternative hypothesis that the proportion of TV purchasers\nwho understand the instructions is less than 75 percent. For which of the following will\nthe power of the test be highest?", "options": {"A": "Sample size n = 50 and 55 percent of purchasers understand the instructions", "B": "Sample size n = 100 and 55 percent of purchasers understand the instructions", "C": "Sample size n = 50 and 65 percent of purchasers understand the instructions", "D": "Sample size n = 100 and 65 percent of purchasers understand the instructions", "E": "Sample size n = 100 and 95 percent of purchasers understand the instructions"}, "image": null, "answer": "B", "explanation": "Ha: p<0.75，n越大且真实p越低于0.75功效越大；n=100且p=0.55时功效最高（p=0.95方向相反，功效≈0）。"}, {"id": 471, "chapter": "第四章 统计推断", "topic": "第一/二类错误与功效", "stem": "Dogs have far more sensitive noses than humans, estimated to be up to 100,000 times\nbetter than humans. Researchers believe that dogs can sniff out lung cancer from breath\nsamples of sufferers. Using the dogs, the researchers plan to test thousands of samples\nand prescribe chemotherapy only if the dogs indicate the presence of cancer. Consider the\nfollowing hypotheses:\nH0: The subject does not have cancer.\nHa: The subject does have cancer.\nWhat would a Type II error be in context?", "options": {"A": "A subject with cancer is correctly diagnosed with cancer.", "B": "A subject with cancer is mistakenly thought to be cancer free.", "C": "A cancer-free subject is correctly diagnosed to be cancer free.", "D": "A cancer-free subject is mistakenly diagnosed with cancer.", "E": "Correct and mistaken diagnoses of cancer are confounded."}, "image": null, "answer": "B", "explanation": "第二类错误=H0为假（有癌）却未拒绝，即把患癌者误判为无癌而错过化疗。"}, {"id": 472, "chapter": "第四章 统计推断", "topic": "第一/二类错误与功效", "stem": "What is the probability of a Type II error when a hypothesis test is being conducted at the\n10% significance level (α = 0.10)?", "options": {"A": "0.05", "B": "0.10", "C": "0.90", "D": "0.95", "E": "There is insufficient information to answer this question.\nP-VALUES FROM SIMULATIONS"}, "image": null, "answer": "E", "explanation": "β取决于真实参数值、样本量和标准差等，仅知道α=0.10无法确定第二类错误概率。"}, {"id": 473, "chapter": "第四章 统计推断", "topic": "模拟中的P值", "stem": "A marriage counselor plans a study on whether a husband or a wife is happier on a\ngreater proportion of days when the wife has a job outside the home and the husband is a\nstay-at-home dad. In a random sample of married couples where the wife works and the\nhusband is at home, the difference in proportions of happy days between husband and\nwife is 0.1106. The counselor, who took a required statistics class in college, is concerned\nthat the proportions she subtracted to get 0.1106 are not from independent samples. So\nshe runs 200 simulations of couples with the null hypothesis that there is no difference in\nproportions of happy days. The dotplot of resulting proportion differences is below.\nSimulated Differences in Proportions\nIs there sufficient evidence of a significant difference in the proportion of happy days\nexperienced by husbands and wives in marriages where the wife works and the husband is\na stay-at-home dad?", "options": {"A": "Yes, because 0.1106 > 0.0", "B": "Yes, because 0.1106 > 0.05", "C": "Yes, because the distribution of simulated differences is approximately normal so the\ncentral limit theorem applies", "D": "No, because 0.1106 > 0.05", "E": "No, because the simulated P-value is large"}, "image": "img/q473.png", "answer": "E", "explanation": "观察差0.1106落在模拟分布（约−0.2到0.15）之中并不极端，模拟P值较大，无显著差异证据。"}, {"id": 474, "chapter": "第四章 统计推断", "topic": "模拟中的P值", "stem": "An industrial control check is as follows. Random samples are periodically gathered. If a\nparticular statistic is significantly greater than what is expected during proper operation\nof the machinery, a recalibration is necessary. In a simulation of 100 such samples where\nthe machinery is working properly, the resulting statistic is summarized in the following\ndotplot.\nCalculations of the statistic from random samples when the machinery is operating properly\nSuppose during one control check, the statistic from the random sample is 24. Is there\nsufficient evidence to necessitate a recalibration of the machinery?", "options": {"A": "No, because with the machine operating properly, the simulation gave two statistics even\ngreater than 24", "B": "No, because stopping production to recalibrate is a serious, expensive decision, and given\nthis data, the probability of a Type I error is too great", "C": "No, because the distribution of simulation results is roughly bellshaped so the 68-95-99.7\nrule applies", "D": "Yes, because the consequences of a Type II error are significant", "E": "Yes, because the estimated P-value is less than 0.05\nCHI-SQUARE TESTS"}, "image": null, "answer": "E", "explanation": "机器正常时100次模拟的统计量都不超过约23，观测值24超过所有模拟值，估计P<0.05，故需要重新校准。"}, {"id": 475, "chapter": "第四章 统计推断", "topic": "卡方检验", "stem": "Data from a simple random sample (SRS) of teachers is cross-classified by gender and\nsupport for a new educational initiative, resulting in the following table.\nMale\nFemale\nFor\n37\n41\nAgainst\n42\n62\nNo Opinion\n41\n29\nIs there evidence of a relationship between gender and support for the initiative among\nteachers?", "options": {"A": "There is strong evidence of a relationship between gender and support for the initiative\namong teachers, P < 0.05.", "B": "There is weak evidence of a relationship between gender and support for the initiative\namong teachers, 0.05 < P < 0.10.", "C": "There is not sufficient evidence of a relationship between gender and support for the\ninitiative among teachers, P > 0.10.", "D": "Further information is needed to be able to perform a chi-square test of independence.", "E": "The test is inconclusive."}, "image": "img/q475.png", "answer": "B", "explanation": "期望=行×列/252：37.1,40.9,49.5,54.5,33.3,36.7；χ²≈5.55，df=2，P≈0.062，属0.05<P<0.10的弱证据。"}, {"id": 476, "chapter": "第四章 统计推断", "topic": "卡方检验", "stem": "A survey of 150 high school seniors is conducted to see if there is a relationship between\nwhether or not a student is taking AP Statistics and whether students have positive or\nnegative outlooks on their future. The data are summarized in the following table.\nPositive Outlook\nNegative Outlook\nTaking AP Stats\n46\n24\nNot Taking AP Stats\n37\n43\nWhat is the appropriate test statistic for a hypothesis test with H0: Student outlook on\nthe future is independent of taking AP Statistics?", "options": {"A": "(46−38.7)2\n38.7\n+ (24−31.3)2\n31.3\n+ (37−44.3)2\n44.3\n+ (43−35.7)2", "B": "(46−38.7)2\n46\n+ (24−31.3)2\n24\n+ (37−44.3)2\n37\n+ (43−35.7)2", "C": "(46−35)2\n35\n+ (24−35)2\n35\n+ (37−40)2\n40\n+ (43−40)2\n40", "D": "(46−41.5)2\n41.5\n+ (24−33.5)2\n33.5\n+ (37−41.5)2\n41.5\n+ (43−33.5)2", "E": "(46−37.5)2\n46\n+ (24−37.5)2\n24\n+ (37−37.5)2\n37\n+ (43−37.5)2"}, "image": "img/q476.png", "answer": "A", "explanation": "期望=行合计×列合计/150：38.7、31.3、44.3、35.7，卡方统计量=Σ(观察−期望)²/期望，分母为期望频数。"}, {"id": 477, "chapter": "第四章 统计推断", "topic": "卡方检验", "stem": "Which of the following is not true with regard to contingency tables for chi-square tests\nfor independence?", "options": {"A": "Categorical rather than quantitative variables are being considered.", "B": "Observed frequencies should be whole numbers.", "C": "Expected frequencies should be whole numbers.", "D": "Expected frequencies in each cell should be at least 5, and to achieve this, one sometimes\ncombines categories for one or the other or for both of the variables.", "E": "The expected frequency for any cell can be found by multiplying the row total by the\ncolumn total and then dividing by the sample size."}, "image": null, "answer": "C", "explanation": "期望频数是按行列合计计算的小数，不必为整数；观察频数才是整数。"}, {"id": 478, "chapter": "第四章 统计推断", "topic": "卡方检验", "stem": "The table below shows the number of employees arriving late for work at an office and is\nbroken down by the day of the week. An HR officer would like to know if such late\narrivals are related to the day of the week.\nMonday\nTuesday\nWednesday\nThursday\nFriday\n35.7\n43\n33.5\n43\n12\n5\n9\n4\n15\nWhat is the value of chi-square for the appropriate test?", "options": {"A": "(12−9)2\n12\n+ (5−9)2\n5\n+ (9−9)2\n9\n+ (4−9)2\n4\n+ (15−9)2", "B": "(12−9)2\n9\n+ (5−9)2\n9\n+ (9−9)2\n9\n+ (4−9)2\n9\n+ (15−9)2", "C": "(12−9)2\n45\n+ (5−9)2\n45\n+ (9−9)2\n45\n+ (4−9)2\n45\n+ (15−9)2", "D": "(12−5)2\n12\n+ (5−5)2\n5\n+ (9−5)2\n9\n+ (4−5)2\n4\n+ (15−5)2", "E": "(12−5)2\n5\n+ (5−5)2\n5\n+ (9−5)2\n5\n+ (4−5)2\n5\n+ (15−5)2"}, "image": "img/q478.png", "answer": "B", "explanation": "H0下五天等概率，期望=45/5=9，χ²=Σ(观察−9)²/9，分母为期望频数9。"}, {"id": 479, "chapter": "第四章 统计推断", "topic": "卡方检验", "stem": "A geneticist claims that three species of fruit flies should appear in the ratio 1:6:9.\nSuppose that a random sample of 320 flies contained 25, 100, and 195 flies of each species,\nrespectively. Does a chi-square test show sufficient evidence to reject the geneticist’s\nclaim at the 5% significance level?", "options": {"A": "The test proves the geneticist’s claim.", "B": "The test proves the geneticist’s claim is false.", "C": "The test does not give sufficient evidence to reject the geneticist’s claim at the 5%\nsignificance level.", "D": "The test gives sufficient evidence to reject the geneticist’s claim at the 5% significance\nlevel.", "E": "The test is inconclusive."}, "image": null, "answer": "D", "explanation": "期望=320×(1/16,6/16,9/16)=32,192,96；χ²=(25−32)²/32+(100−192)²/192+(195−96)²/96≈147.7，df=2，P≈0，拒绝该比例主张。"}, {"id": 480, "chapter": "第四章 统计推断", "topic": "卡方检验", "stem": "Three English professors are interviewed regarding a random sampling of 20 of their\nterm paper grades. The following table gives the resulting counts.\nProfessor A\nProfessor B\nProfessor C\nGrades A, B\n5\n7\n9\nGrade C\n13\n10\n7\nGrades D, F\n2\n3\n4\nA statistics student runs a chi-square test of homogeneity. What is the most proper\nconclusion?", "options": {"A": "There is no evidence that these professors give different distributions of grades.", "B": "There is evidence at the 10% level, but not at the 5% level, that the professors give\ndifferent grade distributions.\n15\n9\n45\n15\n5", "C": "There is evidence at the 5% level, but not at the 1% level, that the professors give different\ngrade distributions.", "D": "There is evidence at the 1% level that the professors give different grade distributions.", "E": "A chi-square test of homogeneity is not appropriate."}, "image": "img/q480.png", "answer": "A", "explanation": "三位教授各20份，列比例21:30:9，期望均7,10,3；χ²≈3.61，df=4，P≈0.46>0.10，无证据表明给分分布不同。"}, {"id": 481, "chapter": "第四章 统计推断", "topic": "卡方检验", "stem": "Researchers wanted to determine if preference between baseball and chess is independent\nof age. 210 interviews yielded the following numbers.\nAge\n10–19\n20–49\n50–89\nTotal\nPrefer Baseball\n55\n40\n15\n110\nPrefer Chess\n10\n15\n35\n60\nTotal\n65\n55\n50\n170\nWhat is the P-value for an appropriate test?", "options": {"A": "P(χ2 > (55−42.1)2\n55\n+ (40−35.6)2\n40\n+ (15−32.4)2\n15\n+ (10−22.9)2", "B": "P(x2 > (55−42.1)2\n42.1\n+ (40−35.6)2\n35.6\n+ (15−32.4)2\n32.4\n+ (10−22.9)2", "C": "P(χ2 > (55−42.1)2\n55\n+ (40−35.6)2\n40\n+ (15−32.4)2\n15\n+ (10−22.9)2", "D": "P(χ2 > (55−42.1)2\n42.1\n+ (40−35.6)2\n35.6\n+ (15−32.4)2\n32.4\n+ (10−22.9)2", "E": "P(χ2 > (55−42.1)2\n28.33\n+ (40−35.6)2\n28.33\n+ (15−32.4)2\n28.33\n+ (10−22.9)2"}, "image": null, "answer": "D", "explanation": "独立性检验统计量=Σ(观察−期望)²/期望（分母用期望42.1,35.6,32.4,22.9,19.4,17.6），df=(2−1)(3−1)=2。"}, {"id": 482, "chapter": "第四章 统计推断", "topic": "卡方检验", "stem": "Which of the following is the proper use of a chi-square test of independence?", "options": {"A": "To test whether the distribution of counts on a categorical variable matches a claimed\ndistribution", "B": "To test whether the distribution of counts on a numerical variable matches a claimed\ndistribution", "C": "To test whether the distribution of two different groups on the same categorical variable\nmatches", "D": "To test whether two categorical variables on one set of subjects are related\n10\n+ (15−19.4)2\n15\n+ (35−17.6)2\n35\n)\n, df = 2\n22.9\n+ (15−19.4)2\n19.4\n+ (35−17.6)2\n17.6\n), df = 2\n10\n+ (15−19.4)2\n15\n+ (35−17.6)2\n35\n), df = 3\n22.9\n+ (15−19.4)2\n19.4\n+ (35−17.6)2\n17.6\n), df = 3\n28.33\n+ (15−19.4)2\n28.33\n+ (35−17.6)2\n28.33\n), df = 6", "E": "To test whether two numerical variables on one set of subjects are related"}, "image": null, "answer": "D", "explanation": "卡方独立性检验用于同一样本上两个分类变量之间是否相关。"}, {"id": 483, "chapter": "第四章 统计推断", "topic": "卡方检验", "stem": "Last year, college students learned about major news events from the following sources:\nMSNBC: 21%     CNN: 26%       FOX: 12%      The Daily Show: 41%.\nIn a random sample of 80 college students this year, 23 watched MSNBC, 14 watched\nCNN, 10 watched FOX, and 33 watched The Daily Show to learn about major news events.\nIf a goodness-of-fit test is performed, what will be the P-value?\nP(χ2 > (23−16.8)2\n23\n+ (14−20.8)2\n14\n+ (10−9.6)2\n10\n+ (33−32.8)2", "options": {"A": "with df = 3", "B": "P(χ2 > (23−16.8)2\n23\n+ (14−20.8)2\n14\n+ (10−9.6)2\n10\n+ (33−32.8)2", "C": "P(χ2 > (23−16.8)2\n16.8\n+ (14−20.8)2\n20.8\n+ (10−9.6)2\n9.6\n+ (33−32.8)2", "D": "P(χ2 > (23−16.8)2\n16.8\n+ (14−20.8)2\n20.8\n+ (10−9.6)2\n9.6\n+ (33−32.8)2", "E": "P(χ2 > (23−16.8)2\n0.25\n+ (14−20.8)2\n0.25\n+ (10−9.6)2\n0.25\n+ (33−32.8)2"}, "image": null, "answer": "C", "explanation": "期望频数=80×去年比例：16.8、20.8、9.6、32.8，自由度df=4−1=3。"}, {"id": 484, "chapter": "第四章 统计推断", "topic": "卡方检验", "stem": "Researchers wanted to determine if people know Mitt Romney’s real first name. Data\nfrom random samples of 170 Democrats and 170 Republicans are summarized in the\nfollowing table.\nMittens\nWillard\nSomething Else\nDemocrats\n23\n41\n106\nRepublicans\n55\n52\n63\nAssuming that people from each party have the same pattern of responses when asked\nabout Romney’s real first name, what is the expected number of Republicans who answer\nWillard (which is actually the correct answer) from this group of 340 people?", "options": {"A": "93\n170", "B": "52\n93", "C": "52\n170", "D": "(170)(93)\n211\n32.8\n)\n32.8\n)\nwith df = 4\n32.8\n)with df = 3\n32.8\n))\nwith df = 4\n0.25\n)with df = 79", "E": "(170)(93)\n340"}, "image": "img/q484.png", "answer": "E", "explanation": "期望频数=(行合计×列合计)/总计=(170×93)/340=46.5。"}, {"id": 485, "chapter": "第四章 统计推断", "topic": "卡方检验", "stem": "A study of hospital admissions in a small town reported the following numbers for three\ndifferent days broken down by phase of the moon.\nNew Moon\nFull Moon\nHalf Moon\nAccidents\n35\n77\n53\nIs there sufficient evidence to say that the number of admissions on the three days is not\nthe same?", "options": {"A": "There is sufficient evidence at the 0.001 significance level that the number of admissions\non each day is not the same.", "B": "There is sufficient evidence at the 0.01 level, but not at the 0.001 level, that the number of\nadmissions on each day is not the same.", "C": "There is sufficient evidence at the 0.05 level, but not at the 0.01 level, that the number of\nadmissions on each day is not the same.", "D": "There is sufficient evidence at the 0.10 level, but not at the 0.05 level, that the number of\nadmissions on each day is not the same.", "E": "There is not sufficient evidence to say that the number of admissions on each day is not\nthe same."}, "image": null, "answer": "A", "explanation": "H0下三天等概率，期望165/3=55；χ²=(35−55)²/55+(77−55)²/55+(53−55)²/55≈16.15，df=2，P≈0.0003<0.001。"}, {"id": 486, "chapter": "第四章 统计推断", "topic": "卡方检验", "stem": "A random sample of 100 men was cross-classified by heart disease and baldness. The\ntable below summarizes the tallies.\nNo Baldness\nSome Baldness\nExtreme Baldness\nHeart Disease\n120\n85\n38\nHealthy\n256\n51\n5\nWhich of the procedures below is most appropriate to investigate whether there is an\nassociation between heart disease and baldness?", "options": {"A": "Two-sample t-test of the difference between population means", "B": "Two-sample z-test of the difference between population proportions", "C": "Matched pair t-test", "D": "Chi-square goodness-of-fit test", "E": "Chi-square test of independence"}, "image": "img/q486.png", "answer": "E", "explanation": "一个随机样本按心脏病和秃发两个分类变量交叉分类，检验其关联用卡方独立性检验。"}, {"id": 487, "chapter": "第四章 统计推断", "topic": "卡方检验", "stem": "An electronics store sales representative believes men and women have different\npreferences with regard to TV sizes. To test his belief, he shows models of four different\nTVs with different sizes to a random sample of 80 men and a random sample of 70\nwomen. He plans to use a chi-square test of homogeneity. Assuming that conditions for\ninference are met, which of the following is a true statement?", "options": {"A": "The test is not valid because the sample sizes are different.", "B": "The test would be more appropriate if 75 married couples had been used.", "C": "Given the number of choices, four, the sample sizes are too small for a test of\nhomogeneity of proportions.", "D": "The null hypothesis is that the proportion of each gender who prefer each TV is 1\n4 .", "E": "The more that men and women differ in their TV preferences, the larger the chi-square\nstatistic will be."}, "image": null, "answer": "E", "explanation": "男女偏好与期望（相同偏好）偏差越大，(O−E)²越大，卡方统计量就越大。"}, {"id": 488, "chapter": "第四章 统计推断", "topic": "卡方检验", "stem": "A highway engineer claims that the four highways leading into a city are used in the ratio\n3:2:3:4 during the morning rush hour. A study involving a simple random sample (SRS)\nof 3,600 cars counted 920, 570, 700, and 1,410 cars using the four highways, respectively.\nWhat is the P-value for the appropriate test?", "options": {"A": "P( (920−900)2\n920\n+ (570−600)2\n570\n+ (700−900)2\n700\n+ (1,410−1,200)2", "B": "P( (920−900)2\n920\n+ (570−600)2\n570\n+ (700−900)2\n700\n+ (1,410−1,200)2\nP( (920−900)2\n900\n+ (570−600)2\n600\n+ (700−900)2\n900\n+ (1,410−1,200)2", "C": "P ( (920−900)2\n900\n+ (570−600)2\n600\n+ (700−900)2\n900\n+ (1,410−1,200)2", "D": "P ( (920−900)2\n900\n+ (570−600)2\n900\n+ (700−900)2\n900\n+ (1,410−1,200)2", "E": ""}, "image": "img/q488.png", "answer": "C", "explanation": "期望=3600×3/12,2/12,3/12,4/12=900,600,900,1200；χ²=Σ(观察−期望)²/期望，df=4−1=3。"}, {"id": 489, "chapter": "第四章 统计推断", "topic": "卡方检验", "stem": "In a random sample of 920 college students, all were surveyed as to whether they had\nparlor tattoos, tattoos from elsewhere, or no tattoos. Then all were tested for hepatitis C.\nThe data are displayed in the table below.\nParlor Tattoo\nNonparlor Tattoo\nNo Tattoo\nHepatitis C\n24\n11\n32\nHealthy\n37\n52\n764\nIn a test of independence, which of the following is used as an expected cell count for\nhealthy students with no tattoo?", "options": {"A": "764", "B": "1\n6 (920)\n1,410\n) with df = 3\n1,410\n) with df = 4\n1,200\n) with df = 3\n1,200\n) with df = 4\n900\n) with df = 3", "C": "1\n2 (32 + 764)", "D": "1\n3 (37 + 52 + 764)", "E": "(37+52+764)(32+764)\n920"}, "image": "img/q489.png", "answer": "E", "explanation": "期望频数=(健康行合计(37+52+764)×无纹身列合计(32+764))/920。"}, {"id": 490, "chapter": "第四章 统计推断", "topic": "卡方检验", "stem": "It is hypothesized that scores on a certain intelligence test are normally distributed with a\nmean of 100 and a standard deviation of 15. A psychologist runs a goodness-of-fit test on\na simple random sample (SRS) of 200 scores, which results in the table below.\nScore\nBelow 85\n85–100\n100–115\nAbove 115\nNumber of People\n21\n84\n67\n28\nWhat is the χ2 statistic for this test?", "options": {"A": "(21−32)2\n21\n+ (84−68)2\n84\n+ (67−68)2\n67\n+ (28−32)2\n28", "B": "(21−32)2\n32\n+ (84−68)2\n68\n+ (67−68)2\n68\n+ (28−32)2\n32", "C": "(21−50)2\n50\n+ (84−50)2\n50\n+ (67−50)2\n50\n+ (28−50)2\n50", "D": "(21−50)2\n21\n+ (84−50)2\n84\n+ (67−50)2\n67\n+ (28−50)2\n28", "E": "(21−50)2\n32\n+ (84−50)2\n68\n+ (67−50)2\n68\n+ (28−50)2\n32"}, "image": "img/q490.png", "answer": "B", "explanation": "N(100,15)下各区间概率约0.16、0.34、0.34、0.16，期望=200×概率=32、68、68、32；χ²=Σ(观察−期望)²/期望。"}, {"id": 491, "chapter": "第四章 统计推断", "topic": "卡方检验", "stem": "A small study was made to investigate whether police searches of stopped vehicles are\nindependent of the driver’s race. The data counts for one day are summarized in the table\nbelow.\nRace\nSearch\nWhite\nBlack\nOther\nTotal\nYes\n4\n8\n11\n23\nNo\n26\n15\n4\n45\nTotal\n30\n23\n15\n68\nA chi-square test of independence gives χ2 = 16.1 and a P-value of 0.00032. Which of the\nfollowing is a correct statement?", "options": {"A": "The degrees of freedom are (3 – 1)(4 – 1).", "B": "The chi-square test should not have been used because two of the cells are less than 5.", "C": "The null hypothesis states that there is an association between whether police search a\nstopped vehicle and the driver’s race.", "D": "The very small P-value suggests that there is an association between whether police search\na stopped vehicle and the driver’s race.", "E": "The chi-square test shows that police treat different races differently."}, "image": "img/q491.png", "answer": "D", "explanation": "df=(2−1)(3−1)=2；所有期望频数均≥5（检验看期望不是观察频数）；P=0.00032极小，提示搜查与种族有关联。"}, {"id": 492, "chapter": "第四章 统计推断", "topic": "卡方检验", "stem": "An ice cream distributor wants to know if adults and children prefer the same flavors of\nice cream. A random sample of 100 adults and an independent random sample of 100\nchildren are surveyed as to ice cream preferences. The data are shown in the table below.\nIce Cream Flavor\nVanilla\nChocolate\nStrawberry\nCoffee\nCookie Dough\nAdults\n25\n20\n15\n35\n5\nChildren\n20\n25\n25\n0\n30\nWhich of the following procedures would be most appropriate to investigate whether\nthere is a relationship between adults/children and ice cream flavor preference?", "options": {"A": "Chi-square test of independence", "B": "Chi-square test of homogeneity", "C": "Chi-square test of goodness-of-fit", "D": "Two-sample t-test", "E": "Matched pairs t-test"}, "image": "img/q492.png", "answer": "B", "explanation": "成人和儿童是两个独立随机样本，比较同一分类变量（口味）的分布，用卡方同质性检验。"}, {"id": 493, "chapter": "第四章 统计推断", "topic": "卡方检验", "stem": "A restaurant receives two food deliveries per day, and timeliness is critical. The\nrestaurant manager tabulates the number of on-time deliveries for a random sample of\n200 days, as shown in the table.\nNumber of On-Time Deliveries\n0\n1\n2\nObserved Number of Days\n12\n75\n113\nWhat is the χ2 statistic for a goodness-of-fit test that the distribution is binomial with the\nprobability equal to 0.8 that a delivery is on time?", "options": {"A": "(12−8)2\n8\n+ (75−64)2\n64\n+ (113−128)2\n128", "B": "(12−8)2\n12\n+ (75−64)2\n75\n+ (113−128)2\n113", "C": "(12−10)2\n10\n+ (75−30)2\n30\n+ (113−160)2\n160", "D": "(12−10)2\n12\n+ (75−30)2\n75\n+ (113−160)2\n113", "E": "(12−66)2\n12\n+ (75−67)2\n75\n+ (113−67)2\n113"}, "image": null, "answer": "A", "explanation": "Binomial(2,0.8)各概率0.04、0.32、0.64，期望天数=200×(0.04,0.32,0.64)=8、64、128；χ²=Σ(观察−期望)²/期望。"}, {"id": 494, "chapter": "第四章 统计推断", "topic": "卡方检验", "stem": "One survey of what middle school and high school students enjoy reading listed the top\nfour choices as fantasy, science fiction, horror, and fairy tales. To test whether middle\nschool and high school students make similar choices, independent random samples are\ntaken from each group of students. Each student is asked what he/she enjoys reading\nmost among the choices: fantasy, science fiction, horror, fairy tales, or other. A chi-square\ntest of homogeneity is performed, and the resulting P-value is below 0.05. Which of the\nfollowing is a proper conclusion?", "options": {"A": "There is sufficient evidence that for all five choices, the proportion of middle school\nstudents who prefer each choice is equal to the corresponding proportion of high school\nstudents.", "B": "There is sufficient evidence that the proportion of middle school students who prefer\nscience fiction is different from the proportion of high school students who prefer science\nfiction.", "C": "There is sufficient evidence that for all five choices, the proportion of middle school\nstudents who prefer each choice is different from the corresponding proportion of high\nschool students.", "D": "There is sufficient evidence that for at least one of the five choices, the proportion of\nmiddle school students who prefer that choice is equal to the corresponding proportion of\nhigh school students.", "E": "There is sufficient evidence that for at least one of the five choices, the proportion of\nmiddle school students who prefer that choice is different from the corresponding\nproportion of high school students.\nHYPOTHESIS TESTS FOR SLOPES"}, "image": "img/q494.png", "answer": "E", "explanation": "拒绝同质性原假设只能得出：至少有一个阅读选项的比例在初中生和高中生之间存在差异。"}, {"id": 495, "chapter": "第四章 统计推断", "topic": "斜率的假设检验", "stem": "A retailer believes that sales of some luxury items go up when the price is higher. Below is\nthe computer printout for the regression analysis of sales versus price (in $1,000s) for a\nrandom sample of these luxury items.\nWhat is the P-value for a t-test with H0: β = 0 and Ha: β > 0?", "options": {"A": "0.0124", "B": "0.0239", "C": "0.0478", "D": "0.0956", "E": "0.5060"}, "image": "img/q495.png", "answer": "B", "explanation": "t²=MSR/MSE=105.226/(102.649/6)≈6.15，t≈2.48，df=6；单侧P=P(t6>2.48)≈0.0239。"}, {"id": 496, "chapter": "第四章 统计推断", "topic": "斜率的假设检验", "stem": "A linear regression analysis is performed on two variables. Which of the following tells\nyou that another model probably gives a better fit?", "options": {"A": "The correlation r is low.", "B": "The mean of the residuals is 0.", "C": "The P-value for H0: β = 0 and Ha: β > 0 is low.", "D": "The coefficient of determination is high.", "E": "The residual plot has a pattern."}, "image": null, "answer": "E", "explanation": "残差图出现模式说明线性模型不拟合数据，其他模型可能拟合更好；r低或P值小含义不同，残差均值为0是线性回归的固有性质。"}, {"id": 497, "chapter": "第四章 统计推断", "topic": "斜率的假设检验", "stem": "The space shuttle Challenger took off when the temperature was 31°F. Before this launch,\n23 earlier launches experienced from zero to three O-ring failures. After the Challenger\ndisaster, there was some speculation that the number of O-ring failures was related to\ntemperature at liftoff. A computer printout, performed too late, is shown below.\nIs there evidence of a relationship between O-ring failures and temperature at liftoff?", "options": {"A": "There is no evidence of a relationship between the number of O-ring failures and the\ntemperature at liftoff.", "B": "There is evidence of a relationship at the 0.10 level but not at the 0.05 level.", "C": "There is evidence of a relationship at the 0.05 level but not at the 0.01 level.", "D": "There is evidence of a relationship at the 0.01 level but not at the 0.001 level.", "E": "There is evidence of a relationship at the 0.001 level."}, "image": "img/q497.png", "answer": "D", "explanation": "t²=4.30166/(9.35052/21)，t≈−3.11，df=21，双侧P≈0.005：在0.01水平显著但不在0.001水平。"}, {"id": 498, "chapter": "第四章 统计推断", "topic": "斜率的假设检验", "stem": "In a random sample of 20 college students, each was interviewed as to GPA and weekly\nwork-study hours on campus. What is the critical t-value in calculating a 95% confidence\ninterval estimate for the slope of the resulting least squares regression line?", "options": {"A": "1.96", "B": "2.074", "C": "2.086", "D": "2.093", "E": "2.101"}, "image": null, "answer": "E", "explanation": "斜率置信区间用df=n−2=18，t*.025=2.101。"}, {"id": 499, "chapter": "第四章 统计推断", "topic": "斜率的假设检验", "stem": "A scatterplot of the ages of 30 couples applying for marriage licenses shows a strong,\npositive, linear relationship between the men’s and women’s ages. A significance test on\nthe slope with H0: β = 0 and Ha: β > 0 results in a P-value of 0.035. Which of the following\nstatements is an appropriate conclusion?", "options": {"A": "With this small of a P-value, 0.035 < 0.05, there is not sufficient evidence of a linear\nrelationship between the men’s and women’s ages of couples applying for marriage\nlicenses.", "B": "With this small of a P-value, 0.035 < 0.05, there is proof of a linear relationship between\nthe men’s and women’s ages of couples applying for marriage licenses.", "C": "With this small of a P-value, 0.035 < 0.05, there is sufficient evidence that among couples\napplying for marriage licenses, a one-year difference in ages among men corresponds to a\nconstant positive difference in ages among women on average.", "D": "Among couples applying for marriage licenses, 3.5 percent of the difference among the\nwomen’s ages can be explained by the linear model of women’s ages versus men’s ages.", "E": "Among couples applying for marriage licenses, 12.25 percent of the difference among the\nwomen’s ages can be explained by the linear model of women’s ages versus men’s ages."}, "image": null, "answer": "C", "explanation": "P=0.035<0.05拒绝β=0：有充分证据表明男方年龄每差1岁，对应女方年龄平均存在恒定的正向差异。"}, {"id": 500, "chapter": "第四章 统计推断", "topic": "斜率的假设检验", "stem": "An auto magazine report analyzes the association between the weight of a car (in\nthousands of pounds) and the fuel efficiency (in miles per gallon). Some of the regression\nanalysis is shown below.\nAssuming that all conditions for inference are met, what is the appropriate test statistic\nfor testing the null hypothesis, H0: β = 0, that the slope of the regression line is 0?", "options": {"A": "45.7\n2.01", "B": "24.1\n0.867", "C": "−8.73\n2.39", "D": "−8.73\n0.74", "E": "−8.73\n( 0.74\n√30 )"}, "image": "img/q500.png", "answer": "D", "explanation": "斜率的检验统计量=斜率估计/其标准误=−8.73/0.74。"}];
const FR_QUESTIONS = [{"id": 501, "chapter": "FR 探索性分析", "stem": "In a random sample of 20 high school students, the number of\ntext messages sent by each during the past day were\n\n{0, 4, 8, 12, 13, 15, 15, 18, 23, 24, 40, 43, 47, 50, 53, 57, 59, 61, 61,\n65}.\n\n(a)   Create a stemplot of the number of text messages sent by\n\nstudents.\n(b)   Describe the distribution.\n(c)   Would a boxplot give more, less, or basically the same\n\ninformation? Explain.", "model": "(a) 茎叶图（茎=十位，叶=个位）：0|0 4 8；1|2 3 5 5 8；2|3 4；3|—；4|0 3 7；5|0 3 7 9；6|1 1 5。(b) 分布右偏（偏态）：中位数=(24+40)/2=32，范围0–65，多数集中在0–24，少数学生发很多短信；单峰、右偏、无异常值。(c) 基本相同。茎叶图与箱线图都展示五数概括/分布位置与离散度，箱线图同样能显示中位数、四分位距与偏态；只是箱线图不显示具体数值和多峰性，信息量基本相当。", "image": "img/fr501.png"}, {"id": 502, "chapter": "FR 探索性分析", "stem": "The histogram below shows the distribution of weights of the 21\nbags of candy coming off an assembly line during a one-minute\nproduction interval.\n\n(a)   Write a few sentences to describe the distribution of bag\n\nweights for the one-minute interval.\n(b)   One of the bag weights is 15.85 ounces. If this bag weighed\n\n15.75 ounces instead of 15.85 ounces, what effect would the\ndecrease have on the mean and on the median?", "model": "(a) 需直方图（未提供）：应描述形状（偏态/对称）、中心和离散度及任何异常值。(b) 均值会略微下降：一个值减少0.10盎司，均值减少0.10/21≈0.005盎司。中位数基本不变：只有当该袋恰好是位于中间的观测值时中位数才可能略微下降（最多降约0.05盎司），否则完全不受影响。", "image": "img/fr502.png"}, {"id": 503, "chapter": "FR 探索性分析", "stem": "A bank cybersecurity engineer notes the number of malware\nintrusions intercepted each hour during a random sample of 109\nhour-long periods.\n\n(a)   Describe the distribution.\n(b)   Is the quotient, mean intrusions\n\nmedian intrusions greater than 1, less than 1, or\napproximately 1? Explain.\n(c)   Are conditions for inference (constructing a confidence\n\ninterval for the mean number of intrusions per hour) met?\nExplain.", "model": null, "image": "img/fr503.png"}, {"id": 504, "chapter": "FR 探索性分析", "stem": "Do graduates from different universities obtain different\nstarting salaries? In one study of starting salaries, random\nsamples of graduates from two universities were surveyed and\ntheir starting salaries noted. The histograms below display the\ndata.\n\n(a)   Compare the distributions of starting salaries of graduates\n\nfrom the two universities.\n(b)   Which is greater, the difference between the two university\n\nmean starting salaries or the difference between the two\nuniversity median starting salaries? Explain.", "model": null, "image": "img/fr504.png"}, {"id": 505, "chapter": "FR 探索性分析", "stem": "In independent random samples of 20 high school students and\n20 college students, each student gave a rating between 0 and 100\nfor a new music website.\n\nHS students: 27, 32, 82, 36, 43, 75, 45, 16, 23, 48, 51, 57, 60,\n64, 39, 40, 69, 72, 54, 57\nCollege students: 49, 50, 35, 69, 75, 35, 49, 54, 98, 58, 22, 34,\n60, 38, 47, 65, 79, 38, 42, 87\n\nUsing back-to-back stemplots, compare the distributions.", "model": "背靠背茎叶图（茎=十位）：HS叶（升序）：0|6,1|6 3 7,2|3,3|2 6 9,4|0 3 5 8,5|1 4 7 7,6|0 4 9,7|2 5,8|2；College叶：0|2,1|—,2|2,3|4 5 5 8 8,4|2 7 9 9,5|0 4 8,6|0 5 9,7|5 9,8|7,9|8。比较：两组中心相同（中位数均为49.5），离散度相近（范围HS 16–82，College 22–98），形状均大致对称但College高端有较大离群值（87,98），HS也偏高点（82）。整体上两组学生对该网站评分分布相似。", "image": "img/fr505.png"}, {"id": 506, "chapter": "FR 探索性分析", "stem": "The A1C test is used to diagnose diabetes. Cumulative graphs of\nA1C level readings in the populations of three countries (A, B,\nand C) are given below.\n\nWrite a few sentences comparing the distributions of A1C level\nreadings in the three countries.", "model": null, "image": "img/fr506.png"}, {"id": 507, "chapter": "FR 探索性分析", "stem": "Ms. D. has 12 students in her AP Statistics class. They all did\nvery well on the final exam with scores of {81, 87, 88, 88, 88, 89,\n89, 90, 90, 90, 90, 95}.\n\n(a)   The trimean is defined as Q1+2Med+Q3\n\n4\n. Compute the trimean\nfor the above data. Show your work.\n(b)   The mean trimmed 50% is computed by trimming the\n\nupper 25% of the scores and the lower 25% of the scores and\nthen computing the mean of the remaining scores. Compute\nthe mean trimmed 50% for the above data. Show your work.\n(c)   More generally, to compute a trimmed mean, you remove\n\nsome of the higher and lower scores and then compute the\nmean of the remaining scores. In what way can the median\nbe considered a trimmed mean?\n(d)   In general, what advantage might both the trimean and a\n\ntrimmed mean have over using the standard mean, ¯x = ∑x\n\nn ?", "model": "(a) n=12：Q1=下6个数的中位数=88，Med=(89+90)/2=89.5，Q3=上6个数的中位数=90。三均值=(88+2×89.5+90)/4=357/4=89.25。(b) 各去掉最低3个和最高3个，剩{88,88,88,89,89,90}，均值=532/6≈88.67。(c) 中位数就是50%截尾均值：从两端各删去一半观测，只剩中间一个（或两个的平均）值。(d) 两者都对极端值和偏态更稳健（抗异常值影响），比普通均值更能代表偏态或含离群值数据的中心。", "image": "img/fr507.png"}, {"id": 508, "chapter": "FR 探索性分析", "stem": "Suppose we calculate the melting points of 10 nuggets claimed to\nbe pure silver and find the mean to be 1,760°F with a standard\ndeviation of 27°F.\n\n(a)   What would be the mean and standard deviation of the\n\nmelting points of the 10 nuggets if the 10 measurements were\nconverted to Celsius? (°F = (1.8)(°C) + 32)\n(b)   How would the conversion above have changed if the sample\n\nsize had been 20 nuggets instead of 10 nuggets?", "model": "(a) C=(F−32)/1.8，线性变换：均值°C=(1760−32)/1.8=960°C；标准差不受加减常数影响，仅除以1.8：s=27/1.8=15°C。(b) 换算公式与样本量无关，方法完全相同：均值仍为(样本均值°F−32)/1.8，标准差仍为s°F/1.8；只是n=20时样本均值与标准差的数值取决于这20块的数据。", "image": "img/fr508.png"}, {"id": 509, "chapter": "FR 探索性分析", "stem": "The mean absolute deviation (MAD) of a set {x1, … , xn} is\n\nn\n∑\n\ndefined to be MAD= 1\n\n|xi −¯x|.\n\nn\n\ni=1\n\n(a)   Compare the MADs of the sets with the two dotplots.\n\n(b)   After comparing the above answers, is this what was\n\nexpected?", "model": null, "image": "img/fr509.png"}, {"id": 510, "chapter": "FR 探索性分析", "stem": "Consider the following dotplot of 21 values.\n\n(a)   Calculate and compare the mean and median.\n(b)   Comment on whether your answer was as expected.", "model": null, "image": "img/fr510.png"}, {"id": 511, "chapter": "FR 探索性分析", "stem": "The selling prices of a simple random sample of 20 Florida\ncondos is summarized below.\n\n0–\n100\n100–\n200\n200–\n300\n300–\n400\n400–\n500\n\nPrice\n($1,000)\n\nFrequency\n1\n0\n10\n5\n4\n\n(a)   Draw a cumulative frequency plot of this data.\n(b)   If given the raw data, would the mean or the median best\n\ndescribe the typical selling price for Florida condos?\nExplain.", "model": "(a) 累积频率点（区间右端点）：(100,1),(200,1),(300,11),(400,16),(500,20)，用线段连接成递增阶梯状折线。(b) 用中位数更好。数据右偏（20套中10套在20–30万，另有9套在30–50万），均值会被高房价拉高，而中位数（约在20–30万区间内）更能代表典型售价。", "image": "img/fr511.png"}, {"id": 512, "chapter": "FR 探索性分析", "stem": "Fifty subjects with advanced heart disease participated in an\nexperiment to compare two new blood pressure medications, A\nand B. The subjects were randomly assigned into two groups, one\ngroup to take medication A and the other medication B. Every\nperson’s blood pressure was taken prior to assignment and again\nafter three months on the medication. The calculated\ndifferences, old pressure minus new pressure (+ represents\nimprovement), are shown in the following table.\n\nValues <\n\nValues\n\nQ1\nQ1\nMedian\nQ3\n\n> Q3\n\nMedication\n\nA\n–2, –1\n0\n3\n10\n12, 15\n\nB\n–2, 2\n3\n4\n5\n6, 10\n\n(a)   Draw parallel boxplots of the above data showing outliers, if\n\nany.\n(b)   Which medication should be chosen if the goal is to have the\n\ngreatest percentage of subjects lower their blood pressures?\nExplain.\n(c)   Which medication should be chosen if the goal is to have the\n\ngreatest mean improvement (lowering) in subjects’ blood\npressures? Explain.", "model": "(a) A：Min≈−2, Q1=0, Med=3, Q3=10, Max=15；IQR=10，上栅栏25、下栅栏−15，A无离群值，箱体长、右偏。B：Min=−2, Q1=3, Med=4, Q3=5, Max=10；IQR=2，栅栏0和8，故−2与10均为离群值（用*标出），箱体很短。(b) 选B：B的25人中仅1人差（−2），改善比例约24/25=96%；A有−2、−1和0，改善比例约22/25=88%。(c) 选A：A的上四分位为10且上方有12、15等大改善值，分布右偏使均值被拉高，平均改善更大；B几乎所有值集中在3–6，均值较小。", "image": "img/fr512.png"}, {"id": 513, "chapter": "FR 探索性分析", "stem": "The salaries of a random sample of 91 faculty members at a\nlarge university are summarized in the following histogram.\n(Assume salaries are at the center of each bar.)\n\n(a)   Construct a boxplot of the above data.\n(b)   What feature does the histogram show that is missed by the\n\nboxplot?\n(c)   What feature is more clearly distinguished in the boxplot\n\nthan in the histogram?", "model": null, "image": "img/fr513.png"}, {"id": 514, "chapter": "FR 探索性分析", "stem": "It is difficult to know when a minute has passed without looking\nat a clock. A random sample of 18 students consisted of 11 boys\nand 7 girls. With eyes closed, each student raised his/her hand\nwhen he/she guessed that a minute had passed. Their times were\nrecorded and are displayed below.\n\n(a)   In either the boy or girl distributions, are any of the times\n\nconsidered to be outliers? Explain.\n\n(b)   If all the times are combined, does the new set of times have\n\nany outliers? Explain.\n(c)   Explain the reason for the answer to (b) in relation to the\n\nanswer to (a).", "model": null, "image": "img/fr514.png"}, {"id": 515, "chapter": "FR 探索性分析", "stem": "Two species of insects were continually genetically modified over\nthe course of a 90-day period of time (their normal life\nexpectancy). The resulting age (in days) and gender distributions\nare given in the population pyramids below.\n\n(a)   In which interval is the median age of the Species B insects?\n\nExplain.\n(b)   In either or both species, have the modifications resulted in\n\nlonger life expectancies for males or females? Explain.\n(c)   Have the most recent modifications resulted in favoring\n\nmore births of a particular gender in either or both species?", "model": null, "image": "img/fr515.png"}, {"id": 516, "chapter": "FR 探索性分析", "stem": "A furniture store sells low-end and high-end outdoor patio\nfurniture. The selling prices ($) for their lounge chairs (low- and\nhigh-end) are summarized in the boxplots below.\n\n(a)   Compare the distributions of the prices of the lowand high-\n\nend lounge chairs.\n(b)   If a single histogram was made of all the lounge chair\n\nprices, describe its shape.", "model": null, "image": "img/fr516.png"}, {"id": 517, "chapter": "FR 探索性分析", "stem": "A cumulative relative frequency plot for scores (maximum\npossible 120) on an arcade video game during one 24-hour period\nis as follows.\n\n(a)   Interpret in context the meaning of the point (50, 0.2).\n(b)   Can you make an estimate for the number of scores below\n\n60?\n\n(c)   What is the median score?\n(d)   What proportion of the scores are over 80?\n(e)   What does the flat portion between 80 and 90 mean?", "model": null, "image": "img/fr517.png"}, {"id": 518, "chapter": "FR 探索性分析", "stem": "Cereal is the breakfast of choice for many children. Nutritional\ncontent varies widely, with dieticians saying that the best cereals\nare low in sugar, high in fiber, and full of protein. Nutritional\ncontent also varies widely among boxes of the same cereal. Below\nis summarized the nutritional content found in samples\nconsisting of 20 boxes taken of each of three cereals, all\nadvertised as “whole grain corn flakes.”\n\n(a)   Compare the fiber contents of the three cereals.\n(b)   If you used only one measurement—sugar, fiber, or protein\n\n—to identify which of these three cereals you were analyzing,\nwhat would you choose and why?\n(c)   If a chemical analysis of an unidentified box of one of these\n\ncereals shows a total of 30 grams of sugar, fiber, and protein\nper serving, which cereal would you guess you have? Explain.\n\nTWO-VARIABLE DATA ANALYSIS\n\nA simple random sample (SRS) of beachfront condo listings in\nMyrtle Beach comparing weekly rent ($) versus size (ft2) yields\nthe following computer output.", "model": null, "image": "img/fr518.png"}, {"id": 519, "chapter": "FR 探索性分析", "stem": "(a)   Is a linear model appropriate for these data? Explain.\n(b)   Interpret the slope of the regression line.\n(c)   Interpret r2 in context.", "model": null, "image": "img/fr519.png"}, {"id": 520, "chapter": "FR 探索性分析", "stem": "The calories and fat content per serving size of 10 brands of\npotato chips are fitted with a least squares regression line with\ncomputer output.\n\n(a)   Is a line an appropriate model? Explain.\n(b)   Interpret the slope of the regression line in context.\n\n(c)   Interpret the y-intercept of the regression line in context.\n(d)   What are the predicted calories for a brand with 10 g of fat\n\nper serving?\n(e)   What are the actual calories for the brand with 10 g of fat\n\nper serving?", "model": null, "image": "img/fr520.png"}, {"id": 521, "chapter": "FR 探索性分析", "stem": "A research group believes it can predict a subject’s ESP\n(telepathy and clairvoyance) testing result based on the average\nSAT math and verbal scores of those given ESP testing. The\nresearch group suspects that gender also makes a difference. A\nstratified random sample of high school students who have taken\nthe SATs are given ESP testing. Below are two least squares\nanalyses relating ESP test results to average SAT math and\nverbal scores for men and for women.\n\nA student has an average SAT math and verbal score of 689 and\nthen receives an ESP test result of 84. What would you guess is\nthe student’s gender? Justify your answer.", "model": null, "image": "img/fr521.png"}, {"id": 522, "chapter": "FR 探索性分析", "stem": "The following is a scatterplot of the number of hurricanes hitting\na Caribbean island each year after 1982 (t gives years since\n1982).\n\n(a)   Draw a histogram of the frequencies of the number of\n\nhurricanes.\n(b)   Name a feature apparent in the scatterplot but not in the\n\nhistogram.\n(c)   Name a feature shown by the histogram but not as obvious\n\nin the scatterplot.", "model": null, "image": "img/fr522.png"}, {"id": 523, "chapter": "FR 探索性分析", "stem": "Data are collected on the distance (in feet) that students sit from\nthe front of the class and their exam averages for the class (on a\n0–100 scale). A scatterplot of exam average y versus distance x\nfrom the front is described as linear, negative, and strong.\n\n(a)   In the above context, what is meant by linear, by negative,\n\nand by strong? The regression equation is ˆy = 97.5 – 1.15x.\n(b)   Interpret the slope in context.\n(c)   One student who sat 12 feet from the front had a residual of\n\n–4.7. What was that student’s exam average?", "model": "(a) 线性：散点大致沿一条直线分布；负相关：坐得离黑板越远，平均考试成绩越低；强：点紧密聚集在直线附近，散布小。(b) 斜率−1.15：距前排每远1英尺，考试成绩平均降低1.15分。(c) ŷ(12)=97.5−1.15×12=97.5−13.8=83.7。残差=y−ŷ=−4.7，故该生实际成绩y=83.7−4.7=79分。", "image": "img/fr523.png"}, {"id": 524, "chapter": "FR 探索性分析", "stem": "It is believed that customers will hesitate buying a luxury item if\nit seems to be priced too high or too low. A new-model boat is\noffered at different prices in eight different showrooms. The\nnumber sold versus price ($1,000) is fitted with a least squares\nregression line, yielding the following summary statistics and\nresidual plot.\n\n(a)   Comment on the use of a linear model for this data.\n(b)   How can this computer output be used to recommend the\n\nbest cost to achieve the most sales? Explain.", "model": null, "image": "img/fr524.png"}, {"id": 525, "chapter": "FR 探索性分析", "stem": "The table below gives weight loss (in pounds) during the first\nmonth for six overweight patients on varying dosages of an\nexperimental drug.\n\nDosage (grams), x\n0.5\n1.0\n1.5\n2.0\n2.5\n3.0\n\nWeight Loss\n\n(pounds), y\n7\n10\n12\n14\n16\n17\n\nLinear regression lines on (x, y), on the transformed data (x, log\ny), and on the transformed data (√x, y) result in the following\ncomputer output, respectively.\n\n(a)   Interpret the coefficient of determination for the\n\ntransformed data (√x, y).\n\n(b)   Compare the three regression lines as to goodness-of-fit for\n\na linear model.", "model": null, "image": "img/fr525.png"}, {"id": 526, "chapter": "FR 探索性分析", "stem": "In a study of salaries of young engineers at a software company,\ntwo regression analyses are run, the first on salary versus years\nof college education and the second on salary versus age. The\ngraphs of the residuals follow.\n\n(a)   Which of the regression lines indicates a better linear fit?\n\nExplain.\n(b)   The two regression lines are used to find estimates for the\n\nsalary of a 40-year-old software engineer with 4 years of\ncollege education. One of the engineers in the sample is 40\nyears old and has 4 years of college education. Which of the\nestimates are underestimates and which are overestimates of\nthe salary of this engineer? Explain.\n\nCHAPTER\n\nFR Collecting and Producing Data\nAnswers for Chapter 6 are on pages 310–314.\n\n6", "model": null, "image": "img/fr526.png"}, {"id": 527, "chapter": "FR 数据收集", "stem": "A researcher wants to determine if watching TV from five feet or\ncloser damages a person’s eyes. The researcher wants to know if\nthis is truth or a myth.\n\n(a)   How can an observational study be performed?\n(b)   How can an experiment be performed?\n(c)   Which approach is more appropriate here? Explain.\n(d)   If an experimental design was implemented, give an ethical\n\nconsideration that would cause the researchers to halt the\nexperiment early.", "model": "(a) 观察研究：抽取样本，调查/测量每个人看电视的距离（≤5英尺或更远）以及时间，随后检查其视力损伤情况，比较两组眼睛健康状况。(b) 实验：将志愿者随机分成两组，一组规定在≤5英尺处看规定时长的电视，另一组在更远处（或作为对照），一段时间后测量并比较两组视力损伤。(c) 观察研究更合适：若近距离看电视确有害，随机指派可能有损受试者健康，伦理上不可行。(d) 伦理考虑：一旦有证据表明近距离看电视正在损害受试者视力，应立即中止实验。", "image": "img/fr527.png"}, {"id": 528, "chapter": "FR 数据收集", "stem": "High A1C levels (a test for diabetes) can be reduced by either a\nlow-fat diet or a medication such as metformin. Researchers\nwould like to test the effectiveness of metformin and to note\nwhether the effectiveness, if any, is enhanced by diet. A random\nsample of adults with high A1C levels, on no special diets, and\nnot on medication, are recruited for a study.\n\n(a)   Conclusions will apply to what population?\n(b)   Explain how you would design a completely randomized\n\nexperiment.\n(c)   How might you incorporate blocking and for what purpose?\n(d)   How might blinding be incorporated in this study and for\n\nwhat purpose?", "model": "(a) 结论适用于：A1C偏高、未服药、未进行特殊饮食的成年人群（与研究样本同质的人群）。(b) 完全随机化设计：将受试者随机分为四组：安慰剂（对照）、仅低脂饮食、仅二甲双胍、饮食+药物（2×2析因），一段时间后比较各组A1C下降值。(c) 按基线A1C水平（或年龄、性别）分块，在每块内随机分配处理，以减少该变量引入的变异，更精确地估计药物和饮食效应。(d) 可采用双盲：给所有人口服外观相同的药片（药物或安慰剂），并让评估A1C结果的测量人员不知分组，以减少安慰剂效应和测量者偏差。", "image": "img/fr528.png"}, {"id": 529, "chapter": "FR 数据收集", "stem": "A company efficiency expert believes that employees who eat at\nleast 1,000 calories at breakfast have higher productivity levels\n\nat work. She interviews a simple random sample (SRS) of 30\nemployees who claim to eat under 1,000 calories at breakfast and\nan SRS of 25 employees who claim to eat over 1,000 calories at\nbreakfast. In each group, she looks up productivity levels on the\njob.\n\n(a)   Explain why this is an observational study and not an\n\nexperiment.\n(b)   Give an example of a possible confounding variable with an\n\nexplanation in the context of this study.\n(c)   If the employees who eat over 1,000 calories have higher\n\nproductivity records, is it reasonable to encourage all\nemployees to eat larger breakfasts? Explain.\n(d)   How could the efficiency expert design an experiment to\n\nstudy caloric intake at breakfast with productivity in the\nworkplace?", "model": "(a) 她没有施加任何处理：只是调查员工早餐热量摄入并查阅其已有的生产率记录，分组是自然形成而非随机分配的。(b) 混杂变量如总体健康意识/生活习惯：注重健康的人可能既吃丰盛早餐又工作更高效，导致无法区分是早餐还是健康习惯提高了生产率。(c) 不合理：这是观察研究，存在混杂变量，只能说明相关性，不能建立因果关系；鼓励所有人多吃早餐没有依据。(d) 实验：随机将员工分成两组，一组提供>1000卡早餐（或要求食用），另一组<1000卡，一段时间后用统一标准测量并比较两组生产率。", "image": "img/fr529.png"}, {"id": 530, "chapter": "FR 数据收集", "stem": "A pastor expects his parishioners to spend some time each week\nin Bible study. He randomly selects 30 parishioners from among\nthose coming to church one Sunday and asks each the number of\nminutes spent reading the Bible during the previous week. Using\nthis data, the pastor’s secretary, who once took a college\nstatistics class, calculates the following.\n\nn = 30 ¯x = 31.2 s = 13.2 Min = 13.5\n\nQ1 = 18.7 Med = 27 Q3 = 41.2 Max = 57\n\nThe pastor asks the secretary to compute a confidence interval\nestimate of the mean number of minutes parishioners read the\nBible weekly. Comment on this study, including necessary\nassumptions and how well they are met. (You are not asked to\ncalculate the confidence interval.)", "model": "该研究存在若干问题：①样本仅来自某个周日的到场教徒，属于便利样本，可能漏掉不常到教堂者，结论推广到全体教徒有偏；自报时间也可能有误差。②构造均值的t置信区间需假设：随机样本（不满足/存疑）；总体近似正态或n≥30。这里n=30属边界情况，且均值31.2>中位数27提示右偏；但Q3+1.5IQR=41.2+33.75≈75，最大值57不是离群值，偏态不算严重，勉强可用t区间，但应说明这些顾虑。", "image": "img/fr530.png"}, {"id": 531, "chapter": "FR 数据收集", "stem": "A manufacturer of circuit boards wishes to test which of two\nsoldering irons leads to fewer soldering errors. The workroom\nconsists of eight large tables, and the manufacturer plans to have\neveryone at a given table use the same soldering iron. On one\nside of the room are windows with a pretty view, and on one side\nis the floor manager’s desk.\n\n(a)   Suppose you decide to block using the Scheme A below (one\n\nblock is white, one gray). How would you use randomization,\nand what is the purpose of the randomization? (You are not\nasked to give an actual randomization procedure.)\n\n(b)   Comment on the strength and weakness of Scheme A as\n\ncompared to blocking Scheme B (one block is white, one\ngray).", "model": null, "image": "img/fr531.png"}, {"id": 532, "chapter": "FR 数据收集", "stem": "A high school math department conducts a study to determine\nwhether a classroom with windows leads to higher exam scores\nthan a classroom without any windows. Two algebra classes are\nscheduled, each with 25 students. It is randomly decided which\nclass will use which classroom. During the year, each teacher\nadministers the same exams. At the end of the academic year,\noverall exam grades are compared.\n\n(a)   Identify the response variable, the treatments, and the\n\nexperimental units.\n(b)   Was randomization properly used? Explain.\n(c)   Was replication properly used? Explain.\n(d)   Teacher is a confounding variable. Explain.", "model": "(a) 反应变量：学年末整体考试成绩；处理：有窗教室 vs 无窗教室；试验单元：学生（两个班共50名学生）。(b) 勉强算有：哪个班用哪间教室是随机决定的，但只有两个班，随机化在班级层面且只有一次，不能平衡班内差异。(c) 不充分：每种处理只有一个班（25名学生虽是重复观测，但处理未在多个班级上重复），班级/教师层面无重复。(d) 两个班由不同教师执教，教师水平、教学风格与是否有窗完全重合，教师效应与处理效应无法区分，故教师是混杂变量。", "image": "img/fr532.png"}, {"id": 533, "chapter": "FR 数据收集", "stem": "How does the American public now feel about leaving\nAfghanistan? Two surveys are conducted. The survey in VFW\n(Veterans of Foreign Wars) magazine asks readers to respond\nconcerning whether or not they support the “retreat from\nAfghanistan.” The survey in the newspaper the Washington Post\nasks readers to respond concerning whether or not they support\nthe “phased troop redeployment from Afghanistan.”\n\n(a)   Give two examples of bias that may have been introduced\n\ngiven the ways in which the samples were selected.\n(b)   Explain how bias may have been introduced given the ways\n\nin which the surveys were worded. Suggest different wording\nto avoid that bias.", "model": "(a) 两例偏差：①自愿回应偏差——只有主动读者会寄回问卷，观点极端者更可能回应；②抽样框/覆盖偏差——VFW杂志读者多为退伍军人（立场偏强硬），华盛顿邮报读者偏自由派，两样本均不能代表美国公众。(b) 措辞偏差：'retreat（撤退）'带负面含义会压低支持率，'phased troop redeployment（分阶段调兵）'较中性甚至正面会抬高支持率。可改用中性措辞，如：'你是否支持将美国军队撤出阿富汗？'", "image": "img/fr533.png"}, {"id": 534, "chapter": "FR 数据收集", "stem": "A popular coffee chain has 11,500 stores in the U.S. and 13,500\nstores outside the U.S. Each store has approximately 10 full-time\nemployees. Suppose the company would like to survey 250 of its\nemployees about new coffee drinks under consideration. Under\ndiscussion are three sampling methods.\n\nMethod 1: A simple random sample\nMethod 2: Randomly pick 125 of their 115,000 U.S. full-time\n\nemployees and randomly pick 125 of their 135,000\nnon-U.S. full-time employees\nMethod 3: Randomly pick 25 of their 25,000 stores and pick all\n\nfull-time employees at these 25 stores\n\n(a) Give a design for carrying out Method 1, and give a\n\ndisadvantage of using Method 1.\n(b)   What is Method 2 called? What is an advantage of Method 2\n\nin this context?\n(c)   What is Method 3 called? What is an advantage of Method 3\n\nin this context?", "model": "(a) 将全部250,000名全职员工编号，用随机数表/随机数生成器抽取250人调查。缺点：员工遍布全球2.5万家门店，逐人联系成本高、费时；且可能美国内外员工比例失衡。(b) 分层随机抽样（按美国/非美国分两层）。优点：保证两类员工都按比例被代表，可估计并比较两层的意见，精度更高。(c) 整群抽样（以门店为群）。优点：只需走访25家门店，对整店员工集中调查，组织方便、成本低。", "image": "img/fr534.png"}, {"id": 535, "chapter": "FR 数据收集", "stem": "A medical insurance adjuster wants to interview a random\nsample of 50 doctors in a city. Suppose there are 25 hospitals,\neach with 50 doctors.\n\n(a)   Describe a procedure for using stratified sampling using the\n\nhospitals as strata.\n(b)   Describe a procedure for using cluster sampling using the\n\nhospitals as clusters.\n(c)   In this context, give an advantage of using stratified\n\nsampling over cluster sampling.\n(d)   In this context, give an advantage of using cluster sampling\n\nover stratified sampling.", "model": "(a) 分层抽样：以25家医院为层，从每家医院的50名医生中随机抽取2名，共50名。(b) 整群抽样：随机抽取1家医院，访谈该院全部50名医生。(c) 分层优点：样本包含每家医院的医生，能给出更精确（变异更小）的全市估计，还可比较各医院。(d) 整群优点：只需前往一家医院，省时省钱、便于实施。", "image": "img/fr535.png"}, {"id": 536, "chapter": "FR 数据收集", "stem": "A reading specialist plans a study to determine if high school\nstudents read faster from a physical book or on an iPad. The\nreading specialist randomly selects 60 high school students for\nthe study.\n\n(a)   Describe a randomization process and an inference\n\nprocedure for the study to be conducted with a completely\nrandomized design.\n(b)   Describe a randomization process and an inference\n\nprocedure for the study to be conducted with a matched\npairs design.\n\nCHAPTER\n\nFR Probability\nAnswers for Chapter 7 are on pages 314–320.\n\n7", "model": "(a) 完全随机化：将60名学生随机均分为两组，一组读纸质书的指定文章，另一组在iPad上读同一文章，记录阅读时间（或答对率）；用两样本t检验比较两组平均阅读时间。(b) 匹配对：每位学生先后各读一篇难度相当的短文，一篇纸质、一篇iPad，顺序由随机决定（抵消顺序效应）；对每位学生计算两介质阅读时间之差，用单样本t检验差值均值是否为0。", "image": "img/fr536.png"}, {"id": 537, "chapter": "FR 概率", "stem": "A chess match is played with a series of games. The winner of a\ngame receives 1 point, while a drawn game gives each player 1\n\n2\npoint. A player who scores 2 points before his or her opponent\nwins the match. Suppose Player A is the stronger player and\nwhen playing Player B will win with a probability of 0.6 and will\ndraw with a probability 0.3. The outcome of any game is\nindependent of the outcome of any other game. Player A and\nPlayer B begin a match.\n\n(a)   What is the probability Player A wins the match in 2 games?\n(b)   What is the probability Player A wins the match after\n\nexactly 3 games?\n(c)   What is the probability that the first game was a draw given\n\nthat Player A wins the match in exactly 3 games?\n(d)   What is the probability Player A wins the second game\n\ngiven that he or she draws the first game?", "model": "设A胜概率0.6，平局0.3（B胜0.1）。(a) P=0.6×0.6=0.36。(b) A恰在第3局获胜：前两局A得2分未封顶且第3局A胜：WDW、DWW：2×0.6×0.3×0.6=0.216；或DDW：0.3×0.3×0.6=0.054；合计0.216+0.054=0.27。(c) P(首局平|3局获胜)=P(DWW)/0.27=0.108/0.27=0.4。(d) 各局独立，故P=0.6。", "image": "img/fr537.png"}, {"id": 538, "chapter": "FR 概率", "stem": "You are asked to choose between two envelopes, one of which has\ntwice as much money as the other. You arbitrarily pick one, open\nit, and find $2. You are then given the chance to switch\nenvelopes. You reason that the other envelope has either $1 or $4,\neach with a probability of 0.5. Applying your understanding of\nexpected value, you calculate 0.5($1) + 0.5($4) = $2.50 and\nconclude that you should switch envelopes. Comment on this\nreasoning.", "model": "该推理有误。看到$2后另一信封为$1或$4的概率并非必然各0.5——这取决于金额的先验分布，$2这个信息本身会改变条件概率。更关键的是：无论打开看到多少金额，同样的论证都会建议换信封，换完后同样的论证又建议换回来，产生矛盾；换信封不可能在没有新信息时带来正的期望收益（对称性），因此期望值$2.50的计算不成立，换与不换期望相同。", "image": "img/fr538.png"}, {"id": 539, "chapter": "FR 概率", "stem": "The probability that Anthony Rizzo of the Chicago Cubs hits a\nhomer on any given at bat is 0.06, and we assume each at bat is\nindependent.\n\n(a)   What is the probability the next homer will be on his fifth at\n\nbat?\n(b)   What is the probability he hits exactly one homer in five at\n\nbats?\n(c)   What is the expected number of homers in every 10 at bats?\n(d)   What is the expected number of at bats until the next\n\nhomer?", "model": "(a) 几何分布：P=(0.94)^4×0.06≈0.047。(b) 二项分布：C(5,1)(0.06)(0.94)^4≈0.236。(c) 期望=10×0.06=0.6支本垒打。(d) 几何分布期望=1/0.06≈16.7次打击。", "image": "img/fr539.png"}, {"id": 540, "chapter": "FR 概率", "stem": "The weights of babies born to nonsmokers have a normal\ndistribution with a mean of 7.0 pounds and a standard deviation\nof 0.8 pounds. Babies are considered low birth weight (LBW) if\nthey weigh less than 5.5 pounds. Note that 5 percent of babies\nborn to smokers are LBW and that 16 percent of pregnant\nwomen are smokers.\n\n(a)   What is the probability that a nonsmoker will have an LBW\n\nbaby?\n(b)   What is the probability a baby is LBW?\n(c)   Given that a baby is LBW, what is the probability the baby\n\nwas born to a nonsmoker?", "model": "设N=非吸烟产妇，S=吸烟（P(S)=0.16）。(a) z=(5.5−7.0)/0.8=−1.875，P(LBW|N)≈0.0304。(b) 全概率：P(LBW)=0.84×0.0304+0.16×0.05≈0.0255+0.008=0.0335。(c) 贝叶斯：P(N|LBW)=0.0255/0.0335≈0.762。", "image": "img/fr540.png"}, {"id": 541, "chapter": "FR 概率", "stem": "An online business charges shipping and handling (S&H) fees\nbased on the sizes of the sales. 30 percent of the company’s sales\nare under $10, 40 percent are between $10 and $25, 20 percent\nare between $25 and $50, 5 percent are between $50 and $100,\nand 5 percent are over $100. The charges are as follows, and note\nthat S&H is free for orders over $100!\n\n10–\n25\n25–\n50\n50–\n100\n>100\n\nSale ($)\n<10\n\n2.50\n5.00\n7.50\n10.00\n0.00\n\nS&H Charge\n\n($)\n\n(a)   What is the mean S&H charge? Show your work.\n(b)   What is the median S&H charge? In other words, what\n\ncharge C has the property that P(x ≥ C) ≥ 0.5 and P(x ≤ C) ≥\n0.5? Explain.", "model": "(a) E=0.30×2.50+0.40×5.00+0.20×7.50+0.05×10.00+0.05×0=0.75+2.00+1.50+0.50+0=4.75美元。(b) 中位数C=5.00美元：累积概率 P(x≤5)=0.30+0.40=0.70≥0.5，P(x≥5)=0.70≥0.5，满足定义。", "image": "img/fr541.png"}, {"id": 542, "chapter": "FR 概率", "stem": "Die A has three 5s, two 3s, and one 1 on its six faces. Die B has\ntwo 2s and four 4s on its six faces. Both dice are fair. Each player\nsimultaneously rolls one of the dice, and the winner is the player\nwith the higher number showing.\n\n(a)   If you want to win, would you rather roll die A or die B?\n\nExplain.\n(b)   If the winner receives whatever shows on his/her winning\n\ndie, what is the expected value for one roll to each player?\nExplain.", "model": "A骰：P(5)=1/2, P(3)=1/3, P(1)=1/6；B骰：P(2)=1/3, P(4)=2/3。(a) P(A胜)=A出5(0.5)必胜 + A出3胜B的2：(1/3)(1/3)=1/9，合计11/18≈0.611>1/2，应选A骰。(b) 获胜者骰面所得的期望：掷A者获胜时得5或3：E_A=0.5×5+1/9×3=17/6≈2.83；掷B者仅在B=4且A为3或1时胜得4：E_B=(2/9+1/9)×4=4/3≈1.33。总期望=77/18≈4.28。", "image": "img/fr542.png"}, {"id": 543, "chapter": "FR 概率", "stem": "The probability distribution for the number of days per week\nthat college students get a good night’s sleep is as follows.\n\nNumber of Days\n0\n1\n2\n3\n4\n\nRelative Frequency\n0.12\n0.41\n0.25\n0.15\n0.07\n\n(a)   Calculate and give a brief interpretation of the mean of this\n\nprobability distribution.\n(b)   In a random sample of 10 college students, there are a total\n\nof 20 good nights of sleep. A new random sample of 50\nstudents is planned. How do you expect the average number\nof good nights of sleep for this new sample to compare to that\nof the first sample? Explain.\n\n(q)   Find the median of the above distribution, where the\n\nmedian M is defined to be a value such that P(x ≥ M) ≥ 0.5\nand P(x ≤ M) ≥ 0.5.", "model": "(a) 均值=0×0.12+1×0.41+2×0.25+3×0.15+4×0.07=0.41+0.50+0.45+0.28=1.64。解释：大学生平均每周约有1.64个晚上睡得好。(b) 第一组样本均值=20/10=2高于总体均值1.64；新样本容量更大（50），其样本均值预期更接近1.64，即平均好眠天数更小、波动更小。(q) 累积概率：P(x≤0)=0.12，P(x≤1)=0.53≥0.5，且P(x≥1)=0.88≥0.5，故中位数M=1。", "image": "img/fr543.png"}, {"id": 544, "chapter": "FR 概率", "stem": "A school must choose between two snow removal services.\nService A charges $2,500 yearly plus $2,000 for every month with\nover three snowfalls necessitating their services. Service B\ncharges $450 per snow removal. Relevant probabilities are\nshown in the following tables.\n\nMonth\nOct\nNov\nDec\nJan\nFeb\nMar\nApr\n\nP(> 3\nsnowfall\nservices)\n0.01\n0.02\n0.18\n0.28\n0.22\n0.13\n0.02\n\nAnnual\nNumber of\n\nSnow\nServices\n6\n7\n8\n9\n10\n11\n12\n\nProbability\n0.05\n0.10\n0.20\n0.25\n0.20\n0.15\n0.05\n\nWhich service should the school use to minimize expected cost?\nJustify your answer.", "model": "E[A]=2500+2000×E[超3次降雪的月数]；E[月数]=0.01+0.02+0.18+0.28+0.22+0.13+0.02=0.86，故E[A]=2500+1720=4220美元。E[B]=450×E[年除雪次数]；E[次数]=6(0.05)+7(0.10)+8(0.20)+9(0.25)+10(0.20)+11(0.15)+12(0.05)=9.1，故E[B]=450×9.1=4095美元。因为4095<4220，应选Service B。", "image": "img/fr544.png"}, {"id": 545, "chapter": "FR 概率", "stem": "Suppose a pregnancy test correctly tests positive for 98 percent\nof pregnant women but also gives a false positive reading for 3\npercent of women who are not pregnant. Suppose 5 percent of\nwomen who purchase this over-thecounter test are actually\npregnant.\n\n(a)   What is the probability a woman tests positive?\n\n(b)   If a woman tests positive, what is the probability she is\n\npregnant?\n(c)   If two women purchase the test and both test positive, what\n\nis the probability that exactly one of the two is pregnant?", "model": "(a) 全概率：P(+)=0.05×0.98+0.95×0.03=0.049+0.0285=0.0775。(b) 贝叶斯：P(孕|+)=0.049/0.0775≈0.632。(c) q=1−0.632=0.368，两次独立均阳性时恰一人怀孕：P=2×0.632×0.368≈0.465。", "image": "img/fr545.png"}, {"id": 546, "chapter": "FR 概率", "stem": "One model of a popular tablet computer has a life expectancy\nthat is roughly normally distributed with a mean of 24 months\nand a standard deviation of 3 months.\n\n(a)   The warranty will repair any computer failing within 18\n\nmonths free of charge. The company’s contracted cost to\nrepair computers is $350. What is the expected value of the\ncost to the company per computer?\n(b)   Suppose the company decides it is willing to extend the\n\nwarranty to an additional 5 percent of the computers. What\nwarranty should the company offer?", "model": "(a) P(寿命<18)=P(z<(18−24)/3=−2)=0.0228，期望成本=0.0228×350≈7.98美元/台。(b) 延保至最低5%：求第5百分位数，z=−1.645，x=24−1.645×3≈19.1个月，即保修期约19个月（原18个月仅覆盖约2.28%）。", "image": "img/fr546.png"}, {"id": 547, "chapter": "FR 概率", "stem": "Simple random samples of younger adults and older adults in a\nlarge city were surveyed as to the number of apps on their smart\nphones. The data are summarized in the following table.\n\nIs there evidence that either of these samples were drawn from\npopulations with roughly normal distributions? Explain.", "model": null, "image": "img/fr547.png"}, {"id": 548, "chapter": "FR 概率", "stem": "Simple random samples of younger adults and older adults in a\nlarge city were surveyed as to the amount of credit card debt,\nand the data are summarized in the following table.\n\nAssume that both samples are drawn from roughly normally\ndistributed populations. Would a greater percentage of younger\nor of older adults more likely be able to pay off their credit debt\nwith $8,000? Explain.", "model": null, "image": "img/fr548.png"}, {"id": 549, "chapter": "FR 概率", "stem": "Medical scientists applying to the CDC for grants are awarded\nscores for their grant applications. These scores follow a roughly\nnormal distribution. All applicants with scores more than 1.5\nstandard deviations above the mean are awarded grants, and all\napplicants with scores that are outliers on the high end are\nawarded additional lab equipment in addition to the grants.\n\n(a)   What is the probability a randomly selected scientist\n\nreceives the grant?\n(b)   What is the probability that at least one out of three\n\nrandomly chosen scientists receives the grant?\n(c)   What is the probability a randomly selected scientist\n\nreceives the grant and the additional lab equipment?\n(d)   What is the probability a randomly selected scientist\n\nreceives the grant but not the additional lab equipment?", "model": "设得分X~N(μ,σ)。(a) P(z>1.5)=1−0.9332=0.0668。(b) P(至少1人)=1−(1−0.0668)^3=1−0.9332^3≈1−0.813=0.187。(c) 高端离群值（z>3）：P(z>3)=0.0013。(d) P(1.5<z≤3)=0.0668−0.0013=0.0655。", "image": "img/fr549.png"}, {"id": 550, "chapter": "FR 概率", "stem": "New Caledonian crows are among the smartest animals on this\nplanet. An experiment testing the length of time it takes these\ncrows to solve a particular puzzle (and receive a food reward)\nfinds that 5 percent of the crows fail. Of those that do solve the\npuzzle, their times are roughly normally distributed with a mean\nof 1.8 minutes and a standard deviation of 0.4 minutes.\n\n(a)   What is the probability a randomly selected crow solves the\n\npuzzle in under 1.5 minutes?\n\n(b)   What is the probability a randomly selected crow has not\n\nsolved the puzzle in 2.0 minutes?", "model": "(a) P=0.95×P(T<1.5|解出)；z=(1.5−1.8)/0.4=−0.75，P(z<−0.75)=0.2266，故P=0.95×0.2266≈0.215。(b) P(2分钟未解出)=P(解不出)+P(能解但用时>2)=0.05+0.95×P(z>(2.0−1.8)/0.4=0.5)=0.05+0.95×0.3085≈0.343。", "image": "img/fr550.png"}, {"id": 551, "chapter": "FR 概率", "stem": "MLB signing bonuses for different position players with\nrespective probabilities are given in the table below.\n\n\nPosition\n\nBonus\nPitcher\nInfielder\nOutfielder\n\n$2,000,000\n0.30\n0.30\n0.20\n\n$5,000,000\n0.10\n0.05\n0.05\n\n(a)   What is the probability a given bonus was for a pitcher?\n(b)   What is the expected value for a bonus?\n(c)   Are position and bonus independent? Explain.", "model": "先求各位置总概率:投手0.40、内野手0.35、外野手0.25。\n(a) P(投手)=0.40。(由表中列相加或按题意汇总)\n(b) E(奖金)=∑x·P(x):200万×0.8+500万×0.2=160+100=260万美元(先求无条件奖金分布:P(2M)=0.8,P(5M)=0.2)。\n(c) 不独立。例如P(投手且2M)=0.30,而P(投手)×P(2M)=0.40×0.80=0.32≠0.30,故位置与奖金不独立。", "image": "img/fr551.png"}, {"id": 552, "chapter": "FR 概率", "stem": "A cereal box is advertised to hold 16 ounces of corn flakes.\nSuppose the amounts per box are roughly normally distributed\nwith a standard deviation of 0.05 ounces and a mean m that the\nproducer can set. An inspector randomly samples two boxes, and\nthe company is fined $10,000 for each box found to be under 16\nounces.\n\n(a)   If the company is willing to accept an expected value of $125\n\nfor the fine, what probability p should it be willing to accept\nwhere p is the probability that a box is under 16 ounces?\n(b)   What should m be set at if the company is willing to accept\n\nan expected value of $125 for the fine?", "model": "设罚款期望=10000×2p=20000p(两箱,每箱不足16盎司罚1万)。\n(a) 令20000p=125? 注意两箱期望罚款=20000p=125⇒p=0.00625? 若按单箱考虑:10000p=62.5⇒p=0.00625(每箱)。\n(b) 需P(X<16)=p,其中X~N(μ,0.05²)。z=(16-μ)/0.05 满足P(Z<z)=p。取z=-2.50(P≈0.0062),则16-μ=0.05×(-2.50)=-0.125,故μ=16.125盎司。", "image": "img/fr552.png"}, {"id": 553, "chapter": "FR 概率", "stem": "The weights of individual apples are approximately normally\ndistributed with a mean of 8 ounces and a standard deviation of\n0.5 ounces. The weights of individual oranges are approximately\n\nnormally distributed with a mean of 6 ounces and a standard\ndeviation of 0.4 ounces. The weights of individual pieces of fruit\nare independent.\n\n(a)   What is the distribution of the total fruit weight of fruit gift\n\nboxes containing 6 randomly selected apples and 6 randomly\nselected oranges?\n(b)   The gift boxes are advertised as containing 5 pounds of\n\nfruit. What is the probability that a gift box contains at least\n5 pounds of fruit?\n(c)   An empty gift box weighs exactly 12 ounces. What is the\n\ndistribution of total weights (box plus fruit) of this gift\noffering?", "model": "(a) 总重T=6苹果+6橘子:μT=6×8+6×6=84盎司;σT=√(6×0.5²+6×0.4²)=√(1.5+0.96)=√2.46≈1.57盎司,近似正态。\n(b) 5磅=80盎司。z=(80-84)/1.57≈-2.55,P(T≥80)=P(Z>-2.55)≈0.9946。\n(c) 加空盒12盎司(常数):μ=84+12=96盎司,标准差不变仍为1.57盎司,仍近似正态。", "image": "img/fr553.png"}, {"id": 554, "chapter": "FR 概率", "stem": "The standard triathlon is a 1.5 km swim, 40 km bike ride, and 10\nkm run. In a random sampling of recent competitions, the mean\nand SD of the participants’ times for each event were as shown\nin the table.\n\n\nSwim\nBike Ride\nRun\n\nMean (minutes)\n30\n90\n70\n\nSD (minutes)\n5\n10\n10\n\nAssume the times for the three legs of the race are each roughly\nnormally distributed and independent.\n\n(a)   What is the distribution of total times to complete the\n\ntriathlon?\n(b)   What is the probability that a participant will complete the\n\ntriathlon in less than 180 minutes?", "model": "(a) 总时间T:μ=30+90+70=190分钟,σ=√(5²+10²+10²)=√225=15分钟,由独立性及各段正态,T近似正态。\n(b) z=(180-190)/15=-0.67,P(T<180)=P(Z<-0.67)≈0.2514,约25%。", "image": "img/fr554.png"}, {"id": 555, "chapter": "FR 概率", "stem": "An adult amusement park ride has a carrying capacity of 1,650\nlb. Suppose the men at the amusement park have a mean weight\n\nof 175 lb with a standard deviation of 15 lb. Suppose the women\nhave a mean weight of 130 lb with a standard deviation of 10 lb.\nAssume both weight distributions are roughly normal.\n\n(a)   What are the mean µSUM and standard deviation µSUM of\n\nthe combined weight of 6 men and 4 women, assuming all\nweights are independent?\n(b)   What is the probability that the 6 men and 4 women will\n\noverload the ride?", "model": "(a) μSUM=6×175+4×130=1050+520=1570磅;σSUM=√(6×15²+4×10²)=√(1350+400)=√1750≈41.8磅(各权重独立)。\n(b) 超载即总重>1650:z=(1650-1570)/41.8≈1.91,P≈0.028,约2.8%的可能会超载。", "image": "img/fr555.png"}, {"id": 556, "chapter": "FR 概率", "stem": "Suppose that women’s times for the 200-meter sprint have a\nroughly normal distribution with a mean of 25.2 seconds and a\nstandard deviation of 1.2 seconds. Suppose that men’s times have\na roughly normal distribution with a mean of 22.8 seconds and a\nstandard deviation of 0.9 seconds. A male and a female sprinter\nare picked at random. Assume their times are independent.\n\n(a)   What is the probability the sum of their sprints is over 50\n\nseconds?\n(b)   What is the probability that the man sprinted faster than\n\nthe woman?", "model": "(a) 和S:μ=25.2+22.8=48秒,σ=√(1.2²+0.9²)=√2.25=1.5秒。z=(50-48)/1.5=1.33,P(S>50)=P(Z>1.33)≈0.0918。\n(b) 差D=男-女:μ=-2.4,σ=1.5秒。男生更快即D<0:z=(0-(-2.4))/1.5=1.6,P(D<0)=P(Z<1.6)≈0.945。", "image": "img/fr556.png"}, {"id": 557, "chapter": "FR 概率", "stem": "Eddie Feigner, the world’s best softball pitcher, routinely threw\nan underhand softball at over 100 mph. Suppose his fastball\nspeeds were roughly normally distributed with a mean of 98.3\nmph and a standard deviation of 3.2 mph.\n\n(a)   Would it have been very unusual for him to throw a 102\n\nmph fastball? Explain.\n(b)   Would it have been very unusual for him to average 102\n\nmph over 35 random fastballs? Explain.", "model": "(a) z=(102-98.3)/3.2≈1.16,P(Z≥1.16)≈0.12,并不算非常异常(约12%的球会更快)。\n(b) 样本均值标准差=3.2/√35≈0.54,z=(102-98.3)/0.54≈6.85,概率远小于0.0001,平均达到102 mph将极其异常。", "image": "img/fr557.png"}, {"id": 558, "chapter": "FR 概率", "stem": "Suppose the number of minutes per day high school students\nspend eating lunch in the school cafeteria is roughly normally\ndistributed with a mean of 19 minutes.\n\n(a)   Which is more likely: a simple random sample (SRS) of 25\n\nstudents eating lunch an average of less than 18 minutes per\nday or an SRS of 75 students eating lunch an average of less\nthan 18 minutes per day? Explain.\n(b)   Suppose the sampling distribution of ¯x for samples of size\n\n100 has a standard deviation of 0.8 minutes. What is the\nprobability of an SRS of 100 students eating lunch an\naverage of more than 21 minutes?\n(c)   Suppose the original population is not roughly normal but,\n\nrather, is skewed right (toward the higher values). How\nwould your calculation in (b) change?\n\nCHAPTER\n\nFR Statistical Inference\nAnswers for Chapter 8 are on pages 320–339.\n\n8", "model": "(a) n=25的样本更可能。样本量越大,均值的标准差σ/√n越小,分布更集中于19分钟,故P(x̄<18)更小;75人样本的均值波动更小,更难低于18。\n(b) σx̄=0.8,z=(21-19)/0.8=2.5,P(x̄>21)=P(Z>2.5)≈0.0062。\n(c) n=100足够大,由中心极限定理x̄的抽样分布仍近似正态,(b)的计算基本不变。", "image": "img/fr558.png"}, {"id": 559, "chapter": "FR 统计推断", "stem": "In a random sample of 150 adults over the age of 45, 30 say they have played in\na band at least one time in their lives.\n\n(a)   Construct a 99% confidence interval for the proportion of all adults over\n\nthe age of 45 who have played in a band at least one time in their lives.\n(b)   Suppose all adults over the age of 45 who have played in a band at least\n\none time in their lives each send in a donation of $10 to the organization\nMusicians without Borders, which aims to empower musicians as social\nactivists. Assuming there are 125,000,000 adults over the age of 45 in the\nU.S., what is a 99% confidence interval for what these donations would\ntotal for this worthwhile charity?", "model": "(a) p̂=30/150=0.2,检查np̂=30、n(1-p̂)=120均≥10。99%CI:0.2±2.576√(0.2×0.8/150)=0.2±2.576×0.0327=0.2±0.084,即(0.116, 0.284)。\n(b) 捐款总额=1.25亿×p,99%CI为125,000,000×(0.116, 0.284)≈($14.5百万, $35.5百万)。", "image": "img/fr559.png"}, {"id": 560, "chapter": "FR 统计推断", "stem": "There are 12,500 high school students in a large city school district.\nAdministrators and teachers want to determine the extent to which parents do\nhomework for their children. In an anonymous survey, 150 of the 500 students\nsay that someone else has done their homework at least once.\n\nOf those 150 students, 90 or 90\n\n150 = 0.6 or 60% say that a parent has done their\nhomework for them at least once.\n\n(a)   Explain how to pick a simple random sample of 500 students for the\n\nanonymous survey.\n(b)   What is wrong with using 0.6 to calculate a confidence interval of the\n\nproportion of all high school students in the city for whom a parent has\ndone their homework for them at least once?\n(c)   Given the above sample, what is an estimate of the number of high school\n\nstudents in the city for whom a parent has done their homework for them\nat least once?", "model": "(a) 给12500名学生编号1~12500,用随机数表或随机数发生器产生号码,遇重复或超范围则跳过,直到选出500个不重复号码,对应对学生调查。\n(b) 0.6是\"曾由他人代做作业\"的150人的子样本中的比例,该子样本并非来自总体的随机样本,不满足推断条件,不能据此对全体学生构造置信区间。\n(c) 估计=12500×(150/500)×0.6=12500×0.18=2250名学生。", "image": "img/fr560.png"}, {"id": 561, "chapter": "FR 统计推断", "stem": "In a random sample of 915 adults, 366 say they believe in ghosts.\n\n(a)   With what margin of error can we find a 95% confidence interval of the\n\nproportion of adults who believe in ghosts?\n(b)   With what confidence can we report a margin of error of ±2 percent in\n\ngiving a confidence interval of the proportion of adults who believe in\n\nghosts?", "model": "(a) p̂=366/915=0.40。ME=1.96×√(0.4×0.6/915)=1.96×0.0162≈0.032,即±3.2%。\n(b) 要求ME=0.02:z=0.02/0.0162≈1.23,置信水平=P(|Z|<1.23)≈2×0.8907-1≈0.78,约78%。", "image": "img/fr561.png"}, {"id": 562, "chapter": "FR 统计推断", "stem": "Among all the banking firms in a large city, five are randomly selected. From\neach of these, 10 employees are randomly chosen.\n\n(a)   Is this a simple random sample (SRS)? Why or why not? Explain why your\n\nanswer would or would not change if you knew that all banking firms in the\ncity had the same number of employees.\n(b)   Suppose that 38 of the 50 selected employees are banking assistants.\n\nCalculate a 95% confidence interval estimate for the proportion of this\ncity’s banking firm employees who are banking assistants.", "model": "(a) 不是SRS。这是整群(聚类)抽样:先随机抽5家银行,再从各家抽10人,并非每个由50人组成的样本组被抽到的机会都相同。即使各银行人数相同,抽样单位仍是银行(群)而非个人,依旧不是SRS。\n(b) p̂=38/50=0.76,95%CI:0.76±1.96√(0.76×0.24/50)=0.76±1.96×0.0604=0.76±0.118,即(0.642, 0.878)。", "image": "img/fr562.png"}, {"id": 563, "chapter": "FR 统计推断", "stem": "A survey is to be conducted to determine the proportion of young adults who\nearn more than $7.25 per hour (minimum wage). Two sampling methods are\nbeing considered. Method A involves standing on a downtown street corner and\nrandomly picking several young adults to interview every 10 minutes during a\n12-hour period. Method B involves posting the question on several popular\nyoung adult websites; the viewer simply has to click on one of two possible\nanswers to participate.\n\n(a)   State a possible source of bias for Method A, and describe how it may\n\naffect the results.\n(b)   State a possible source of bias for Method B, and describe how it may\n\naffect the results.\n(c)   How many young adults should be interviewed to estimate the proportion\n\nof the young adult population who earn more than $7.25 to within ±0.05\nwith 95% confidence?\n(d)   If separate estimates are desired for males and females, identify and\n\ndescribe a proper sampling method.", "model": "(a) 抽样偏差/便利样本:仅在市中心街头抽样,遗漏不上街或不在此处出现的年轻人,且市区人群收入可能偏高,可能高估时薪超过$7.25的比例。\n(b) 自愿回答偏差+网络样本:只有访问特定网站的年轻人自愿点击参与,代表性强偏,结果无法外推,方向难以确定但可信度低。\n(c) 取p̂=0.5最保守:n=1.96²×0.25/0.05²=384.2,需调查385人。\n(d) 分层随机抽样:按性别把年轻人总体分为两层,在每层内分别简单随机抽样,可分别估计男女并合并估计总体。", "image": "img/fr563.png"}, {"id": 564, "chapter": "FR 统计推断", "stem": "A senator’s approval rating stood at 65 percent before she took a crucial vote.\n\n(a)   Her staff believes the rating is still around 65 percent. To confirm this,\n\nhow large of a simple random sample (SRS) should the staff sample to\nobtain a 94% confidence interval estimate with a margin of error ≤3.5%?\n(b)   The senator’s staff randomly samples 700 people and finds 432 people\n\napprove of the senator’s job performance. Is there evidence that the rating\nhas changed from 65 percent? Perform an appropriate statistical test.\n(c)   In part (b) above, suppose the staff suspects the rating has gone down. Is\n\nthere evidence that the approval rating has slipped down from 65 percent?\nPerform an appropriate statistical test.\n(d)   Are the answers in (b) and (c) contradictory? Explain.", "model": "(a) 用p=0.65:n=(1.88×√(0.65×0.35)/0.035)²=(1.88×0.4770/0.035)²=(25.66)²≈658,取658人(用p̂=0.65代入得657~658均可)。\n(b) p̂=432/700=0.617,z=(0.617-0.65)/√(0.65×0.35/700)=(-0.033)/0.018=-1.82,双侧P≈0.069>0.05,无充分证据表明支持率变化。\n(c) 单侧检验Ha:p<0.65:P≈0.034<0.05,有证据表明支持率下滑。\n(d) 不矛盾:两次检验数据相同,只是(c)用了单侧备择,把\"上升或下降\"的双侧拒绝域全部集中在下降一侧,故更易拒绝。", "image": "img/fr564.png"}, {"id": 565, "chapter": "FR 统计推断", "stem": "Below are all the scores of a school’s AP Statistics students on a practice 40\nquestion multiple-choice exam.\n\n33 31 37 39 27 31 40 36 27 27\n27 30 34 38 27 29 27 38 37 40\n33 36 29 26 34 32 39 32 39 36\n32 32 25 31 26 40 33 37 29 26\n35 26 37 33 27 28 32 37 33 32\n\n(a)   Using the following line from a random number table, explain and carry\n\nout a procedure to select a simple random sample of size 10 from the\npopulation above.\n\n77219 48190 20235 26836 23590\n44492 14607 09431 75299 42662\n\n(b)   Using this sample, and assuming all conditions for inference are met,\n\nconstruct a 90 percent confidence interval for the population mean µ.\n(c)   The true population mean is 32.44. Is it in your interval, and is this\n\nunexpected?", "model": "(a) 给50个成绩编号01~50,从随机数表依次读两位数,舍去00、>50及重复者:77✗,21✓,94✗,81✗,90✗,20✓,23✓(52✗,68✗),36✓,23重复,59✗,04✓,44✓,49✓,21重复,46✓,07✓,09✓…得21,20,23,36,04,44,49,46,07,09号学生。\n(b) 由抽中的10个成绩算x̄与s,90%t区间(df=9):x̄±1.833×s/√10(需条件满足)。\n(c) 视样本而定:该区间是90%之一,约有90%的机会包含真值32.44;无论是否包含都不意外——置信区间是对方法而非单次区间的保证。", "image": "img/fr565.png"}, {"id": 566, "chapter": "FR 统计推断", "stem": "The goals scored per game during a 21-game span for each of two hockey teams\nis shown below.\n\n(a)   The standard deviation of goals scored for Team A is 1.5. Explain what this\n\nsays about variability.\n(b)   Looking at the dotplots, what can be said about the difference between\n\ngoals scored by the two teams? Explain.\n(c)   A 95% confidence interval estimate for the difference in mean goals scored\n\nper game is (–0.17, 1.88). From this calculation, is there evidence of a\ndifference? Explain.", "model": null, "image": "img/fr566.png"}, {"id": 567, "chapter": "FR 统计推断", "stem": "In 2015, processed meat was classified as a carcinogen by the World Health\nOrganization (WHO). In a representative sample of eight months, a student\ncounts how many times a month he eats processed meat at school. In an\n\nindependent representative sample of eight months, he counts how many times\nhe eats processed meat at home. The data are shown in the following table.\n\nSchool\n4\n5\n5\n3\n6\n4\n5\n7\n\nHome\n4\n3\n4\n4\n3\n2\n5\n4\n\nCalculate a 90% confidence interval estimate for the difference between the\nmean number of times a month this student eats processed meat at school and\nat home.", "model": "两独立样本均值差的t区间。x̄1(学校)=4.875,s1≈1.246;x̄2(家)=3.625,s2≈0.916。差=1.25,SE=√(1.246²/8+0.916²/8)=√(0.194+0.105)=√0.299≈0.547。df取7,t*=1.895。90%CI:1.25±1.895×0.547=1.25±1.04,即(0.21, 2.29)。学校均值每月约比家多0.2~2.3次。", "image": "img/fr567.png"}, {"id": 568, "chapter": "FR 统计推断", "stem": "In a random sample of eight students taking a college writing class, the weights\nin ounces of their term papers and their resulting grades are tabulated below.\nAssume all conditions for inference are satisfied.\n\nWeight (oz)\n12.1\n11.1\n11.1\n6.5\n4.7\n10.7\n5.9\n14.4\n\nGrade\n78\n79\n76\n65\n67\n79\n77\n94\n\n(a)   Predict the mean grade for all students who turn in term papers weighing\n\n1 pound (16 ounces).\n(b)   Find a 95% confidence interval for the average increase in grade for each\n\nadditional ounce in weight.", "model": "回归成绩y对重量x:x̄=9.5625,ȳ=76.875,b=Sxy/Sxx≈134.0/82.3≈1.63,a=76.875-1.63×9.5625≈61.3。\n(a) x=16盎司:ŷ=61.3+1.63×16≈87.4分。\n(b) s≈√(SSE/6)≈5.9,SE(b)=s/√Sxx≈5.9/9.07≈0.65。95%CI(df=6,t*=2.447):1.63±2.447×0.65=1.63±1.59,约(0.04, 3.22),即每增1盎司成绩平均提高0.04~3.22分。", "image": "img/fr568.png"}, {"id": 569, "chapter": "FR 统计推断", "stem": "Data on the number of ice cream cones sold on a weekday night and outside\ntemperature for a simple random sample (SRS) of 10 summer days are\ngathered. A scatterplot and regression output follow.\n\n(a)   From the above output, what is the equation of the regression line?\n(b)   Interpret the slope of the regression line in context.\n(c)   Assuming all conditions for inference are satisfied, compute the margin of\n\nerror for a 95% confidence interval for the slope.\n\nA dispatcher for a trucking firm believes the greater the number of hours slept\nbefore a trucker begins a long haul, the higher the trucker will score on an\nalertness test required at the halfway distance. The dispatcher looks up the\nrecords of a random sample of 18 long-haul truckers and has them fill out a\nquestionnaire about hours of sleep. Some computer graphical output is shown\nbelow.", "model": null, "image": "img/fr569.png"}, {"id": 570, "chapter": "FR 统计推断", "stem": "(a)   Is this an observational study or an experiment? Explain.\n(b)   Comment on the design of the study.\n(c)   The dispatcher took a statistics course long ago and plans to do a test of\n\nsignificance for evidence of an association between score and hours sleep.\nComment on whether the necessary assumptions are met for inference on\nslope for a least squares regression line.", "model": null, "image": "img/fr570.png"}, {"id": 571, "chapter": "FR 统计推断", "stem": "A random sample of adults with various THC levels (a urine test for cannabis)\nwere given a concentration test consisting of a series of small objects, each of\nwhich could be fit into an appropriate shaped hole. Each person had five\nminutes to find the proper holes for as many objects as possible. Computer\nregression output of number of objects successfully placed plotted against\nurine THC level (100 ng/mL) is as follows.\n\n(a)   What is the equation of the regression line?\n(b)   Find a 95% confidence interval for the slope, and interpret in context.\n\nAssume all conditions for inference are satisfied.\n(c)   Find a 95% confidence interval for the y-intercept, and interpret in\n\ncontext. Assume all conditions for inference are satisfied.", "model": null, "image": "img/fr571.png"}, {"id": 572, "chapter": "FR 统计推断", "stem": "A D1 university recruiter claims that 10 percent of its baseball players go on to\nplay professionally after graduation. A reporter contacts a simple random\n\nsample (SRS) of baseball players who graduated during the past 20 years and\nfinds that only 32 out of 450 went on to play professionally. Is there sufficient\nevidence to write an article disputing the university’s claim? Give statistical\njustification for your conclusion.", "model": "H0:p=0.10,Ha:p<0.10。p̂=32/450=0.0711,检查np0=45、n(1-p0)=405≥10。z=(0.0711-0.10)/√(0.10×0.90/450)=(-0.0289)/0.01414≈-2.04。单侧P≈0.021(双侧≈0.041)<0.05,拒绝H0。样本比例显著低于10%,记者有统计依据质疑该校说法。", "image": "img/fr572.png"}, {"id": 573, "chapter": "FR 统计推断", "stem": "In past years, 3 percent of all job applicants lied about their education. The HR\ndivision of a major company believes the true figure is now higher and plans to\ninvestigate a simple random sample (SRS) of applicants to test the hypothesis.\n\n(a)   Explain why it would not be appropriate to run a one-proportion z-test on\n\nan SRS of 150 applicants.\n(b)   What is the minimum sample size necessary to run this hypothesis test?\n(c)   Suppose the HR division uses your result from part (b) and finds that 16 of\n\nthe applicants lied about their salary. Is this sufficient evidence to say that\nthe percentage of applicants lying about their salary is now over 3 percent?", "model": "(a) H0:p=0.03,n=150时np0=150×0.03=4.5<10(成功数期望不足),不满足正态近似条件,不宜做单比例z检验。\n(b) 需np0≥10且n(1-p0)≥10:由0.03n≥10得n≥333.3,最小样本量334。\n(c) p̂=16/334≈0.048,np0=10.02满足条件。z=(0.048-0.03)/√(0.03×0.97/334)=0.018/0.00935≈1.93,单侧P≈0.027<0.05,有充分证据表明说谎比例已超过3%。", "image": "img/fr573.png"}, {"id": 574, "chapter": "FR 统计推断", "stem": "(a) In a random survey of 500 men who asked a woman’s father for permission\nbefore asking the woman for her hand in marriage, 143 said the marriage\nended in divorce. In a random survey of 500 men who did not first ask a\nwoman’s father for permission, 169 of the marriages ended in divorce. Is there\nconvincing statistical evidence that men who ask a woman’s father for\npermission before asking the woman have marriages with lower divorce rates?\n\n(b) Based on the above study, a marriage counselor tells young men that if they\nwant to lower the probability of divorce, they should ask a father’s permission\nbefore asking a woman for her hand in marriage. Is the counselor’s advice\njustified based on this study? Explain.", "model": "(a) H0:p1=p2,Ha:p1<p2。p̂1=143/500=0.286,p̂2=169/500=0.338,合并p̂=0.312。SE=√(0.312×0.688×(2/500))≈0.0293,z=(0.286-0.338)/0.0293≈-1.78,单侧P≈0.037<0.05,拒绝H0:有充分统计证据表明先征求父亲同意的离婚率更低。\n(b) 不合理。这是观察研究而非实验,存在大量混杂变量(如传统观念、家庭背景同时影响是否征求意见与婚姻稳定性),不能据此建立因果关系,咨询师的建议得不到研究支持。", "image": "img/fr574.png"}, {"id": 575, "chapter": "FR 统计推断", "stem": "It is difficult to distinguish between marshmallows and mushrooms by taste\nalone if one is not allowed to see or smell. A person claims he can distinguish\nbetween these, and the following test is designed. He will be given a sample of\neach in random order to taste while blindfolded with his nose pinched. This\nwill be repeated 16 times. Let p be the proportion of times the person answers\ncorrectly.\n\n(a)   What are the null and alternative hypotheses?\n(b)   Suppose he correctly answers in 12 out of the 16 trials. What is the\n\nprobability of answering exactly 12 of 16 if he is simply guessing?\n(c)   What is the P-value if he answers 12 of 16, and interpret this in context.\n(d)   Is there sufficient evidence to reject the null hypothesis? Give an answer\n\nin context.", "model": "(a) H0:p=0.5(纯猜测),Ha:p>0.5(确有辨别能力)。\n(b) P(X=12)=C(16,12)(0.5)^16=1820/65536≈0.0278。\n(c) P-value=P(X≥12)=(1820+560+120+16+1)/65536=2517/65536≈0.038。若他只是瞎猜,16次中答对12次及以上的概率约3.8%。\n(d) 在α=0.05下0.038<0.05,拒绝H0,有足够证据认为他确能区分棉花糖与蘑菇(答对率显著高于猜测)。", "image": "img/fr575.png"}, {"id": 576, "chapter": "FR 统计推断", "stem": "A company advertises it has a process that can extract a mean of 35 grams of\ndissolved salts from 1 liter of seawater. A geologist believes the true figure is\nlower. Using this process, a sample of fifteen 1-liter containers\n\nof seawater from 15 random locations yields a mean of 34.82 grams of\ndissolved salts with a standard deviation of 0.65 grams. Assume the sample\ndistribution is symmetric and unimodal with no outliers.\n\n(a)   Is there sufficient evidence for the geologist to dispute the advertisement?\n\nJustify your answer.\n(b)   A large-scale test of a second company’s process shows yields of dissolved\n\nsalts that are roughly normally distributed with a mean of 34.75 grams and\na standard deviation of 0.83 grams. What is the probability that using this\nsecond process, a 1-liter container of seawater will yield at least 35 grams\nof dissolved salts?\n(c)   What is the probability that using this second process on 10 randomly\n\nselected 1-liter containers of seawater, at least 2 of them yield at least 35\ngrams of dissolved salts?", "model": "(a) H0:μ=35,Ha:μ<35,单样本t检验。t=(34.82-35)/(0.65/√15)=(-0.18)/0.168≈-1.07,df=14,单侧P≈0.15>0.05,不能拒绝H0,地质学家暂无充分证据否定广告。\n(b) X~N(34.75,0.83²):z=(35-34.75)/0.83=0.30,P(X≥35)≈0.382。\n(c) 单罐概率p=0.382,n=10。P(至少2罐)=1-P(0)-P(1)=1-0.618^10-10×0.382×0.618^9≈1-0.008-0.051≈0.941。", "image": "img/fr576.png"}, {"id": 577, "chapter": "FR 统计推断", "stem": "The cash register totals are noted at a random sampling of 100 sales at each of\n2 competing hardware stores.\n\nStore\n$5\n$10\n$25\n$50\nMean\nSD\n\nA\n10\n35\n48\n7\n$19.50\n$11.56\n\nB\n40\n30\n15\n15\n$16.25\n$15.72\n\n(a)   Is the mean sale in Store A significantly greater than the mean sale in\n\nStore B? Explain.\n(b)   Is the proportion of $50 sales in Store A significantly less than the\n\nproportion of $50 sales in Store B? Explain.\n(c)   Using the above results, what should each hardware store emphasize to its\n\nstockholders?", "model": "(a) 两样本t检验:H0:μA=μB,Ha:μA>μB。差=19.50-16.25=3.25,SE=√(11.56²/100+15.72²/100)=√(1.336+2.471)≈1.95,t=3.25/1.95≈1.67,df≈174,单侧P≈0.048<0.05,勉强显著:A店平均销售额显著更高(但仅略低于临界,证据边缘)。\n(b) p̂A=0.07,p̂B=0.15,合并0.11,SE=√(2×0.11×0.89/100)=0.044,z=(0.07-0.15)/0.044≈-1.81,单侧P≈0.035<0.05,A店$50大额交易比例显著更低。\n(c) A店向股东强调更高的平均单笔销售额;B店可强调其$50大额交易比例显著更高(但平均较低,应说明客群差异)。", "image": "img/fr577.png"}, {"id": 578, "chapter": "FR 统计推断", "stem": "To test whether the company’s secretaries type letters quicker from listening to\nsomeone speak live or from recorded dictation, an office manager randomly\nselects five secretaries. He instructs them to time themselves (in seconds)\ntyping a long letter while someone reads the letter to them and then time\nthemselves again typing the same letter from a recording of someone reading\nthe letter. The results are summarized in the following table.\n\nSecretary\n1\n2\n3\n4\n5\n\nLive Dictation Time (in seconds)\n241\n247\n217\n255\n255\n\nRecorded Dictation Time (in\n\nseconds)\n239\n248\n217\n257\n259\n\n(a)   Comment on the design of this experiment.\n(b)   Is the office manager justified in claiming that recorded dictation leads to\n\nslower typing speeds? Support your answer with appropriate statistical\nevidence.", "model": "(a) 配对设计合理(同一秘书打同一封信,控制个体差异),但顺序未随机化:所有秘书都先现场后录音,练习/疲劳等顺序效应与处理效应混杂;且样本仅5人,难以检验正态性。\n(b) 差d=现场-录音:2,-1,0,-2,-4,d̄=-1,s≈2.24。H0:μd=0。t=-1/(2.24/√5)≈-1.00,df=4,双侧P≈0.37>0.05,不能拒绝H0:无充分证据表明录音听写打字更慢。", "image": "img/fr578.png"}, {"id": 579, "chapter": "FR 统计推断", "stem": "In a random sample of 20 junior boxers, their weights are noted and a\nhistogram and summary statistics are as follows.\n\nTheir trainer wants all of those over 167 pounds to lose weight.\n\n(a)   Is 167 pounds in the 95% confidence interval for the mean weight?\n(b)   Is there evidence at the 5% significance level that the mean weight is less\n\nthan 167 pounds? Perform an appropriate hypothesis test.\n(c)   Is there a contradiction between the answers in (a) and (b) above? Explain.", "model": null, "image": "img/fr579.png"}, {"id": 580, "chapter": "FR 统计推断", "stem": "Each of 300 students was randomly (coin toss) placed into either Professor A’s\nor Professor B’s section of a required college writing course. At the end of the\nsemester, students gave a score from 1 to 5 with “poor” = 1 and “great” = 5 for\ntheir professor. The results are shown in the following table.\n\nScore\n1\n2\n3\n4\n5\n\nProfessor A Frequency\n16\n41\n53\n38\n13\n\nProfessor B Frequency\n45\n50\n29\n11\n4\n\n(a)   Use a graphical display to compare the scores received. Write a few\n\nsentences, based on your graphs, to compare Professor A and Professor B.\n\n(b)   Does there appear to be significantly greater satisfaction with Professor A\n\nas compared to Professor B? Give a statistical justification for your answer.", "model": "(a) 画并列(分段)条形图或双向对比图:A评分集中在3~4分,B集中在1~2分;A分布近似居中偏右,B左偏(低分)。Professor A明显获得更高评价、满意度更高,B不满意者众多。\n(b) 比较\"满意\"(4或5分)比例:A=51/161≈0.317,B=15/139≈0.108,合并p̂=66/300=0.22。SE=√(0.22×0.78×(1/161+1/139))≈0.0479,z=(0.317-0.108)/0.0479≈4.4,P<0.0001。有充分证据表明学生对A教授的满意度显著更高。", "image": "img/fr580.png"}, {"id": 581, "chapter": "FR 统计推断", "stem": "A handyman wishes to test which radial arm saw can cut through lumber more\nquickly. He picks out a random sampling of five pieces of lumber of various\nspecies and thicknesses. The cutting times (in seconds) are summarized in the\nfollowing table.\n\nBoard\nA\nB\nC\nD\nE\n\nSaw S\n3.1\n6.8\n1.5\n4.2\n2.8\n\nSaw T\n3.3\n6.7\n1.8\n4.5\n3.0\n\n(a)   What is the mean cutting time for each saw?\n(b)   The handyman performs a two-sample t-test with H0: µS = µT and Ha: µS ≠\n\nµT , which results in a P-value of 0.886. He concludes that there is not\nsignificant evidence of a difference in cutting times. Comment on his choice\nof test.\n(c)   Perform the proper test to decide if there is significant evidence of a\n\ndifference in cutting times. Use a significance level of 0.10.", "model": "(a) 均值S=(3.1+6.8+1.5+4.2+2.8)/5=3.68秒;T=(3.3+6.7+1.8+4.5+3.0)/5=3.86秒。\n(b) 不当:同一块木板被两把锯各切一次,是配对设计,应做配对t检验;两样本t检验忽略了数据配对、损失了功效。\n(c) 差d=S-T:-0.2,0.1,-0.3,-0.3,-0.2,d̄=-0.18,s≈0.164。t=-0.18/(0.164/√5)≈-2.45,df=4,双侧P≈0.070<0.10,拒绝H0:在0.10水平有显著证据表明两锯切割时间不同(T略慢)。", "image": "img/fr581.png"}, {"id": 582, "chapter": "FR 统计推断", "stem": "At schools using an innovative math program, a simple random sample (SRS)\nof 100 students results in an average score of 178 with a standard deviation of\n27 on a state test. At schools using a traditional approach, an SRS of 150\nstudents results in an average score of 171 with a standard deviation of 31 on\nthe same state test.\n\n(a)   Is there evidence that students using the innovative approach have a\n\nhigher average score than students using the traditional approach? Give\nstatistical justification for your answer.\n(b)   Suppose a study using this design resulted in a P-value less than 0.01.\n\nWould it be reasonable for all school boards to push for adoption of the\ninnovative approach? Explain.\n(c)   Assuming standard deviations of 27 and 31 as listed above, how large a\n\nsample (same number for both) should be used to be 95 percent sure of\nknowing the difference in scores to within 5 points?", "model": "(a) H0:μ1=μ2,Ha:μ1>μ2。t=(178-171)/√(27²/100+31²/150)=7/√(7.29+6.41)=7/3.70≈1.89,df≈244,单侧P≈0.030<0.05,有证据创新教学法平均分更高。\n(b) 不合理。这是观察研究,存在自选择与学校背景等混杂因素,不能推断因果;且样本学校未必代表所有学区,外推需谨慎。\n(c) 1.96×√((729+961)/n)=5⇒√(1690/n)=2.551⇒n=1690/6.51≈260,每组约260人。", "image": "img/fr582.png"}, {"id": 583, "chapter": "FR 统计推断", "stem": "An athletic trainer wishes to determine if a newly designed gym shoe enhances\nathletic performance. Ten professional high jumpers competitively jump on\ntwo successive days. For each jumper, a coin toss determines whether he uses\n\nhis old gym shoes or the new pair on the first day. Each jumper then switches\nshoes on the next day. Their jump heights (feet) are as follows.\n\n(a)   To determine if there is sufficient statistical evidence on whether\n\nprofessional high jumpers improve their jumps when wearing the new\nshoes, what statistical test should be used, what are the hypotheses, and are\nthe conditions for inference met? (You are not asked to perform the test.)\n(b)   Does knowing a jumper’s jump height with the old shoes help predict his\n\njump height using the new shoes? Perform an appropriate statistical test.", "model": null, "image": "img/fr583.png"}, {"id": 584, "chapter": "FR 统计推断", "stem": "In a baseball game, the slugging average is the mean number of bases per hit.\nIt can be calculated using the following formula.\n\nSlugging average = (Singles) + (Doubles × 2) + (Triples  × 3) + (Home runs × 4)\n\nTotal at bats\n\nIn a random sampling of 75 hits from each of two major league teams, the\ndistributions of singles (1B), doubles (2B), triples (3B), and homers (HR) are\nshown in the following table.\n\n\n1B\n2B\n3B\nHR\n\nTeam A\n44\n17\n10\n4\n\nTeam B\n34\n22\n12\n7\n\nIs there evidence that the two teams have a different slugging average? Give\nstatistical justification for your answer.", "model": "把每支安打折算垒数:A队样本总垒数=44×1+17×2+10×3+4×4=124,均值1.653;s1≈0.90;B队=34+44+36+28=142,均值1.893;s2≈0.99。两样本t:H0:μA=μB,Ha:μA≠μB。SE=√(0.813/75+0.975/75)≈0.154,t=(1.653-1.893)/0.154≈-1.55,df≈147,双侧P≈0.12>0.05。不能拒绝H0:无充分证据表明两队长打率不同。", "image": "img/fr584.png"}, {"id": 585, "chapter": "FR 统计推断", "stem": "Can a particular video game improve a batter’s reaction time? Batters’\nreaction times (fraction of a second between the ball leaving a pitcher’s hand\nand the start of a swing) are measured before and after playing the video game\nfor 25 hours.\n\n(a)   What is the appropriate hypothesis test, the hypotheses, and the conditions\n\nto check?\n(b)   Suppose the test is run and no statistically significant improvement is\n\ndetected in batter reaction times after the video game training. If the\nresearcher plans a second test, name two specific changes that can be made\nto increase the power of the test. Explain your choices.", "model": "(a) 配对t检验(同一击球手训练前后配对)。H0:μd=0(或≥0),Ha:μd<0,d=训练后-训练前(反应时间减少为改善)。条件:样本随机/击球手相互独立;差值近似正态(样本小时需画差值图检查,或无强偏斜离群值)。\n(b) 提高功效的两法:①增大样本量(更多击球手),使标准误减小、更易检出差异;②提高显著性水平α(如0.05→0.10),临界值变小,更易拒绝H0(但增加第一类错误风险)。也可延长训练时间以加大真实效应。", "image": "img/fr585.png"}, {"id": 586, "chapter": "FR 统计推断", "stem": "A study is performed on 20 different models of luxury SUVs to see if a new gas\nadditive is effective in reducing carbon dioxide (CO2) emissions. Data on CO2\nemissions (pounds per gallon of gas) are collected before and after input of the\nadditive. A partial computer output follows.\n\nN\nMean\nStDev\nSE Mean\n\nBefore\n20\n20.60\n7.35\n1.64\n\nAfter\n20\n20.05\n6.84\n1.53\n\nDifference\n20\n0.55\n1.90\n0.42\n\n(a)   Assuming that graphical displays indicate that assumptions of normality\n\nare reasonable, construct a 95% confidence interval for the mean\ndifference in CO2 emissions before and after the additive. Interpret your\ninterval in context.\n(b)   Does this confidence interval give sufficient evidence that this additive\n\nreduces mean CO2 emissions for luxury SUVs?", "model": "(a) 配对t区间:差d̄=0.55,SE=0.42,df=19,t*=2.093。95%CI:0.55±2.093×0.42=0.55±0.88,即(-0.33, 1.43)。解释:有95%把握认为添加剂使每加仑CO₂排放平均减少量(前-后)在-0.33到1.43磅之间。\n(b) 区间包含0,故无充分证据表明添加剂显著降低平均CO₂排放。", "image": "img/fr586.png"}, {"id": 587, "chapter": "FR 统计推断", "stem": "A World Health Organization (WHO) report gives average life expectancies\nfor 10 randomly chosen countries in each of two regions of the world:\n\nRegion 1: 63, 72, 48, 67, 68, 70, 59, 51, 63, 52\n\nRegion 2: 78, 73, 57, 69, 74, 86, 70, 66, 91, 77\n\n(a)   Create a back-to-back stemplot to display this data.\n(b)   Compare the two distributions.\n(c)   A two-sample t-test was performed on the above data with H0: µ1 – µ2 = 0\n\nand Ha: µ1 – µ2 < 0. Conditions for inference were met, the test statistic was\nt = –3.134, and the P-value was 0.0029. Write a proper conclusion.", "model": "(a) 茎叶图(茎=期望值十位):Region1: 4|8, 5|129, 6|3378, 7|02;Region2: 5|7, 6|69, 7|03478, 8|6, 9|1(背靠背排列)。\n(b) Region2均值明显更高(约74.1 vs 61.3),两者离散程度相近(R1略集中);Region1整体偏低、集中在50-72,Region2分布更高更宽(含86、91两个高值)。\n(c) P=0.0029<0.05,拒绝H0:有充分证据表明Region 1的平均预期寿命低于Region 2。", "image": "img/fr587.png"}, {"id": 588, "chapter": "FR 统计推断", "stem": "A random sample of five men were measured for waist sizes (in inches) and\npercent body fat, giving the following data.\n\nWaist (inches)\n32\n35\n36\n39\n44\n\nBody Fat (%)\n12\n23\n24\n30\n40\n\n(a)   Is there evidence of a positive linear relationship? You may refer to the\n\nfollowing computer regression printouts.\n\n(b)   Predict the percent body fat for a man with a 37-inch waist.\n(c)   Give an approximate range for percent body fat for a man with a 37-inch\n\nwaist.", "model": "回归(体脂对腰围):b=Sxy/Sxx=184.2/82.8≈2.22,a=25.8-2.22×37.2≈-57.0,r≈0.987。\n(a) H0:β=0,Ha:β>0。t=r√(n-2)/√(1-r²)=0.987×1.732/0.162≈10.6,df=3,P<0.005(单侧),拒绝H0:有充分证据表明正相关。\n(b) ŷ=-57.0+2.22×37≈25.3%体脂。\n(c) s≈√(SSE/3)≈1.9,粗略区间约ŷ±2s≈25.3±3.8,即约21.5%~29.1%(近似预测范围,非精确预测区间)。", "image": "img/fr588.png"}, {"id": 589, "chapter": "FR 统计推断", "stem": "Doctors are planning a clinical trial of a new “molecularly targeted therapy”\nfor the treatment of leukemia. A random sample of children with leukemia will\nbe treated. After a suitable time period, their disease remission rate will be\ncompared with children being treated with standard chemotherapy for\nleukemia.\n\n(a)   What are the null and alternative hypotheses for this significance test?\n(b)   What is a Type I error in this context, and what is the consequence of\n\ncommitting this error?\n(c)   What is a Type II error in this context, and what is the consequence of\n\ncommitting this error?", "model": "(a) 设p₁=新疗法缓解率,p₂=标准化疗缓解率。H0:p₁=p₂(或p₁≤p₂),Ha:p₁>p₂(新疗法缓解率更高)。\n(b) 第一类错误:新疗法实际不比化疗好,却判断其更好。后果:患儿放弃有效化疗改用无效新疗法,延误治疗。\n(c) 第二类错误:新疗法实际更好,却未检验出差异。后果:患儿失去接受更有效疗法的机会,继续承受疗效较差的化疗。", "image": "img/fr589.png"}, {"id": 590, "chapter": "FR 统计推断", "stem": "Executives at a company that manufactures a cooler are thinking of moving to\nthe manufacture of a better cooler but only if a test shows that the new cooler\nwill keep drinks cold for over 24 hours. The company’s research department\nproposes a study with H0: µ = 24 and Ha: µ > 24.\n\n(a)   Describe a Type II error in context of this study, and describe a possible\n\nconsequence.\n\n(b)   Which significance level, α = 0.05 or α = 0.10, would result in a smaller\n\nprobability of a Type II error? Explain.\n(c)   The statistician in the research department estimates the power of the\n\nproposed test to be 0.85 if the true mean is µ = 25 hours. What does this\nmean in the context of this study?", "model": "(a) 第二类错误:H0(μ=24)为假(实际保温超过24小时)却未拒绝H0,公司误以为新冰箱不达标。后果:放弃生产实际更好的产品,错失市场与利润。\n(b) α=0.10。显著性水平越大,拒绝域越大,越容易拒绝H0,检验功效越高,从而第二类错误的概率β越小。\n(c) 功效0.85意味着:若真实平均保温时间确为25小时,该检验有85%的概率正确拒绝H0、判定新冰箱保温超过24小时(β=0.15)。", "image": "img/fr590.png"}, {"id": 591, "chapter": "FR 统计推断", "stem": "In each of the following examples, explain why it is or is not proper to run a\nchi-square goodness-of-fit test.\n\n(a)   The ingredient list on a jar of fancy mixed nuts indicates 10% Brazil nuts,\n\n10% cashews, 20% almonds, and the rest peanuts. Suppose you separate\nand weigh the contents of a 24-ounce jar and find 3 ounces of Brazil nuts, 2\nounces of cashews, 4 ounces of almonds, and 15 ounces of peanuts. Does the\njar follow the advertised pattern?\n(b)   A company website claims that in a bag of its large candies, 10% are red,\n\n20% are blue, 40% are yellow, and 30% are orange. Suppose you open a\nbag with 3 red, 4 blue, 2 yellow, and 10 orange candies. Does the bag follow\nthe advertised pattern?\n(c)   Genetic theory predicts that offspring in a certain experiment should\n\nappear in the ratio 1:3:9. Suppose the offspring number 66, 175, and 980,\nrespectively. Is there sufficient evidence that the number of the dominant\noffspring (980) is greater than expected by the genetic model?", "model": "(a) 适合。把各类重量换算成计数(24盎司中期望为2.4/2.4/4.8/14.4盎司,观测3/2/4/15),类别互斥、总数固定、以比例形式给出期望,可用卡方拟合优度检验(注意个别期望计数如2.4偏小,结论需谨慎)。\n(b) 不适合。期望计数为1.9、3.8、7.6、5.7,多个小于5,不满足\"期望计数≥5(至少大多数)\"的条件,检验不可靠。\n(c) 不适合。拟合优度检验是针对整个分布的整体性检验,无法回答\"显性后代数多于预期\"这一单侧问题;且980远大于期望,应改用单比例z检验。", "image": "img/fr591.png"}, {"id": 592, "chapter": "FR 统计推断", "stem": "In a survey of all 880 students at a college, the number of times during the\nacademic semester in which a student pulled an all-nighter is tabulated and\nshown in the following table.\n\nAll-Nighters Pulled\n0\n1\n2\n3\n4\n5\n\nNumber of Students\n280\n210\n175\n150\n50\n15\n\n(a)   What are µ and σ for all-nighters pulled during a semester for this\n\npopulation?\n(b)   If a simple random sample (SRS) of size n = 50 is taken from this\n\npopulation, describe the sampling distribution of ¯x.\n(c)   A college counselor believes that over the years, the number of all-nighters\n\npulled by students during a semester are 0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5 in the ratio\n15:10:10:10:4:1. Perform an appropriate hypothesis test.", "model": "(a) μ=(0×280+1×210+2×175+3×150+4×50+5×15)/880=1285/880≈1.46次。E[X²]=3435/880≈3.903,σ=√(3.903-1.46²)=√(3.903-2.132)≈1.33次。\n(b) x̄近似正态(总体右偏但n=50较大,CLT):中心=1.46,SD=1.33/√50≈0.19。\n(c) 卡方拟合优度。比例15:10:10:10:4:1(和40)⇒期望计数330,220,220,220,88,22。χ²≈7.58+0.45+9.20+22.27+16.41+2.23≈58.1,df=5,P≈0<0.05,拒绝H0:熬夜次数分布不符合15:10:10:10:4:1。", "image": "img/fr592.png"}, {"id": 593, "chapter": "FR 统计推断", "stem": "A random survey of 250 high school teachers classified job satisfaction versus\nsubject taught with the following results.\n\n\nHappiness Level\n\nSubject Taught\nUnhappy\nMildly Happy\nVery Happy\n\nMath\n10\n25\n30\n\nEnglish\n20\n55\n45\n\nHistory\n20\n25\n20\n\n(a)   What is the probability that a randomly selected teacher from this sample\n\nteaches history and is very happy?\n(b)   What is the probability that a person selected at random from this sample\n\nis mildly happy given that he/she teaches math?\n(c)   What is the probability that a person selected at random from this sample\n\nteaches English given that he/she is unhappy?\n(d)   Is there evidence of a relationship between happiness level and subject\n\ntaught? Explain.", "model": "行合计:数学65、英语120、历史65;列合计:不悦50、略悦105、很悦95,总250。\n(a) P(历史且很悦)=20/250=0.08。\n(b) P(略悦|数学)=25/65≈0.385。\n(c) P(英语|不悦)=20/50=0.40。\n(d) 卡方独立性检验:期望计数如数学行13/27.3/24.7、英语行24/50.4/45.6、历史行13/27.3/24.7。χ²≈0.69+0.19+1.14+0.67+0.42+0.01+3.77+0.19+1.14≈8.2,df=4,P≈0.084>0.05,不能拒绝H0:无充分证据表明幸福感与所教科目有关。", "image": "img/fr593.png"}, {"id": 594, "chapter": "FR 统计推断", "stem": "Random samples of high school students in 2014, 2016, and 2018 were\nanonymously surveyed about whether or not they had ever cheated on a quiz or\ntest. The resulting counts are as shown in the table.\n\n\n2014\n2016\n2018\n\nAdmitted to Cheating\n78\n67\n51\n\nClaimed to Never Having Cheated\n44\n40\n59\n\nWhat does a chi-square test of homogeneity indicate?", "model": "卡方齐性检验。H0:三年作弊比例分布相同。合计:作弊196、不作弊143,总数339;各年期望作弊数为70.5/61.9/63.6,不作弊51.5/45.1/46.4。χ²≈0.79+0.43+2.50+1.08+0.58+3.42≈8.8,df=2,P≈0.012<0.05,拒绝H0:三年间作弊情况分布有显著变化,承认作弊的比例呈下降趋势(2014约64%→2018约46%)。", "image": "img/fr594.png"}, {"id": 595, "chapter": "FR 统计推断", "stem": "During the past year, the proportions of teens eating at Starbucks, Chick-fil-A,\nand Chipotle have been 0.3, 0.5, and 0.2, respectively. A random survey of 250\nteens this past month indicates 71, 136, and 43 visits to Starbucks, Chick-fil-A,\nand Chipotle, respectively.\n\n(a)   Is there evidence that the overall pattern of restaurant visits has changed?\n\nGive statistical justification for your answer.\n(b)   A sales manager at Starbucks would like to know her company’s\n\nproportion of this past month’s restaurant visits with 95% confidence.\nWhat is the margin of error?\n\n(c)   A sales manager at Chick-fil-A would like to know his company’s\n\nproportion of last month’s restaurant visits within a margin of error of\n±0.03. What would be the underlying level of confidence?", "model": "(a) 卡方拟合优度:H0:比例仍为0.3/0.5/0.2。期望计数75/125/50。χ²=(71-75)²/75+(136-125)²/125+(43-50)²/50≈0.21+0.97+0.98=2.16,df=2,P≈0.34>0.05,不能拒绝H0:无充分证据表明光顾模式改变。\n(b) p̂=71/250=0.284,ME=1.96√(0.284×0.716/250)≈1.96×0.0285≈0.056,即±5.6%。\n(c) p̂=136/250=0.544,SE=√(0.544×0.456/250)≈0.0315,z=0.03/0.0315≈0.95,置信水平≈P(|Z|<0.95)≈66%。", "image": "img/fr595.png"}, {"id": 596, "chapter": "FR 统计推断", "stem": "A social studies teacher believes that high school students taking social studies\nclasses are more likely than students taking other classes to read newspapers.\nShe randomly picks 40 of her social studies students and 50 students from her\nother classes, questions them, and gathers the following data.\n\nRead\nNewspapers\n\nDon’t Read\nNewspapers\n\nHer Social Studies\n\nStudents\n37\n13\n\nHer Other Students\n15\n35\n\n(a)   Are the intended and sampled populations the same or different, and how\n\ndoes this affect the study?\n(b)   Discuss the appropriateness of using a chi-square test of homogeneity.\n(c)   Discuss the appropriateness of using a z-test for differences of proportions.\n(d)   Which would be most appropriate for a quick impression of the data:\n\nsegmented bar charts, back-to-back stemplots, or parallel boxplots? Draw\nthis visual display.", "model": "(a) 目标群体是全校(或更大范围)修社会课与其他课的学生,而样本只是她自己教的学生,二者不同;存在教师本人带来的选择偏差/混淆(教法、要求不同),结果只能推及她自己的课堂,不能推广。\n(b) 齐性检验不恰当:两组并非从两个总体中随机抽取的独立样本,而是她便利挑选的学生,随机抽样条件不满足。\n(c) z检验同样要求随机样本,故条件同样存疑;但若仅就现有数据作探索性比较,两比例差异(92.5% vs 30%)极大,方向明确。\n(d) 分段条形图最合适(分类数据)。两个条:社会课学生读报37/40=92.5%,其他学生15/50=30%,每条内按\"读/不读\"分段填色对比。", "image": "img/fr596.png"}, {"id": 597, "chapter": "FR 统计推断", "stem": "A random sample of 500 medical records of people who recently had major\nheart attacks is analyzed for numbers of died versus survived and for\neducation level of the subjects. Some computer output is as follows.\n\nObserved (and Expected) Counts\n\nNo High School\n\nHigh School\n\nCollege\n\nDegree\n\nDegree\n\nDegree\n\nDied\n18 (9.9)\n38 (42.1)\n24 (28)\n\nSurvived\n44 (52.1)\n225 (220.9)\n151 (147)\n\n(a)   Is there an association between whether or not people survive major heart\n\nattacks and their education levels? Do an appropriate statistical test.\n(b)   Do the segmented bar charts give any additional insights into this study?\n\nExplain.", "model": "(a) 卡方独立性检验(自由度2)。χ²=(18-9.9)²/9.9+(38-42.1)²/42.1+(24-28)²/28+(44-52.1)²/52.1+(225-220.9)²/220.9+(151-147)²/147≈6.63+0.40+0.57+1.26+0.08+0.11≈9.04,P≈0.011<0.05,拒绝H0:心脏病存活与否与教育水平存在显著关联。\n(b) 有:分段条形图直观显示死亡率随教育程度下降(无高中18.3%→高中14.5%→大学13.7%),说明关联主要来自\"无高中\"人群死亡率明显偏高,能补充说明关联的方向与来源。", "image": "img/fr597.png"}, {"id": 598, "chapter": "FR 统计推断", "stem": "A random sampling of a professor’s grades (A or B, C or D, F) in both intro\nand advanced courses yields the following data.\n\n\nA, B\nC, D\nF\n\nIntro\n35\n55\n10\n\nAdvanced\n22\n15\n3\n\n(a)   A chi-square test of independence gives χ2 = 4.749. What is a proper\n\nconclusion?\n(b)   Is there evidence that this professor gives a higher proportion of A, B\n\ngrades in his advanced courses than in his intro courses? Give statistical\njustification for your answer.", "model": "(a) df=(3-1)(2-1)=2,χ²=4.749⇒P≈0.093>0.05,不能拒绝H0:无充分证据表明课程类型与给牌等级有关联。\n(b) H0:p1=p2,Ha:p1>p2。p̂高级=22/40=0.55,p̂入门=35/100=0.35,合并57/140=0.407。SE=√(0.407×0.593×(1/40+1/100))≈0.092,z=0.20/0.092≈2.17,单侧P≈0.015<0.05,拒绝H0:有充分证据表明高级课程A、B等级比例更高。", "image": "img/fr598.png"}, {"id": 599, "chapter": "FR 统计推断", "stem": "500 patients took part in a clinical study using T-cell therapy to treat a\nparticular lymphatic cancer. The following data show the number of these\npatients who survived for each given number of years.\n\nSurvival Years\n0\n1\n2\n3\n4 or more\n\nObserved Number of Patients\n69\n106\n153\n98\n74\n\nIt is hypothesized that the distribution follows the Poisson distribution:\n\nP(x) =\n2x\nx! e−2, where e = 2.71828 and x is the number of survival years.\n\n(a)   Using this Poisson distribution, calculate the probabilities of 0, 1, 2, 3, and\n\n4 or more survival years.\n(b)   Using this Poisson distribution, calculate the expected number of patients\n\nout of 500 who will survive 0, 1, 2, 3, and 4 or more years.\n(c)   Perform a goodness-of-fit test for this data and the given Poisson\n\ndistribution.", "model": "(a) λ=2的泊松:P(0)=e⁻²≈0.1353;P(1)=2e⁻²≈0.2707;P(2)=2e⁻²≈0.2707;P(3)=(8/6)e⁻²≈0.1804;P(≥4)=1-0.8571≈0.1429。\n(b) 期望人数(×500):67.7,135.3,135.3,90.2,71.4。\n(c) 拟合优度检验:χ²=(69-67.7)²/67.7+(106-135.3)²/135.3+(153-135.3)²/135.3+(98-90.2)²/90.2+(74-71.4)²/71.4≈0.03+6.35+2.31+0.67+0.09≈9.5,df=4,P≈0.05>0.05,不能拒绝H0:数据与该泊松分布相容。", "image": "img/fr599.png"}, {"id": 600, "chapter": "FR 统计推断", "stem": "In an educational study, there is interest in whether or not there is more\nvariability in the mean verbal scores of 8-year-olds or of 3-year-olds. Summary\nstatistics are given below.\n\n\nSample Size\nMean\nStDev\n\nAge 8\n60\n93.5\n5.4\n\nAge 3\n60\n78.6\n2.7\n\nTo test H0: σ8 = σ3 versus Ha: σ8 > σ3, the researchers will use the ratio of the\nsample standard deviations, s8\n\ns3 , as a test statistic.\n\n(a)   What is the observed test statistic from the data of this study?\n(b)   A simulation was conducted to study the sampling distribution of this test\n\nstatistic. For each repetition of the simulation, two random samples were\ngenerated using appropriate population means with identical standard\ndeviations, and the ratio of the sample standard deviations was noted. A\nhistogram of the simulated ratios is given below.\n\nIs there statistically significant evidence that there is more variability in the\nmean verbal scores of 8-year-olds than of 3-year-olds?", "model": null, "image": "img/fr600.png"}];
